\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BATTLE OVER ABORTION PILLS WYOMING GOV SIGNS BAN AS TEXAS BRACES FOR RULING\b0\par\par\uc2 542 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BOLTON, TRUMP, PROTEST, COHEN, PENCE\par \par \uc2 BANK FAILURE, CREDIT SUISSE, \uc2 UBS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INTERNATIONAL BANK UNCERTAINTY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPORTS SAY \uc2 UBS\uc2  IS IN TALKS TO TAKE OVER CREDIT SUISSE\par \par \uc2 PUTIN, UKRAINE, CHINA, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTIN MAKES VISIT TO CRIMEA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARKS NINTH ANNIVERSARY OF RUSSIA'S ANNEXATION OF REGION\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILLS, HAWKINS, LONDON, PERSONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE OVER ABORTION PILLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WYOMING GOV SIGNS BAN AS TEXAS BRACES FOR RULING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the battle over abortion and a renewed focus on abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Wyoming's governor signing a ban into law while Texas awaits a judge's ruling on the issue. ABC's Jaclyn Lee is here now with more. Jaclyn, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, good morning. In Wyoming, the ban on those pills takes effect July 1st with 13 states already banning all forms of abortion. A pending court case in Texas could transform the abortion rights landscape entirely.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) New this morning, a growing push by conservative states to outlaw abortion pills. On Friday, Wyoming becoming the first state to make abortion pills illegal with more states expected to follow suit. It makes exceptions for rape, incest, or if the mother's life is in danger. Wyoming Governor \uc2 Mark Gordon\uc2  also allowing a bill to pass that makes obtaining an abortion a felony with abortion providers facing up in five years in prison for performing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIFE IS A HUMAN RIGHT ACT\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Texas is bracing for a federal judge's ruling that could overturn the FDA approval of the abortion pill. The judge appointed by former President Trump. Proponents argue there are complications with the pill causing harm to women.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We know in the pro-life movement that there are significant real risks associated with chemical abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Women's rights advocates protesting outside the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY LONDON (ABORTION RIGHTS ACTIVIST)\par \par \uc2 Medication abortion is the safest and most accessible option now for millions of women across the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILL PROPOSED IN FLORIDA BANS ABORTION AFTER SIX WEEKS\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Over in Florida, Republicans advanced a bill proposing an abortion ban after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JENNA PERSONS (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 What's before you is an attempt to set out a good policy before the state of Florida that promotes life.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abortion rights advocates say many women don't know they're pregnant at six weeks, essentially making this an abortion ban, which they say will have lasting effects across the country.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL-JOHNSON\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO PLANNED PARENTHOOD)\par \par \uc2 People will continue to identify ways to make decisions about their own bodies. These states and these state lawmakers and these governors are just making it harder for them to do so.\par \par \uc2 JACLYN LEE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in regards to the Texas ruling, the judge said he will rule as soon as possible. Gio?\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many watching that. All right, Jaclyn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230413ej3j0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GOOD MORNING AMERICA\b0\par\par\uc2 550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. Donald Trump's prediction.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER PRESIDENT SAYS HE EXPECTS TO BE ARRESTED TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DONALD TRUMP'S PREDICTION\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The former president saying he expects to be arrested on Tuesday following the investigation into alleged hush money payments. A look into the claims. Plus, former Vice President Pence weighs in, in an exclusive one-on-one with \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 UBS\uc2  REPORTEDLY IN TAKEOVER TALKS WITH CREDIT SUISSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BANK WATCH\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Bank watch. \uc2 UBS\uc2  reportedly in takeover talks to merge with struggling \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . The urgent efforts to boost confidence in the banking system. How Warren Buffet is reportedly getting involved and what the crisis could all mean for a Fed decision this week on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN LEADER PAYS VISIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUTIN IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Putin in Ukraine. The Russian leader paying a surprise visit to occupied territory. The defiant gesture and the look ahead to his critical meeting this week with China's President Xi.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING PUSH TO OUTLAW ACCESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Abortion battleground. The growing push by conservative states to outlaw access. Abortion pills declared illegal in Wyoming. Other states expected to follow suit, and the proposal in Florida to ban abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEARED KIDNAPPED IN MEXICO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISSING MOM\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Missing mom. The American mother of seven feared kidnapped in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 ZONIA LOPEZ (DAUGHTER OF MARIA DEL CARMEN LOPEZ)\par \par \uc2 We have not heard from her.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2 's reward for information on her whereabouts.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SETTLEMENTS WITH UNIVERSITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLLEGE ATHLETE'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 College athlete's death. A wrestler collapsing after an intense workout, dying of heatstroke. The multimillion-dollar settlement by the university as his parents speak out this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAUGHT ON CAMERA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRASH LANDING\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Crash landing caught on camera. A pilot with engine trouble forced to land on this beach, shocking a passerby walking her dog.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW OVERNIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARCH MADNESS!\par \par \uc2 SPORTS COMMENTATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Wins it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And March Madness. Arkansas upsets number one seed Kansas, punching a ticket to the Sweet 16.\par \par \uc2 SPORTS COMMENTATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Peters, right corner, he's going to take the three, and he hit it again.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the Cinderella story for number 15 seed Princeton. Plus, the comeback queens of the court.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUNDAY, MARCH 19\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from ABC News in New York, this is "Good Morning America."\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 There's just, like, a brackets graveyard all across the country right now.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yours is broken again?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah. It was broken yesterday. It's more broke if you can even call it that. Yes, but it's been a lot of fun, so much to watch.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. It has.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We'll have more on that coming up later in the show. Good morning, America. We do have a lot cover this morning. Happy to have Gio Benitez back at the desk with us here.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230413ej3j0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER WIND CHILLS AND FREEZE WARNINGS: ARCTIC BLAST\par \par \uc2 ARCTIC, NORTHEAST, FREEZE\par \par \uc2 TURKEY, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , FINLAND, PARLIAMENT, PUTIN, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 XI, PUTIN, CHINA, RUSSIA, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, BYTEDANCE, BIDEN, JEAN-PIERRE, TSARYNNY\par \par \uc2 SVB, BIDEN, BANKS, BANKRUPTCY, MCBRIDE\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DOCUMENTS, GRAND JURY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 , BAN\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, LABOR, STRIKES, FRANCE, RETIREMENT AGE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, ORIGIN, RACCOON DOG, CHINA\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEIGH DICKINSON, PURDUE, MARCH MADNESS, ANDERSON\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This music video. Okay, welcome back to "Gma" on this Saturday morning. It may not be paranoia if you feel like you're being watched. Coming up, we're going to show you how some businesses are using surveillance software to collect data on customers the minute they walk in the door and what that means for your privacy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Ten out of ten for the song, though.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I mean, so good, so good.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The music video gets an 11 from me.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It kind of feels like cameras are watching us, though, right?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, a little bit. A little bit, right? Oh, good. We get no privacy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW: ABORTION PILL BAN\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All right, let's take a look at some of the other big stories that we're following on this Saturday morning. Happening right now, Wyoming is now the first state to ban abortion pills. Governor Mark Gordon signing the bill Friday night. The pills are already outlawed in 13 states that have blanket bans on all forms of abortion.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RADIOACTIVE WATER LEAK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also right now, cleanup under way after 400,000 gallons of radioactive water leaked from a nuclear plant near Minnesota. The news being made public for the first time four months after that leak. The state's pollution control agency says that there's no immediate threat to residents' health and safety. They say the leak was stopped and has not contaminated Minnesota's drinking water.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And this morning, TV and movie fans around the world are celebrating actor Lance Reddick. He was best known for his roles in the series "The Wire" and the "John Wick" films. His rep said the 60-year-old died of natural causes at his LA home. His death comes days before the release of the fourth installment of "John Wick." Keanu Reeves says the film will be dedicated to Reddick.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This was such a shock.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It was. Like you said, 60 is so young and so many of us know him from "The Wire," incredible.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That show, one of the greatest shows on television.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Ever, ever.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 For sure.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230418ej3i00035\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH-STAKES ABORTION PILL HEARING CHALLENGE TO \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVAL OF DRUG COULD LEAD TO NATIONWIDE BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 REAPER DRONE, RUSSIA, AUSTIN, MILLEY\par \par \uc2 NORTH KOREA, MISSILE LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH KOREA FIRES THIRD MISSILE IN A WEEK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAUNCHES ICBM AS U.S. ALLIES SOUTH KOREA AND JAPAN MEET FOR KEY SUMMIT\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, STOCKS, CREDIT SUISSE, BANK COLLAPSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON WALL STREET AS BANK FEARS SPREAD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONCERNS OVER CREDIT SUISSE SPARK STOCK SELL OFF\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, COHEN, DANIELS, GUO\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR STORM MOVING EAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 19 STATES ON ALERT FOR HEAVY SNOW, FLOODING AND AVALANCHES\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, BAN, SECURITY, CHINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIKTOK FACES POSSIBLE U.S. BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITE HOUSE DEMANDS CHINESE OWNERS SELL VIDEO APP OVER NATIONAL SECURITY CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 SAFETY SUMMIT, AIR SCARE, SANTA, \uc2 FAA\uc2 , HOMENDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 FAA\uc2  SAFETY SUMMIT ADDRESSES UPTICK IN CLOSE CALLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AGENCY CALLING FOR NEW TECHNOLOGY TO PREVENT RUNWAY INCURSIONS\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, MIFEPRISTONE, HEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES ABORTION PILL HEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHALLENGE TO \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVAL OF DRUG COULD LEAD TO NATIONWIDE BAN\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the hearing in a federal court in Texas that could lead to a ban of the abortion pill nationwide. Mireya Villarreal was in the courtroom for the arguments and joins us from Amarillo. Good morning, Mireya.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Robin. Yeah, that courtroom here in Amarillo was absolutely packed, just 19 seats for the general public and 19 seats for the media.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, attorneys for the plaintiff, a Christian conservative legal advocacy group, challenged the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's more than 20-year approval of the drug mifepristone, commonly known as the abortion pill, claiming the \uc2 FDA\uc2  exceeded its regulatory authority when it approved the drug.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA RESPONSE\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Attorneys representing the \uc2 FDA\uc2  standing by their approval, stating, "Mifepristone is safe and effective for the medical termination of intrauterine pregnancy under certain conditions." Now, the US district judge hearing the case, a Trump appointee, hearing arguments for more than four hours yesterday. His decision could suspend the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's approval and take mifepristone off the market and only surgical procedures in clinics would be allowed in states where abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, already, both sides are planning to file an appeal depending on the outcome. And some are even predicting that this could go to the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 . Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Mireya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230413ej3g00009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH-STAKES ABORTION PILL HEARING FEDERAL JUDGE SET TO TAKE UP CASE IN TEXAS\b0\par\par\uc2 503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER WALLOP\par \par \uc2 NOR'EASTER, SNOW, WINDS, RAIN, HIGH TIDE\par \par \uc2 RAIN, FLOODING, EMERGENCY, RIVERS, WINDS\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, WINDS\par \par \uc2 DRONE, US, RUSSIA, TENSIONS, RYDER, ANTONOV\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, DRONE, RUSSIA, DOWNED, BLACK SEA\par \par \uc2 BANKS, COLLAPSE, SVB, SEC, REGULATORS, ATKINS, COMPTON\par \par \uc2 EPA\uc2 , PFAS, WATER, HARMFUL, LIMIT, MUNICIPALITIES\par \par \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , TOURNAMENT, TEAMS, MEN, WOMEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRONG WINDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BEST IN SHOW"\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN, "RUST", HUTCHINS, REED, LLOYD\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON, INVESTIGATION, BIDEN, CONCIERGE\par \par \uc2 CHATBOT, AI, CHAT GPT, KNIGHT, PIERCEY, FOWLER\par \par \uc2 FRENCH BULLDOG, DINARDO, AKC, TOP DOG\par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, NOR'EASTER, TRACK\par \par \uc2 ABORTION PILL, HEARING, MIFEPRISTONE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to a hearing that could decide whether the abortion pill will be banned nationwide.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES ABORTION PILL HEARING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FEDERAL JUDGE SET TO TAKE UP CASE IN TEXAS THAT COULD LEAD TO NATIONWIDE BAN\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A federal judge is set to hear the case this morning in Texas. Mireya Villarreal joins us from there. Good morning, Mireya.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, George. From Amarillo, this small Texas town is now the backdrop for the big issue of abortion. What's at stake right now is the ability to get what's commonly referred to as the abortion pill, mifepristone. And already, you can see right behind me there are dozens of people that are waiting at the doors of the courthouse trying to get in, claiming the few spots that are available inside.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, in November, anti-abortion groups filed a lawsuit against the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , questioning the safety and effectiveness of mifepristone, a drug that was approved more than 20 years ago by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . The plaintiffs' lawyers claimed the \uc2 FDA\uc2  exceeded its authority and they are asking the judge to pull the drug, effectively forcing the agency to try and get it reapproved, which could take years and, in effect, could impact 64 million women in this country. Now, this morning, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is likely going to argue it regularly reviews the data on the drug and has no concerns about its safety or of its effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , of course, will be here. We will hear from both sides of this trial or this hearing. Now, the federal judge in this case seemingly tried to keep people from hearing about the case in a closed-door meeting late last week. He actually said it would be much better if we didn't advertise this hearing at all. And, of course, it already looks like it has backfired because abortion rights groups are already calling on women to descend here in Texas, in Amarillo, and get ready to protest depending on the outcome of this hearing. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Mireya, we know so many will be looking at the outcome of all of that. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230418ej3f0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NIGHTLINE\b0\par\par\uc2 284 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is "Nightline."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION PILL CHALLENGE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight...\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26abortion pill challenge. A small Texas town at the center of a national culture war.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION (ACTIVIST\par \par \uc2 My main concern is the babies.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION (ACTIVIST\par \par \uc2 Women have the right to have abortions.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The pill that's the latest salvo in the abortion debate.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 I'm incredibly worried that a well-studied, safe, and effective means for people to end pregnancy is going to be taken off the market.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The conservative judge whose decision could impact women in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOING PUBLIC\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, going public. World-class distance runner and two-time Olympian Kara Goucher speaking out.\par \par \uc2 KARA GOUCHER (AUTHOR "THE LONGEST RACE")\par \par \uc2 It's really hard. I feel a lot of shame.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The abuse she says she endured at the hands of her one-time coach.\par \par \uc2 KARA GOUCHER (AUTHOR "THE LONGEST RACE")\par \par \uc2 Really, I was just sort of frozen not knowing what to say or do or accept that this was happening.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And the change she's fighting for.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 You say in the book that you finally realized that the power is not in your legs. Where have you found your power?\par \par \uc2 KARA GOUCHER (AUTHOR "THE LONGEST RACE")\par \par \uc2 In my voice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING SOUL\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And celebrating soul. The tributes tonight for "What You Won't Do For Love" singer Bobby Caldwell.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Nightline" will be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020230316ej3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ABORTION PILL CHALLENGE TEXAS JUDGE TO DECIDE ON POTENTIAL NATIONWIDE BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 2,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, GARIEPY, MIFEPRISTONE, LAWSUIT, BLAKE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. We begin tonight with a major test of abortion access in America after Roe v. Wade was overturned. And it's playing out in a tiny Texas courthouse. At issue, a crucial pill used to end pregnancies in the first ten weeks. The decision by one federal judge could pull the pill off the market and have implications that go far beyond abortion. ABC's Mireya Villareal is in Amarillo.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the small Texas City of Amarillo, the future of abortion access for women in all 50 states hangs in the balance.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION (ACTIVIST\par \par \uc2 My main concern is the babies. That is my main concern.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION (ACTIVIST\par \par \uc2 Women have the right to have abortions. And you can't turn the rights around. It's not okay.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In an unprecedented high-stakes case, a federal judge is deciding whether to overturn decades-long \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of a pill used in medication abortions in the first ten weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION (ACTIVIST\par \par \uc2 Our point is to protect the American public, to protect the men, the women, and the children.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 Folks are out here to protest, and we are running a kangaroo court because so is he.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The hearing today so sensitive, the judge tried to keep it quiet in the days leading up to it for security reasons. His decision could potentially pull the abortion medication mifepristone off the shelf, further limiting access for 64 million women, even in states where the procedure remains legal.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 I'm incredibly worried that a well-studied, safe and effective means for people to end pregnancy is going to be taken off the market.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Seeing the reversal of Roe was a great triumph. But it's very much phase one in our movement.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This case, the latest flashpoint in the long-running abortion debate, further inflamed when the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade last summer, ending the federally protected right to abortion that women held for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 We won't go back. We trust women.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This case has the potential to dramatically change the landscape of abortion access in a way that I'm not sure any single decision has really done since the Supreme Court overturned Roe and Dobb.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The drug was approved in September 2000 by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 . Since then, it has been used safely by millions of women to end unwanted pregnancies in conjunction with a second drug, misoprostol. Medication abortions account for over half of all abortions in the United States, according to Guttmacher, an organization that supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 One mifepristone is one tablet. It's an anti-progestin, and what does that is it causes the pregnancy to weaken its attachment to the wall of the uterus. And then the second medication misoprostol is sent to the pharmacy, and that medication helps the uterus to contract and expel the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Aileen Gariepy is the director of Complex Family Planning at Weill Cornell medicine.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 Medication abortion is incredibly safe. It's safer than Tylenol. It is more well-studied than most prescription medicines on the market.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The lawsuit claims otherwise. The anti-abortion plaintiffs making the unprecedented argument that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  exceeded its authority and ignored intense side effects because of pressure to approve the drug. It seeks to withdraw approval for the drug, taking it off the market immediately. In states where abortion is legal, only in-clinic procedures would be allowed. The suit was filed by conservative legal group, The Alliance Defending Freedom, on behalf of anti-abortion doctors and organizations.\par \par \uc2 JULIE MARIE BLAKE (SENIOR COUNSEL ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM)\par \par \uc2 Any court to look at the law and to look at the science should agree that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has failed its responsibility to protect women and girls and that it's time to remove these drugs from the market or, at a minimum, to put back important safeguards on their use.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A 2008 congressional watchdog review found no wrongdoing in the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process involving the drug. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  standing by their approval stating, "Mifepristone is safe and effective for the medical termination on intrauterine pregnancy under certain conditions," calling the \uc2 ADF\uc2 's claims "without merit."\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 It's not based in fact. It's not based in science, it's not based in the medical literature.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All of this happening in a state that often serves as a testing ground for anti-abortion legislation.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Texas has been at the center of both legal and political fights around abortion for, you know, more than half a century.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The case striking fear among abortion rights advocates.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 I am willing to just go out there and stand, because what this moment calls for is political courage.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Rachel Carmona is executive director of the Women's March. She was outside the court protesting.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The idea that this drug has been on the market for so long, what does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 I mean, everything about the case is irregular. We're talking about that for a case that's been approved for so long. The merits of the case are questionable.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If it's not about the merits of the case, then what is this about?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 In my opinion, it's about a politician who is in robes and moving a political agenda forward.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The case also has the potential to impact medications far beyond abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think if these plaintiffs are successful in getting a district court to essentially override the scientific judgment of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , then, you know, the sky the limit in terms of what kinds of scientific questions could be subject to judicial second-guessing.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 I think other drugs could face judicial scrutiny. We don't see this kind of legislative interference with anything that involves men's reproductive health. We don't see it with penile implants, we don't see it with Viagra.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But for anti-abortion activists, today's hearing was another step towards stopping abortion in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 We're advocating against direct abortions, intentional abortions, where a pill, two pills, are being taken to intentionally end the life of a whole, living human being.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kristan Hawkins is the president of Students for Life of America. The group has been a vocal and vigorous presence at nearly every major abortion event.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 Get your pro-life signs, guys.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We were with her in 2018 as she embarked on a tour that lobbied for \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's Supreme Court confirmation.\par \par \uc2 KRISTAN HAWKINS (PRESIDENT STUDENTS FOR LIFE OF AMERICA)\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of credit that goes to the Trump-Pence administration for the appointments of strict constitutionalist judges. Ending Roe ended a horrific wrong that was done to our country.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The case is now in the hands of US District Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, nominated by President Trump, he was confirmed by the Senate in 2019 by a vote of 52 to 46. He's known for his conservative views on issues like abortion, LGBTQ rights, and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW J KACSMARYK (US DISTRICT JUDGE)\par \par \uc2 Senators, I think it is reasonable to ask if I can abide by an oath that requires me to fairly and impartially apply the law as it is written, regardless of my religious beliefs. Yes, I think that is part of your advice and consent function.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (DEM) (RHODE ISLAND)\par \par \uc2 And can you give me that assurance?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW J KACSMARYK (US DISTRICT JUDGE)\par \par \uc2 I will. Yes, I do.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know just from his short time on the bench that he has sided with quite conservative causes. I think that the plaintiffs in this case have a good sense that this judge is likely to be sympathetic to their effort based on his background.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But those who know him personally say he's fair and impartial.\par \par \uc2 SHERRI STATLER\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO CHRISTIAN HOMES AND FAMILY SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 I think Judge Kacsmaryk, Matthew, is one of the smartest people I know, always willing to listen and to hear all sides of a situation and consider its long-term impact.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Sherri Sattler has known Judge Kacsmaryk for at least 25 years. She runs Christian Homes and Family Services where Kacsmaryk served on the board.\par \par \uc2 SHERRI STATLER\uc2  (PRESIDENT & CEO CHRISTIAN HOMES AND FAMILY SERVICES)\par \par \uc2 Christian Homes and Family Services is about changing lives and building Christian families. And we do that by providing free maternity services to young women with unplanned pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kacsmaryk resigned from the board once he was appointed a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's no accident this case was filed in this district, in front of this judge. So, this is the only judge who sits in this particular court in Texas. And so, filing a lawsuit here guarantees that you will draw this judge. And for the most part, those have been successful challenges to Biden administration policies of various sorts.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Citing security threats, Judge Kacsmaryk initially tried to keep the date from the public, only adding it to the court docket on Monday after objections from advocacy groups and media organizations.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's quite unusual to deliberately keep secret the date and time and even existence of a hearing in any case, let alone a high-profile case like this one.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Already, providers have debated what to do if access to medication for abortion is restricted.\par \par \uc2 DR AILEEN GARIEPY (DIRECTOR COMPLEX FAMILY PLANNING)\par \par \uc2 There are other ways that people might still be able to do medication abortion. One regimen is to use misoprostol only. But that regimen requires higher doses of misoprostol to be taken more frequently. It's still highly effective, it's still incredibly safe.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What will happen if the abortion pill gets taken out of the market?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 The community will come together. Women have been getting abortion for millennia, as long as we have lived. So, they will not stop people getting abortions.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As for the next step in this legal battle, whoever wins, the case will likely be headed to the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals and maybe on to the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Mireya.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOING PUBLIC\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, the two-time Olympian who's speaking out about a former running coach and the two sexual assaults she says she endured. Why she's decided to go public.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020230316ej3f00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MAJOR ABORTION PILL CASE TEXAS JUDGE TO DECIDE ON POTENTIAL NATIONWIDE BAN\b0\par\par\uc2 608 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, REAPER DRONE, AUSTIN, MILLEY\par \par \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 , BUTTIGIEG, TRAVEL SAFETY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SWEEPING AIR SAFETY REVIEW\par \par \uc2 FIRE, ARREST, WENGUI, DOWNEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MYSTERIOUS FIRE AFTER ARREST\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS CROSS-COUNTRY STORM\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, CASSIDY, STOCKS, CREDIT SUISSE, KELLEHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STOCKS DROP ON BANK FEARS\par \par \uc2 ARREST, MURDER, OTIENO, BASKERVILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVEN VA DEPUTIES CHARGED\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BLAKE, MIFEPRISTONE, LAWSUIT, CARMONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR ABORTION PILL CASE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, to the issue of abortion, and all eyes now on a Texas judge and a decision coming that could affect women across this country on an abortion pill that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says has been used safely for more than 20 years in this country. ABC's Mireya Villarreal in Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXAS JUDGE TO DECIDE ON POTENTIAL NATIONWIDE BAN\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, all eyes on a Texas judge whose decision could pull a key abortion pill off the market in a ruling that could affect millions of women nationwide. The \uc2 FDA\uc2 's more than 20-year approval of the drug mifepristone challenged by the \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 , a Christian conservative legal advocacy group. The lawsuit claiming the \uc2 FDA\uc2  "exceeded its regulatory authority" when it approved the drug.\par \par \uc2 JULIE MARIE BLAKE (SENIOR COUNSEL ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM)\par \par \uc2 Any court to look at the law and to look at the science should agree that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has failed its responsibility to protect women and girls, and that it's time to remove these drugs from the market or, at a minimum, to put back important safeguards on their use.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 FDA\uc2  standing by their approval, stating mifepristone is "safe and effective for the medical termination of intrauterine pregnancy under certain conditions," calling the \uc2 ADF\uc2 's claims without merit. Today, US District judge Matthew Kaczmarek, a Trump appointee, hearing arguments for more than four hours. Julie Sims (OC) was in the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 ANTI-ABORTION ADVOCATE (MALE)\par \par \uc2 My main concern is about the babies. That is my main concern.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IF JUDGE RULES TO SUSPEND \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVAL\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A ruling to suspend the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's approval would temporarily take mifepristone off the market. Only in-clinic procedures would be allowed in states where abortion is legal. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , medication abortions count for more than half of all abortions in the US.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O'LEARY CARMONA (EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR WOMEN'S MARCH)\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone has been approved for, I think, 23 years. It's more, it's safer than Tylenol. It's safer than penicillin.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, this case is likely to head to appeal regardless of the outcome. It would end up in the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals and some even predicting it could go all the way to the US Supreme court. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mireya Villarreal tonight. Mireya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMBASSADOR OF INDIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE CONFIRMS ERIC GARCETTI\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this evening on Capitol Hill following a two-year battle on the Hill. The Senate confirming Eric Garcetti as US ambassador to India.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The former mayor of Los Angeles approved for the post. The final vote was 52 to 42. Seven Republicans voted in favor of the nomination.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here tonight, the hunt for a suspect who tried to steal helicopter from the city airport, then crashing that chopper. And the tree coming down in the San Antonio Zoo, you'll see the images tonight, seven people hurt.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020230316ej3f00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 UNPRECEDENTED ABORTION LAWSUIT? THREE TEXAS WOMEN SUED FOR WRONGFUL DEATH\b0\par\par\uc2 532 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Saturday\par \uc2 WNSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 STORM, FLOODING, BAKER, TRACK, FORECAST\par \par \uc2 SILICON VALLEY BANK, BAJAJ, ANCHYSHKIN, KALB, ZANDI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECOND-LARGEST BANK FAILURE EVER\par \par \uc2 BERMEA, SEARCH, MEXICO, RIOS, SAENZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN MISSING IN MEXICO\par \par \uc2 KHODAKARAMREZAEI, SADEGHI, NASERI, SHOOTING, LOWE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEVELOPING STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE: PODCASTER KILLED BY STALKER\par \par \uc2 OIL DRILLING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR OIL DRILLING PROJECT\par \par \uc2 LAWSUIT, ABORTION, SILVA, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW DEVELOPMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNPRECEDENTED ABORTION LAWSUIT?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to a maybe an unprecedented abortion lawsuit in the state of Texas. Three Texas women are being sued by a friend's ex-husband. He's alleging that they helped her obtain abortion pills to terminate her pregnancy. The couple divorced last year, two months before that abortion supposedly took place. Here's ABC's Ike Ejiochi.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THREE TEXAS WOMEN SUED FOR WRONGFUL DEATH\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, a first lawsuit of its kind in the latest efforts to punish people who assist abortions in states where it's increasingly criminalized. Marcus Silva, of Galveston County, Texas, filing a civil suit against three friends of his now ex-wife for allegedly helping her obtain pills used to terminate her pregnancy last year. The suit, a wrongful death suit on behalf of Mr. Silva's unborn child, alleges that assisting a self-managed abortion qualifies as murder under Texas state law. In a statement to ABC News, a lawyer from a firm representing Silva, saying, abortion harms not only the unborn children who are killed, but also the fathers who have had their fatherhood stolen from them.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a photo from the suit, two of the defendants and Ms. Silva are seen here dressed for Halloween in costumes from "The Handmaid's Tale" TV series. Ms. Silva's ex-husband demanding more than $1 million from each of the defendants, citing Texas's near-total ban on abortions in 2021.\par \par \uc2 WENDY DAVIS (SENIOR ADVISOR PLANNED PARENTHOOD TEXAS VOTES)\par \par \uc2 It's been clear that, for years, anti-abortion activists have been and will continue to use the judicial system as an instrument of threats and intimidation.\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The lawsuit relying heavily on information allegedly found in text messages between Silva's ex-wife and the defendants. In those texts, discussions about how to obtain the pills, saying, "We have pills here in Houston. So, no, you wouldn't have to fly." One text allegedly explaining that Ms. Silva can do it at home. "We can take the day off and do it at my place if you want."\par \par \uc2 IKE EJIOCHI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, the husband, Marcus Silva, is not purr suing charges against his ex-wife. The suit notes she's exempt from civil and criminal liability. Whit?\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER\par \par \uc2 (OC) Quite the story. Ike, thank you. There's still much more ahead on "World News Tonight" this Saturday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD NEWS TONIGHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The former Alabama basketball player now charged with the murder of a 23-year-old woman. And chilling new surveillance video of the suspect in the \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  shootings. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNSA000020230314ej3b00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REMEMBERING PETERSON ZAH FIRST PRESIDENT OF NAVAJO NATION PASSES AWAY AT 85\b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENDLESS WINTER\par \par \uc2 RAINS, SNOW, FLOODING, EMERGENCY, DAM\par \par \uc2 RAIN, SNOW, ROOFS, COLLAPSE, HOMES, BUSINESSES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, WINDS,\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GRAND JURY, DANIELS, TESTIFY, INDICTMENT\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL, FALL, CONCUSSION, HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, ATM, FRAUD, TRELHA, "POLITICO", \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 GUNMAN, SHOOTING, JEHOVAH'S WITNESS, CONGREGATION\par \par \uc2 DERAILMENTS, WHEEL, RAILROADS, MARKEY, TOXIC, SHAW\par \par \uc2 JOBS, WALL ST, DOW, UNEMPLOYMENT, ECONOMY, FED\par \par \uc2 NCAA, MARCH MADNESS, TEAMS, SELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, KILLINGS, CARTEL, APOLOGY, ROBINETTE, ORANGE, WOODARD\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN, "RUST", SHOOTING, HUTCHINS, FIREARM, SPIRO\par \par \uc2 OSCARS, NOMINEES, RACES, WINNERS, HISTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW: WINTER STORM EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 WINTER, SNOW, FLOODING, PATH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, LEGAL, NEW YORK, GRAND JURY\par \par \uc2 ASHTON, FDA, MAMMOGRAMS, DENSE, BREAST, CANCER\par \par \uc2 ZAH, NAVAJO, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Rebecca. Well, this morning we are remembering the life and legacy of the first President of the Navajo Nation. Peterson Zah served the Navajo Nation as chairman before being elected president in 1990 on the nation's largest tribal reservation. He made his mark on Native American education at his reservation and \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2 . His passion was inspiring students, especially tribal youth, to attend college. Peterson Zah passed away after a long illness. He was 85 years old.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:13 AI AND BREAST CANCER DETECTION\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up in our "Gma" Morning Menu, our series "The Tech Effect" looks at how artificial intelligence is helping doctors detect breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:15 OSCARS PRODUCERS SPEAK OUT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also this morning, we're counting down to the Oscars. The producers are sharing some secrets about the show.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:38 OSCARS FASHION FORECAST\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, style expert Ade Samuel is here with a fashion forecast of what we'll see on the champagne carpet, it's not red, it's champagne, from some of Hollywood's biggest style icons. And Lori Bergamotto is here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:21 THE RIGHT STUFF: TOP HAIR TOOLS!\par \par \uc2 LORI BERGAMOTTO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. I am here, and I went to the pros, "GMA's" amazing hair and makeup artists, to find out what the best tools are to get your glam on. All that coming up right here on "Gma."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA, FRIDAY, MAR 10\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230407ej3a0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW \uc2 FDA\uc2  MANDATE ON MAMMOGRAMS PROVIDERS MUST INFORM WOMEN IF THEY HAVE DENSE BREAST TISSUE\b0\par\par\uc2 726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENDLESS WINTER\par \par \uc2 RAINS, SNOW, FLOODING, EMERGENCY, DAM\par \par \uc2 RAIN, SNOW, ROOFS, COLLAPSE, HOMES, BUSINESSES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, WINDS,\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GRAND JURY, DANIELS, TESTIFY, INDICTMENT\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL, FALL, CONCUSSION, HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, ATM, FRAUD, TRELHA, "POLITICO", \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 GUNMAN, SHOOTING, JEHOVAH'S WITNESS, CONGREGATION\par \par \uc2 DERAILMENTS, WHEEL, RAILROADS, MARKEY, TOXIC, SHAW\par \par \uc2 JOBS, WALL ST, DOW, UNEMPLOYMENT, ECONOMY, FED\par \par \uc2 NCAA, MARCH MADNESS, TEAMS, SELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, KILLINGS, CARTEL, APOLOGY, ROBINETTE, ORANGE, WOODARD\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN, "RUST", SHOOTING, HUTCHINS, FIREARM, SPIRO\par \par \uc2 OSCARS, NOMINEES, RACES, WINNERS, HISTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW: WINTER STORM EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 WINTER, SNOW, FLOODING, PATH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, LEGAL, NEW YORK, GRAND JURY\par \par \uc2 ASHTON, \uc2 FDA\uc2 , MAMMOGRAMS, DENSE, BREAST, CANCER\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A right, George, we turn now to a "Gma" health alert. With one in eight women likely to be diagnosed with breast cancer in their lifetime, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is issuing a new rule requiring mammogram facilities to inform women if they have dense breast tissue. Our chief medical correspondent Doctor Jennifer Ashton is here to break it down. Doctor Ashton, so good to see you on this one. Why is it so important? What does it change for women?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Rebecca, file this one under the category of better late than never. And for a lot of women, this won't change anything because they're already informed if they have dense breast tissue, but half of women over the age of 40 do have this entity on their mammograms, which means they have a lot of white tissue. It can't be detected on self-exam and then it can make it harder for a mammogram to detect breast cancer because on a mammogram, a tumor looks white, so it's like looking for a snowball in a snowstorm basically.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW \uc2 FDA\uc2  MANDATE ON BREAST SCREENING: WHAT DENSE BREAST TISSUE INDICATES ABOUT CANCER RISK\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So what do women need to do, especially when they talk to their doctors and when they go through the process of mammograms and their health care regimen?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN AND BREAST HEALTH. WHAT ALL WOMEN SHOULD KNOW ABOUT CARE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, I think it's important, we've said it before. There's no such thing as one size fits all, so you have to talk about what can reduce risk and what can increase early detection. In terms of reducing risk, limiting alcohol, exercising, keeping your body weight in a healthy range all have been shown to lower your risk. When you talk about increasing detection, you have to talk to your health care provider about when to start screening and with what imaging modality. Mammogram being really the gold standard, but also using possibly mri or ultrasound. And then if you have a history of certain types of cancers in your family, genetic testing for about ten percent of women is really important, as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIGRAINE TREATMENT BREAKTHROUGH? \uc2 FDA\uc2  APPROVES NASAL SPRAY THAT MAY RELIEVE SYMPTOMS WITHIN 30 MINUTES\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As so many of us do here, Doctor Ashton, there's this other big medical headline that just came out this morning, the fda has just approved the first nasal spray for migraines. That's going to impact a lot of people as well.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, it sure is, Rebecca, and I'm actually one of them. This would be sold under the brand name Zavspread. It is a nasal spray, so it would be the first one in the class of migraine medication. Now \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved, it can work as quickly as 15 to 30 minutes, very well-tolerated in clinical trials and possibly expected to be in pharmacies by this summer, so that will be good news for people. You will need a prescription, of course.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, 15 to 30 minutes, that is important help when you are dealing with a migraine.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah. It sure is, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Doctor Ashton, thanks so much for joining us. All right, Michael.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230407ej3a0000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AI AND BREAST CANCER DETECTION HOW THE GROUNDBREAKING TECH IS BEING USED BY DOCTORS\b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENDLESS WINTER\par \par \uc2 RAINS, SNOW, FLOODING, EMERGENCY, DAM\par \par \uc2 RAIN, SNOW, ROOFS, COLLAPSE, HOMES, BUSINESSES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, WINDS,\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GRAND JURY, DANIELS, TESTIFY, INDICTMENT\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL, FALL, CONCUSSION, HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, ATM, FRAUD, TRELHA, "POLITICO", \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DESANTIS\par \par \uc2 GUNMAN, SHOOTING, JEHOVAH'S WITNESS, CONGREGATION\par \par \uc2 DERAILMENTS, WHEEL, RAILROADS, MARKEY, TOXIC, SHAW\par \par \uc2 JOBS, WALL ST, DOW, UNEMPLOYMENT, ECONOMY, FED\par \par \uc2 NCAA, MARCH MADNESS, TEAMS, SELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, KILLINGS, CARTEL, APOLOGY, ROBINETTE, ORANGE, WOODARD\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN, "RUST", SHOOTING, HUTCHINS, FIREARM, SPIRO\par \par \uc2 OSCARS, NOMINEES, RACES, WINNERS, HISTORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW: WINTER STORM EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 WINTER, SNOW, FLOODING, PATH\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, LEGAL, NEW YORK, GRAND JURY\par \par \uc2 ASHTON, FDA, MAMMOGRAMS, DENSE, BREAST, CANCER\par \par \uc2 ZAH, NAVAJO, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 AI, BREAST CANCER, TECH, LEHMAN, VADASZY, NORTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good shot of Times Square right there. And welcome back to "GMA" with our cover story. It's part of our "Tech Effect" series and Rebecca, you're looking at the impact of A1 in our lives.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, we keep hearing about A1, AI...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) AI (inaudible) okay.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Artificial intelligence, Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) AI. I don't know what I was thinking.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I like A1 on my steak, isn't that where it goes?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was like (inaudible) I was pulling out my steak.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You saved me, though, thank you. A1, where did that come from?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's going to change your world, Michael. And we're hearing more and more about this technology, and this morning, it could be used to help save lives and now detect breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) From \uc2 Boston Dynamics\uc2  to ChatGPT, the era of artificial intelligence or ai is here to stay. The groundbreaking technology now being used in breast cancer detection.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR CONSTANCE LEHMAN (RADIOLOGIST MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 It's really changed the paradigm. It's changed the value of the mammogram itself and that's really exciting.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While early detection is key, some estimates suggest screening mammograms miss about one in eight breast cancers. To reduce that number, doctors are now using ai called computer assisted detection or CAD in more and more breast imaging centers across the US.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW "COMPUTER ASSISTED DETECTION" COULD HELP SAVE LIVES\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR LARRY NORTON (MEDICAL DIRECTOR MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 The major advantage to AI right now is it speeds up the reading of the mammogram so that a radiologist can see more mammograms accurately over a certain period of time.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And studies have shown that CAD can also find cancers that may have been missed by humans. And that's not all. It helps review images, assess breast density, flag high-risk mammograms, and can even tell a technologist a mammogram needs to be redone. And while the US is on the front lines of ai research, overseas Hungary is becoming a major testing ground.\par \par \uc2 ANDRAS VADASZY (DIRECTOR MAMMA KLINKA)\par \par \uc2 We save more lives with the help of AI. I mean, it's fantastic, it's a good feeling.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yet mixing medicine and ai is not without its problems. Ai and breast cancer screenings can be associated with high false positive rates.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR LARRY NORTON (MEDICAL DIRECTOR MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING)\par \par \uc2 Machine learning is very good for what you've taught it. Machines, when they see something that they have no experience with, they're not very good at identifying it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One recent study finding that humans and ai working together are capable of detecting 2.6 more breast cancers with fewer false alarms. So for now, the future looks bright.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR CONSTANCE LEHMAN (RADIOLOGIST MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 The tools of AI are going to advance breast cancer detection, risk assessment, cancer prevention, not just detecting early, but actually preventing cancers.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Some very helpful news for families. Ai and breast cancer detection is never used without the partnership of a medical doctor. And the way most breast cancer is missed is by missing an annual mammogram, roughly half the people who should be getting annual mammograms are not, and that is really where they're missing most breast cancers. And let's get back, guys, to Lara for more on the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230407ej3a0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WOMEN SUE TEXAS OVER ABORTION BAN SAY THEY WERE DENIED PROCEDURE DESPITE FACING MEDICAL EMERGENCIES\b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KIDNAPPING ORDEAL\par \par \uc2 ABDUCTION, MEXICO, CARTELS, PRICE, WILLIAMS, MCGEE\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY, VIDEO, FOX NEWS, FIRESTORM, MCCONNELL\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , CAPITOL HILL, MIDAIR ATTACK, SUSPECT, OLSEN, DERAILMENTS\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAINFALL, SNOW, FLOODING, ROOFS\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, FLOODING, SNOW\par \par \uc2 INTEREST RATES, FED, INFLATION, CREDIT CARDS, LOANS, MORTGAGES\par \par \uc2 OSBORN, HERO, BURNING, CAR, RESCUE, VIKINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW FORECASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CRUMBLEY, APPEAL, CHARGES, MURRAY, RIORDAN, LEHMAN, SMITH\par \par \uc2 MEXICO, TRAVEL, WARNING, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , STATE DEPT, PRICE, KEYES\par \par \uc2 SUGAR, ARTIFICIAL, HEALTH, GUT, MICROBIOME, INSULIN\par \par \uc2 RHIANNA, "RUDE BOY", SENIORS, VIDEO, VIRAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CYCLONE FREDDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MEXICO, KIDNAPPING, RESCUE, WILLIAMS, MCGEE, PRICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICAN KIDNAPPING SURVIVORS BACK IN US\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS: TWO KILLED, TWO RESCUED AFTER MEXICO ABDUCTION HORROR\par \par \uc2 JETBLUE, SPIRIT, MERGER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING RUNDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRLINE MERGER ON HOLD?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUSTICE DEPARTMENT SUES TO STOP JETBLUE-SPIRIT DEAL\par \par \uc2 ASTHMA, ALBUTEROL, SHORTAGE, ACORN PHARMACEUTICALS\par \par \uc2 ALLERGY, SEASON, SPRING, LEAF-OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING RUNDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALLERGY SEASON GETTING LONGER?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT AS THE FIRST DAY OF SPRING NEARS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAWSUIT, ZARGARIAN, HALL\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a shot of Times Square there here in New York City. And back now on "GMA" with the five women suing the state of Texas, saying they were denied abortions even though they were facing medical emergencies that put their lives in danger. Mireya Villarreal is in Dallas with more for us. Good morning, Mireya.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN SUE TEXAS OVER ABORTION BAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAY THEY WERE DENIED PROCEDURE DESPITE FACING MEDICAL EMERGENCIES\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, good morning, Michael. These mothers were more than happy to find out they were pregnant. They were picking baby names and planning baby showers, and abortion was the last thing they wanted. And while they were able to get the procedure done outside of Texas, they are now fighting for women who don't have that option.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZURAWSKI ET AL V. STATE OF TEXAS ET AL\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, five women suing the state of Texas, Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , and the state's medical board, alleging they were denied necessary and potentially lifesaving obstetrical care because medical professionals throughout the state fear liability under Texas' abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN HALL (PLAINTIFF IN LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 What happened to the people standing behind me was a violation of their Texas constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the lawsuit, Anna Zargarian's water broke just four months into her pregnancy, leaving the baby without any amniotic sac inside the womb.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM PRESS CONFERENCE\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN (PLAINTIFF IN LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 And they said, "Even with the best neonatal intensive care, a fetus cannot survive outside the womb at 19 weeks." My heart broke into a million pieces.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EARLY AMNIOTIC SAC RUPTURE\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Aborting is typically advised in these cases because the mother could face sepsis, a life-threatening condition that happens when the body has an extreme response to infection. When the amniotic sac ruptures too early, the risk of infection increases for both mother and baby.\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN (PLAINTIFF IN LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 The child I was so excited for wasn't going to live, and I needed an abortion to preserve my health, but couldn't get one in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The lawsuit backed by the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , an abortion rights group, isn't asking for the state's abortion ban to be lifted. Instead, they're trying to get a judge to provide medical clarity on the portion of the law that allows women to get an abortion in emergency cases. For Lauren Hall, another plaintiff in the suit, that emergency came after her 18-week scan.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN HALL (PLAINTIFF IN LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 The maternal fetal specialist sadly told us that our baby had anencephaly, a condition that causes a baby to develop with no skull and very little brain matter.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She flew to Seattle to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN HALL (PLAINTIFF IN LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 I love Texas and it kills me that my own state does not seem to care if I live or die.\par \par \uc2 MIREYA VILLARREAL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Texas Attorney General's Office wouldn't comment specifically on this lawsuit, but they did send us a statement saying in part that the AG is committed to doing everything in his power to protect mothers, families and unborn children while also defending Texas law. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, Mireya.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230406ej380000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:30 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR WINTER STORM: WEATHER ALERT\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, SNOW, FLOODING, WINDS, EMERGENCY, STRANDED\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, SNOW, SUPPLIES, KILEY, HOLEYFIELD, ROWE, PRITCHETT\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, SENTENCED, MURDERS, GUILTY, LIFE\par \par \uc2 CALIFORNIA, TRANSFORMERS, ARRESTED, KARASEV\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, CANCEROUS, LESION, TREATMENT, PHYSICAL\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, MISSILE\par \par \uc2 VIOLENCE, WEST BANK, PALESTINIANS, ISRAELIS\par \par \uc2 TRACKS, DERAILMENT, TOXIC, RESIDENTS, HEALTH, OUTRAGE\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON, JUSTICE, DIPLOMATIC INTERVENTION, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 VIETNAM, HERO, HONORED, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 SIZEMORE, ANEURYSM, 61\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "Gma" on this Saturday morning. Bts member J-Hope dropped his new music video featuring American rapper J Cole who he has called his long-time inspiration. The collaboration comes a decade after BTS adapted J Cole's song "Born Sinner" into their song "Born Singer."\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's so cool.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Nice song.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also I heard that not only is K-pop hot, but (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yes, girl, yes.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 That's for sure, yes. I, I, yeah. That's a big thing in my family, yep.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Really? You guys were already hip to it? Imagine that. Wow.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I mean, in my family, but (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, geez (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION PILL CONTROVERSY\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 All right, let's take a look at some of the other big stories that we're following on this Saturday morning. Happening right now, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  revealing it will not make abortion medication available in 20 Republican-led states after threats of legal action. That's even as abortions and the abortion pill are still legal in some of those states. The White House calling threats to pharmacies for selling FDA-approved medication dangerous and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS CHASE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also right now, an armed carjacker is in police custody after taking officers on quite a ride near Los Angeles. The man is seen shooting at officers with what appears to be a rifle out the driver's side window of a pickup truck police say that he stole. A more than an hour-long chase ending when the man crashed the truck and then tried to take another before police took him down.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And 33 mushers are, and dozens of dogs are getting ready to participate in this years' Iditarod Trail Sled Dog Race. The teams will take off with a ceremonial start this morning and then the official start this afternoon for their 1,000-mile dash from Anchorage.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 My dog, Copper, thinks he's a sled dog. Gets us out on the leash and just pulls us right down the street. Yep.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 I knew you were going to start talking about Copper. I was waiting for it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He's a good boy, though.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He is.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 He's a good boy.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230330ej340000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,284 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2023 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 CA, LUNAR NEW YEAR, SHOOTING, BALLROOM, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAP: MONTEREY PARK, CA\par \par \uc2 CA, MASS, SHOOTINGS, SUSPECT, HATE, CRIME\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, CLASSIFIED, DOCUMENTS, HOME, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 COONS, BIDEN, DOCS, SEARCH, TRUMP, POLL, 2024, UK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ SEARCH FINDS SIX NEW CLASSIFIED 'ITEMS' IN BIDEN'S DE HOME\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL, UK, RU, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , TANKS, CRIMEA, BIDEN, GREENE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAR IN UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY APPEALS FROM MORE TANKS FROM THE WEST\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, IPSOS, POLL, DOCS, TRUMP, APPROVAL\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, DOCS, TRUMP, 2024, DEBT, MCCARTHY, ROE\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (REP-CA) (SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE)\par \par \uc2 Here we are, we had Democrats in one power, one party power, increased spending from $4 trillion to $7 trillion. They added $10 trillion of debt the ten years. The path that the Democrats are going, they're gonna bankrupt.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  (TREASURY SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 It can't be negotiated over whether or not we're going to pay our bills. And not to do so would have devastating economic consequences.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The United States hit its debt limit on Thursday, setting the stage for intense negotiations - over how to raise the borrowing cap and raising fears of a looming financial crisis. Let's bring in the Roundtable, \uc2 Sarah Isgur\uc2 , former Trump Justice Department spokesperson, now an ABC News analyst, former North Dakota Senator Heidi Heitkamp, our senior congressional correspondent Rachel Scott, and Alex Burns, columnist and associate editor for global politics at "Politico."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOJ SEARCH FINDS SIX NEW CLASSIFIED 'ITEMS' IN BIDEN'S DE HOME\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome, everybody. We're gonna get to the debt ceiling in a minute. But I got to start with you, Rachel. Let's pick where Rick Klein let off. More Americans think what Trump did was inappropriate, 77 percent, but still a majority think what Biden did was inappropriate, too. And this latest discovery makes it worse.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sixty-four percent of Americans believe that President Biden handled these classified documents inappropriately and that was before we got the news last night of these latest batch of documents that were found. We have no idea just how many. The reality is, this is a political liability for two presidents, one current, one former, one that has already launched a 2024 campaign, one that's expected to launch a 202 - 4 campaign.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And for Republicans, I think, they're looking at one key part of what was in that statement last night, the word Senate. How far back do these documents go?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's a long, long time ago.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A long time. He spent 36 years in the Senate. He left in 2009. That's almost 15 years ago. So, there's some big questions that President Biden needs to answer.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not only that, but has he gone through those documents at all? That's another question. The Democrats, of course, Alex, are, are scrambling to say, you, you heard Senator Coons, this, this, this is all gonna be okay. There, there is, backing up what, what President Biden said, it's there, there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ON CLASSIFIED DOCS PROBE: 'THERE'S NO THERE THERE'\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, I think the Democrats who are saying that are speaking from a place of hope rather than sort of deep knowledge, right? It's pretty obvious at this point that people in the leadership roles in the party don't know what the next turn of the screw is in this investigation, including people who work in the White House, who are pretty up front about the fact that they don't really know where this is headed next.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLL: MOST THINK BIDEN, TRUMP INAPPROPRIATELY HANDLED DOCS\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 Martha, I thought that the poll that, that Rick outlined is such a useful and important reality check for some of the, sort of post-midterm election confidence that you heard from Democrats about President Biden. You see in that poll all kinds of signs that the public is deeply distrustful of the Republican Party, deeply uncomfortable with the Republican Congress. But by no means is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  this ten-foot tall political figure who is sort of hugely popular and resilient against all odds, that he has done a very good job.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 Democrats have done a very good of disqualifying the Republican Party for a big stretch of the American middle. But it's pretty clear that the American people have real questions about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's handling of some of the most important issues in the day, of the day, including these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) - Exactly, and, and Senator Coons, aside from saying all these different things that Americans are thinking out, about, they are indeed thinking about that. Sarah, political fallout there will be. But you worked in the Justice Department, when you look at this from a legal angle, what, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW POLL: 64% SAY BIDEN'S HANDLING OF DOCS INAPPROPRIATE\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Clearly, the Biden team working overtime to make sure that they don't trip over that obstruction line, you know, really emphasizing that this was a consensual search by the Department of Justice and \uc2 FBI\uc2  at his residence. You know, I've worked with Rob Hur very closely, who's now the special counsel now in charge of investigating the President and these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's an incredibly experienced, competent federal prosecutor who was doing the day-to-day oversight of the Mueller investigation for about a year. But I've got to tell you something about federal prosecutors. They are like hunting dogs and they don't like traipsing through the marsh without coming back with something in their mouth. And so if I were the Biden team, I would be nervous.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AG GARLAND APPOINTS SPECIAL COUNSEL TO INVESTIGATE BIDEN DOCS\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Heidi, this will obviously, as Alex said, as we will all say, make its way into the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How, how does he handle this going forward? Can he really keep saying no there there? I mean, how long will this take, Sarah, do you think?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A lightning fast investigation at the Department of Justice takes a long time.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, exactly.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The worst thing the President could have said there's no there, there, I've, I've done, you know...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No regrets.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I have to regrets. The easy answer was, look, this obviously is something that should have not happened. I should not have had classified documents in my possession at, at various levels. We're gonna get to the bottom of this. But I, in no way, did this intentionally. I think that we have to look at a process on how we handle classified documents going forward. That would take the air out of the balloon.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN DEFENDS HANDLING OF CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you make this about a comparison to Trump, when you make this about, you know, defensive posture, you're going to lose this debate. And so, they need a course correction on how they handle this.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and the drip, drip, drip.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  shouldn't be the bar.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not, oh, well, I did better than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, legally and politically I'm fine? Both of them could've committed crimes even if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's was worse.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP REMAINS ONLY DECLARED 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and speaking of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Rachel, he's getting ready to hold one of his first official events of 2024 as a presidential candidate. He's the only announced candidate so far for the - Republicans. But are, are there any potential Republican candidates backing away now because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in there?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think that that's the most interesting part about all of this. You have a former president, a leader of the Republican Party that comes out, says that he's running again and it has not cleared the field at all, you know, people that were so close to him, I mean, his own advisers, people that worked in his administration that are closely considering running against him, even Nikki Haley this week. We've seen her words hinting at that possible run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And even amongst some Republicans, as we look ahead to this event that he's supposed to be holding in, in South Carolina, Congresswoman Nancy Mace, a Republican, she says that she's keeping her powder dry, essentially. She's waiting to see who else comes out and announces a bid for 2024.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Sarah, over in the House, Republicans announced those committee assignments that we talked about. There was Kevin McCarthy in, in a hug with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . What do these appointments say about the GOP strategy going forward?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW GOP COMMITTEE ASSIGNMENTS PROMPT CONTROVERSY\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well certainly, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  coming out early and hard for Kevin McCarthy's speakership has cemented that relationship. But, look, the idea was that Democrats stripped \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , for instance, of her committeeship. We were supposed to believe that was a punishment, when in fact it raised her name ID within the party. She raised $3 million and Democrats loved that because they could make her the face of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It reminds me a little bit of, you know, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  spending $50 million to help the "extreme MAGA Republicans" in those Republican primaries. Both sides have done this. AOC is one of the least effective members of Congress, and yet, the Republicans focus on her as well. So, I think that these committeeships don't really mean anything in the grand scheme of things. And if anything, it's each side trying to lower the temperature from their more extreme members.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Alex, are Democrats basically giddy behind the scenes, although publicly, as Sarah said, they're saying, "Oh, they're the MAGA extremists."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH BLASTS 'MAGA EXTREMISTS' ON HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 Oh, for sure, and some of them are not even confining it to behind the scenes. It's not, not just Margorie Taylor Greene. \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , look, Democrats feel, and I think with pretty good reason, that the sooner they can move on from the special counsel phase of all this and into the, sort of, over-the-top theatrical House investigations phase of this, the better it will be for them politically.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE GOP LAUNCHES INVESTIGATION INTO BIDEN ADMIN\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 I mean, there are people, plenty of people in the Biden White House, people serving on Capitol Hill, some of them elected officials themselves, who remember the 1990s and how the, the sort of story turned on \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's investigations once you saw the Republican House of Representatives, the Republican House of Representatives run really hog wild, but I think they have every expectation and with good reason that's likely to happen here.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, but, Rachel, is there any possibility that this could this backfire on Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think when you look at these committee assignments, and as you were talking, Sarah, I was thinking about what Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  has said, and I think it's sobering for the American people to look at someone who has questioned whether or not a plane hit the Pentagon on September 11th, who has made anti-Semitic claims that I will not repeat here. It was has even suggested that former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  should, should face death because she believes that she committed treason.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is now someone who is a top Republican on key committees. That's very sobering. And I think Kevin McCarthy, I think what we're seeing is these back-door negotiations, these deals that were made in order for him to secure the speaker's gavel, we're gonna see those come to light now, and this is just one example.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Heidi, there's, I've got to move to the debt ceiling. We've been talking about the debt ceiling, but in a way, that's kind of put off...\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...for a couple of months, although they say they're taking extraordinary measures now. How do they come to terms on that? You've got Kevin McCarthy wanting spending cuts and the Biden administration saying, no, no, no, no...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIVIDED CONGRESS FACES DEBT CEILING SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, the, the...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...no negotiations.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...the, the bottom line is, Kevin McCarthy has now carved himself out in some degree bringing Trump back into the fold, saying, "I wouldn't be speaker without Trump." They, he has put himself as the leader of the Republican Party. So, my argument that I would give to Kevin McCarthy is, "Look, you don't want to clean debt ceiling. Even though you, you voted for it three times during the Trump administration, you don't want a clean debt ceiling. You give us a bill, why do you need a negotiation?"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WH: BIDEN WILL MEET WITH MCCARTHY ABOUT DEBT CEILING\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, I think that when somebody says, it becomes a tit-for-tat. You know, "The, the Democrats won't come to the table, therefore we're gonna threaten the full faith and credit of the United States," I think that's loser for Republicans. Show us what the ideas are. That's America wants. They want to get to the bottom of debt and deficit, but not in the political context. They want to see real leadership. This is Kevin McCarthy's opportunity.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I want to say something about - \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  being on committees. You don't know how much damage she's gonna do until that first question that she gets. If she becomes a lightning rod with her questions on witnesses, this was not a good idea to put her on a committee.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 And, and...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 50 YEARS AFTER ROE, ABORTION BATTLES START OVER\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and, and, Sarah, today is the 50th anniversary of the Supreme Court's ruling establishing the right to abortion, Roe versus Wade. Of course, it's now been overturned. We saw anti-abortion protesters up on the Hill this week, this March For Life. What do they focus on now?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is a big question. You know, the, the pro-life movement can really be divided into a few sides. There was the "overturn Roe v. Wade" side of it, but there's also a side that wants to reduce the number of abortions in the country, and I think that's where you're gonna move to the states now. You're already seeing lots of states pursue different laws. And politically it's put someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in an interesting position as he's looking to announce.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because Florida actually does not have a particularly stringent anti-abortion law at this point. But you know, as a bigger picture, I think it's worth noting that Roe v. Wade became a feminist slogan, if you will, but so many of the biggest feminist changes that we've seen in our country didn't have to do with that, and so many of the strides that we made, I think it's, it's worth reflecting on that 50 years later. Because we're in sort of a Third Wave feminism now where women are outpacing men by almost every metric. And it's something to be really proud of.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how, do you see the GOP making this a winning issue?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 Abortion? Well, there's certainly no evidence so far.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So far? None at all.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 And, and I think it's one other thing that's remarkable that, you know, you had 50 years to prepare for what you were gonna propose after Roe v. Wade got overturned, and it, and the whole party got really caught flat-footed in the last election. And look, I, I think you see Republicans in Washington and in some of the states trying to be really deliberate about the fights they choose and don't choose on this, but the anti-abortion movement wants to go pretty far. And a Republican presidential primary is a really tough environment to make a nuanced case for, sort of, how to reduce the number of abortions without a total ban when you're campaigning in a place Iowa or South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 If I could just real quickly, on the debt ceiling issue, I was talking to a, a senior Republican strategist on Friday and asking who is the person in the Republican Party right now who can go out and make an articulate case for reducing the size of government, making tough trade-offs on spending the way \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  used to do when he was speaker or, or budget chair, the way John Kasich did in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BURNS (POLITICO ASSOCIATE EDITOR FOR GLOBAL POLITICS & COLUMNIST)\par \par \uc2 And the person said, "You know, honestly, I don't know." It's just not a sort of, part of the keyboard that you have seen folks on the - on the Hill play in quite a long time. And it's a real messaging challenge. Because if you're gonna convince the American people to be with you on this issue, you really need to, sort of, communicate with them in a different way about making tough choices.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The deficit, debt ceiling's a tough one. Thank you very much. Thanks for all of, to all of you for joining us this morning. Coming up, for the first time, America's largest cities are led by Black mayors. \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2  spoke with three of them about breaking barriers and the challenges ahead. That's next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020230126ej1m00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 50TH ANNIVERSARY OF ROE V WADE\b0\par\par\uc2 168 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, VICTIMS, SUSPECT, LUNA, LUNAR NEW YEAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENSE STANDOFF AFTER DEADLY MASS SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, AAPI, VICTIMS, COMMUNITY, GRIEF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEARCH FOR ANSWERS\par \par \uc2 NOR'EASTER, STORM, SNOW, RAIN, ADVISORIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASSIVE NOR'EASTER STRIKES\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , SEARCH, DOJ, DOCS, SAMS, POLL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FBI SEARCH REVEALS MORE DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, ARRESTS, ACTIVIST, SCHIERBAUM, VIOLENCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIERY PROTESTS ERUPT\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, TANKS, GERMANY, COON, RUSSIA CIVILIANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GERMANY GIVES GREEN LIGHT FOR TANKS\par \par \uc2 ROE V WADE, ANNIVERSARY, HARRIS, ABORTION PILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 50TH ANNIVERSARY OF ROE V WADE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And such heart-wrenching images there. Matt, thank you. Back here at home, the White House is signaling support for reproductive rights on the 50th anniversary of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Florida announcing the President has issued a Presidential memorandum aimed at ensuring access to the abortion pill. Across the country, activists braved bitter weather to show their support for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020230124ej1m00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RECONNECTING MIND & BODY HOW RESTORATIVE FITNESS CAN HELP BREAST CANCER PATIENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 January 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HANDLING OF CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS: GROWING QUESTIONS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, WHITE HOUSE, CLASSIFIED, DOCUMENTS, PROBE, JEAN-PIE, GARLAND\par \par \uc2 ISGUR FLORES, BIDEN, DOCUMENTS, CLASSIFIED, HOME, PRESIDENT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, TAX, PENALTY, FINE, FRAUD, CASE\par \par \uc2 TREASURY, CONGRESS, WARNING, HARM, NATION, YELLEN\par \par \uc2 COVID, BOOSTER, FDA, CDC, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 EXPLOSIONS, KYIV, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MISSILES\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, LEOPARD, CRIMINAL, ESCAPE, ZOO\par \par \uc2 TORNADOES, CLEANUP, RESCUE, MISSING, JACKSON, WALLACE\par \par \uc2 FRIDAY, MEGA MILLIONS, JACKPOT, WINNER, MAINE\par \par \uc2 PATERSON, BLUM, NGUYEN, MOGEN, SMITH, GARCIA, GONCALVES, AMAYA\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL MERIT, STUDENTS, MIYARES, GROSS, YASHAR\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , WEEKEND, WILD CARD, QUARTERBACKS\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, SURPRISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PROBE, JEAN-PIERRE, CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN CLASSIFIED DOCUMENTS INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT & THE WHITE HOUSE NOT COMMENTING ON PROBE\par \par \uc2 OBESITY, GUIDELINES, VACCINATIONS, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW CHILDHOOD OBESITY GUIDELINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AAP EMPHASIZES PROACTIVE MEASURES\par \par \uc2 SHAKIRA, PIQUE, "BZRP MUSIC SESSIONS #53"\par \par \uc2 "LUXURY BOOM", FRY, ZHANG, FLORES\par \par \uc2 "TRUTH & LIES: THE INFORMANT", LEHR, "WHITE HOT HATE"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOING INSIDE "TRUTH AND LIES: THE INFORMANT"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC AUDIO'S TRUE CRIME PODCAST IS BACK FOR SEASON 2\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SERIES, "SCHITT'S CREEK"\par \par \uc2 WELLNESS, SHOPPING, DEALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS WITH TORY JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 HEISEY, "RALLY GOOD ACTUALLY", DIVORCE\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, TIPS, RECOVERY, HARRIS, YOGA, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to "GMA" and "Thriving in Pink" bringing breast cancer awareness beyond October and into the new year as Americans resolve to get back to the gym.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECONNECTING MIND & BODY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW RESTORATIVE FITNESS CAN HELP BREAST CANCER PATIENTS & SURVIVORS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This morning, we're focusing on breast cancer patients and survivors and the mind/body benefits of yoga. TV host, certified health coach and breast cancer survivor Samantha Harris joins us now. Thanks so much for being with us this morning. So, you've been very open about your journey.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How are you doing now?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I am at now 49, more fit, healthier and more vibrant than I have ever been.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think your story is particularly interesting because you actually found a lump 11 days after a clear mammogram. And you say that's in part because you were just so aware of what was going on with your body.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I was. I think that really being able to exercise regularly, to be nourishing your body allows you to be more in tune with it and where those changes are. I was changing after a workout and I found that lump 11 days later. And the only thing is, it's easy to stick your head in the sand. I got a clear mammogram, I'm good to go. But I pursued it. I listened to that inner voice, I had to get quiet. And something what allowed me to get quiet is kind of what we're doing right here, which is turning inward, yoga.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. It's such a good reminder for people to be advocates for themselves no matter what's happening.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's a lot of talk pertaining about fitness routines. We don't often talk about exercising after breast cancer or surgery, but that's what we're talking about this morning.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Well, yeah. After the new year, it's all about go, go, go, hit the gym hard, lose the weight and we leave out this entire population of the breast cancer community, those in treatment, those going through recovery. And so, it's very exciting because now, I know when I, I'm certified as a trainer. Before my days on "Dancing with the Stars" and all that I did in the hosting world, I got into yoga but for, I'll be honest, Madonna's chaturanga arms. They look so strong.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, her arms were everything.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 And she's a dedicated yogi. But what I realize was the gift it gave me, which is to turn inward, to get quiet, to learn about my mindfulness and breath work. After cancer, I didn't know how to move safely. And my entire chest wall, I had a double mastectomy, two stage reconstruction. I had three surgeries in 2014. And I, as a trainer, was lost for how do I move my body safely. And so, I wish there had been a program, which is something special that we're launching today.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, you're going to demonstrate a move for us. And this is something that breast cancer patients and survivors can do?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Yes. So, first of all, just today, we're launching Yoga Works Pink, which is a dedicated first ever program for yoga that has been created for and tailored to the entire breast cancer community. And the best part that Yoga Works and Susan G. Komen is doing is allowing it to be accessible for three months at no cost, which is why we're going through that recovery. So, one move that is really important is called Virabhadrasana II or, more lovingly, warrior II, which we are all warriors. So, it's a lunge position where your back foot is at an angle. You really open up the pelvis and build up lower body strength. Now, traditionally, your arms are out stretched.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you, I was not able to move my arms this high up with my limited mobility range of motion, and so many breast cancer survivors going through that recovery process can do. So, we can start with either hands on hips or move to your heart center with palms together. And eventually, arms pointing downward and eventually, when you have that range of motion built up.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how is this good for those who are in their breast cancer journey?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, the stability, mobility, as we're coming out of recovery, you can't push yourself up out of a chair, so building up core strength, opening up the hips, we hold a lot of emotion, all of the breath work, the mindfulness that's in yoga allows us to activate that parasympathetic nervous system that rest or digest, so that we can calm that anxiety and the fear. We can also then turn to Virabhadrasana I, which is warrior I starting with your arms there on the hips, heart center or low. And eventually, as that mobility works, all the way up. All of these poses really help as they're adapted through Yoga Works Pink because we can then level up as we build back our strength, reclaim our body once again.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 And I really changed the trajectory of my own career as a TV host and I still have a show on Game Show Network called "Tug of Words." Super fun. If you like word play, you can check that out every day. But I became a certified health coach, and that's what I do with my Your Healthiest Healthy community.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So very cool.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA HARRIS (CERTIFIED HEALTH COACH / BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We were talking about yoga here. Don't mind us. Thank you so much for watching. We will see you right back here tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Go do the child's pose.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A little Shavasana, Whit? A little Shavasana?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Okay, you got it. Bye, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230201ej1e00094\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FDA APPROVES PRESCRIPTION ABORTION DRUG FOR RETAIL PHARMACIES MIFEPRISTONE WILL SOON BE AVAILABLE TO END PREGNANCIES UP TO TEN WEEKS\b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 January 2023\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2023, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE FOR SPEAKER\par \par \uc2 HOUSE, MCCARTHY, SPEAKER, REPUBLICANS, VOTES\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, MUDSLIDES, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 STORM, TRACK, RAIN, FLOODING, WINDS\par \par \uc2 HAMLIN, HOSPITAL, COLLAPSE, \uc2 NFL\uc2 , GLENN, HAYTHE, DAWKINS\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, ATTACK, RUSSIA, SOLDIERS, DRONES\par \par \uc2 SANTOS, CONGRESS, LIES, INVESTIGATIONS\par \par \uc2 MEGA MILLIONS, JACKPOT, DRAWING, BILLION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADO THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM: "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 MURDERS, KOHBERGER, EXTRADITION, PARIS, CHARGES, LLOYD\par \par \uc2 SINGER, COLLEGE ADMISSIONS, SENTENCING\par \par \uc2 RENNER, SNOW PLOW, ACCIDENT, HOSPITAL, INJURIES, BALAAM\par \par \uc2 HALF COURT, THUNDER, WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOW FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH DRAMA ON CAPITOL HILL: SPEAKER BATTLE\par \par \uc2 SPEAKER, BATTLE, MCCARTHY, REBELLION\par \par \uc2 STORM, WEST, RAIN, MUDSLIDES, SNOW\par \par \uc2 FDA, ABORTION, PHARMACIES, MIFEPRISTONE, PREGNANCIES\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Robin. We turn now to the new rules from the fda. For the first time, the fda is allowing retail pharmacies to fill prescriptions for abortion pills. Erielle Reshef is here to break down what the new rules mean for women and some states. Good morning, Erielle.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning to you, Linsey. This move will make abortion more accessible in some places. On Tuesday, the fda announcing that it will allow pharmacies to distribute the abortion pill Mifepristone directly to patients. Pharmacies will need to apply for certification and it does require a prescription. The pill can be used within the first ten to 11 weeks of pregnancy. The medication will be available in a brand name and a generic version from two different drugmakers.\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This drug was approved for use in 2000, but it was more tightly regulated then. This change will also permanently remove restrictions on mailing those pills, only in states where abortion is legal. But the new rule also opens the door for a fresh round of legal challenges, as you can imagine, abortion bans. Some targeting this specific medication are already in effect in more than a dozen states. Guys.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Erielle, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:11 WYNONNA JUDD OPENS UP\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up on our "GMA" Morning Menu, country superstar Wynonna Judd on the challenges she's facing right now and how she's working hard on her mental health.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:14 CPR 101\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also this morning, after Damar Hamlin's terrifying on-field collapse, the importance of cpr and the first three things everyone should do if they see someone collapse like that. Doctor Ashton gives us a demonstration.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:39 TWINS BORN "A YEAR" APART\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Plus, the Texas twins born a year apart, sort of. Their mom and dad join us live to share their girls' unique story.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 8:30 WINSTON DUKE LIVE!\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're both beautiful. And "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" star, Winston Duke, he is bringing it, coming in strong. He is here live. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA, WEDNESDAY , JAN 4\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230127ej140000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 STRESS-INDUCED MISCARRIAGE? ROYALS CLAIM MEDIA COVERAGE CONTRIBUTED TO PREGNANCY LOSS\b0\par\par\uc2 709 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUDDENLY SUSPENDED\par \par \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 , MUSK, SUSPENSION, JOURNALISTS, DOXXING\par \par \uc2 STORMS, SNOW, ICE, RAIN, HOCHUL, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 STORM, ICE, SNOWFALL, BLIZZARD, COLD\par \par \uc2 INFECTIONS, COVID, MICHIELS, JHA, BROWNSTEIN, STREP\par \par \uc2 BORDER, MIGRANTS, SPIKE, RESOURCES, LEESER, D'AGOSTINO\par \par \uc2 GUILTY, DEAN, JEFFERSON, CONVICTED, BATES, CARR\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, AAA, ROADS, WEATHER, FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , FUTBOL, WORLD CUP, MESSI, MBAPPE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TORNADO OUTBREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BEAUTY AND THE BEAST"\par \par \uc2 STUDENTS, MURDERS, LANIER, HYUNDAI, GONCALVES\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, SHIPPING, USPS, SORTING, DEADLINES\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, STUDY, ALGORITHM, AHMED, CONTENT, SOUTER\par \par \uc2 49ERS, PURDY, MR. IRRELEVANT, TD, WINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEMPERATURE OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: TWITTER BAN\par \par \uc2 TWITTER, MUSK, JOURNALISTS, SUSPENDS, POLICIES\par \par \uc2 STORM, SEVERE, SNOW, ICE, RAIN\par \par \uc2 SHIPPING, HOLIDAYS, CUTOFF, USPS, DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 MOORE, OLYMPIC, BASKETBALL, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 PERRY, CONFESSION, SUICIDE, "TWITCH"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 STRESS, MISCARRIAGE, HARRY, MEGHAN\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Michael. Thanks so much. We're going to turn now to that high-profile Harry and Meghan documentary, and Harry's claim in that documentary that the stress from media contributed to Meghan's miscarriage. Our chief medical correspondent, Doctor Jennifer Ashton is here with more on this. This certainly, Jen, good morning. It certainly has a lot of folks talking, a lot of women in particular asking questions. So is there any medical evidence that stress can contribute to a miscarriage?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, I think that if stress contributed to a miscarriage, the human race would have ended a long time ago. I mean, the reality is I think it's completely normal and natural when a couple suffers a pregnancy loss to look for answers or an explanation or blame and a lot of times there is none. But we know that stress and women are built for this, so if it were easy as a stressful day, a stressful life, a stressful job, a stressful home environment, we wouldn't have any human beings left on the planet.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And I think we're having a little bit of a problem with your microphone, so I'm going to hand you that right there...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...so that we can actually hear because this is a really important conversation. So this, you are an expert in this. What can, do we know, medically cause a miscarriage?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MISCARRIAGE CAUSES: HEALTH EXPERTS SAY STRESS NOT CONNECTED TO PREGNANCY LOSS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think there are some common factors that when we do find a cause for pregnancy loss, we think of, number one, you know, the embryo is just not developing properly. There can be chromosomal abnormalities. That's probably the most common cause, but then when you look at just how common this is, an estimated one out of every ten pregnancies ends in miscarriage, the medical term is spontaneous abortion really. A lot of people think we should rename it from the word miscarriage. 80 percent of these losses occur in the first 12 or 13 weeks and it is more common as women get older. Over the age of 40, one in three pregnancies ends in early loss.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And those numbers are so important so you know you're not alone. This happens to so many women.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when those women come to you after they've had a miscarriage, what do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I tell them it's a loss. Give yourself time to grieve as an individual, as a couple. Oftentimes, the physical healing is much easier than the emotional healing, but the thing that I start with and I end with is remain hopeful. That's the thing that I've learned after doing this for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It can be a long journey. Good words there, Jen.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You bet.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you so much. George, we're going to throw it over to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020230111eicg00067\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE MIDTERM SURPRISE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ELECTIONS, TRUMP, WARNOCK, WALKER, FROST, SANDERS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. Tonight, pollsters and pundits perhaps scratching their heads as voters defied expectations in the midterms. We still don't know the winners of several key races. But one thing we do know, many of the candidates endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not fare well.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One day post-midterms and America still doesn't know who will control Congress. As the country waits in a throwback to 2020, Senate control once again could come down to the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BRAD RAFFENSPERGER (REP) (GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE)\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, everyone wants to know that we have honest and fair elections. And we do. I'll ask the voters to come out and vote one last time.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The hotly contested Senate race between the incumbent Democrat, Raphael Warnock, and the Trump-backed former football star, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , now headed for a runoff on December 6th. The results of that race, like others across the country, defying expectations of a red wave.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If anything, it was somewhat of a red ripple, maybe nothing at all in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 With their votes, the American people have spoken and proven once again that democracy is who we are.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At a press conference at the White House today, President Biden stressing the need to reach across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 On this election season, the American people made it clear. They don't want every day going forward to be a constant political battle. There's too much of that going on. There's too much that we have to do.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT, 197; REPUBLICAN, 211\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Twenty-four hours after the election, Republicans still expected to take the House, but by a much more humble margin than anticipated.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They were talking big at the beginning of the cycle. They thought this was going to be a wipeout. They thought they'd be in there with lots of room to spare. If Republicans end up controlling the House, it's going to be by a kind of margin like the Democrats have had it, maybe 10 seats, maybe 15 tops.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So far, the Senate races have resulted in an even power split. Each party holding 48 seats with races in Nevada and Arizona still undecided.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Midterm elections are usually brutal for the party in power. Despite all of the headwinds, despite all the history, despite all the headlines around inflation and the concerns that people have, the Democrats did way better than expected.\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Republican Party has been going through an identity crisis for quite some time since the arrival of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now it seems that there is some consequence to going down that path electorally.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some political observers say the biggest loser of the night, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsement is still the most valuable endorsement in Republican primaries, but he is a loser when it comes to general elections. He was a loser dramatically in these midterms. But we think about it, since he became president, the Republicans lost the House, they lost the Senate, they lost the White House. It's a very bad track record.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At his election watch party in Mar-a-Lago, he was reportedly fuming as many of the most high-profile candidates with the Trump seal of approval went down.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's not sitting well with him. I'm told he is in a foul mood. He said, kind of set the tone earlier in the day when he said, "If my candidates win, then I should get the credit. If they lose, then I shouldn't get any blame at all."\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 We want Oz to get some of the juice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His candidate for the Pennsylvania Senate seat, celebrity Dr. Mehmet Oz, soundly defeated by Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENATOR-ELECT JOHN FETTERMAN (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I never expected that we were going to turn these red counties blue. But we did what we needed to do, and we had that conversation across every one of those counties.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Trump's pick for governor of Pennsylvania, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , one of the candidates who repeatedly lied about the 2020 election outcome, lost as well. But one of Trump's endorsements who did win, "Hillbilly Elegy" author J.D. Vance in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR-ELECT J.D. VANCE (REP) (OHIO)\par \par \uc2 Whether you voted for me or not, the thing that I promise to do is go to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and fight every single day for the people of Ohio. Thanks to you, we get an opportunity to do just that.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While Trump may have lost some luster, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  shining bright. He didn't seek or receive Trump's endorsement, but he didn't need the former president's help, winning by nearly 20 points.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for a historic landslide victory.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trump is just gearing up to take on DeSantis. He sees DeSantis as somebody that he created. And, in fact, when DeSantis ran the first time, he would not have won without Trump. But DeSantis has gone his own way, and I think that really bothers Trump.\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This flirtation with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as the heir apparent to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been going on for about a year now. And it's clear that the DeSantis camp has been listening. They are doing and engaging in things that look as though he's laying the foundation for a presidential run and to potentially challenge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Georgia, voters easily re-elected Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who stood up to Trump when the former president questioned Georgia's 2020 election results. But the state's voters will be forced back to the polls in next month's Senate runoff.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (DEM) (SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I remain as committed to this work as I have ever been. And I look forward to continuing on that journey together over the next six years.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I'm telling you right now, I'm like Ricky Bobby. I don't come to lose. And I told you, he's going to be tough to beat. He's going to be tough to beat.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Endorsed by Trump, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been plagued by scandal after scandal, including accusations from two women who say the staunchly anti-abortion candidate paid for their abortions. I spoke to one of them just last week, who says Walker actually pressured her into getting the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I told my parents that I had a miscarriage because I couldn't tell them the truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why, in your mind, did you feel like you couldn't tell them the truth?\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It just was very shameful. And I felt like I had been manipulated.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says it's not just the hypocrisy, but the dishonesty that inspired her to come forward. ABC News has not independently confirmed the other accuser's story. Walker has denied those claims, calling them lies. But voters still seem to be split on his competence for office. According to ABC News exit polling, about 33% of Georgia voters say that Walker shows good judgment while more than 50% say the same of his opponent.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We saw the scandals, all the headlines. And it seemed to many Georgians that he was just too far out of the mainstream for them to want to pull that lever for them.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Major social issues like abortion driving voters, especially suburban women, to the polls. In California, Vermont, and Michigan, voters approved amendments to their state's constitutions that would codify abortion rights, squashing any likelihood of future bans on the procedure. Meanwhile, voters in Kentucky, a reliably red state, rejected a measure that would have prevented any legal challenges to that state's abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hell hath no fury like women mobilizing to vote when their rights are at stake. And the Dobbs decision has clearly demonstrated that women voters were paying attention, that they were not going to stand by and allow extremist positions to infringe on their personal rights and liberty for their own reproductive choices.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The midterms were also full of historic firsts. Author and political newcomer Wes Moore, a Democrat, became the first black person elected governor in Maryland's history. He spoke to my colleague, Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR-ELECT WES MOORE (DEM) (MARYLAND)\par \par \uc2 It's remarkably humbling. You know, last night, I had a chance to, in my acceptance address, I honored my grandmother who actually passed away last week at the age of 95, who was really the matriarch of my family. Despite us losing her last week that, tonight, she's got the best seat in the House. One of the last things she got a chance to do was vote for her grandson to be the governor of a state that she loved.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR-ELECT MAURA HEALEY (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Massachusetts, \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  not only became the first woman elected governor in that state...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR-ELECT MAURA HEALEY (DEM) (MASSACHUSETTS)\par \par \uc2 I hope tonight shows you that you can be whatever, whoever, you want to be.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...but also the first openly lesbian governor in the nation. Meanwhile, Trump's former press secretary, \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 , became the first woman to be elected governor in Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR-ELECT \uc2 SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS\uc2  (REP) (ARKANSAS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much to the people of Arkansas, who have entrusted this responsibility to me. I will be forever grateful to each of you, and I promise I won't let you down.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT MAXWELL FROST (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 We made history.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Maxwell Frost, a 25-year-old Democrat from Florida, became the first Gen Z-er elected to Congress.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT MAXWELL FROST (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Gen Z, half of us are not old enough to vote yet. The youngest Gen Z-er is 13 right now. And so, what that shows me there's a lot of hope and promise in this nation as young people are starting to rise up and really step into their political power.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLIMATE CONSERVATIVE\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When we come back, the young conservative activist working to elect Republicans to fight climate change.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221118eib90005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELECTION NIGHT 2022 ELECTION NIGHT COVERAGE\b0\par\par\uc2 23,388 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, RESULTS, FETTERMAN, ZELENSKYY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: 218 TO CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM: 184; REP: 207\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you back with us here for our election night coverage here on ABC. Now about to 12:37. Many of you in the east are now rejoining our coverage, and we're grateful for that. Still many storylines unfolding at this hour. We continue to follow those key numbers. Remember, control of both the House and the Senate at stake here tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: 51 TO CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM, 46; REP, 46\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Republicans need a net gain of one Senate seat. The math hasn't gotten there yet tonight. They need a net gain of five House seats to take back control of the House. The math not there yet, either. But working in both directions. It's been a sort of a split story across the country. You've got New Hampshire, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  holding on. In Colorado, Michael Bennet holding on to their Senate seats. But a lot of other questions. You know, in Ohio, J.D. Vance has won now. He beat the Democrat Tim Ryan. In Arizona, it's still too early to call. And in Pennsylvania, tonight, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  against Dr. Oz, another key Senate race, virtually a dead heat tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rick Klein was showing us how much of the outstanding vote is in the Philadelphia area, which would tend to bode well for the Democratic candidate. So, we continue to follow that very closely. We also follow tonight the issue of abortion rights across this country going into tonight. We knew the economy, inflation, and abortion rights top of mind in many places. And so, let's go through what we have so far. ABC News can now report that, in Michigan, they voted, 54% voted yes to proposal 3.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROP 22-3: PROTECT REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS IN STATE CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And a yes vote means they will support providing a state constitutional right to reproductive freedom, which is defined as the right to make and effectuate decisions about all matters relating to pregnancy. So, they voted yes tonight. That has passed in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CALIFORNIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROP 1 GUARANTEE RIGHT TO AN ABORTION IN STATE CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The California proposition, proposition 1, also has passed tonight by a vote of 69% to 31% there. A yes vote in California supporting amending the state constitution for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VERMONT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROP 5 GUARANTEE REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS IN STATE CONSTITUTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Vermont tonight, it's proposal five in Vermont, also passing, 76% to 24%. A yes vote there in Vermont supporting amending the Vermont constitution. Let's get right over to Rachel Scott, tracking this all night long. And this has been an issue across the country, where they're amending constitutions, it's passed tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION RIGHTS IN AMERICA\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, David. So, I'm going to circle the states where that has passed, protecting access to abortion. California, Michigan, and Vermont. And just take a look at the map here. This is the landscape after Roe vs. Wade was overturned. The orange here are all the states that have banned abortion. The result is you get abortion deserts across the country. This has been a defining issue for Democrats. It's proven to be, especially seeing that these three measures passed. There are also two measures on the ballot to restrict access to abortion. That's going to be in Montana and Kentucky. Still don't know the results of those yet. But I can tell you that, if Kentucky does move to restrict access to abortion, this would add just another state to the already sweeping ban that we're seeing across this region.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The sort of blocks of color tell the story of the country tonight in both directions on this deeply personal issue. I want to bring in Juju Chang, who is live in Michigan not only covering the governor's race there, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 's race being challenged by Tudor Dixon, but also this abortion rights proposition on the ballot that, Juju, we now learned has passed tonight.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. And the folks who have brought that issue to the voters say that tonight's result show that Michigan has paved the way for future efforts to restore the rights and protections of Roe v. Wade nationwide. Now, there are a couple of ways in which abortion has become operationalized in Michigan. Some of it we knew coming in. We knew that female voters were out-registering male voters by a factor of 8%. And that Democrats were out-registering Republicans by 18%. And some of that came to light. In addition, we covered a new kind of political organizing with Red Wine and Blue.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was a social gathering where they would harness the social networks of suburban women who were energized around abortion, asking them to text their friends and family to get out to vote. And using abortion as an animating issue to get women out. Now, this has lifted a number of Democratic candidates, including Governor Whitmer. You can see behind me, the stage is being set for her to come out. She has enjoyed a comfortable lead in the polls, but still it's very tight. Wayne County has yet to report. Some of the down ballot issues have also come in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , 54.3%; TUDOR DIXON, 47.9%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the down ballot candidates have come out. The attorney general, the secretary of state, they've all prevailed. And many tonight see abortion as the issue moving forward to get energized voters in the blue states ahead, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Juju, while we have you, we want to just review these numbers with you of where the governor's race stands tonight. If we could put the boards back up tonight. This race, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  facing challenger Tudor Dixon tonight. As you can see, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  leading 50% to 48%, it's a two-point race with only 55% of the vote in. It's been hovering around the same number for quite some time. I don't know if you are hearing anything from the campaign or not or what you're sensing on the ground there, Juju.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What we're hearing is that Wayne County, which includes metropolitan Detroit, has yet to report, obviously that's a predominantly African American district that goes very blue. But, you know, we've also pointed out throughout the evening, David, that Michigan voters in the exit polls said that abortion, unlike other states, was the number one issue on their minds. Putting it at 47% compared to 28% for inflation. And that was also seen in some of the Senate races, in \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 's race, as you pointed out. But honestly, I think the Dobbs effect, this Supreme Court overturning Roe has definitely animated Michigan in a way to get out the vote tonight. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju Chang, incredible reporting as always from Michigan. Juju, thank you. I want to bring in Linsey Davis, who has been tracking the exit polls both nationally. And you've heard Juju mention there the exit polls out of Michigan. I'm curious, the comparison, how they match up or not?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I want to just add on to what Juju was saying, because we're seeing that abortion is not just energizing those in blue states, but in red states. Of course, this was Kansas that started the trend this summer in voting to support and protect reproductive rights. And Michigan, again, as she just suggested, 45%. You're talking about half of Michigan voters said that abortion was the top of mind, their number one issue.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The same is true in Pennsylvania. Now, nationally, we don't see that. Nationally, we see abortion about a quarter, just a little more than a quarter of those voters said that that was their issue.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But where this is really interesting, David, in voters between the ages of 18 to 19, 44% said that, 18 to 29, 44% said that abortion was their top issue. That's more than double their concern about inflation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you have to wonder, had abortion rights been on the ballot in proposition form in other states, if you would have seen more numbers like what you saw in the exit polls in Michigan. It's really interesting.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as Cecilia pointed out before, too, had this election happened more closely to the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade, would we have found a different result, as well, more pro-Democrats?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring in Kate Shaw, who covers the Supreme Court for us. And, Kate, I mean, listen, a couple of weeks, obviously, makes the difference when you look at the polling. Nate Silver showed us, from FiveThirtyEight earlier, the sort of gulf between the two parties when Roe was overturned. And then as we got closer to the election, the race tightened significantly. But in states where abortion rights were on the ballot, you know, the exit polling reflects that.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. So, not every state allows for this kind of direct democracy, where you can put these kinds of questions directly on the ballot to amend the state constitution, but lots of states do allow that to happen. And you have to wonder why more states didn't sort of push to put abortion explicitly, protections for abortion or, you know, in either direction, on the ballot in order to drive turnout. But I do think you're seeing, in Michigan, that was the core of the message. It was on the ballot independently and at the center of all of the campaigns. And I think we're see what the results look like.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Kate Shaw. Thanks, Kate, very much. Rick Klein, I wanted to come back over to you for Michigan. We heard Juju Chang saying Wayne County, which is the Detroit metro area, that they haven't received the returns yet or at least all of the returns. Is that affecting why we're not getting any closer to knowing who's going to win this race for governor in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITMER, 51.5%; DIXON, 46.8%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAYNE COUNTY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHITMER, 69.3%; DIXON, 29.3%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. That's exactly right. Look, we've got less than half of the vote in there. We know this is heavily Democratic territory. But remember the two things you have to keep in mind. Where is the vote that's not in and what kind of vote are we talking about? Both of those things matter. So, the Democrats would tend to benefit from the fact that there's so much vote out from a Democratic city, for the fact there's so much election day vote that still needs to be reported is something that would tend to benefit the Republicans. So, our team is going to be very cautious in making all of these projections. They don't want to get too far ahead of the data. We're talking about 130,000-vote edge.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Clearly, Governor Whitmer is in a better position right now than Tudor Dixon, especially when you look at some of the benchmarks that she's seeing around the state. This tells me that some of the parts that she's gotten back so far are probably more Democratic than the rest of the county. So, I think waiting on some of these other results in Wayne County is almost certainly what's holding this decision up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The race for governor in Michigan. So, let's go back to Pennsylvania and then we'll do Georgia as well for those who are rejoining us in the east. So, now, we have the whole country back with us on the air. And these are races that still have not been called. In Pennsylvania, you were telling us moments ago that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is still in the hunt here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN, 49.3%; OZ, 48.2%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHILADELPHIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN, 80.8%; OZ, 17.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and it's a similar story where a lot of the vote is out out of Philadelphia. Now, that is going to be Democratic vote based on where it's from. But because of when it's from, it's possible that it's more heavily Republican. So, that's why we're going to be cautious. 56,000 votes seems like a lot. It's possible to make that up. But if you look at where things are out right now, they are still in some Democratic strongholds. As I go through county by county, as I look at where \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the FiveThirtyEight benchmarks, I don't see how the votes get there. But again, there's no upside in being too aggressive on a call like this. Just even looking out in western Pennsylvania, where \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is from, the fact that he's overperforming where he needs to go.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a county that is always on the map. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was able to bring Erie County back to the Democrats when he won. And guess what? \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is doing a little bit better than the benchmark would suggest, a little bit better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I talked to the Fetterman campaign. They said their map is basically the Biden map and they seem to be recreating that and maybe a little more than that. Right now, it would seem to be on track for doing a little better. That's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's hometown, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is doing three points better than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. That's incredible. We love hearing that tonight. Listen, if he gets a win out of Pennsylvania, he won't mind that number that you just cited. Now, the reason you think, and we're being conservative on this and careful obviously, but you're talking about day-of vote.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is still today's vote. Sometimes that can lean more Republican, but it's still in heavily Democratic areas that we're waiting for the vote to come in.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. And specifically in Philadelphia, we had a Republican lawsuit that was meant to create another barrier to a quick report, that would create some more procedural hurdles of matching all of the data before it was released. The commissioners there that oversee the election decided just this morning they were going to be more cautious in releasing that vote. And that's why we are seeing the delay out of Philadelphia. Otherwise, I think we'd see a lot more vote in right now. And if the vote was like the other vote, you'd be seeing big numbers presumably for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, this is an incredible race to watch. And we knew we'd be watching Pennsylvania at this hour. Now, let's go to Georgia, Rick, and where that stands now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 49.1%; WALKER, 48.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Yeah. So, in Georgia, right now, just in the last 10, 20 minutes or so, we've seen the numbers flip. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  now has a numeric advantage over \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And what you're seeing, with this Warnock campaign, is he's hitting the benchmarks just about everywhere.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FULTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 73.0%; WALKER, 25.2%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can look right here in Fulton County, just a touch above. This is big, big votes. We heard Steve Osunsami talking about Gwinnett County.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GWINNETT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 59.5%; WALKER, 38.1%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Again, just a point above. In fact, it was the data from this county, just north of Atlanta, these northern suburbs that have gone so Democratic recently. That is such an important thing for the Warnock campaign. We're also seeing him overperform slightly what he needs to do in Savannah. I'm looking at these numbers right now and I'm saying, okay, 12,000-vote edge, that's nothing. But the race is to 50, and that's going to be hard to get based on what's out there now. We're talking about 94% of the expected vote that's in right now. It's going to be hard to get past 50% and that would mean a runoff.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, I know you're also tracking Georgia, because you traveled through covering this campaign. You want to show us the counties you were in? And, again, this would appear, I mean, you guys were reporting this together night after night on "World News," that we'd be looking at Pennsylvania and Georgia this late in the game. And here we are, dead heat in both of these states.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've been laser focused on this race, because it just has been so tight, it couldn't get any closer than this Georgia Senate race. I was on the ground in this state right as those allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  broke that he paid for a woman's abortion. We started in Atlanta, we followed him all across the rural parts of the state. An interesting strategy that he was doing here, trying to firm up some reliably red parts of the state, right? Rural voters, things, voters that would back him. It was interesting talking to voters right here in Cobb County, for instance, where Republican and independent-leaning conservatives told me that they saw this more so as a choice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COBB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 56.1%; WALKER, 41.1%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They saw it as a choice between the Democrats running the Senate or Republicans running the Senate. They didn't agree with anything, everything that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  said, even some of them that had abortion at the top of their list, maybe had some issues, maybe were turned off by those allegations that he denied, but they saw this as a clear choice between which party is going to be in control in Washington.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, this is incredible, Jon. Let's widen out, big picture here. It's now 1:00 AM in the morning, almost, ten minutes from now, and we're dealing with not necessarily when it comes to the Senate, anyway, any massive wave on either side. I mean, it looks like, you know, we said at the beginning of the night, crucial midterms in a divided America. And look at Pennsylvania, look at Georgia, 49-49 in both states.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Here's what I would tell you, big picture. I can't tell you if the Republicans ultimately are going to win the Senate, they might. I can't tell you if they're likely to win the House, it sure looks like they could. But what I can tell you is the biggest loser tonight is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . His candidates, his hand-picked candidates, lost in states Republicans thought they could win. In New Hampshire, with Bolduc, in Pennsylvania with Mastriano. The fact that you have his hand-picked candidates for Senate in Pennsylvania, Oz, in Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , you know, both, you know, struggling. You know, again, we don't know what the results are there, but these were states Republicans thought they were going to be able to flip.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And on the flip side of this, who won tonight on the Republican side? It was Republicans who went up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It was \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia winning.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Georgia, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was, it was \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  in New Hampshire, really tough critic of Trump, who didn't have Trump's endorsement, who won handily. It was \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who didn't have Trump's endorsement and has won by a huge margin in Florida. So, the people that stood either opposed to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or not with help from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have won. Those that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought to this race have so far either lost or are losing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, a follow-up to that, though, let's drill down to the congressional districts because you're talking about all the big-ticket races, right, and that's absolutely true. But we still are dealing with a number, hey, Rick, can you pull up the election deniers, I think we were dealing with 100 or so election deniers winning in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP CANDIDATES \uc2 WHO\uc2  QUESTIONED THE 2020 ELECTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And again, this is not a race for Senate or a race for governor, but this is still representative of what we're going to see in Congress as far as people who don't believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election in 2020.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. But I have to say, though, even on this point, and that's an alarming number, no doubt. But in terms of the offices that really matter for the administrating of elections, the election deniers are not winning by and large. If you look at Mastriano, Pennsylvania, he appoints, the governor of Pennsylvania appoints the secretary of state. He lost decisively. Mark Finchum, the, running for secretary of state out in Arizona, visited, you know, was there at the Capitol on January 6th, we haven't called that race yet, but he's trailing badly. The secretary of state candidate in Michigan for the Republicans, another election denier, lost. So, you know, yes, a lot of these House members who voted to overturn results are getting re-elected, not surprising. But in those races that really matter in terms of administering elections, you know, so far, not a big wave there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just to follow-up to this conversation. Terry Moran, does this mean, though, when you're looking at the House of Representatives, when it comes to election deniers, it's a hyper local issue in America and depends on the community you're representing?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, maybe, but I think a lot of those are incumbent Republicans already. It is a loyalty test within the Republican Party, but I'd like to mention a name that hasn't been mentioned here. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . It turns out that there are a small but significant number of \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  Republicans that cannot go along with the craziest end of the election denying business. And that can hurt people like Kari Lake. That can hurt people like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . And there is that segment of the Republican Party that did not go along with Trump certainly in 2020 and that are growing farther and farther away from him. It is a bad night for Trump. And while it's not a good night for \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , she's not going to be in Congress, her influence, and she really is a stand-in for all of those ordinary Republicans who are not that way and don't like people who are that way and won't go out and vote for them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very interesting. And if Kevin McCarthy does become House speaker, he still, Martha, deals with a fractured Congress, given that graphic.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He certainly does. And I know you've got to go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips in our nation's capital. The House and Senate at stake as America votes. And we are following the flood of results coming in on this high-stakes midterm election night. So glad you're streaming with us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TONY EVERS, 50.0%; TIM MICHELS, 48.9%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to those candidates at battle in key races, like in the state of Wisconsin, where incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is locked in a close race with his GOP challenger, \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANDELA BARNES, 48.3%; RON JOHNSON, 51.6%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And all eyes on the Senate race, as well, between incumbent Republican Ron Johnson and Democratic challenger and current Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's exactly where we find our Alex Perez. He is reporting from Wisconsin tonight. Alex, this race still very tight this hour. Give us a sense of where things stand.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Kyra. What we've been seeing the last couple of hours is that Senator Ron Johnson has been widening his lead over Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, who is running against him. But, of course, Kyra, something we have to keep in mind here is that, we are still waiting for a significant portion of the vote from some of the densely populated areas like Milwaukee County. So, you really can't call this yet until we're able to get all of that vote in, Kyra, but a competitive race but, right now, it appears that Senator Johnson is pulling ahead.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Alex, Johnson and Barnes definitely two very different candidates. So, break down some of the dynamics here in this race and what these candidates actually ran on.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Kyra, they really could not be any more different. Johnson, we're talking about, 67-year-old Trump loyalist, election denier, who really made it a point to, when he's out there campaigning, make this about inflation, the economy and also crime. Mandela Barnes making this more about abortion rights, Kyra. And we will see what the voters here will decide once those final returns are in, Kyra.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alex, thank you so much. It's "Your Voice, Your Vote" tonight. Results coming in across America still flooding in. Control of Congress remains up for grabs. So glad you're streaming with us here on ABC News Live.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from New York City and across the country, "Election Night 2022." Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back, everyone. It is morning here in the east, 1:00 AM. The polls have closed in Alaska, the 50th and final state tonight. When the sun rises, Americans will be waking up to some surprises, but still some questions that need to be answered tonight. We're tracking all of these key races, still many of them undecided. Let's take a look at things where they stand for those of you just joining in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROJECTED WINNER: \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know 10:00 PM in the west, again, 1:00 AM here in the east. In New Hampshire, tonight, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , the incumbent, held on to her seat. That's a hold, again, for the Democrats in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROJECTED WINNER: J.D. VANCE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Ohio, tonight, J.D. Vance beating Tim Ryan. That's a hold as well, filling the seat of Rob Portman.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROJECTED WINNER: MARCO RUBIO\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Florida, tonight, the Senate race there called very early on, Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a very good night for the Republicans in Florida. He holds that seat obviously. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  also winning decisively in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROJECTED WINNER: TED BUDD\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In North Carolina, the Senate race, Representative \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  beating Cheri Beasley. That again is a hold.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , 49.4%; MEHMET OZ, 48.1%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Pennsylvania, this is too close to call at this hour. We're being extremely careful with this race. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  with a slight edge over Dr. Mehmet Oz. As Rick Klein was showing us at the big board earlier, much of the outstanding vote is in the Philadelphia area, which tends to lean Democratic. But at the same time, much of the vote that still hasn't been tabulated here is day-of vote, which at times can be more Republican. So, that's why we're being very careful with the state of Pennsylvania tonight. But it's looking like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is still in the hunt here certainly against Dr. Oz. The question is, can Dr. Oz eke out enough votes in the remaining votes here in the Philadelphia area? That's why we haven't called Pennsylvania tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAPHAEL WARNOCK, 49.2%, \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , 48.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Georgia, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent, with a very slight lead over his challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Again, we were saying these races were tight going into tonight, and look at where they are, 49%-48%, essentially tied in Georgia as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK KELLY, 57.2%; BLAKE MASTERS, 40.5%)2\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Arizona Senate race tonight. The incumbent there, Senator Mark Kelly, seeking his first full term, of course, astronaut, a combat veteran, married to former Congresswoman \uc2 Gabrielle Giffords\uc2 , up against \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 . This has been holding at 54% of the vote. Authorities in Arizona say they continue to tabulate, they're following their process, said we haven't got new numbers in a while, so this remains at a stalemate. Mark Kelly with a lead but still a lot of outstanding votes to be counted there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO, 54.3%; ADAM LAXALT, 43.0%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Nevada, tonight, the Senate race there, all eyes on Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the incumbent there. This is the first time we're starting to see some numbers. This is the first major batch of numbers coming in from Nevada tonight, 44% of the vote in and the incumbent holding the lead there against her challenger, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . Again, many were labeling her as one of the most endangered Democratic incumbents. She's the first Latina senator. But they also said this would come down to the ground game, remembering the Harry Reid machine in Nevada. They went door to door, the Culinary Union in those final days.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Again, if she's able to pull this out, a lot of people will turn to that Nevada machine that had been built even before her, helped bring her to the Senate in 2016. Will it help her eke it out tonight or will the remaining vote bring \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  into more even standing with \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  at this hour?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANDELA BARNES, 49.2%, RON JOHNSON, 50.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We want to go to Wisconsin, where Ron Johnson seeking a third term. Look at this race, 50% to 49%. The lieutenant governor, Mandela Barnes, 35 years old, giving Ron Johnson a very big challenge tonight, 88% of the vote in. And, again, we should dive into the areas where that vote is still outstanding. Rick Klein over at the big board pulling it up as I speak, so let's get right over to Rick. And, Rick, wow, 49%-50%. What are we still waiting on here?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MILWAUKEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BARNES, 69.8%; JOHNSON, 30.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Milwaukee, Milwaukee, Milwaukee, right? 77% of the vote in in Milwaukee, but this is a heavily Democratic area. And you can see Mandela Barnes is beating where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in this very close race. And just as tellingly, right on the mark with the benchmark, which means either he's going to continue to pile up votes out of Milwaukee, which always, always, always takes a long time to count. It is just a perennial part of covering an election. We're seeing in Waukesha, in Washington County, in Ozaukee County, you see most of the vote in as we are in much of the rest of the state. I've been struck by numbers we're seeing up in the Green Bay area as well. Mandela Barnes is almost getting where he needs to go even in tough red part parts the state.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But until we see more out of Milwaukee, it's going to be hard to project the state. It is such a big city. If you want to take a look, I mean, these are the population centers. They happen to be the biggest Democratic areas. Dane County, again, there's a good, decent amount of vote that's out there. And that is heavily, heavily for Mandela Barnes right there around the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2 . This is a little bit of a tougher county in Waukesha. But again, Barnes is at least holding his own in so many of these suburbs and then he's just racking up the big numbers in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we look at these numbers, Heidi Heitkamp, you said that Mandela Barnes was still in the game. And look at this, 49%-50%. And we're waiting for outstanding votes in the Milwaukee area.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I mean, this is, this is all about a very unpopular, you know, second-term senator running in a state that is legitimately purple and great organization on the ground. And what we're hearing is the youth vote is going to play a huge part of this. And I have to just say, in all these races, we've been told abortion isn't important, abortion isn't important, abortion isn't important. I think what you're seeing over and over and over again is women are coming out, men are coming out, and abortion is on the ballot. And that's what you're seeing there. Johnson's a hard-liner, right to life, running in a state that literally is not.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that reminds me of what Linsey said just moments ago in the national exit polling, 18 to 29, abortion rights is a key issue on this midterm election. And we know in Wisconsin there is a large youth vote also helping to bring Mandela Barnes to where he is here tonight. I just want to go in the outstanding votes in a number of these states where it's now 49%-49%. So, we've got Wisconsin that's a dead heat. All right, this could also be the Senate race that determines, in the end, control of the Senate. Pennsylvania, this is fascinating. I know they're being extraordinarily careful with this. But again, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Dr. Oz. 49%-48% and the outstanding area is Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHILADELPHIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN, 80.8%; OZ, 17.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and that's just it, David, is that this is where it gets hard to see how Oz makes up votes. If you take a look at the vote that we have in already, we've got about three-quarters of the election day, but we've also got a lot of early by-mail votes. So, you would expect that these numbers would actually grow for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  out of Philadelphia, unless there's some quirk in the type of vote, the way it's being processed, the way it's coming in. There really isn't enough out there. Delco is another big county right outside Philadelphia. But, again, this is Fetterman territory. And we've got a decent sample, and a large enough sample out of there to know that it's coming in for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, again, the key with these things, it becomes about two different big questions. Who or where is the vote out of? What type of voter we talking about? And in both those cases, you've got to be cautious. Right now, who the vote is would tend to benefit the Republican. But the where the vote is, these are blue parts of the state. And the point that I've been making all night, David, is that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is holding his own just about everywhere. The fact that he's able to run up big numbers there in the Philadelphia area, the western part of the state, those are about two-thirds of the state right there. He's even competitive in some other parts of the central, in the central part of the state. This is a clinic and he is following the model that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was able to enact to win the state back from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Outperforming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in his own hometown?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LUZERNE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN, 44.4%; OZ, 53.2 %\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, it tells you a lot right there in Scranton. You look at the Biden numbers, a couple of points ahead of that. I've been looking next door in Luzerne County, where you've got Wilkes-Barre. And, again, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , you know, not necessarily winning but doing well enough to tap into something real.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, so we've been talking about Pennsylvania. So, let's head over to Nate Silver. If you were to give Pennsylvania, hypothetically, you know, 1:00 in the morning, to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , let's just see what it does to the Democrats' chances.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WHAT IF?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we were at 39, and this would bounce up a lot, I would anticipate, to 68.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They still have a ways to go. And if Dr. Oz pulls it out in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Then that would flip it completely around and put Republicans pretty high, I would think, 82. But Democrats have much reason to feel good about Pennsylvania for all the reasons that you and Rick were talking about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. All right. Well, they're still counting votes in Pennsylvania. I know you're looking at Nevada. We just got our first major batch of numbers coming in in this race. You've been reporting on this, Rachel, because \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  got a lot of attention, whether warranted or not, as being one of the most endangered senators. The polling going in was very close. But \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  started to get a lead in these final days by just a point or two. And so far, tell us where we're seeing the vote coming in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLARK COUNTY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORTEZ MASTO, 55.0%; LAXALT, 42.2%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, I'm paying attention right now to the area just outside and around Las Vegas. This is Clark County here. You can see she's overperforming where President Biden did in 2020. Let's look at the FiveThirtyEight benchmark here, overperforming in the FiveThirtyEight benchmark as well. As Heidi Heitkamp was talking, I was really thinking about some of the voters that I met on the ground in Nevada. We drove about 150 miles to a place called Caliente outside of Las Vegas. And I met a conservative Republican who said that she was on the fence. She didn't know if she wanted to vote for \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  because abortion rights was her number one issue.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she was actually considering voting for the Democrat. And this is exactly the type of voter that Cortez Masto is betting on in this race right now. She has made abortion rights a very key defining issue as Laxalt has leaned into the economy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it's interesting. You were saying, if she's going to win, it's going to be Clark County. And this is only 64% of the vote and she's outperforming Biden and her benchmark.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So far. So far, she is. But again, it's still early. Only 44% of the vote expected in right now, David. And in Nevada, every single voter was mailed a mail-in ballot. So, still really early to tell.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott watching the numbers coming in from Nevada. There we have it. ABC News can now project, and this is a major headline, 1:10 in the morning here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS PROJECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , ABC News projecting, will win Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , SWITCH, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is a switch. There's going to be a lot of talk about the 11th hour help that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  received from former President Obama, from President Biden, from Oprah Winfrey, who she introduced the country to Dr. Oz on "The Oprah Show" for many years. In the 11th hour she offered her endorsement, not to Dr. Oz, but to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And also, this whole debate about whether or not when he got on that debate stage and showed, you know, what it's like to recover from a stroke, whether that would hurt or help. And he argued until the very last day, including last night in making his campaign speeches, that he'd grown more empathetic. "This allows me to understand people, families, and what they go through when they get knocked down."\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, it's not just switch, it's the first switch of the night. And it makes a big, big difference in the battle for the Senate. As you can see, the Democrats now have a real path. And you can see, and you can see lots of other things move in front of it. But look, that's the first time we've had a switch all night. Look, the other ones that we projected, those are Republicans holding Republican seats, these are Democrats holding Democratic seats. Remember, the Democrats had to hold serve tonight. They just have to hold the seats they already had. Now, they're winning a seat that had been held by a Republican. Now, they're in a commanding position with other states on the map.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM, 47; REP, 46\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If they're able to hold on in Arizona, that would put them at 49. And then they're just one little old seat away. It could come in Nevada where we're starting to get some data or it could come in Georgia, where there's at least the possibility of a runoff. But regardless, this is an enormous pickup for them. A big boost for Democrats. And as you mentioned, David, one of the few places \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  personally went, invested a lot of energy and a lot of attention in that state going head-to-head with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that's going to, a lot of people are going to ask, you know, people kept \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at bay, at arm's length. And this was a state in the end that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  said, come home, come home to Pennsylvania. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stood there. The President said, "I know Pennsylvania, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is Pennsylvania." Let's find out what the exit polls said about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Linsey, you've been diving into this, what voters in Pennsylvania said today. And wow, this is quite a headline. ABC News now projecting \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , this is a switch, major headline, a switch to the Democrats in the Senate for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very significant. We look at, again, Pennsylvania and Michigan, the only states that said abortion was number one before inflation. Abortion was an issue that Dr. Oz was a little bit slippery on. He wouldn't really, he kind of dodged often just as much as Governor Sununu did with you, he kind of played that dodge game whether or not he would support \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA: IS FETTERMAN IN GOOD ENOUGH HEALTH TO REPRESENT STATE?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Fetterman's health, in the end, not enough of an issue. The question for Pennsylvania voters, is Fetterman in good enough health to represent the state? By a slim margin, but he did make it to the 50% mark there. His medical doctor did release a note saying that he was fit to run as senator. And then with Oz's views, again, initially he was running really closely to Trump, really celebrating that endorsement that he got. At a certain point then, he really moved away and said, well, I don't consider myself to be MAGA, I consider myself to be more of a moderate. But again, we saw earlier, we don't have it pulled up right now to show you but, in the end, it seemed to matter to a lot of voters that they really didn't believe that Oz lived in Pennsylvania long enough. And I think that that really played a big role here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you have to wonder if the Oz campaign is going to look back at the last two days and ask themselves, was it smart to have former President Trump right there in Pennsylvania? They did bring in \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  the next day, a much more moderate Republican. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was there with Dr. Oz in the final argument when Dr. Oz was trying to tell voters, I'll be a moderate Republican. I want to get right to Eva Pilgrim live in Pennsylvania. She knows Pennsylvania very well. Any reaction from the Fetterman campaign tonight?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, the Fetterman campaign is obviously in a very good mood. This crowd behind us has been cheering as they were getting the numbers coming just a short time ago. I think we're going to see them cheering here again just now, celebrating. This was not something that they were expecting to get a result on tonight. A lot of people were expecting that this was going to go on for several days. The state of Pennsylvania, local officials, bracing people for the fact that this vote could take a while. And to have a result tonight is a big win for this campaign. You heard Linsey talking about the fact that a lot of people didn't view Dr. Oz as a true Pennsylvanians.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when I was talking to people specifically in those suburbs around Philadelphia, you have to think about the families that live in those collar counties, they have lived there for generation after generation. Those are counties where kids go off to school and they come back to raise their family generation after generation. They are entrenched in those communities. And people repeatedly told me, he was not a Pennsylvanian. That was something that came up repeatedly. The other big thing that came up with those suburban women, those moms, was the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even if they weren't big fans of the policies that Fetterman supported, that issue of abortion was what swayed them to vote for him. And the other group that I heard that from as well were fathers repeatedly telling me the same thing. They had children, they had daughters, they were concerned about that issue, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, that point is really landing. Eva, thanks so much. And from the exit polls from Linsey showing abortion rights the top issue in Pennsylvania, not only for women but for men. And you were just looking at the images there up on the big screen. Former President Obama, President Biden, arm in arm with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in those final days.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And also, Josh Shapiro, the very strong Democrat who ran for governor there and won by a larger margin earlier in the night, going to be talk about how he helped \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  get over the finish line as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile, not diminishing in any way the victory here from \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Incredible that you have a man who was once mayor of Braddock, right? Lieutenant governor who suffers a stroke and gets up on that debate stage anyway knowing he'd have to read closed captioning to survive the debate. And he's now won tonight.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was quite emotional watching the debate. Chris and I were there in October. Mr. Oz and Mr. Shapiro but Mr. Fetterman was not there. I was concerned that he would not have the energy to continue the race. But you know what, you cannot knock down Big John. He knows how to stay in touch with the people. He had a great social media campaign, a great ground game. And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  campaigned saying, come, come, come.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile, let's listen to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He's at the podium right now, the new senator from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Fetterman. Fetterman. Fetterman. Fetterman. Fetterman. Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I, I'm not really sure really what to say right now, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We love you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 I am...\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Just say you're a winner.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Here's to the working class. We did it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I mean...\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We love you, John.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 So, I am, I'm so humbled. Thank you so much. Really. Thank you. Thank you. Like, it's, what is it, it's like 1:30 in the morning and you're still here hanging in? We want...\par \par \uc2 SUPPORTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 We love you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PROJECTED WINNER PENNSYLVANIA SENATE)\par \par \uc2 We launched this campaign almost two years ago. And we had our slogan. It's on every one of those signs right now. Every county, every vote. Every county, every vote. And that's exactly what happened. We jammed them up