 Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's exactly where our Steve Osunsami is in Atlanta with the Warnock campaign. Steve, what are we seeing at this hour?\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, the Warnock campaign is still here. They have family members and other supporters who have moved to the back of the stage. And the reason why they're still here is because, when you look at the vote total, the latest vote totals we've looked at, Warnock is ahead by about 30,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The percentage is still underneath what it would take to avoid a runoff. But they believe that there are enough outstanding votes in the Atlanta metro counties to possibly, and I underline that, possibly put them over the finish line. Right now, if you were to look at that vote and also include the military provisional ballots that are going to come at the end of the week, we're still probably looking at a runoff situation, which can take place at an election four weeks from tonight.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Steve, you know, young voters giving Senator Warnock clearly a bit of a boost here. But it looks like Republicans are standing by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  despite those controversies in the final weeks of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. You know, I've been making the argument all night that, you know, people who I've talked with who live in town, who were voting for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , had concerns about it, became more emboldened and inspired to vote for the man after some of these allegations broke. There were people who were telling me they were questioning the timing of this and that this felt like dirty tricks and that, you know, where were these women coming forward before? And so, there are some people who would say that it may have actually galvanized the vote a little bit for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who denied these allegations right and left.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Steve Osunsami, it's going to be a late morning. Good to see you. Another projection tonight, or I guess this morning we should say.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TONY EVERS, 50.9%; TIM MICHELS, 48.0%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News projecting that incumbent Democrat Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  winning another term there in Wisconsin, beating out GOP challenger \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 . It's "Your Vote, Your Voice" all morning as results from across America still flooding in and control of Congress remains up for grabs. We are so glad you are streaming with us here on ABC News Live.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 ABC News "Election Night 2022" continues. Here again, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM, 47; REP, 47\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There you go, now you hear me loud and clear, right? We're good? These are the numbers on the floor, let's take a look. It's the story of the night. Control of the Senate still not determined. And remember, we said this at the very beginning of the evening. The Senate, Republicans need a net gain of just one seat. So, this is basically 50, the race to 51, for Republicans, 50 for the Democrats, because obviously Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is still the tiebreaking vote. They would be happy with 50 seats plus \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Republicans need to get to 51. They need a net gain of one seat to take back control of the Senate. And we have this major headline out of Pennsylvania moments ago, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , of course, flipping Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He is the new senator from Pennsylvania. He now heads to Washington. A lot of this is still up for grabs tonight with very close races, particularly in Georgia. It's a dead heat. In Nevada, early numbers coming in. In Arizona, we're waiting for more results. All of these very close Senate races. Let's take a look at one race, though, we can project. ABC News ready to project the Utah Senate race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: UTAH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIKE LEE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  seeking a third term. The Republican in that state, he was facing a challenge from Evan McMullin, the independent. Democrats teaming up with the independent, but \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  will head back to the Senate. Again, you don't need me to tell you, all eyes across this nation watching this race. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with a slight, and I mean slight edge over \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , 95% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO, 54.1%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 ADAM LAXALT\uc2 , 43.2%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To Nevada, tonight, we do actually have some numbers to update this. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , 54% to \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 's 43%. Approaching half the vote in. And again, Clark County isn't fully accounted for. Clark County largely believed to be the place because it's the home of Las Vegas, many union workers. If \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is able to pull this out given the polling going in, it would likely be in Clark County. We don't have all the numbers from there, so this race has a long way to go tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK KELLY, 57.2%; BLAKE MASTERS, 40.5%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Arizona, the incumbent Senator Mark Kelly seeking his first full term. You can see there leading \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 . 54% of the vote in but a sizable lead early on. We're waiting for more information on the areas where this vote is coming from and who is left to be tabulated obviously. But Mark Kelly got to be happy with at least an early lead against \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 . Another very close race tonight is in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANDELA BARNES, 49.1%; RON JOHNSON 50.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Senate race there. Ron Johnson, the incumbent, leading but very slightly by a little more than a percent with 88% of the vote in. The vote that's still not fully accounted for is the vote in and around Milwaukee, which obviously is a largely Democratic area. So, this race obviously still too close to be called. I want to bring in Rick Klein who's over at the big board. And, Rick, first of all, let's just take these one at a time.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because, at this point, the story of the night is all about who is going to control the Senate when this is all said and done. And in Georgia, you know, we've watched them go, Walker was in the lead for just a short amount of time tonight. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  now back in the lead by a percentage point. What are we waiting for in this state?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, keep in mind, this is a state that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by about 11,000, 12,000 votes. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is winning by almost exactly the same margin. The key here is, really, there's two different ways that Warnock has to win. He has to beat \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the actual vote and he has to top 50% to be projected as the winner tonight. I am really struck by how he is performing in the Atlanta area.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FULTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 73%; WALKER, 25.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is, of course, the place where Democrats want to do really, really well. And they're almost exactly hitting their numbers. Nate and his team, those are really smart people, because they're hitting the numbers almost exactly. I swear to you, these numbers were there earlier in the night, and he's doing exactly what he needs to do. DeKalb County, also Atlanta and the Atlanta environs. And then if you look up a little bit north of the city, these are places that Republicans often just romp in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHEROKEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 29.3%; WALKER, 67.3%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORSYTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK, 32.3%; WALKER, 64.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the fact that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  can take almost a third of the vote in, say, Forsythe County, that's a big deal for his campaign. And that shows you why he's able to show at least a numeric lead right here with, you know, north of 96% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That he's doing well in deep red counties, really says something, keeping him in the game. Take a break from the Senate race just to bring you this other race. ABC News ready to project now that, in Michigan, the race for governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , will remain the governor there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She was seeking another term, she has now won, beating her opponent Tudor Dixon 52% to 46% in the end. Of course, abortion rights also on the bat local. A lot of people are going to dissect how powerful those things were together.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The race for governor and obviously the proposition that passed tonight. I want to get back to the Senate races now. Rachel, I know you were diving in to Nevada, right, at this hour and what we know so far?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. And so, less than half the vote in so far. These are very early results that we are seeing. But with 44% of the vote in right now, you are seeing the incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  with a slight lead. Again, this is a race that has tightened really in the final stretch ahead of the election. We're paying a lot of the attention, as you mentioned, to Clark County. This is where Las Vegas is. And mostly where 70% of voters actually live there. We know \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , I was on the ground covering this race in the final stretch. He was really campaigning in the more rural parts of the state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's just, let's just drill down on this a little bit. How is he doing versus Trump? And how is she doing versus Biden, say, not, Clark County, we can do that first. But we'll go to the more Republican county in a second. Let's start with Clark.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's start with Clark County. So, right now, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , 55%. She's overperforming where President Biden was in 2020. Take a look at \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican. He's underperforming the number than Trump was.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And 60% of the vote in, there's still significant vote in the Las Vegas area to come in.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. So, if this continues, he's going to need to make up ground here in some more of the rural counties. Again, these are a lot smaller here. Less voters do live here. But take a look. I mean, right now, 95% of the vote in. He's still underperforming where former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in 2020.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. All right, thanks. We want to go back over to Arizona. Thank you, Rachel. Rick is now digging into Arizona. And, again, we're kind of holding at 54% of the vote here. They say there's nothing wrong in Arizona, right, that this is part of the process? What's their explanation?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, David, I know I'm supposed to play the smart guy on TV.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But I don't have the answers to everything.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this is one that I don't totally know. We don't have that much visibility into what's holding things up other than they're saying this is the process that they intended to follow. That meant that they were going to release most of the votes that were cast well in advance of election day, and then get out election day votes. And then the wait would come for things that came late in the mail, Sunday, Monday, Tuesday. And might even be arriving, as long as it's postmarked in time beyond that. This is what we do know, though, Maricopa County is the heart of this state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARICOPA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KELLY, 59.1%; MASTERS, 38.7%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you can see, it's about 60% of the voting population. They're saying they have just about all of the election day vote in. They're waiting on some of this early by-mail vote. So, that would tell you a little something about the margins. The thing is about Maricopa County, this is a place that is a battleground. It is a lot of people that moved into the state. It's the fastest-growing county in the whole country. It was a place where \uc2 John McCain\uc2  used to win with huge margins. It has now become a very closely contested area. You can see in our FiveThirtyEight benchmark, you'd think it'd be about a tie. The fact that Mark Kelly is up again, it really depends on the rest of the vote that's out there. I would expect that the margins start to collapse as more numbers come in. But that's a pretty big number right now, looking at 17 points.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when you're talking about election day vote, that tends to lean Republican day of, right?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It does. And there's not a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know there is (inaudible) mail-in, about 90% of people do mail-ins?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. There's not a lot of it in Arizona. But, yes, you'd expect advantage there. And we know that the Republicans have been spreading lots of false claims about elections and election security in Arizona. That is definitely going to be part of this. But if you look at where Mark Kelly needs to be doing well, this is his home county, Pima County. This is Tucson, that's where his wife, \uc2 Gabrielle Giffords\uc2 , was a congresswoman. He's running above where he needs to be in those places. And these redder parts, there are just not, there's not as many people live there. These are just smaller counties. And that's where \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is picking up his vote.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, waiting on Arizona, waiting on Nevada, waiting on Georgia. Look at the big wall here. These are the numbers. Again, the race to 50 for the Democrats, they have a net gain of one. The race to 51 for Republicans to take control of the Senate if they'd like to, which they obviously would. The night is still young, apparently.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 48, +1 NET GAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICAN: 47\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the big headline this last hour, the lieutenant governor in Pennsylvania, the new senator from Pennsylvania, that is a flip. Yvette Simpson watching our coverage from our Washington, DC bureau. And, Yvette, what do you make what was you're seeing tonight? How do you label it? How do you define it? What do you make of the \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  win?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I think if Republicans had their way, this would not be the day they were having. So, we'll consider that a victory. There were certainly some races that were a little too close for comfort. But the fact that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  pulled it off is such a victory. Mandela Barnes, if we had a little bit more investment, we could see Wisconsin. And we're holding out for him. And, of course, Cheri Beasley, a race where we had a little more investment. So, I think when we get out of this race as Democrats, we're going to say, where can we make a few more investments going into 2024 and release some strongholds, especially in the southern states, where we are seeing a lot of the losses.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Before we get to 2024, let me ask about Wisconsin because it's still so close tonight. If this is a race where, in the end, Mandela Barnes does not win, are there going to be serious questions for the Democrats about why they didn't do more to help him?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. He's a lieutenant governor, he's won in the state before. You think about all the hype around \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , rightly so. But Mandela Barnes also deserves that hype. A little bit more investment. He was running against an incumbent. And look at how close he's gotten. And so, I think we will be asking these questions if he does fall short. And whether he does or not, I think we need to talk about making sure we're making these investments with candidates like Mandela going forward.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yvette, thanks so much. \uc2 Sarah Isgur\uc2 , a former Trump administration official with the Justice Department also, worked with the Bush administration. Sarah, there was talk about the potential of a red wave. I think everyone acknowledges here there doesn't feel, particularly when you're looking at the Senate like there's been a wave for either party tonight. What do you make of what you're seeing?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is a shocking night for a lot of Republicans. What I'm hearing is that they were expecting a red wave, if not a red tsunami. They were looking at states like Colorado, like Washington State, those races were called early after the polls closed. And so, I think you could see this as a cross-wave election in which you had certain dynamics working against Democrats. The economy, gas prices, the President's approval numbers. But you also had a cross-current working against Republicans. Whether it was Trump, abortion, or simply not making the case that they were a party ready to lead. And so, the result has been a lot of incumbents win. It looks like the status quo wins out. That voters were just happy with the status quo when, in fact, I think turbulence under the water looking at these numbers in a few days.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A cross-wave election, I'm not sure I've heard that before. But I get totally what you mean, Sarah. I think both parties are going to be asking questions about what it is we witnessed unfold here tonight. Sarah, thank you. I know Mary Bruce, as we continue to look at the Senate numbers, and I think we should stay focused. This is the story of the hour, who is going to control the Senate? We await to see where the House lands given sort of the contextual history of modern times, the party in power in the White House generally loses seats. We know the bar was very low for Republicans tonight, just a net gain of five seats. The math, no one has been able to project anything with the House. And you have a statement now from House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is out with a statement. She is saying, the Democrats have strongly outperformed expectations. And as we've been discussing, this is not the night that Republicans were expecting. It's not the night the Democrats were expecting either. I mean, they had pretty much conceded that they were going to lose the House. They were still hopeful about the Senate. But the fact that you are not seeing that red wave, that red tsunami, this is a big deal for Democrats. And I think it's a big deal for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . It's a really big deal for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I think you're going to see him, when we do hear from him, which we expect will be at some point tomorrow, that he's going to have a lot of the touting to do here.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's a lot of the question about the strategy that he had played out leading up to this election, whether he was focused not enough on the economy, too much on the bigger picture of what was at stake in our democracy here. I think you are going to see that the President, given especially the bad night that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to be feeling pretty good about all of this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Linsey Davis, you're diving into the numbers. Mary, thank you. You're seeing some evidence of how Democrats might have picked up some independent voters in the House?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARTY IDENTIFICATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INDEPENDENT: 31%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE: 49%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICAN CANDIDATE: 47%\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sure. Take a look at this, David. I mean, independents tend to be able to vote for the winning side. If this is any indication, any ability to read the tea leaves, you have the independent voters who just by two points are voting more toward the Democratic candidate, 49% to 47%. And so, this really is evidence of Democrats really being able to hold their own and perhaps why we're not seeing that red wave that many anticipated with the exception of, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did say, was it just this week, that Democrats are going to surprise the living devil out of a lot of people. And perhaps they've done that. The man who just one day, yesterday, became the, 50 years ago, the youngest US senator. Really keeping that hope alive and perhaps in the end he was right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, what are they saying at the White House tonight? How is the President feeling? And I'm sure he's been making calls to people who won tonight. He's got to be happy with what he's seeing.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. He finally stopped the calls. He made a lot of them to supporters. Look, I can tell you, in text exchanges that I'm having from here on the desk with friends of the White House and people inside the White House, getting increasingly more exclamation marks inside these text messages. One of the last ones ended with "phew." They're breathing a bit of a sigh of relief. They're not counting their chickens just yet. But, look, certainly, they feel very good. I think what we're going to hear a lot of right now in terms of the spin, and it is a fact, they're framing this as Biden is having a better midterm night than any president since Bush. And that is a big thing for them in terms of the history that we're looking at here.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because just look at the storylines that we were coming into tonight with the President's approval rating, the economy, that lag in enthusiasm on abortion, the Trump factor. Was this a referendum on Biden's approval because of those low approval ratings? I think Mary just said we're expecting to hear from the president tomorrow. I expect, when we do, we will see that pep in the step that we often see from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that he likes to get that little shuffle going.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. But just think about something you just said. It stopped me when you said he's having, according to your sources and, listen, the math might bear this out, the best midterm election for a first-term president since \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , at least at this point.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And remember, that was right after 9/11 and this country was going to war. I mean, he's fighting all the historical norms with inflation at a 40-year high. You know, obviously the economy's a tale of two economies. You've got two jobs available for every one American available to take the job. But people are feeling this, gas prices and at the groceries. And for them, in some of these cases, to stick with the Democrats does speak volumes. And I think people are going to try to figure out what really drove voters?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm struck by a tweet that the former press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  put out not too long ago. She said, "So, Democrats have a Florida problem, but Republicans have a Trump problem. That seems harder to solve." I think that's what we're look at over the next few weeks in terms of how this Biden administration decides they can govern, legislatively, looking for potentially at a split Congress if they end up do end up losing the House. And what this means, we keep talking about it, but this is a very real part of this conversation, what this means for 2024. And, frankly, does this push him to decide to run again?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jon, it really does feel like a lot of this tonight was a Biden-Trump sort of rematch, even before a potential rematch in 2024. There was a lot of the people, you know, unhappy with the current state of affairs, but still, for some reason, deciding to stay the course despite all of the headlines about the economy coming at them.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just teased that he's going to make an announcement, a big announcement next week presumably about running for president. What \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done today, what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done in these midterms is he's given the Democrats a lifeline. I mean, you look at the big losses, as we talked about, David, the big losses that in places where Republicans thought they were going to win. Most of those were in places where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was directly implicated, either because he chose the candidate, he supported the candidate, his visit to Pennsylvania on Saturday, I think, is going to be talked about a lot. You know, Oz wasn't too thrilled to have that visit, I am sure, but he had to be there because there would be no candidate Oz if it weren't for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, so, yeah, I think this was a bit of, if it was round one of Biden and Trump, Trump didn't win this one.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor Christie, what do you make of tonight?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first, it's disappointing at the moment in a macro sense for Republicans because the hype beforehand was that it was going to be much better than what it's being. Now, if you come out of this in the next 48 hours with a, you know, or let's say in the next month in the Senate side with a Republican majority in the Senate, and you leave in the next couple of days with a Republican majority in the House, I think they'll be pretty happy. You know, it's not going to be huge but it's going to be taking control, again, both of those chambers. But the issue that Jon was raising is an important one because I remember the 2016 campaign, I was in it. And I remember \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying then, we're just going to keep winning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: REPS NEED 51 TO CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM, 48; REP, 47\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: 218 TO CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM, 188; REP, 207\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're going to do more winning and more winning or just so much winning that you're going to ask me to stop winning so much. You're going to get tired of winning. Well, if you look at tonight and the time before this, we lost both Houses of Congress in 2018. We lost the White House in 2020. We lost two winnable Senate seats in Georgia in a runoff in 2020. And now, tonight, you're going to have lost a Pennsylvania Senate seat, you look to be on your way to lose one in Arizona, you lost in New Hampshire, and you're in a runoff in Georgia. You know, that seems like a lot of losing. And I think that the party's going to have to decide in the aftermath of this.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even if we take majorities in both Houses, are we going to be the party of me or the party of us? And if we're going to be the party of me, and that me as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think general election voters are sending my party a really big message.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's the question from \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  for the Republican Party tonight. Donna Brazile, what do you make of what you're seeing?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, clearly, I feel a lot better, because the challenge was to make this a choice, not a referendum against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . There was a lot of the energy in the end from Democrats, especially young people. Early voting went as planned. For the three states that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  cared about, because of the Electoral College in 2024, Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania, governors who are firmly going to be able to control the process. So, yes, it's, it feels good. But clearly, there are some more danger signs.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. We're going to check on these races that are dead even. The Senate races that still could control and will determine who controls the Senate. We'll be right back after this.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election Night 2022." You can also watch on ABC News Live alongside commentary and election maps from FiveThirtyEight on Apple News.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are grateful you have stayed with us here tonight. ABC News live coverage approaching 2:00 AM in the morning. We are still on the air because there are still significant unanswered questions tonight. We started out the night with two key numbers. Republicans need a net gain of five seats in the House to take control of the House. Historically speaking, the party in control of the White House generally has a very difficult midterm. Tonight, the math isn't there yet, though, to say the Republicans are going to take back the House. The math also isn't there yet to determine who's going to control the Senate. You're looking at the numbers up on the screen. This is a race for the Democrats to 50.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fifty seats plus Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with the tiebreaking vote, and they will remain in control of the Senate. Republicans, as you can see, 47 seats. They need to get to 51 to take back control of the Senate. Now, all of this with Georgia hanging in the balance. Take a look tonight, the Georgia Senate race, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with a very slight lead, 96% of the vote in, over his challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . 49.1% to 48.8%. It doesn't get any closer than this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAPHAEL WARNOCK, 49.1%; \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , 48.8%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO, 53.1%; ADAM LAXALT, 44.2%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Nevada tonight, 54% of the vote in. The incumbent, Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the first Latina senator in the US, seeking her second term, largely believed to be an endangered incumbent, but she leads \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  at this hour. And some of these results include the key Clark County results, not all of them, but, of course, that's the home to Las Vegas. And if she's going to win this, the Democrat would need all the votes of Las Vegas they can get, especially with the help from union workers there and what we've been talking about here tonight, the Harry Reid machine that they did employ in these final weeks of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK KELLY, 57.2%; BLAKE MASTERS, 40.5%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now to the next race that's still undecided tonight, the Arizona Senate race. Mark Kelly, 57% to 40%. The challenger there, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , supported by former President Trump, an election denier in that state, then saying \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won. There was encouragement from the former president to stick with the election denier story as the Republican candidate for governor did, Kari Lake, hugely supported by the former president. We're tracking that race as well. But, again, the Senate race still an unanswered question with Mark Kelly and in a commanding lead with 54% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WISCONSIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MANDELA BARNES, 49.1%; RON JOHNSON, 50.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Very close in Wisconsin tonight. Ron Johnson trying to hold on to his seat. The incumbent in the lead by about a percentage point up against lieutenant governor, Mandela Barnes, with 49%. Again, Mandela Barnes looking to defeat Ron Johnson tonight, very close. The outstanding vote there with 93% of the vote in, in and around the Milwaukee area. So, you can see, as we go through each one of these states, these are unanswered questions. Any number of the Senate races could get to that magic number of 50 for the Democrats or 51 for the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What we haven't with us tonight is some sort of massive red wave or blue wave across the country. Instead, we have witnessed what we have known for several years now, we are in a deeply divided country. Let's head back over to Rick at the big board. And, Rick, Georgia tonight, we knew this would come down to Georgia and most likely Nevada. And we're looking at both of those states tonight.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 100%, David. And we talk about 50. Forget the numbers of 49 versus 48. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's number really doesn't matter at this point. What matters is whether \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or actually Walker for that matter, anyone gets to 50%. You have to get to 50% if you're going to be able to avoid a runoff. And this is what we're looking at at this hour. Our team thinks there's about 100,000 votes out there, mostly election day votes, in a and around Atlanta and the suburbs. In Cobb County, we think it's about 40,000 votes. Over in Gwinnett County, we think it's another 30,000 votes out there. And in DeKalb County, maybe 28,000 or so votes.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the thing is, these are places that are favoring the Democrats, right? So, if you've got those 100,000 votes, and there are some share of them that go toward the Democrat, maybe that number ticks up a little bit toward 50%. But, man, it's going to be a squeaker to get close to it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, again, as Rick Klein points out, he's been saying this for days, if we don't get 50%, we are headed to a runoff in Georgia. We're going to be back with most of the country in just minutes. For those of you in the west, it's time for local news. But I'll see you here with the whole team in just a moment with everybody else.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from New York City and across the country, "Election Night 2022." Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. 2:00 AM here in the east. I remember doing the overnight show here at ABC. It's great to be back. All right, so let's look at where we are in the House and in the Senate because these are the unanswered questions tonight. Again, Republicans need only a net gain of five seats in the House to take control of the House. You've got the number up, but you showed us the contest that's really, really important, what the party in the White House generally has faced. And if you just look back at recent times, it spells major losses.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL APPROVAL RATING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE SEATS GAINED/LOST\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Typically, yes. Especially when you have a president like President Biden whose approval rating is so low. I mean, look at former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , 2010, 44%, his approval rating, 63 seats lost in the House. 2018, former President Trump, 40%, right where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is right now, 40 seats lost in the House.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, where are we? Where do we things stand right now? What do we think we're looking at here?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE SWITCHED SEATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP TO DEM: 3 SEATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEM TO REP: 9 SEATS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Let's take a look at where things stand right now, David. So far, you have had three Republican seats switch to Democrats. You've had nine Democratic seats switch to Republicans. So, this is nowhere near the Republican red wave at this point that someone like Leader Kevin McCarthy wanted to see. In fact, we still have to project about 40 House races that we believe could decide control of the House. What we have listed here is sort of our battleground zone.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) On the left are seats held by Democrats. On the right, seats held by Republicans. Republicans want to see a lot of red. They're not really seeing it. Democrats are holding a lot of seats right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, the historical wins, Nate Silver, are still in the Republicans' favor. But what kind of number, give me a ballpark that you're comfortable with of where you think this night, this day, this week could end when it comes to the House.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 '" modeling would put the range at 10 to 15 if you had to guess. But there are, to Rachel's point, a lot of seats that are still uncalled. Three are also three seats Democrats won that were not on this list that were more favorable for Republicans than our model thought. The problem is they have to win the vast majority of these or find other unexpected victories. It's not impossible, but it's kind of like, you have to have a coin come up heads eight out of ten times or something. It's not that easy actually.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Well, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy, at this point, that's still his title, right? This is what he said moments ago, we've been waiting for him all night long, the time had been pushed back a bit. Here's what he said about what he's seeing in the House right now. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (REP-CA) (HOUSE MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight, we've built upon those gains two years ago. And it is clear that we are going to take the House back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, \uc2 Jon Karl\uc2 , what do you make of that? This is not where they thought they would be at this point in the night, but they're still convinced they're heading in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And as Nate said, I mean, they have good reason to think that they can take the House back. But the bottom line is, it's nowhere near the night they thought they were going to have and it's not a certainty that they're going to take the House back. And because the range is likely to be smaller, I mean, they may pick up, like, you know, as Nate suggesting, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," 10 to 15 seats. That gives Kevin McCarthy a five to ten-seat majority to work with. That's a really tough majority to govern any conference, especially one as unruly as the Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's interesting. If he ends up somewhere 10 to 15 seats, I want to go back to that board to shows the history. I mean, that's not like the shellacking that Obama talked about, the thumping from \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's second term. You know, the 40 seats that Trump lost. That's a much different picture.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is. Although what McCarthy would say is, he has a point here. They did pick up 20 seats in 2020. So, they kind of banked, you know, those seats in the last, in the last election. And they're building off that. So, you know, they don't need to get 63 seats like the Republicans got.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. Control is control. I mean, look at the Democrats in the Senate these last couple of years, 50 plus one and they were happy with it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They'd be happy if we were ending there tonight.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It all changes once you have control, even if it's by a single seat.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But how does governing, for Kevin McCarthy, differ depending on the size of the majority, given how fractured the Republican Party is in the House?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, he has a group of, you know, far-right, Freedom Caucus, pro-Trump, very, very hard-core pro-Trump Republicans. And if he only has a majority of five to ten seats, I mean, any small group can basically hold them hostage and say, we're not going to go along. You effectively don't have control. So, that's, that's the challenge. And there are, you know, but what's interesting is some of the Republicans that won tonight were more moderate. So, and by the way, one race I've been watching is \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  in Colorado. Way too close to call right now, but she's actually losing right now in Colorado with 84% of the vote in. She is about two points down.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, still a lot of unanswered questions in the House. Let's take you back over to the Senate now at 2:05 in the morning and let's check it out. Okay. So, Georgia first. You know, I feel like a broken record. We've been talking about Georgia but, again, dead heat. And \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the areas that are still outstanding, he has potential just to keep padding this very slight lead that he has.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Well, there's four states that are on our board that are potentially the majority makers for the Democrats. They really only have to get one of them back in their column if they hold the others tonight. And in Georgia, we're waiting on a big number of votes hear in Atlanta and the suburbs. These are areas that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has done pretty well. They're areas that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did very well. We're talking about 100,000 votes. And the key is, does \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  get enough out of those areas to get him up higher than 50% to avoid a runoff? Otherwise, we're going to be back in four weeks' time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gosh. If he doesn't get past, you know, 50%, exactly, we're back to December 6th with the runoff. For hypothetical purposes, give Georgia, let's say they pull it off, to the Democrats. Where are we?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Then they need just one more. So, that becomes comparably easy, they're at 92%.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. So, then you're talking \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada, in Clark County (inaudible) vote that still hasn't come in. If we want to look at Nevada, we can actually get a sense of what we know so far even though it's just a partial view of the vote. Let's give Georgia, by the way, to the Republicans if \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  ends up pulling this out in these final areas to come in, just a few percentage points left.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, that shows how the odds have shifted over the course of the night. Even with Georgia, it's more or less a 50/50 race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. That's kind of extraordinary. Okay. So, now, in Nevada, what do we have? What do we know?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we just heard from the secretary of state's office that they're not expecting a large number of votes out of counties that have already started reporting. This is where the people live, frankly. It's the Las Vegas area and the Reno area. Between the two of them, you've got more than 80% of the state. We still have a blank area. We're going to get this filled in, but they aren't that populous in terms of county. So, the expectation out in Nevada is that these numbers aren't going to change substantially tonight. You see \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  with a relative narrow lead. She's building it, though, on the back of the kind of lead that you need in Clark County. That's Las Vegas. That's really the most, by far the most populous part and, you know, hitting the benchmark almost exactly, maybe slightly above. So, we're not expecting a huge jump in additional data tonight out of Nevada, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this is interesting. So, she's just barely above the benchmark where she needs to be to be having a decent night or morning or next day. But in and around Reno, what is she doing as far as her benchmark is concerned?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you can see this is another area where it's just a touch. So, the question will become, what is the type of outstanding vote in these two most populous counties? The other counties are just aren't likely to change the margins. This is the recipe for winning if you're a Democrat or a Republican, frankly, you've got to hold your own in Las Vegas, you've got to hold your own in Reno because the rest of the state is going to be pretty red.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Arizona. I know we're still sort of at a standstill, right, where the vote is?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. But we are expecting more data at some point tonight. They're expecting to get more in there. And, look, these numbers are definitely going to get closer because of the type of vote that's outstanding. But, David, the fact that you've got a pretty substantial lead in Maricopa County for Mark Kelly is a very good sign for his campaign. They're going to be waiting for this next release of information to see if that holds.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We remember. We remember that Biden only barely won the state.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's go back and just look at the Biden number, too. He's beating his benchmark by 10 percentage points and he's beating Biden.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And beating Biden by about eight points. I mean, this is something, you know, Mark Kelly has an image that is separate from that of the national party, astronaut, obviously Gabby Gifford's husband. He's someone who's been able to separate himself from the national party to a pretty significant degree. And that's why he's able to outperform what some other Democrats might.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm curious. Back over there, Terry Moran, what do you think we're witnessing here tonight? There's certainly no wave to be found. I heard the argument for a cross-wave, I hadn't heard that before. But what is this that we're seeing here tonight other than just a divided America, 49-49 in some of these key states that we knew would determine the outcome in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Divided, yes, David. But it feels to me like the center held. The center won. America's middle class, middle of the road country. And it turns out that spinning wild, provenly false conspiracy theories about our elections, you know, is not the best political strategy and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who I've been thinking of a line that he used in that speech he gave on democracy, he says, I think I know this country. And it turns out that maybe he did. That maybe all of the talk about the red wave, he sensed that there was something deeper in the country, an attachment to the democracy without the wild conspiracy theories and it's not such a good idea to go against the majority of the country on a woman's right to choose. That also damaged Republicans. So, at the end of the day, maybe in this divided country, the lesson both parties can learn is move to the center.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really interesting. It makes me think of Marcy Kaptur in Ohio. Her final closing argument was, in the middle, let's meet in the middle. And it worked for her in a Republican-leaning district where she had to reintroduce herself. She ended up holding her seat in Congress. And, Heidi Heitkamp, I saw you nodding your head yes. Do you think that that is sort of one of the things that we can draw from tonight? That that the middle held, as Terry said.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, absolutely. And I think that when you look it, people want people who talk common sense, who don't promise things that they know they can't deliver. I think they're tired of the polarizing politics. And what they see in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a return to chaos, is a return to, you know, wild lies and falsehoods. And they're saying we don't want to go there. We want this country to change and be more, you know, equal economically for all of us. But we can't go back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put himself on the ballot by picking candidates, by campaigning, and I think it hurt the Republican Party badly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Maria Elena, one of the stories tonight is, the Republican Party is going to be doing a post-mortem. They did very well in Florida, as you reported very early in the night. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  probably the happiest Republican of the night. Among them, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  in New Hampshire as well. And not a surprise that both men are considered 2024 contenders.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right. They fit very good with Latinos. But in south Florida, not so much, in central Florida, where you have more Puerto Ricans and Dominicans. And I think, you know, I've been observing the Latino vote for decades, for three or four decades now. And one thing that I see is, yes, there was a small shift in 2020 toward Republicans. This time around, through the sources that I've been talking to, not much has changed. Maybe the only thing they're observing now is that there was less of a turnout. And what that tells you is that both parties really are ignoring this community. And they can't do that anymore because, remember, every 30 seconds, a Latino turns 18, they add a million voters every year.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, they can't afford to ignore them anymore because they do have influence in many of these states not just places like Arizona, like Florida, like Nevada, but even Georgia, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. So, Latinos are in every state of the country. They're going to continue to grow more. So, both parties need to pay attention there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there's no question the Latino voters could determine control of the Senate when you're looking at Nevada and Clark County.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Definitely.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're still waiting for full results coming in from Vegas and elsewhere, in Nevada and Arizona tonight. \uc2 Byron Pitts\uc2 , what's your takeaway tonight?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm thinking about something that \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  said at the very beginning of this evening. American voters are smart. And I'm also mindful of what Tip O'Neill once said, that all politics is local. What we saw despite the weight of history on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , despite the presence of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that many of these races were decided by the quality of the candidate. You look at Governor Kemp in Georgia, a conservative. He won because his record said he could return. You look at someone like Wes Moore in Maryland, a moderate. His life experience suggested that he might lead.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then my last thought is, if there is in fact a runoff in Georgia, as it appears that we're headed in that direction, the national Republicans, national Democrats, better like sweet tea and barbecue because they can't win, neither party can win there with big fancy TV ads produced in LA and New York. Whoever wins in Georgia, if it gets to a runoff, it'll be a ground game. Who's on the ground? Who's getting their base out to vote?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Steve Osunsami better be getting ready. I want to take everybody to the big board here because these numbers say it all. Look at the independent candidate. We haven't said his name once. Let's show this, Chase Oliver, 2% of the vote. And that's the 2% that could prevent them from getting to 50%, Rick Klein, which takes us to a runoff potentially.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAPHAEL WARNOCK, 49.1%; \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , 48.8%; CHASE OLIVER, 2.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah. That's your Libertarian candidate. And, look, I think a lot of those voters, it's safe to assume, were basically people that couldn't pull the lever for Herschel Walker. We've been talking all night about how Brian Kemp won as handily as he did and the fact that Herschel Walker is underperforming his vote by as many hundreds of thousands of votes, likely to be, at the end of the night. A lot of people parked their vote there or just left it blank. And that sets up a fascinating runoff because then are they people that just would never vote for Walker because of his many scandals?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Or are they someone who, with control of the Senate is truly on the line, they have to buck up and vote team red at the end of the day and go with Herschel Walker? It is fascinating. And, look, third-party candidates, they take from both. But I look at that tonight and think, those are people that were potentially going to vote for Herschel Walker. They wanted to register some kind of disapproval with incumbent, they just chose someone that they knew wouldn't win.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, listen, I know Terry Moran had been reporting extensively on the camo-wearing folks who were gathered at the ballot box in Arizona. Those images. So, I want to take this to Pierre Thomas, our chief justice correspondent, because we also had, obviously in recent days, the attack on Speaker Pelosi's husband. You had warnings about escalating rhetoric in this country and, you know, dare I say, there's a sigh of relief, obviously in this room as well, that we haven't had any reports tonight of problems, anything close to that level around the country.\par \par \uc2 PIERRE THOMAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. You had one situation in Wisconsin where a knife-wielding man did show up at the polling station, but they were able to get that situation under control in about 30 minutes. So, law enforcement tonight is breathing a sigh of relief. But they are concerned about the days after the election in terms of, will people accept the results? But I do think when Biden put the issue of American democracy being on the line, along with people voting on the issue of abortion, I think the images of January 6th still resonate with people. So, when he raised that had issue, perhaps people were thinking about, we don't need extreme politics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Kate Shaw, I'm reminded of something Mary Bruce said at the very beginning of the night, which is pay attention to abortion rights and where it's on the ballot in this country and the candidates had a made it an issue front and center. And I know a lot of the polling show that the Supreme Court overturning Roe was weeks and weeks ago, and that had faded in the minds of voters. But I think we've been shown something else tonight.\par \par \uc2 KATE SHAW (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it turns out, I don't think it did fade in the minds of many voters. Right? So, you've got to remember, Donald Trump transformed the Supreme Court. He put three justices on the court. He said they're going to overturn Roe and they did. And it looks like delivering on that promise actually has backfired in terms of the electoral fortunes, at least as things look right now, of the Republican Party. So, it does seem to me that abortion was a critical issue in this election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We heard Kari Lake, Dan Abrams, the readout of what she said to her supporters tonight when Whit Johnson was reporting from Arizona saying, we're not going to give this to the cheaters or what have you. This would appear to be more rhetoric, not anything substantial legally by any means.\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. So, you've got to separate out complaints for the court of public opinion and complaints you're actually going to lay out in a court of law. In a court of law, you need evidence. You need facts. You need something to back up a claim of an election that is stolen or misdeeds. In the court of public opinion, she can say whatever she wants. And that's the key. Will there be potential challenges in the courts? Possibly. I think it will depend on how close it is. But I think it is important to take a step back. This has been one of the most scrutinized elections ever.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATIE HOBBS, 55.3%; KARI LAKE, 44.7%\par \par \uc2 DAN ABRAMS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) More poll workers, more people watching, more scrutiny than any other election maybe in history. And you know what? There were some minor hiccups. There were some minor issues. Yeah, there's a power outage here, there's a problem here with a vote tabulator for a little while, but they still have the backup paper ballots ready to go and they just have to hand to somebody else in Maricopa County. My point is, when you think about how many millions of people voted tonight and how carefully this election was watched, things went pretty darn well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Knock on wood. But I think you're absolutely right, Dan. And I think at the end of the weeks and weeks of coverage leading up to this, that is definitely a major takeaway of tonight. Mary Bruce, I know you were talking with your sources on both sides of the aisle in Washington. Republicans still encouraged by the House. Democrats breathing a sigh of relief over the Senate. At the end of this night, where are they?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think Democrats are still very encouraged. Look, even at the end of this, right, even at the end of this night, the end of this weak, next week, Republicans are in control of the House. We don't know what's going to happen with the Senate. Just that they've beaten expectations, beaten the odds, that is huge for Democrats because it sends a message about what Americans care about. And at the core of it, that's what elections are about. They tell us what matters to Americans. And what the President, what Democrats have taken away from this is something that they didn't necessarily expect, which I think is that they feel that their message is resonating. Everyone was braced for huge concerns about the economy.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The President was being slammed for the way he tackled that issue leading into this. The fact that it seems that abortion resonated more than people thought it would. The fact that the President's message on the importance of democracy and what's at stake may have resonated more. That sends a huge message. But then you have to look forward. And the reality is, the President and this White House are still going to face, and Democrats are going to face real head winds up ahead. You are going to be headed to a very busy lame duck session potentially.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if it does seem likely that Republicans could take House, then that means that the President's agenda could be dead on arrival. And that creates real challenges for 2024. On the flip side, you have Donald Trump saying that, you know, teasing a potential run. Are we still going to see that on Tuesday if he is getting this strong of a message on a night like tonight? A lot of unanswered questions (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I think that's a very good question. Governor Chris Christie, there's a very real possibility at the end of tonight or whenever this ends, if Republicans do control the House, it may be a very slim majority in the Senate and we saw what the Democrats did with the Senate. They were happy with 50 plus the tiebreaking vote. So, Republicans will be happy if they're in control no matter what. But it's still not what the headlines, what the polling, what Republicans thought going into tonight.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, sure. You know, we didn't meet expectations. So, that's not good. On the other hand, the purpose of elections and the legislative body is to win enough seats to control the gavels and the agenda. And so, if we get to December 5th in Georgia and control is up and Herschel Walker wins, and you have control of the Senate and we will control of the House by the time all this is done, then you met your objectives. You've done what you needed to do with a Democratic president in the White House, right? So, you know, those things are the things that Republicans will be looking at in the near term. I think, in the long term, we have to look in these individual states like Pennsylvania, like Wisconsin, like Michigan, where we're going to have at best a split decision.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in some places, having lost everything in those states and know that those states are key to winning the presidency in '24 and say, okay, why is this not working with general election voters? And who can work with those voters and who can't? And who sells and who doesn't? And I think there's a big message for us to look at after tonight, which is, the quality of the candidate matters and that candidates appeal broadly to the American people is what's going to matter in a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Quality of the candidate matters.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna Brazile, what's the Democratic takeaway tonight?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I'm looking at this map and I'd say it really hurts that the only state that I can go back and do some campaigning in down south is Georgia. I mean, Democrats need to look at the fact that we fell short in North Carolina, Ohio and, of course, I'm really distraught about Florida. I mean, 537 votes. Al Gore would have been president and, David, I would be sitting in that chair. But be that as it is, I think Democrats need to go back and figure out, in the last three weeks, we had to get our act together and defend a lot of blue states. People don't understand how much money was poured into these blue states over the last three weeks to ensure that Republicans would not make gains in these gubernatorial races. I think that's number one.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Number two, we've got to do a better job with minorities. I don't know about you, Maria, but, you know, you're not a partisan. I'm a partisan. But getting called at the last minute to go and campaign at my age is not what I like to do anymore. So, early investments. Making sure that you're talking to voters. And you're not waiting until the last minute to, my nieces and nephews do not watch, David, they're watching us, they're streaming us. If you're going to spend $6 billion, know how to talk to young people. That's another thing. Joe Biden, he made this a choice not a referendum. When a candidate said, I wanted to campaign without, you know, all of the Washington people, he said, fine with me. I think Joe Biden had a good night tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Did you just put your nieces and nephews down? Are they still, are they?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're probably asking for tuition.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, I get it. I get it. Listen, I want to put a tweet up because this is somewhat newsworthy at this hour. This us from Gabriel Sterling, the chief operating officer, Georgia's Secretary of State's Office. Let's look at this tweet on the screen. You can see it lower, at the bottom there. He says, "While county officials are still doing the detailed work on counting the votes, we feel it is safe to say there will be a runoff for the US Senate here in Georgia slated for December 6th." Here we go again, Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And I was just texting with him, where is the vote out? And he says, there really isn't much. There's a little bit in Cobb County, some early by-mail votes that's still out there. But, yeah, we're headed to a runoff. You see the margin there. It's really hard to see how Raphael Warnock is able to exceed 50% based on the votes that's outstanding. So, yeah, we get to do it all over again. And the cook thing is I get, I hope I get to use this toy all over again. We've got the whole graphics team. I can't thank them enough for making this amazing, amazing mechanism for storytelling. It's just been a really, really compelling night to be able to bring all that information to our viewers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, let me ask you, Rick, what does history tell us with the runoff? Which party is better equipped, better organized, more suited for a runoff to perform again at this kind of level?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It used to be the Republicans until they flipped the script last time. And, you know, this, the whole runoff law was kind of anachronism. It was set up on another generation to try to block Democrats, particularly minority Democrats. But to the point that Donna made earlier, the team that Georgia was able to put together, powered by what Stacey Abrams did in 2018, delivered the Senate with two seats on the same night in 2020, of course, just the night before the January 6th riot at the Capitol. And the idea that now three Senate seats in the space of two years in this one state could control the Senate both times is sort of mind-boggling.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But we've literally been there and done that before. And so have Republicans. They learned lessons from the last time as well. They know what they want and don't want to happen. And I think Donald Trump not being in the state will be part of that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, let's see. We'll see. Rachel Scott, you covered Congress for us. It's going to be a different Congress. We just don't know the answer who is going to be in control of really either chamber. A slim, slim chance for the Democrats right in the House. But historically and the math, likely not. The question is, if it's Speaker Kevin McCarthy, how big is his majority? And in the Senate, we just simply don't know given what's outstanding. It could be really either party.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it just has major implications for the next two years and the future of President Biden's legislative agenda. I was down on the ground in Georgia one day before the runoff just the last time around talking to voters. And you think about how that win for Democrats solidified so much of what Democrats actually ran on this midterm election. That could all change again. There are just so much hanging in the balance. And I think Rick was really spot on there for those Republican voters that just had that question about Herschel Walker, maybe just skipped over that box on the ballot. How much does that change? Control of the Senate all hinges again on this one race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, party allegiance becomes extremely important when you're dealing with a runoff. So, Nate, do you have any magic math here? Where does the probability stand for the Senate?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, Democrats need two out of four. So, it's more deterministic than probabilistic at this point in some sense. And by the way, the pollsters had a pretty good night. I have to say that before (inaudible). They get a lot of crap in a lot of elections, but they got most these races were characterized in roughly the right way.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And so, give us, this is where it stands tonight.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, give us a little bit of the hypothetical here. What's in the middle is still to be decided.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, I mean, again, though, if you see Arizona there. I mean, that's (inaudible) it get called at some point tonight and Wisconsin there. That's a fun one. Let's see how that shift things. 75%. Then you have two coin flips basically. You have to win one of those two coin flips for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a very good chance and this is where the pollsters, Nate Silver among them said, Nevada and Georgia, down to those two. And look at that, that's a very real possibility. Jon Karl?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, look, we talked earlier today that we needed to have patience and humility. Patience because we knew results would take a long time and humility because we really didn't know how voters, how this was going to turn out. And I think that's the way that the night has played out. I mean, we're going to bed tonight, I think, without knowing, you know, who is going to control the House or the Senate. That was not the, that was not what most people thought. People thought this was going to be, there was a consensus was, you know, Republicans are going to have a pretty good night. They may have a good night in the House. They may win the House. They may win the Senate. But, you know, it's not going to be a decisive win one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really interesting. Before I get the last of the final thoughts here. I did want to remind everybody that there's another audience for this and it's overseas. And, Martha, you know this well. Rachel covering Congress. Well, there have been questions in these final weeks about funding, say, for Ukraine. We have a war. We have Vladimir Putin threatening nuclear weapons, obviously. Sabre-rattling if you will. James Longman and our team are over there in Kyiv. And obviously, we asked for them to check in with us. Zelenskyy told me in the fall that he was watching the midterms very closely. I asked him, you know, what was his take. And the politician that he is, he said I'm not going there but I am watching. So, let's take a look at what James reported in for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 JAMES LONGMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, Ukrainians are watching this election with some nervousness. Make no mistake, without American help, they don't feel they can win against Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 JAMES LONGMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, they have heard some of the skepticism among Republicans about the level of support that Kyiv is receiving and they know that the United States, just like their European partners, have domestic economic concerns that may make ongoing help for Ukraine politically less popular. Now, if Republicans do take the House, it's unlikely there'd be a major drop-off in help for Ukraine. The American public is still broadly supportive of defending against Vladimir Putin's forces. But how much funding and for how long? Well, that may become an issue. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) James Longman and our team in Kyiv. And we thank all of our teams who've been covering this war in Ukraine. And here's what President Zelenskyy told me when I asked him, are you following the midterms in the US. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have midterms coming, the midterm elections. And are you at all concerned about the balance of power and continuing support from the US?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE)\par \par \uc2 Of course. All these details will have interest on Ukraine. Yes, I follow but that is a decision of American people. And I don't want to, even if I can, even if I can. If I could, I don't want to, to have any influence on this decision.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, that's what President Zelenskyy said. And he's fully aware that, of the rhetoric here at home before talking about Ukraine funding. There's Marjorie Taylor Green, over the weekend, saying we will not give another, you know, whatever to Ukraine when it comes to money and the crowd cheering. Kevin McCarthy has backed off of what was said a few weeks back.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That it's not going to be a blank check? Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. But they're fully aware that the balance of control here in Washington does determine the path forward with how we handle Ukraine and all of our foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It does matter. And James had a point, that they are watching this. They don't want to say anything specific about that. He certainly didn't want to say anything specific to you. But they desperately need that funding to continue. I don't think either side at this point can win the war. So, Ukraine really has to keep pushing and pushing. But if you look at our economy, and you look at inflation, how long are Americans going to support this? If the Republicans control Congress, do they start saying, wait a minute, we've got to worry about things at home, and then Russia is a real threat. I also think you may see if Republicans control investigations into the Afghanistan situation, that everyone thinks was a disaster, but they may start investigating that and pushing on that as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Martha, thanks. And, Linsey, I know you were diving into the sort of point that Terry made earlier and Heidi Heitkamp, that the middle in this country held. Did anything bear out of the exit polling?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, one thing that I think that is really interesting, and Pierre kind of asked the question. Will voters accept the results? Overwhelmingly, 79% of Americans who voted tonight said that they trust their states, they're confident in the state's election. But, look, I started out tonight kind of with the Ray Charlie soundtrack in my mind. Georgia on my mind. Then it kind of went to Gladys Knight with "Midnight Train to Georgia," right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that was two hours ago.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to go back to the point, yes, yes. That's right. We're beyond that. We missed that train. But I want to go back to something that Chris Christie said as it refers to the runoff in Georgia. He was expecting that, potentially, Kemp was going to be able Herschel Walker into the end zone. Now, in the runoff, Walker's going to be on his own. He's going to, he's not going to have the support of voters who are also voting for the top ticket for governor. And something also that's interesting, the gerrymandering, the redistricting that happened between last election and this election that should have favored the Republicans, and we really didn't see that. But I'll leave you with this final, let's see, here we go. The question for Democratic candidates. Should Biden run for president in 2024? By a slim majority, 52% of Democrats said yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's an interesting number. Thank you for that. And I remember sitting down with Biden less than a year ago. And he said to me, I do plan on running. He said, I'm a great respecter of fate, I believe were his words. And he's repeated that answer a couple of times since then. And that is a big question. I'm sure he was waiting to see what would happen tonight. But I would have to think the White House and the President quietly has got to be pleased with holding the Senate at least where it is right now.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Certainly. And there have been meetings happening behind the scenes already. The White House aides say they would be doing political malpractice if they weren't planning for the president to start, to launch a run even though he has not declared that he will, in fact, run. Again, he is "I'm thinking about it," as he told you. It seems like this is a decision that he's going to announce now that the midterms are over at some point in the coming weeks if not months, even though we now have this potential Trump announcement lingering, looming, if you will. And the President has said that he still firmly believes that he would be the best one to take on Donald Trump if there were to be a rematch.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, David, just as we are closing the night out here, I'm thinking back to how tough it was for these first two years of the President's term to get his big-ticket agenda items. He wanted to be a president along the lines of FDR. How hard it was for him to get that massive climate and tax legislation passed. How hard it was for him to get something like infrastructure passed. He didn't get, go as far as he wanted to on guns or voting rights for that matter. And so, now, I look at the front-burner things that are on the agenda for him and I question how much he will be able to get done if, in fact, Republicans take the House and it seems like they may. Immigration. The President promised to fix immigration in this country and nothing has happened on immigration since he took office.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And very, very front burner, and this is a really important one. The fate of those DREAMers in this country, those 800,000 people who were brought to this country illegally as children, hangs in the balance. There could be a court ruling any day, any week. What is going to happen to them if there is a divided Congress and they can't even agree? Now, most Americans agree that they should be granted some kind of status. Washington can't get their act together on this one. So, it remains to be seen what happens there. That's a very important issue on the front burner.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that. Huge issue and all those questions you posed. And, Jon Karl, just one last question to you. Given what we heard from Pierre Thomas, no major issues. No major threats, especially in light of what we've been reporting on these last couple of weeks with the escalating rhetoric. And then what Linsey just said, the level of trust that despite all of the rhetoric out there about the elections, that Americans by and large trust democracy.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And we've seen candidates so far anyway who lost concede the races, trusting democracy. I tell you. One thing I am hearing, though, David, as a final thought. I'm hearing a lot of anger from Republicans. A lot of anger from Republicans, not at the functioning of our democracy but at Donald Trump who they are very much, at least privately, to me, pointing to as the reason why this night didn't work out the way they thought it would. And Donald Trump is teasing an announced run for president next week. This will be very interesting to see if we start seeing some of that anger expressed publicly because they really do point to these races and say, we could have won and we should have won, we would have won if he hadn't done what he did.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whatever that announcement was or going to be if that evolves now given what we've seen tonight on election night. What is also uncertain is this runoff, though. It looks more and more likely. So, everybody, mark it on your calendar. I can't wait to spend, listen, I spent my birthday with all of you, so let's meet back here in a month. The best team in television. No, honestly. Honestly. And our thanks to Mark up in the control room and to Lily and to the whole team, just phenomenal. So, thank you all at home. We are grateful that you've tuned in tonight. Hours into this, there are still unanswered questions. Much more first thing in the morning on "Good Morning America." The whole team will be there. And then I'll see you tomorrow night for "World News Tonight." I'm David Muir. From all of us here at ABC News, have a good evening, a good morning. See you soon.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020221118eib900002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIFE ON THE CAMPAIGN TRAIL CATCHING UP WITH THE "POWER TRIP" POLITICAL EMBEDS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,248 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ELECTION, BIDEN, WALKER, TRUMP, WARNOCK, OZ, FETTERMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION DAY 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES MIDTERM SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS HEAD TO THE POLLS AS PARTIES BATTLE FOR CONTROL OF CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT'S AT STAKE IN BATTLE FOR CONTROL OF HOUSE & SENATE\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, WALKER, WARNOCK\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, OZ, FETTERMAN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, DENIERS, LAKE, BOLDUC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "AVENGERS: ENDGAME"\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NECK-AND-NECK IN NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY RACES FOR GOVERNOR & SENATE COME DOWN TO THE WIRE\par \par \uc2 ARIZONA, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE GROUND IN ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSE RACES FOR SENATE & GOVERNOR AS VOTERS HEAD TO THE POLLS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, JACKPOT, LOTTERY, DELAY, DRAWING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERBALL DRAWING DELAYED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD $1.9 BILLION JACKPOT STILL HANGING IN THE BALANCE\par \par \uc2 UPS, SINGING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION DAY: HIGH-STAKES MIDTERMS SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, ELECTIONS, POLLS, BATTLE, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 SHOWDOWN, ELECTION, GOVERNOR, RACE, NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 FLORIDA, SENATE, GOVERNOR, RACES, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, MIDTERMS, ABORTION, VOTING, MARIJUANA\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, ELECTION, RESULTS, WAIT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, AGENDA, CONGRESS, REPUBLICAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUBTROPICAL STORM NICOLE: CURRENT ALERTS\par \par \uc2 WILSON, SURROGATE, BIRTH, DAUGHTER, SMITH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 SLEEP, REFRESHED, CABRERA, PELAYO\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", EVANS, "PEOPLE", KEITH\par \par \uc2 LOHAN, "FALLING FOR CHRISTMAS", "MEAN GIRLS"\par \par \uc2 "WHAT'S FOR DESSERT?", SAFFITZ\par \par \uc2 YANKOVIC, "WEIRD"\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, ELECTION, CAMPAIGN, EMBEDS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are back now with two colleagues who have been embedded in midterm campaigns for months. We've been following their journeys on "Power Trip." Miles Cohen joins us from Florida, Lalee Ibssa joins us from Georgia. Great to see you both this morning. Oh, to be young. You've been going 24/7 for a few months and you both look terrific this morning.\par \par \uc2 LALEE IBSSA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Miles, what stands out most to you from your time on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 MILES COHEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, good morning, George. I've been in 12 states, 39 cities, crisscrossing the country following Governor DeSantis and other candidates and it is just an experience where you meet people that you never thought you'd meet, you talk to voters, you hear about the issues that matter to them, and you get to cover them. And, you know, I wouldn't trade it for the world and you look up and it's election day.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, no, it is finally here. Lalee, you've been in Georgia, so many key campaigns in Georgia. You got a tough Governor's race there. You've, of course, that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 /Raphael Warnock Senate race, as well. And it does seem possible that you could be there longer if it heads to a runoff for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 LALEE IBSSA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, I'm definitely not ready to pack my bags right now. I do anticipate that I'll probably be here for another month, but I'm excited. This is a dream job to be on the campaign trail, so if I have to stay here for another more month I'm okay with that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, you had to cover your first real, I guess, campaign scandal. All those allegations about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and abortion. How did that affect the way you looked at the race and what was your reaction to what you were hearing from the voters there?\par \par \uc2 LALEE IBSSA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you know, the abortion allegations have plagued \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  for much of his campaign. Talking to voters, though, they tell me that they are unfazed by these allegations. One voter told me I don't care if he actually performed the abortion himself, I am still voting for him. I think what that shows me is we might be entering this new world of politics where someone's personal life and what they've done in the past doesn't matter as much as that big "D" or "R" next to their name. We'll find out tonight if that is the case.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That seems pretty clear. Miles, you've really been covering two campaigns in one, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is running for re-election as governor of Florida, but he's also been out all across the country, kind of a shadow campaign potentially for President in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MILES COHEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, George. Look, Governor DeSantis has a re-election campaign right now. He's running for Governor, but he's got a war chest of over $200 million that he's fund raised throughout this campaign. He's traveled to states like New Mexico, to Arizona. I've been there with him and listening to his message and it's a national one. He talks about President Biden. He doesn't mention his opponent \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and so, you know, we'll see what happens after election day, but I imagine you'll see some pieces fall into place after that.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Miles, you've been pretty fearless about questioning Governor DeSantis, his team didn't always like it. Are they letting you back into his events?\par \par \uc2 MILES COHEN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, George, access is something that I've had to navigate. I have, you know, continued to ask my questions. One time comes to mind, I was in Matlacha, Florida and I was asking, you know, Governor DeSantis about evacuation orders. I had talked to one of his supporters and she told me no one had knocked on her door and she wanted answers, so, you know, I asked him that question at a press conference and since I've found ways to get into other campaign events and, you know, just doing what I can to do my job.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you're doing it well. And Lalee, how did this experience help you grow as a journalist?\par \par \uc2 LALEE IBSSA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, this experience has been one of a lifetime. It's a job that I have wanted for so long, so the fact that I am able to live out my dreams is something I definitely do not take for granted at all. I have just learned how to really stay ready and to, you know, take the time to really understand someone's personal life and their upbringing because so much of that shapes their political decisions. And talking to people at the grocery store, at gas stations, anywhere and everywhere I can, I have really just seen, especially in a state like Georgia, politics is so ingrained in everyday life and that's been a really amazing thing to see for myself.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're all benefitting from both of your commitments. Thank you both very much. You can see Miles, Lalee and our other embedded reporters in the final episode of "Power Trip" for this election cycle drops this Sunday on \uc2 Hulu\uc2 . We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And ahead on "Gma3," we have more election day coverage for you and our full team coverage of election night starts at 8:00 PM on ABC streaming live on ABC News starting at 7:00.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Have a great day, everyone.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221206eib80000x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HIGH-PROFILE BALLOT INITIATIVES VOTES TO DECIDE ON ABORTION, VOTING AND MARIJUANA\b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ELECTION, BIDEN, WALKER, TRUMP, WARNOCK, OZ, FETTERMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION DAY 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES MIDTERM SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS HEAD TO THE POLLS AS PARTIES BATTLE FOR CONTROL OF CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT'S AT STAKE IN BATTLE FOR CONTROL OF HOUSE & SENATE\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, WALKER, WARNOCK\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, OZ, FETTERMAN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, INFLATION, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, DENIERS, LAKE, BOLDUC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "AVENGERS: ENDGAME"\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NECK-AND-NECK IN NEVADA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY RACES FOR GOVERNOR & SENATE COME DOWN TO THE WIRE\par \par \uc2 ARIZONA, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE GROUND IN ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLOSE RACES FOR SENATE & GOVERNOR AS VOTERS HEAD TO THE POLLS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, JACKPOT, LOTTERY, DELAY, DRAWING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERBALL DRAWING DELAYED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD $1.9 BILLION JACKPOT STILL HANGING IN THE BALANCE\par \par \uc2 UPS, SINGING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION DAY: HIGH-STAKES MIDTERMS SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, ELECTIONS, POLLS, BATTLE, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 SHOWDOWN, ELECTION, GOVERNOR, RACE, NEW YORK\par \par \uc2 FLORIDA, SENATE, GOVERNOR, RACES, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, MIDTERMS, ABORTION, VOTING, MARIJUANA\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know what that music means, it means it's midterm election day. Millions of Americans heading to the polls and our chief White House correspondent, Cecilia Vega has more on the popular ballot initiatives around the country. Good morning, Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My walk up song, George. Good morning to you. We got more than 100 different ballot measures this time around the country. So much of this election, we've been talking about this for so long, centered around access to abortion in the wake of that Supreme Court ruling and this issue literally is on the ballot in five states, so let's start right there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BALLOT INITIATIVES 2022: ABORTION\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can see this map here behind me. Kentucky voters, they're going to decide whether to amend the state constitution to say there is not a guaranteed right to abortion. Then, you've got Montana considering whether to declare an embryo or a fetus a legal person with a right to medical care if it survives delivery or an abortion, and they could impose fines or even jail time on health care workers who don't provide that care. Then you go to California, Michigan, and Vermont. Voters there are going to decide whether to enshrine abortion rights, protecting them in the state constitution. We've been talking about this for so long now, Democrats have been campaigning heavily on this issue of abortion for months, hoping it rallies voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, the big question today is whether they focus too heavily on it and whether, frankly, this issue that's actually on the ballot in those five states is going to turn voters out, especially in that key battleground in places like Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, that is, that is a big question right now. And how about gun control?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BALLOT INITIATIVES 2022: GUN CONTROL\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Big one on gun control. I'll tell you about this. We've got two different approaches, approaches to that issue. So let me tell you about Iowa. They could make it harder to impose new restrictions on gun laws there. Then in Oregon, you've got the potential opposite direction looking to ban high-capacity magazines and requiring things like background checks and training.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BALLOT INITIATIVES 2022: LEGALIZATION OF MARIJUANA\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And then, we're also talking about, today, legalizing recreational marijuana. That's on the ballot in five states, you can see the map here, potentially joining 19 states and DC where it's already legal. But, this is interesting here, George. We're talking about a number of conservative states this time, like Arkansas and the Dakotas, so see where it all goes tonight.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia Vega, thanks very much. T.J.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221206eib80000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELECTION NIGHT 2022 MIDTERM ELECTION NIGHT COVERAGE\b0\par\par\uc2 45,730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Special Report\par \uc2 ABSR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, RESULTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you with us on this crucial election night in America. We are witnessing what could be one of the most consequential midterm elections in US history. In a country deeply divided, millions of Americans across this country tonight determining who will control Congress, the House, the Senate. Of course, the key issues, the economy, inflation, abortion rights, crime and immigration. President Biden and the Democrats hoping their handling of the economy so far, their efforts to respond to inflation and to gas prices, they hope will convince Americans at the polls to stay the course. But this White House also knows Americans are feeling this, the prices they're paying for everything.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will they turn to the Republicans instead? The party in the White House almost always faces a tough midterm election with very few exceptions. We'll dig into that tonight. And take a look this evening. These are the two numbers you will hear over and over again from the team here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNORS: IN PLAY, 36\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: IN PLAY, 435; REPUBLICANS NEED 5\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: IN PLAY, 35; REPUBLICANS NEED 1\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the Senate, Republicans need a net gain of just one seat to take control of the Senate. In the House, Republicans need a net gain of just five seats to take back control of the House. We are tracking it all, 35 Senate races, 36 gubernatorial races and all 435 House seats up for grabs tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The ABC News powerhouse political team here in the studio and across the entire country tonight. What our correspondents are hearing from the campaigns, what they're seeing on the ground there. I'm joined here at the desk tonight by Jon Karl, Cecilia Vega, Linsey Davis, Martha Raddatz, the entire team. Over at the big boards, Rick Klein, Rachel Scott and, of course, Nate Silver from FiveThirtyEight crunching all of the numbers for us. But I want to get right to Linsey Davis because, just after 7:00 tonight, and we have our first exit polls, which give us a real window into what's driving voters.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's gas and groceries, as we assumed. Many are going to remember when James Carville famously said back in 1992, "It's the economy, stupid." Thirty years later, those words ring true once again.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's going to love you tonight.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, giving him top billing right off the top of the show. So, let's look at the important issues as far as top of mind for those preliminary polls that we have coming in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 32% saying inflation was the most important issue while they were voting, followed closely by abortion, 27%. More than a quarter of those voting saying that was their important issue there, followed then by crime, gun policy, and immigration. But if we look in particular at inflation, because we're at the highest levels that we've seen since the 1980s, that puts Democrats in a bit of a precarious position, because this election cycle at least, if you take a look at who voters are saying they trust to handle inflation, 54% to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: TRUST TO HANDLE INFLATION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you see a 12-point margin right there. And, of course, the big name that looms large that's not on the ballot tonight, Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: PRESIDENT BIDEN JOB PERFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And take a look at his approval rating here, 45%. Now, that's pretty much in line with what we've seen during the midterms historically. In fact, it's the same exact approval rating, 45%, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in his midterm in 2018. And just for that little tidbit for the cocktail party, the lowest ever was 27% with Truman. That was the year after he had dropped the atomic bomb. It might as well be that bad for Biden, because you have a number of those Democratic candidates who didn't want to campaign with him. In fact, in those crucial states of Nevada, Arizona, Georgia, he didn't even show up.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was really that fine line, do I let him come or do I keep him at bay? And we watched that play out over and over again in so many of these key races. We're going to, as soon as you get the exits from Georgia, just tap me on the shoulder. We'll come back to you, Linsey.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Will do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's get right over to Rick Klein, our political director at the big board tonight. I always say, the number of big boards reflect how important the midterm is. We've got a crucial midterm tonight, they're expanding by the moment. Rick, first of all, Georgia tonight. Do we have any numbers yet coming in from Georgia?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 14; REPUBLICAN: 21\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No numbers in yet, David. That is going to be critical. We're going to be watching this all night long. Of course, it was so important, it swung the whole Senate last time around. And keep in mind tonight, not only are we going to be waiting on vote, we're waiting on whether anyone hits 50%. A chance of a runoff four weeks from tonight if no one does that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You and I have talked so much in the weeks leading up to election day about the key states that really could decide it all. There are three main states that the nation now knows these Senate races are basically a dead heat going into tonight.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, a 50/50 Senate, so every state could make or break a majority. But there are three that are almost certainly going to do that. And whoever wins two out of three is going to win the whole Senate. Georgia is one of them, as we discussed. Pennsylvania is such an interesting state. We've seen that Oz-Fetterman match-up got so much attention. That's the Democrats' best chance to pick up a seat that is now held by a Republican who's retiring and leaving the Senate. That Oz-Fetterman match-up, we're watching that all night and maybe beyond. In addition to that, out in Nevada, that's probably Republicans' best chance. They're looking around, they see Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  as probably the most vulnerable Democrat anywhere on the map. Again, we're going to be waiting on a lot of this as we get some data in out of the East Coast. We're going to get some key indications.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm so curious, though, early in the night about a couple of other states that haven't gotten quite as much attention. North Carolina, whether or not this could be a sleeper race. And up in New Hampshire, watching the numbers get so tight in these last few days before the election for Maggie Hassan who's trying to hold on to her seat.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you're always looking for big surprises. And New Hampshire, Democrats have not been keyed in on this. They thought Senator Hassan would be fine, particularly when the Republicans went to a candidate that is more aligned with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the MAGA movement. Don Bolduc, though, has been coming on strong against Senator Hassan. That is an absolute must-hold for Democrats. And let's look at North Carolina and Ohio. In both of those cases, you have Democrats who are running surprisingly strong races in states that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, does Tim Ryan, is he able to hold off J.D. Vance in Ohio in a state that has gone so red? That's tough. And Cheri Beasley, Cheri Beasley down in North Carolina, there hasn't been a lot of national attention on her in her race against Congressman Ted Budd but she has defied a lot of the polling expectations. And if the Democrats win either one of those states, it is a much different night.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, going to track that with you all night long. In the meantime, we'll get over to Rachel Scott. Thanks, Rick. Rachel covers Congress for us. She's here in New York for election night. And, Rachel, this is the map, what, two years ago?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two years ago, this is where things stood. And let's take a look at where things stand at this hour. It is a blank slate tonight. But you will see this map start to fill in as polls start to close.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 12; REPUBLICAN: 31\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're already starting to get a few results in so far, no surprises just yet.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE - OUTGOING MAKE UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 222 SEATS; REPUBLICAN: 213 SEATS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's take a look at where the current House stands at this moment, 222 seats held by Democrats, 213 held by Republicans. Unlike the Senate, every two years, all 435 seats up for grabs. David, you said it at the top, the number five. That is what we are watching tonight because that's what Republicans would need to net by the end of the night in order to take control of the Senate. So, the magic number that I have my eye on, 218, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 218. We'll come to you often. And five seats is not a lot for the other party to try to take back the House. And we've seen what happened in previous midterm elections in modern times. And you've got the numbers right here tonight. Because each one of these past presidents were kind of at least the last three, in the 40% range, low 40s.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right. So, we did some homework for everyone and we went back to see how former presidents fared in the first midterm election year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL APPROVAL RATING: HOUSE SEATS GAINED/LOST\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, as you could see, 2010, former President Barack Obama, 44%. Well, take a look here, he lost 63 seats in the House. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 2018, 40% approval rating, 40 seats in the House. The only exception here, you see in 2002, former President George Bush, this was right after the 9/11 attacks. He actually, his approval rating was very high, at 63%, they gained seats. But former president, President Biden right now, his approval rating tracking where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in 2018.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel Scott covering Congress. And she'll track this all night long. Let's go over to Nate Silver from FiveThirtyEight because, Nate, people have been checking FiveThirtyEight online the last several weeks probably multiple times a day and we've watched the numbers shifted slightly in the House.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICANS ARE FAVORED TO WIN IN THE HOUSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS: 16 IN 100; REPUBLICANS: 84 IN 100\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, in the House, you have a pretty clear favorite, you take all that data, all the polls, all the history, it mostly comes out on the GOP side. I would not weight the 16% down to zero. Aaron Judge, the best baseball player in the world, hits a home run 11% of the time, but you'd much rather be the 84.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And there are scenarios through when this is a pretty big wave, 30 or 40 seats not the most likely scenario, but you do see that at this end of the chart.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And let's take a look at the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICANS ARE SLIGHTLY FAVORED TO WIN THE SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS: 41 IN 100; REPUBLICANS: 59 IN 100\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the Senate, it's more competitive. I would not say it's a tossup or a dead heat. You'd say that GOP is slightly favored as our language, but there has been, as you were saying, David, a big shift. So, I can show you how this forecast has moved over time.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW THE SENATE FORECAST HAS CHANGED\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Over the summer, after Roe v. Wade was overturned, Democrats were polling very well in states like Wisconsin, Arizona, North Carolina, they might actually look like they were going to gain seats maybe even I mean in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, you can really see it there on your graph.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can almost see, it's like the day that Roe v. Wade is overturned, right, you see a trajectory that is very clear right there, but then they come all the way back down to Earth. Looks like a crypto stock or something over the past few days. There is a big downside case here for Democrats, but still 41% is not nothing. It would not be a surprise if they hold on these key races. But the upside scenario of the summer looks a little bit like a false pretense now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which is why you saw on the 11th hour the return to the economy, the economy, the economy for the Democrats. But the question tonight will be, was it too late as we start to watch these come in? As wait for the first numbers from Georgia, I want to bring in Jon Karl, our chief Washington correspondent. Because, Jon, Herschel Walker is, was picked by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, there is no Herschel Walker candidacy without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Herschel Walker wasn't even living in Georgia when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reached out, recruiting. He wasn't thinking about running, it wasn't on anybody's radar screen. He recruited him to run, obviously \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used to own the New Jersey Generals, Herschel Walker played for them.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He loves Herschel Walker, got him to, got him to get into this race. He actually, the Republican establishment, the Republican leaders in Georgia and Washington had been trying to recruit somebody else, David Purdue, who Trump was trying to get to run against the governor, Governor Kemp in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, this is all on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And let me tell you, this was the state that Republicans thought was their best chance to knock off a Democrat. They thought Warnock was the most vulnerable Democratic senator up for re-election. And now, it's a tossup race. If they win, Republicans win. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will take credit. If Herschel Walker loses, you'll see a lot of Republicans pointing the finger right at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pointing right at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Let's bring in Cecilia Vega, our chief White House correspondent. What are we hearing from the White House? I mean, publicly, they are hopeful. But privately, what are they bracing for potentially?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I can tell you, David, leave it to President Joe Biden to say what's on his mind, perhaps not what the Democrats would want to say out loud, he's known for sort of going there. And just late yesterday, he said, look, he is actually concerned about the House, and that is what you have long heard in the last few months leading up to this from Democrats privately. Not very many of them would be willing to go on the record. He, the President says that he is hopeful about the Senate, but there is a very, very serious reality check setting in. The Democrats realize that the tides are against them right now.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They've got the economy, this uphill battle, they have been trying to wage politically on the campaign trail across the country on that one. They've got the history that Rachel was just pointing out there. The history of midterms are not on their side. The President doesn't want this to be a referendum on him but, in fact, that's what we're seeing in the polls.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it involves gas prices and groceries, that's what you're up against. Martha Raddatz, you've been traveling the country. And it's interesting, a lot of talk at the 11th hour from President Biden about democracy being back on the ballot, the first major election obviously since January 6th. And yet when you asked about it, people care that they care most about the economy.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They care most about the economy. They care most about their grocery bills. They care most about gas prices. I was out in Nevada, and you'd even bring up January 6th and they would turn it to the economy. You bring up anything else and they would turn it back to the economy, back to inflation. They are hurting, especially in Nevada. That's a hospitality industry. And so, it's hotel workers, restaurant workers. They are truly hurting. Although it's very interesting, when you look out there, David, because \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  has been behind and slightly behind. Others have really tanked in the last three weeks, because if you look at her numbers three weeks ago, she's down about 0.7, since then, she hasn't fallen much further. Meanwhile, all these other races have tightened. And that's probably Harry Reid, Harry Reid, the Senate majority leader.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Harry Reid machine, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And he died, of course, in 2015, but that machine still works. I saw those culinary union workers out door-to-door, door-to-door, not everybody answered, but that's a machine out there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if she pulls it out in the end, they're going to say this was the ground game.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely, ground game. Exactly, what they'll say.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm getting word tonight, we're getting our first numbers in from Georgia in the Senate race. So, we're going to take a look at that right after the break. I know Governor Christie is here, Donna Brazile, Heidi Heitkamp, they're all waiting to jump in, too. We'll be back. Election night coverage here on ABC continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election Night 2022." You can also watch on ABC News Live alongside commentary and election maps from FiveThirtyEight on Apple News.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. 7:18 here in the east. Our election night coverage continues here on ABC News.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The night is very young, but we're already getting our first numbers in from the state of Georgia in that key Senate race. Let's get right over to the big board and Rick Klein, our political director. And, Rick, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 67.8%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 30.9%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yeah, David, this is an interesting story that I think tells us a lot about how Americans are voting in a year like this. The difference between people voting early and voting on election day. Right now, you see a big lead for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , up 140,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA GOVERNOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHARLIE CRIST: 48%; RON DESANTIS: 52%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, guess what? That's not going to last, not with those kinds of margins.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FULTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 75.3%; WALKER: 23.3%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Most of the votes that we've gotten in so far is right there in Fulton County, Atlanta. This is Warnock's base. As you can see, this is a part of the state that Joe Biden carried with 72% of the vote. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  now getting a little bit more than that. And maybe just as important tonight, our friends at FiveThirtyEight calculated the numbers, how well does \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or Herschel Walker have to do in every, in every one of these counties? \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is running a little bit ahead of that. So, yeah, he should be feeling pretty good about the fact that we've got about 63% of the vote out of Fulton County. But here's one little catch. Most of that so far is early in-person vote, we know that that skews heavily Democratic.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We've only, we've gotten, most of that vote in as well, so it's possible that those margins shrink pretty significantly as things go on. And look, this is a state where it's got a history of early voting. We expect that, overall, we're going to see something like 60% of the votes in this state to be early or absentee. And among those voters so far, it's a 67/31 race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, it's interesting. Go back to Fulton County for a second, Rick. And I want to show people at home that benchmark one more time, because that's going to be, that's a new tool this year that we have for our coverage. And it's super helpful because it gives you an idea of where \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  needs to be performing in Fulton County. And it's early, but he's outperforming where he needs to be.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. This tells you where he needs to hit to break even. Basically, either one of these candidates in any county in the state has to hit that number. And we can do it for any of the battleground states. And this is the heart of what \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  needs to do. This zone here around the 285 perimeter outside Atlanta. And in that outer ring of suburbs, we don't have a lot of data in for those places yet, but that's where it will be interesting to see if those benchmarks get hit. And as you can see, there's more Republican parts to the state where Herschel Walker is starting to hit those same kinds of benchmarks.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He has to be doing about 55%, you know, right here in southern part, it's more African-American part of the state, a more rural part of the state. He's doing a little bit under that right now, but again, most of the vote we're getting in so far has been early in-person vote as opposed to election day.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I can't over state it, it's early but it does show you how that benchmark is going to be super helpful as we go through the night here. Real quick, before we get to Linsey Davis on the exit polls, I want to bring in Jon Karl because you heard Rick talk about the mail-in vote in Georgia right out of the gate here. That's going to be different for a state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is really important as we look at the early returns coming in. Different states count the votes in different ways. So, for instance, as you saw Rick go through, right now, we're seeing mostly that early vote. And in the United States right now, there tends to be a trend where Democrats vote early, vote by mail, Republicans tend to be more likely to vote election day. So, in the state of Pennsylvania, you remember from 2020, it looked like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a huge lead on election night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 67.5%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 31.1%\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But that was because the way Pennsylvania counts its votes, they count the day of vote first. They don't even begin in 2020, they didn't even begin to open up the ballots to start looking at signatures and to verify the ballots until after polls closed.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you had to wait actually, in that case, several days. Michigan's another state where the day of vote tends to be counted early. Republicans are going to look like they're doing better. In some states, it's exactly the reverse. In Arizona, you know, Joe Biden had a pretty big lead early on. It was whittled down as it went on because of the way Arizona counts its votes. So, when you look at this, just remember, you're going to see what we call blue mirages or red mirages, depending how the states process their votes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's why we're here for hours tonight. And by the way, in Pennsylvania, they did change it slightly this year, they're starting those mail-in ballots this morning, they started counting, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. And in Florida, they are counting the votes as they come in. So, Florida has been counting votes for several weeks. Florida's going to have results very quickly. Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, it's going to be a little quicker than last time, but still much slower than a state like Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, but hopefully, we're not here till Saturday. Okay, remember when we're all here Saturday.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, let's bring in Linsey Davis. You've got some exits from Georgia, right?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, Atlanta, let's keep in mind, second-highest inflation in the entire country. Second only to Phoenix. So, it makes sense then that when you look at what was top of mind for Georgia voters, 37% said inflation. That's about 5% higher than what we're seeing nationally. Everything else is relatively nearing what we're seeing from across the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But what's so interesting, David, is what we're also seeing in exit polls, it's the overwhelming majority of people in Georgia made up their minds more than a month ago. More...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's interesting.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Before even the latest allegations came out against Herschel Walker about paying for abortions. So, look at this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA: HOW IMPORTANT IS IT FOR YOUR PARTY TO CONTROL THE SENATE?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I find this just fascinating, how important is it for your party to control the Senate? 94% said this was about control of the Senate, not so important about the candidate. And this really speaks to you what we saw last month, the headline of the "USA Today" that said, I don't care if Herschel Walker paid for an abortion or if he blew up the planet Alderaan.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's really interesting. Party is driving their decision there in Georgia. But we're going to be looking in other states about whether or not a split-ticket choices are going to play out tonight, as well. So, we'll dive into that again. And then I want to get to Steve Osunsami, who covers Georgia, he lives there in Georgia. He's at \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  headquarters. And, Steve, listen, it's early, but they have to like that benchmark reading we just show, where \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is at least in Fulton County is outperforming where he needs to be at this point in the night.\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, the Fulton County, all of the surrounding counties in the metro area, are important to \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  if he's going to succeed tonight, because that's where all the blue votes are. About six million voters live in the Atlanta metro area and these are the bluest counties of the state. And I will say something, David, you and I have talked about that, you know, for all the polls showing that this race is tied, I will underline that the change in demographics in Georgia actually favor the Democrats, because when Stacey Abrams, for example, when she ran four years ago, she lost by about 50,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 64.2%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 34.4%\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Since then, about 50,000 people have moved to the Atlanta metro area, the blue counties each year. And that helps Democrats. There are more potential voters, they argue, in this state. And so, these numbers so far look encouraging for the Democrats. But, of course, as Rick has pointed out, it is early in the night, we've seen this happen many times before, where leads, large leads evaporate. It will be interesting, I'd keep a close eye on what the total vote that happened today, the election the votes that were cast today, what that number is. And then, of course, we're all looking at the third party candidate, especially in the Senate race, to see what that percentage is, if that pushes this into a runoff, if it's close between the other two candidates. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But Steve, this has been an interesting example of one of these cases where the Democratic incumbent has distanced himself from President Biden. He was asked whether Biden would come campaign for him. He said, it's not about who I'm campaigning with, it's about who I'm campaigning for.\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He has, because, you know, he's looking at the issues that people care about here. You know, as Linsey just shared, inflation is top of mind. I talked with a number of voters this morning, both red and blue voters, who all talked about inflation, the price of gas, economic concerns. And what at least I'm hearing on the ground is that, while the left had hoped that issued like abortion would drive, for example, white suburban women to the polls, the main issue still is economic. That's what's really driving voters, that's what's on the mind of voters here in this election. And I heard a number of voters who told me that, you know, they're actually going to vote a split ticket. This was on the left and the right, who care about the economy, of course, but also about these more traditional blue issues, as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Steve O. in Georgia, Steve, we'll be coming back at you, many times I'm sure throughout the night again. Ohio, the first numbers are coming in. We are set to look at Ohio and North Carolina where the polls close in about four minutes. We'll look at those numbers the moment they come in and we'll go back to Georgia, as well. ABC News's live coverage of the 2022 midterms continues right after this here on ABC.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage of the midterm election 2022. It is now 7:30 here in the east and we are tracking several key Senate races in the country. Obviously, Georgia closed a half an hour ago, the Senate race there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 60.1%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 38.5%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  facing a challenge from Herschel Walker. We continue to get numbers in, 12% of the vote in and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with a sizable lead. But again, huge part of the rural areas of Georgia still yet to be counted. Two more states now have closed, North Carolina, the Senate race there, a bit of a sleeper race, not getting a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY; TED BUDD\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cheri Beasley up against Congressman Ted Budd, Budd supported by former President Trump. He was polling well with a slight lead over Cheri Beasley, but she was a very strong candidate. We'll wait for numbers in that race, as well. Ohio, the polls have now closed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIM RYAN; J.D. VANCE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Congressman Tim Ryan, the Democrat in this race, up against Republican J.D. Vance. J.D. Vance holding a polling lead of about a couple of points, though. That's still very close for Ohio, since we know where Ohio has gone for Republicans the last couple of cycles. Tim Ryan hoping, though, to trade Congress for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIKE DEWINE: PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) There is one race we can project at this hour, and it's also from Ohio. The race for governor, the very popular Republican governor there, Mike DeWine, will serve a second term in Ohio, not entirely a surprise there. And from Governor DeWine, I want to bring it over to Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , former governor of New Jersey. Chris, how you feeling about tonight? Governor DeWine not a surprise there. He took some heat during COVID, but then regained in popularity almost soon thereafter.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I mean, he took very little heat during COVID, David. He, I think, most people cross the state found his response to be reasonable. And they like Mike DeWine. And that's really what it comes down to. They like him. And then when you look at Georgia, one of the things that we didn't talk about before, Georgia, in the election integrity law, changed the way they count also. So, they've been counting mail-in votes and early votes from the time they've come in.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, a lot of what you'll see here in the beginning is those results, is mail-in and early vote. Interesting part of that is, you know, if you look at it, Warnock, you would expect to be doing as well as he's doing right now, and that early mail-in. When we get to start counting election day votes, you're going to see a much different result.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris, let me ask about Governor DeWine, and whether or not you think his popularity in the state is going to help bring J.D. Vance across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question. Look, a statistic I used to use when I was chairman of the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2 , when talking to donors. In states where the Senate race and the governor's race is both on the ticket, when the governor wins, the Senate candidate of the same party wins 85% of the time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, it is very powerful to have a popular governor at the top of the ticket for the Senate race. And that, I will tell you, is the biggest hope for Herschel Walker in Georgia. Brian Kemp is going to win that race, I think he's going to win it by a fairly substantial margin, given that he won by a point over Stacey Abrams four years ago. We're probably looking more at five or six points. And that, it could be Brian Kemp could be the first person in American history to drag Herschel Walker over the goal line.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, that's true, maybe he'll get the Heisman at the end of the night. Let's bring it back over to Rick Klein at the big board because you were comparing how \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is performing up against Stacey Abrams in that governor's race.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this is fascinating because we're seeing it play out in real time right now. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is up about 22 points over Herschel Walker. That's not going to last. As we talked about, this is mostly the early vote. You can see him hitting the benchmarks that he needs to hit in Fulton County, where most of the vote in. So, keep in mind, 22 points, 112,000 votes that he's up right now. Let's compare that to what's going on in the governor's race at the same time in the night. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABRAMS: 56.2%; KEMP: 43.3%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Stacey Abrams is only at 56%-43%. So, instead of 22 points, you're talking about 13 points. So, that's 40,000, 50,000 votes that are either skipped on the Senate line or just going for one versus the other.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , 59.6%; HERSCHEL WALKER, 39.0%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: STACEY ABRAMS, 56.2%; BRIAN KEMP, 43.3%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Governor Kemp is getting a lot of people voting for him that are probably then going and voting for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or just skipping the Senate race or maybe going to the third-party candidate. These are the same voters, right? These are the same votes that are being cast and being counted. And right now, we're seeing that Stacey Abrams is significantly behind where \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is. And conversely, Brian Kemp significantly ahead of where Herschel Walker is.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to take that point over to Donna Brazile because it was just two years ago, what's changed for Stacey Abrams? Has she lost a little bit of the magic or are the times different? Is this a different moment on the country?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, as you well know, all political wins are turbulent. And for Stacey Abrams, 2022 is a turbulent year. 2018, she's running as a challenger, it was an open seat. So, she put in place what we saw in 2020 that gave Democrats an opportunity to turn that state blue. And so, while she might be falling behind in the early vote, the Stacey Abrams operation, her playbook that has even gotten Georgia on the minds of Democrats. Chris and I were talking about the last time I went down to Georgia, other than to watch the Saints beat, you know, the Falcons. And as, when Bill Clinton was there in '92, Joe Biden. So, Georgia is now in play for the rest of our lifetime, because of Stacey Abrams.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  wins tonight, he wins in part because of the machine that she helped set up.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I would hope he would congratulate and help pull her over like you said. Hopefully, he's the quarterback on this one.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And one of the things, David, to look at Kemp. Now, the Kemp difference here is I think the best thing that happened to Brian Kemp, besides his performances as governor, is when he was opposed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think what you're going to see as we get into the suburbs outside Atlanta, four years ago, Brian Kemp didn't do great in those suburbs. I'm predicting it tonight he's going to do very, very well because those suburban women who do not like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have now sided with Brian Kemp because he fought Trump and won in the primary. And so, they've now become allies of Kemp, where four years ago, they were not.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we know all eyes on Georgia in the last presidential race and Governor Christie brings up a great point, Jon. You and I have actually talked about this off-camera. If Kemp wins tonight, this would signal that you can stand up to Trump, you can stand up for, you know, a carefully counted election and stand by the results and still win.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, Brian Kemp refused to do what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted, which was to reconvene a special session of the legislature to overturn the election results in 2020. He said no. Trump went after him, went after him hard, recruited David Purdue to run against him in the Republican primary. Kemp crushed him, Purdue got like 20 some points, absolutely crushed him. This shows you can be a Republican, a conservative Republican, defy \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come after you, come after you hard, and still survive.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're going to track the secretary of state's race in Georgia, as well.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Brad Raffensperger. I mean, another one, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  recruited a Republican member of the House, Jody Heist, to leave his seat in the House to run for secretary of state in Georgia against Brad Raffensperger. And Brad Raffensperger won that Republican primary and he's very much the front-runner to win, to win the general election tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Nightline" anchor Byron Pitts, I know it's early, but what do you make of these numbers for Brian Kemp tonight?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, I love sports analogies. So, picking up on them, to this point, Kemp has given Herschel Walker the Heisman. He's kept his distance. In fact, a couple days ago, they had an event where Walker, every Republican attended except for Herschel Walker. That's one thing. The other thing I want to mention about, something unusual when I think about Georgia that works to the advantage of I think Warnock to some degree about the ground game. He's pastor of Ebenezer Baptist Church. In black America, that is the big house, that reaches all across the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, Georgia has the third-largest number of HBCUs in the country. One of them is in Savannah, where Warnock is from. And I think that will impact the race as well. Kemp, as I mentioned, is very popular. David, I want to give you one number, 39.1. That's how much unemployment has dropped in Georgia in the past seven years. So, the economy is important there, but relative to many parts of the country, folks are feeling pretty good about the economy. So, I think some of the other issues will what matter there (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to hit on something you said there, though, because if you watch their campaign rallies or their campaign events, I should call them, you know where I'm going with this.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even in just the last two to three days before election day, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  was in a church setting or a local community center setting. He had Herschel Walker at the football games. It speaks volumes about the difference that they're trying to show in these final days.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yeah. I call it the church ladies versus the tailgaters, right? You go to a Herschel Walker, you go to a Warnock event, it's about 30 people, mostly black, mostly women. You go to a Herschel Walker event, it's hundreds of people, mostly white, mostly women. At a Warnock event, he talks about policy, he talks about character, he talks about credentials. At a Walker event, they talk about when he beat Tennessee when he was a running back. People there line up to get his autograph. And something that you never see in politics, Herschel Walker will wait to take a picture with every single person there. It goes on for hours. And so, it speaks to the different dynamics that go on in Georgia in this race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, I want to bring in Heidi Heitkamp, former senator from North Dakota. Hey, Heidi.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One thing that isn't getting talked about here is how Warnock became a United States senator. When he came in, he used the machine that Johnny Isaacson, whose seat he took, he actually hired their people to do the kind of constituent services. He has been myopically focused on Georgia. And when you think about Stacey, and I love her to death, but she became the face of the Democratic Party. So, she was much more aligned with traditional progressive Democratic ideals, where Warnock put his head down, did his work, brought in Johnny's folks to help do constituent services and I think you see this in the numbers.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You also see Stacey Abrams, you see a lot of celebrities coming into Georgia, you don't see that in \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 's church settings.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, on purpose. And Warnock wanted that image, that I am here doing the work for the people of Georgia. I am not a celebrity, I am your United States senator. And I'm not saying that was Stacey's, you know, image, but I think that after her, you know, remarkable organizational skills and what she was able to do in Georgia, she became the face of progressive African-American women. And I think there's some resistance to that celebrity in Georgia, where Warnock just did what he needed to do to, yeah, make those connections with the people.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I would be remiss, since you mentioned progressive black women, I would be remiss if I didn't say progressive black women did not go to church and listen to the sermons and the games and watch them. The Democratic Party would not be in the White House today.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, black women have done our job.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm not saying that despairingly, but I'm saying that that's what you see, I think, in these differences. The Democratic brand in states like ours are really, really...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You have a popular Republican governor. You've got to give him some dabs for that, too.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I think he's getting credited, at least so far tonight, than well ahead of Stacey Abrams. Mary Bruce, you were on the ground in Georgia when we were covering all of these allegations. And it was interesting what Linsey Davis pointed out here at the desk moments ago that people made up their mind weeks ago. You took note of the reaction from people when another set of allegations is being reported, it didn't sit well with a lot of folks in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was there actually the day after the second woman came forward and revealed that, according to her, that Herschel Walker pressured her to have an abortion, paid for an abortion. On the ground at his rally, there were more people than folks on the ground they had ever seen in his rallies. To them on the ground, didn't matter. And it was really surprising. And to Linsey's point about going to some of the data already, the loyalty to party ahead of the candidate was really on display when I was there.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I was amazed, a lot of his supporters who I talked to said, look, he is someone who deserves grace, who deserves forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'll never forget a woman named Kathy who was a diehard Herschel Walker supporter, she said, look, that was his sinning phase. Even if the allegations were true, over and over again, Herschel Walker supporters who I talked said it simply doesn't matter. It's about the man he is today. And to Byron's point, the rallies were huge, he was energizing the crowd, they were drawn to that star power and what he was promising, even if it wasn't a lot of details, they liked the overall message. And they were going to stick with him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah. You had to wonder, and I know that the women who did come forward said they were apolitical, it was not why, it was because of his anti-abortion rights stance. But you did wonder at the time, if this would backfire in any way and help rally the cause behind Herschel Walker, those who supported him already. Linsey, you were digging at the numbers, you found something more here?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, what's so interesting here, one in three voters in Georgia are black, right? And so, when we're talking about the black vote here in particular, 89% going overwhelmingly for Rachel (sic) Warnock. But he is underperforming 4% less of the black vote than where he was during the special election last year in 2021.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA: RACE AND GENDER; BLACK MEN: -11%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 84%, \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 ; 13% HERSCHEL WALKER\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in particular, he's underperforming with black men by 6%. So, those voters are actually going to Herschel Walker, only 3% less with black women. And again, as Donna Brazile has pointed out, black women have time and time again been called the backbone of the Democratic Party. And we're seeing this bear out, that black women are still more likely to go for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And again, these are the exit polls, when they're saying coming out. But Rick, what do you have by way of numbers, 29% of the vote now in in Georgia? What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we're seeing as the votes flows in. We're actually getting the first in-person vote in at least a couple of places.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 60.7%; WALKER: 37.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, we're seeing \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  continue to hit the numbers he needs to do, particularly in Fulton County, as we discussed. This is a huge one. We heard Governor Christie talking a little bit before about the Georgia suburbs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GWINNETT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 65.7%; WALKER: 32.4%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Gwinnett County a great example of that. And when we look at the benchmark numbers, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  ahead of where he needs to be to run an even race. He feels good about this. Again, most of the vote we have so far is early vote or early in person. We're still seeing a lot of split-ticket voters. I just checked the numbers a moment ago and we're seeing, again, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with a much larger lead than his ticket mate has at the moment. We're going to see this shrink at the night goes on, there's no question of my mind, with only less than a third of the expected vote in. This is not the kind of margin we're going to see. But so far, at least \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is hitting the kind of numbers he needs to.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jon?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There's an important caveat to that. An important caveat and that is, first of all, Fulton County is counting much faster than the rest of the state. So, you know, 63% of the vote is in Fulton County, that is a Democratic county. 63% of the vote in, compared to just 29% statewide. And the other thing is, of that vote, it's almost entirely early vote that has been counted so far in Fulton County. That is going to be the Democratic votes in the Democratic county. That's why you're still seeing on our board a big lead for Warnock over Walker. That lead is going to shrink considerably, may or may not disappear. I mean, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, you're looking at heavily Democratic votes being counted so far.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 56.4%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 42.1%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And a very important to point out. I see first numbers coming in from North Carolina. Rachel, we'll come to you right after the break. It's now 15 minutes before 8:00. We know, at the top of the hour, Pennsylvania, New Hampshire, Florida, all closing. All key states. So, don't go anywhere. We'll be right back with our live coverage right here on ABC News. The midterms 2022.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage of midterm election night here in America. A crucial night ahead as, you don't need me to tell you now, 48 minutes past 7:00. A couple of key states have closed.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: KENTUCKY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAND PAUL, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We can project in Kentucky, in that Senate race there, I mentioned it some time ago, we can now say that Senator Rand Paul will serve a third term in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY: 57.5%; TED BUDD: 40.8%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're also tracking North Carolina at this hour, not projecting anything there, the numbers are still very early, 36% of the vote in, and you can see the Democrat there, Cheri Beasley with a sizable lead. But again, it's very early. A lot of the rural areas in North Carolina still have to be tabulated. Ted Budd, of course, the Republican member of Congress, backed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring it over to Rachel Scott at the big board, because you looked at some of this the metropolitan areas and Cheri Beasley is performing quite well.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She is. And we're really paying attention to what we're calling sort of the research triangle here. A bunch of college and universities around this area near Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WAKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEASLEY: 68.2%; BUDD: 30.0%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's look at Wake County right now. With 54% of the vote expected in, Cheri Beasley is outperforming where President Biden did in this county two years ago. But again, David, you said, it is very early here. No in-person election day vote in just yet. This is early, by mail, early in person votes, so we will see these numbers start to shift.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Curious, does the benchmark tool work on that one as well?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It sure does, David. So, let's take a look, 62% would be the benchmark. Over performing there at 68%.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) At least for now. Let's get to Stephanie Ramos, she's live in North Carolina for us part of our election night team. And, Stephanie, this race has been a little under the radar. And I know Democrats will be asking if she comes very close, but doesn't pull this out, should they have spent more time on Cheri Beasley?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, David. And they just cranked up the music here. Everyone here has a little extra pep in their step with those early votes, early numbers coming in. But as you said, this Senate race really hasn't gotten that much attention, not much national attention, but it does have the possibility to make history tonight. If Beasley wins tonight and is elected to the Senate, she would be the third black woman elected to the Senate following Kamala Harris and Carole Mosley Braun. It is going to be tough. President Biden's approval rating here is very low and Trump looms large over the Republican opponent in this race. Representative Ted Budd, a three-term congressman.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RAMOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it is going to be tricky, but what we heard last night at Beasley's campaign rally, the last-ditch effort to get voters out there to vote for her, is that in order to win this, she needs a significant amount of votes in cities where a majority are black voters. Cities like Charlotte and Raleigh where we are tonight, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Stephanie Ramos there in North Carolina. We'll come back to you often, Stephanie. And give us a shout out if you learn anything more from the campaign. Donna Brazile, you know, North Carolina has broken the hearts of Democrats so many times before.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, I'm a southerner. It has broken a lot of hearts. But here's one thing, Cheri Beasley knows politics. She's a former Supreme Court justice, they call her Justice Beasley. She lost her last statewide race by 400 votes. This is an uphill climb because, you know, when your party is not invested in presidential years and you come in midterm years, you have to do a lot of ground work.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY: 56.6%; TED BUDD: 41.7%\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's gotten support from Chuck Schumer, the traditional Democratic allies. Could she have used a little bit more money? Yes. But you know what, she's running a fabulous campaign.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she was very careful on that debate stage to distance herself between President Biden and what she would like to do if she makes it to Congress, to the Senate. She actually said neither party is doing it right right now.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's correct.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Which has been an answer we've heard over and over again this campaign cycle. Bring it over to Governor Christie because you are hearing from folks on the ground in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Georgia, on real votes. So, both mail-in vote and early vote, one of the early trends is that Walker is consistently trailing Kemp in that early vote by 3% to 3.5%. So, what talked about before, how well does Kemp do, what's his margin? If Walker is going 3% to 3.5% lower consistently in the early vote, what's that tell you about whether Walker will have enough to get over the goal line or not? Kemp is going to need to get in 54%, 55% of the vote if Walker consistently is three, three-and-a-half points behind Warnock for Warnock to be able, for Walker to be able to win. So, you're seeing a choice even amongst the Republicans where Walker's not doing as well as Kemp's doing across and that's the entire state. They're saying that's consistent in every county they've looked at so far.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to be asking this question about other states, too. Ohio, Governor DeWine, extremely popular there, we already projected he win because that was an easy one. The question is, does he help J.D. Vance finish as well?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And is it enough? I mean, even with a win, do they win by enough to be able to make up the drag with what Mitch McConnell called some, you know, some less-than-great candidates.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring in our House district in to this conversation, Rachel Scott. You're looking at Virginia. And you and I on "World News Tonight" have been talking the last couple of nights about what to watch early on election night for people who are watching us at home, and we're grateful for everybody's watching. We're going to be here for hours tonight. This is a bellwether race in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 20; REPUBLICAN: 50\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRGINIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LURIA: 41.2%; KIGGANS: 58.7%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It sure is, David. And I really have my eye on two different districts here. Starting in Virginia's second district, this is Elaine Luria's district. She's a member of the January 6th select committee here. She's running in a very tough race against Jen Kiggans in here, 27% of the vote expected in. We are seeing that Kiggans is doing well, but this is all election day vote. This is a district where President Biden won by just 3% of the vote. Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin won here by 10% of the vote. So, this is a Democrats that are facing a lot of head winds. She flipped this seat in 2018. She knows that her role on the January 6th committee may have turned some conservative voters off in this area. But, David, she says regardless of the result tonight, it is worth it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott over at the board, she's tracking the House races. And Elaine Luria, who had that high-profile role on the January 6th select committee in Washington, she famously said, Rachel, that if I lose this race because of my role on the January 6th committee, it still will have been worth it. Rachel, we'll come back to that race often as soon as you have more. Our live coverage here in New York City of election night 2022 continues in a moment. The whole team is here. 8:00 is a key hour, several key Senate races we're tracking, the first results expected to come in. We'll see you in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening on this critical election night in America. It is now 8:00 PM in the east, 5:00 PM in the west. At this hour, polls are now closing in 16 states, including the key battlegrounds of Pennsylvania, Florida, and New Hampshire. The polls have already closed in Ohio and in Georgia tonight. All eyes on the high-stakes Senate races there. And let's take a look tonight at the numbers coming in already.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RON DESANTIS, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You can see, in Florida, at this hour, ABC News can now project that Ron DeSantis has been re-elected as governor. Talk will turn to whether or not he runs for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARCO RUBIO, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now to the Senate in Florida. Senator Marco Rubio is taking a third term, he will return to the Senate. It was a race against Congresswoman Val Demings, one-time police chief in Orlando, considered a very solid candidate. A lot of talk about her as a potential running mate a couple of years ago for Joe Biden. But Marco Rubio easily wins re-election to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 55.0%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 43.5%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Georgia at this hour, a race we continue to watch. The results, 31% of the vote in, still very early, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with an early lead, the incumbent, over Herschel Walker, but this is expected to tighten as the night goes on.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN FETTERMAN; MEHMET OZ\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Pennsylvania tonight, the polls have just closed, we're tracking this. The numbers expected at any moment. Our first batch of data, John Fetterman up against Mehmet Oz, obviously in Pennsylvania, the whole country aware of this race. We'll come back to that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAGGIE HASSAN: 64.8%; DONALD BOLDUC: 34.6%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up to the north in New Hampshire, Senator Maggie Hassan seeking a second term, she won the last time by just about 1,000 votes. 7% of the vote in, very early tonight, a sizable lead over Don Bolduc, an election denier until he said Biden did win, he changed. A little bit of whiplash there in that state. But the polls in New Hampshire have grown very tight going into election day. This is going to be a race we watch very closely tonight. We're also watching North Carolina at this hour, the Senate race there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY: 54.1%; TED BUDD: 44.2%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cheri Beasley up against Congressman Ted Budd. Cheri Beasley in the lead, but again early, 45% of the vote in in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIM RYAN: 58.5%; J.D. VANCE: 41.4%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To Ohio we go. The Democrat there, Congressman Tim Ryan, ten-term congressman, trying to get elected to Senate in Ohio, which has gone Republican the last couple of presidential cycles, up against J.D. Vance, supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  there. Tim Ryan with an early lead. But, again, it's very early. Governor Mike DeWine winning his race for governor in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those are some of the races we're following at this hour. Our entire team standing by live in the studio and across the country. ABC News election night in America starts right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 This is an ABC News special. "Election Night 2022." Tonight, the stakes could not be higher. Who will control Congress and statehouses across the country? The key races that could determine everything, from your wallet to safety in our streets, even our climate. A woman's right to choose and President Biden's agenda. It's all on the line tonight. So many Americans casting their vote early. A record 45 million engaging in democracy like never before. Results already coming in, our correspondents are live all across the country, ready to break it all down. "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election Night 2022." Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you with us here on this election night in America. We are witnessing what could be one of the most defining midterm elections in US history. Millions of Americans across the country tonight determining who will control the Senate, who will control the House. The key issues, the economy, inflation, abortion rights, crime, and immigration. And in recent days, President Biden arguing protecting our democracy is also on the ballot. Of course, the President and the Democrats hoping their handling of the economy so far, their efforts to respond to inflation will be enough to convince Americans to stay the course.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But this White House knows Americans are feeling this, the prices they're paying for everything from gas to groceries. Will the voters turn to the Republicans on the economy instead? The party in the White House, as you know at home, almost always faces a tough midterm election with very few exceptions. We'll dive into that tonight. And take a look at this evening. These are the two numbers you will hear over and over tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNORS: IN PLAY, 36\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: IN PLAY, 435; REPUBLICANS NEED 5\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: IN PLAY, 35; REPUBLICANS NEED 1\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the Senate, Republicans need a net gain of just one seat to take control of the Senate. In the House, Republicans need a net gain of just five to take back control of the House. Again, Republicans need one seat in the Senate, five seats in the House to take back control. We'll be tracking it all, 35 Senate races, 36 gubernatorial races, and all 435 House seats up for grabs tonight. ABC News powerhouse political team here in the studio and our correspondents across the country tonight. What they are hearing from the campaigns, what they're seeing on the ground. You can see they are ready to go live. I'm joined here at the desk by Jon Karl tonight, Cecilia Vega, my friends Linsey Davis, Martha Raddatz.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Everybody is here, including the three big boards tonight, Rick Klein, Rachel Scott, Nate Silver from FiveThirtyEight. We have a lot to get to. But I want to get right to Linsey Davis because the exit polls are already telling a portrait of America tonight. The issues driving the voters to polls.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they are what we thought they were going to be, gas and groceries. I had an opportunity to crisscross the country, South Carolina to South Dakota and asked the candidates, what's most important to the voters in your state? They all said at that time, it's the economy. That was anecdotal, now we have the numbers bearing it out. Take a look at what the voters are saying.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) 32%, most important issue when voting, inflation, followed not far from behind by abortion. So, we're talking about more than a quarter people saying that was top of mind for them, and then crime, gun policy, and immigration. But I do want to focus for a moment on the inflation because, again, we haven't seen numbers like this since the '80s.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that doesn't bode so well for the Democrats because, by and large, this election cycle, you have people who are saying, by 54%, we trust the Republicans to handle inflation compared to 42%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: TRUST TO HANDLE INFLATION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we're talking about a 12-point margin there. And, of course, the name that looms large that's not on the ballot, Joe Biden. Take a look at his approval ratings here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONWIDE: PRESIDENT BIDEN JOB PERFORMANCE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 45%. Now, by and large, that's pretty much in line with what we've seen for presidents in the past midterms. It's exactly 45% is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had in 2018. And I know you're asking, who had the lowest ever, David, right, you want to know?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Please.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 27%, that was Harry Truman back in 1946, just one year after he dropped the atomic bomb. But it might as well be just that bad for Biden because he had a number of Democratic candidates who didn't want to campaign with him, especially in those key states Nevada, Arizona, and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's always that delicate dance whether you let him campaign or keep an arm's length. And we've seen that in so many of the candidate s particularly in these very close Senate races, where they were basically tied going into election night. I want to get right over to the big board with Rick Klein because I know, we're tracking several of the states that viewers are going to watch very closely here tonight. Rick, you and I have been talking about the last couple of nights about three states in particular, Georgia, Pennsylvania, and Nevada. Why are these three states just so important tonight?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, David, tell me who's going to win two out of those three and I'll tell you who is going to control the Senate. And here's why.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 54.4%; WALKER: 44.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Georgia was so pivotal in the last election. It could easily be the majority maker all over again. The race there isn't just about who gets more votes, it's also about who gets to 50% to avoid a runoff. Pennsylvania is the best opportunity that Democrats have for a pickup anywhere on the map. They have a retiring Republican senator in a state that Joe Biden won two years ago. John Fetterman and Dr. Oz getting all that attention. And Nevada out west, we're going to be waiting in a while on this one, but that has been seen as the best opportunity for Republicans to knock off an incumbent Democrat.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The first Latina senator, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in a very tight race there.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) See if the ground game works in the very end. For those watching, and it's early tonight, a couple of sleeper races that didn't get as much attention, North Carolina, New Hampshire, you say could also be very telling.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And we're starting to see the first numbers in out of New Hampshire, as we cover Maggie Hassan with a little bit of a lead opened up with not a lot of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HASSAN: 62.7%; BOLDUC: 36.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is so key for the Democrats to hold onto. If they lose that, it is very bad news for their prospects.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEASLEY: 53.8%; BUDD: 44.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're keeping an eye on what Cheri Beasley is doing in North Carolina. Again, this is mostly early vote and mostly vote from Democratic parts of the state. I would expect the margins to narrow, if Ted Budd doesn't take it over entirely.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 57.4%; VANCE: 42.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're looking at Ohio tonight, where Tim Ryan has a lead in the early vote that's come in.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN: 84.0%; OZ: 14.6%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're just getting David, our first taste of numbers in Pennsylvania. I just checked, it's the Pittsburgh area, which happens to be John Fetterman's hometown. And it's all early vote, so don't expect this to last, but we're just starting to see that roll in. This is going to be one we're watching for a long time.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's going to be a very long night ahead. We're grateful to have you, Rick, and everybody else here, as well. Let's dive into Georgia because we have some numbers that close some time ago, again, this is the race between the incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Herschel Walker. Very tight. And so far, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  performing slightly above the benchmark where he needs to be?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. We're keeping an eye on some of the most populous counties, because that's, frankly, where the Democrats live and where most of the voters are in the state. And you take a look on Fulton County, the Atlanta area, most of the vote we've had in so far is the early in-person that's going to skew heavily Democratic. That said, take a look at how Joe Biden did there, out 72% of the vote. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is ahead of that. And for our purposes, maybe just as important, what does he need to do to break even? What does he need to do in this part of the state? He needs to get about 72%. Right now, sitting at 75%. Again, a lot of votes left.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is really fascinating because this is a new tool for viewers at home to sort of follow along the night with us. This is the benchmark, where he would need to be performing in order to have a decent night at the night of the night. He's actually outperforming in Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly, both candidates, we see the number there, we can do it in just every battleground states tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm curious, big governor's race in Georgia as well. You've got the rematch between Brian Kemp and Stacey Abrams. Brian Kemp outperforming Herschel Walker at this point?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABRAMS: 51.1%; KEMP: 48.4%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and I just looked at the numbers. And it looks like so far, tonight, of course, both Democrats are leading in those early returns, there are about 60,000 people who voted for Brian Kemp and didn't vote for Herschel Walker. Either they voted for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or they decided to vote for the third-party candidate or they didn't vote for anybody at all. That's a big problem for Republicans. Brian Kemp, we see it in the pre-election polls, is much better positioned to be re-elected than Herschel Walker is to win a term.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We said it earlier, Georgia on my mind, and we're going to be talking about Georgia all night long. Rick, thanks. I want to go over to Rachel Scott, who covers Congress for us. And, Rachel, we already have returns coming in tonight in several of these districts.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do. And this is where things stand at this hour. You will see this map start to fill in as we start to get some projections.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 77; REPUBLICAN 111\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE - TO SWITCH CONTROL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPS NEED 5 SEATS\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, you mentioned it at the very top. Five, remember, that is the number that we are tracking tonight. This is what Republicans need to net by the end of the night in order to take the House. I want to take you over to the race grid, because we've done all the homework for you. All 435 House seats are up for grabs, every two years. We have crunched it down to what will decide control of the House. And you're seeing it listed on your screen here. The left are seats held by Democrats. The right, seats held by Republicans. And take a look, David, we're already starting to get some results in tonight. Republicans flipping three seats held by Democrats right there in Florida.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in Georgia, looks like they picked up a race here, the Democrat has won in that race?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA: DISTRICT 07\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MCBATH: 63.8%; GONSALVES: 36.2%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, this is Lucy McBath, gun control activist here. She was able to hang onto this seat. This will be a good sign for Democrats, they wanted to hang onto this seat. She's actually running in a different district, had to introduce herself to new voters because many of the congressional maps were redrawn in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But the most important thing here is there have been three flips by the Republicans already tonight. When you mention that number, that five seats to pick up the House, historically speaking, that is a very low hurdle for the Republicans to have to meet tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It really is. And when we look back in history, we kind of did some homework here and seen how former president fared when it came to their approval ratings and how they did in the first midterm election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENTIAL APPROVAL RATINGS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE SEATS GAINED/LOST\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you can see here, 2010, former President Barack Obama, 44% approval rating, lost 63 seats in the House.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Called it a shellacking, right?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He called it shellacking. He said the losses were humbling, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 2018, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 40% approval ratings, lost 40 seats in the House. So, tonight, voters will decide whether Democrats will gain seats or lose seats in the House.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what's so interesting here is they're all sort of in the low 40 range. So, we have seen this before, historically speaking, we just have to see how it bears out with the losses. And if you are looking at George W. Bush here, we should point out, we don't want to take this away from him, but this was shortly after 9/11. The country was at war. He didn't lose seats in the first midterm, it was the second one where he said it was a thumping, right? I want to go to Virginia, though, because you were talking about this race earlier and I think we should bring our viewers up to speed. These are sort of the bellwether races very early on the night. This is Virginia 7 and this involved a representative of Congress, take us through it. Spanberger, Abigail Spanberger, how she's doing.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 77; REPUBLICAN 111\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIRGINIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SPANBERGER: 45.2%; VEGA: 54.6%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Abigail Spanberger, who barely won her race the last time around in 2020, she actually flipped this seat in 2018. She's a moderate. She's one of actually five Democrats who did not vote for House Speaker Nancy Pelosi, now running in a very tough race. We've got 77% of the vote expected in. Let's take a look here. Most of this is election day vote, which is likely why you're seeing the Republican in the lead here, because most Republicans do vote on election day. This is going to be a tough race. But, David, why we watching this race, because Republicans don't necessarily need to win here. But if they are able to flip this seat, they are on track for a very good night.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Elaine Luria, who's on the January 6th committee, another Democrat in Virginia, who said, if her role in the January 6th committee cause her her seat, so be it. She said it will all have been worth it. But look, her opponent is in the lead.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LURIA: 41.3%; KIGGANS: 58.6%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Her opponent is in the lead. We're getting some Election Day results in right now, 58%. She's in the lead. 51% of the vote expected in. So, it's still a little early tonight. But you're right, David. She knew that her role on the January 6th committee would possibly turn off some of the conservative voters in this district. She said she has no regrets. These are two veterans running against each other in a district where one out of every five constituents was in the military.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, two Navy vets in this race. We'll come back to this often throughout the night. Rachel, thank you. I want to bring in Nate Silver.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Every year, we come to you at the election board with the probability. I just wanted to show people at home, right now, we've obviously put Florida in the Republican column for Marco Rubio. And where does the probability stand now?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WHAT IF?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So at 63, its up from 59 at the start of the night. This was not enough said, it's impressive that Rubio won so quickly, usually Florida is a swing state. It's been called by 8:00 PM but it doesn't shift numbers so much, because it was baked in to the cake so to speak. But Georgia, if that were to go for Herschel Walker, you'll see a much bigger change, I would expect here. In that what if, that would go to 80%, based on that one call alone. What if they also win Pennsylvania? As Rick said, it starts to become very, very difficult for Democrats. They lose their best opportunity down to just 7% chance.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 93 in a 100. Let's just, for Democrats watching at home saying, why are you giving those states away? Let's go, let's give them to the Democrats and (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pretty symmetric, put Georgia over there, throw Pennsylvania over there, and those two seats swing everything most of the time, now they're at 89%.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 89% which explains why there's been so much attention on Georgia and Pennsylvania after the election.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Any new numbers here on Georgia coming in yet, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we haven't seen that much, but I'm keeping an eye on this ticket splitters, because you just keep watching these margins right now to see \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with about a ten-point margin over Herschel Walker. Again, these numbers, we don't want to read too much into this, because most of the vote we've gotten in has been early vote and it's also been a vote that has been overwhelmingly from Democratic parts of the state in the Atlanta area. But if you flip over to the governor's race, you start to see this different portrait of the evening emerging. Brian Kemp, in his race, very close too Stacey Abrams, only three points behind. That is a big story line I think. We're seeing it in New Hampshire, we're seeing it in Ohio, across the nation tonight. Governors and different candidates doing differently in the same party.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Brian Kemp, but at least according to the numbers, outperforming Herschel Walker. Herschel Walker, Jon Karl, handpicked by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, first of all, this is incredible. Rick mentioned something there are about 70,000 people in Georgia who we already know voted for Kemp, the Republican candidate for governor, the Republican governor, who did not vote for Herschel Walker, the Republican Senate candidate. This, you have to say, comes down in part to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 54.3%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 44.1%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: STACEY ABRAMS: 51.0%; BRIAN KEMP: 48.4%\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  recruited Herschel Walker. Herschel Walker was actually living in Texas at the time, said come back to the place where you played college football decades ago, run. Got him to run against what all the other Republican leadership in the state and in Washington wanted somebody else to run. Trump got Walker to run. And Kemp, on the other side, you know, was targeted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for defeat. He recruited a candidate to run against him. So, the Trump candidate is running considerably behind the candidate that defied \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Just to your right, Cecilia Vega, our chief White House correspondent. We have a few seconds left in this break and I wanted to just ask you real quick before we go where the White House is tonight. I know they put a hopeful face forward in front of the public. But what are they bracing for potentially tonight?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, they're bracing for potentially big losses. They are very hopeful about the Senate. But the reality is, they have long been aware Democrats in general, have long been aware, but very privately, that the House is theirs to lose. You heard President Biden just yesterday say that he is hopeful on the Senate, but he knows that the House is going really to be tough one for them to keep.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Martha Raddatz, you traveled this country, and you asked about, we heard President Biden talking about democracy being on the ballot at the 11th hour here in this election. People care about democracy, they do voice their concerns, but it all came down to the economy everywhere you went.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It truly did, and especially in Nevada. I was in Nevada about ten days ago. Everyone I asked about democracy or any other issues, would turn it right back around to the economy. How much their groceries cost, how about those gas bills and especially in Nevada, the hospitality industry, where they are restaurant workers, people who clean rooms, they care about this. They've got unions, the culinary union going door-to-door-to-door, trying to bring out that vote. You've talked about Harry Reid's machine there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) His machine. Will it work in Nevada? That's a little later in the night, but we can't wait for Nevada, too. All right, we're just getting started here on election night here on ABC. We're going to check back in on Georgia, on Pennsylvania tonight, New Hampshire, Ohio, all these key Senate races. The polls have now closed, the numbers are coming in. Our entire team here. We'll be back with live coverage here on ABC right after the break.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 36; REPUBLICAN: 36\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back. It is election night in America, 8:30 here in the east. Polls have now closed in Arkansas. We do have results coming in from a number of key races. And ABC News can now project, in New Hampshire, the race the for governor, the Republican there, a very popular Chris Sununu.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHRIS SUNUNU: PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He was seeking his fourth two-year term. He has stood up to Trump and critical of him before. He's also been asked if he's going to run for president in 2024. He said he doesn't know. But tonight, voters in New Hampshire re-electing Chris Sununu, governor there in their state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , I got to say, the question here would be, do the wins of Mr. Sununu carry Don Bolduc win his very close race against Maggie Hassan? In the last couple of days, we have watched the polls tightened in that race.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, know over the last 20 years, that when there is a governor's race and Senate race in the same state. Now, when the governor wins, 85% of the time, the Senate candidate of the same party wins. So, Chris Sununu winning early, winning by a significant margin, should help Don Bolduc a lot. The flip side is, if Don Bolduc loses, he's in that 15% of people in the last 20 years who had his governor win the race at the top of the ticket and he doesn't win the Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And wasn't able to pull it off. We know Maggie Hassan won this race by about 1,017 votes last time around. Early numbers and they show her with a sizable lead, but that was expected given where these numbers are coming from?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HAMPSHIRE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: MAGGIE HASSAN 60.8%; DONALD BOLDUC: 38.7%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: TOM SHERMAN: 48.7%; CHRIS SUNUNU: 51.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yeah, and there's only about 10% of the vote in, so we need to be patient on New Hampshire. This is more, this call is more about Sununu's strength than it is about anything relative in the Senate. But I am interested so far to see how Maggie Hassan is running ahead of where she needs to, including in the southeastern part of the state. That is important for her to keep those margins up as more votes flows in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What's the benchmark show us? And she's outperforming Joe Biden in New Hampshire. I'm curious where she is. That's every interesting.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Way ahead, but very early.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very early in the night. Again, Maggie Hassan taking an early lead against Don Bolduc. A little bit of whiplash for Don Bolduc. Jon Karl, he was one of the candidates who denied the election, said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not win, and then said this fall he did win. So, they're not buying this in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As soon as he got the nomination, suddenly he became a believer in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Jon Karl, the whole team here, we'll be back. Just getting started here on ABC. Our live coverage continues in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News Live, "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election Night 2022." Here now, Kyra Phillips.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS LIVE\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips in our nation's capital on a pivotal midterm election night. Thank you for streaming with us. We are tracking results across the country for you. Many people speculating that Florida's Ron DeSantis could be a 2024 presidential contender. But tonight, he is projected to remain the governor of Florida.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RON DESANTIS: PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARCO RUBIO, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in the Senate race, ABC News projecting Republican Senator Marco Rubio will beat Val Demings for re-election and remain in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: STACEY ABRAMS: 49.4%; BRIAN KEMP: 50.1%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 52.7%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 45.7%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, to Georgia, in that race for governor, Democrat Stacey Abrams hoping to become the first black woman governor in the nation, with just 41% of the vote, Abrams there at 49.4%, while Republican Brian Kemp at 50.1% of the vote. Still, though, it is early. And then, as we talk about the balance of power in the Senate, that could hinge on the state's race between Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican Herschel Walker. With 40% of the vote in there, you'll see that Warnock carrying an early lead at 52.7% with Herschel Walker at 45.7%. But, of course, it could be days until full results come in. We are watching those races closely.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in ABC News senior national correspondent Steve Osunsami, he's there in Atlanta. Steve, you are with the Warnock campaign. How are they feeling about the vote tonight as results start to come in?\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As the results have come in and they've seen the incumbent senator ahead, there have been loud cheers in the room, they were praying earlier in the night. They're still cheering because, even as that lead continues to drop, Senator Warnock is still ahead, but it is early. The votes that have come in have been mostly from very friendly counties to the blue team. And as we see, as we'll see this evening, as more of those votes come from the south of the state, from the center of the state, and from the northeast of the state, we should expect those totals that are now favoring Warnock to drop, and that margin to decrease.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, Herschel Walker faced those multiple allegations, Steve, involving abortion in the final weeks of the campaign. Did it change voters' minds heading up to the polls today?\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I don't think so. I think a lot of people had their minds made up, you know, long before these allegations came out. And, in fact, this is anecdotal, based on different people I've talked with. And for our viewers to understand, I've lived in this state for 26 years. I put a daughter through college here, you know, I know Georgians of both political stripes. And one of the things that I saw here, and as the allegations came out, it in some ways energized voters on the right. What I was hearing from my Republican neighbors and friends is that they felt that the timing of these allegations were poor. And that caused them to want to come out and support Herschel Walker.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, well, we are also watching the governor's race between incumbent Republican Brian Kemp and Democratic challenger Stacey Abrams. Abrams' campaign hasn't seem to have the same momentum as it did in 2018. What's changed, Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEVE OSUNSAMI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think the big thing that matters in this race that people aren't talking about enough is the current governor's response to the COVID-19 crisis. His opening of the state, reopening of the state during the shutdown was very popular among many small business owners on both sides. In particular, I know many of black small business owner that told me that they are voting for Brian Kemp because of this.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) All right, Steve Osunsami there in Atlanta, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: TENNESSEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILL LEE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And tonight, it is your voice, your vote, as we continue to follow the results coming in right now. Governor for Tennessee, projected winner, Bill Lee. More news ahead.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage, election night in America. It is now 8:30 here in the east. Polls now closed in Arkansas as well. We do have results coming in from a number of key races. Let's take a look at what we can project at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RON DESANTIS, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Again, Florida, Governor Ron DeSantis has been re-elected as governor of Florida. The conversation immediately turns to whether or not he runs in 2024. Everyone is ready to jump in on that one.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ALABAMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KAY IVEY, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, Alabama tonight, Governor Kay Ivey, seeking a second full term, re-elected there in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: TENNESSEE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILL LEE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Tennessee, tonight, Governor Bill Lee re-elected to a second term there. The races we're watching at 8:30, of course, the Georgia Senate race tonight, between Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the incumbent, up against Herschel Walker, the Republican.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : 511.9%; HERSCHEL WALKER: 46.4%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As you can see there, 42% of the vote in. Right now, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with a sizable lead, Rick Klein is going to tell us why these numbers appear the way they do at this point in the night and how this could likely tighten as we go forward.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN FETTERMAN: 80.1%; MEHMET OZ: 17.9%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pennsylvania tonight, the first numbers coming in, John Fetterman, the Democrat there, up against Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz. They both want to take over for Pat Toomey. As you can see, these are very early numbers. Some of these numbers are actually from the Pittsburgh area, which we know Fetterman is from, which explains why he's taking such a sizable lead early on up against Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY: 52.3%; TED BUDD: 45.9%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In North Carolina tonight, Cheri Beasley, the former Supreme Court chief justice in that state up against Republican Congressman Ted Budd also with a sizable lead. 55% of the vote, and she's up 52% to 45%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIM RYAN: 56.9%; J.D. VANCE: 43.0%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Ohio, a state we're watching, it's gone Republican the last presidential cycle and the one before that as well. The Congressman Tim Ryan, the Democrat there, out with an early lead over J.D. Vance, of course, supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . 29% of the vote in, very early in Ohio. If he was able to keep this lead, that would be a major storyline of the night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAGGIE HASSAN: 60.9%; DONALD BOLDUC: 38.1%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And to the northeast, New Hampshire in New England tonight. Democrat Senator Maggie Hassan seeking a second term in that state, opening up a widening lead here up against Don Bolduc. 60% to 38%. She won this race last time with just about 1,017 votes. She knew that these polls got very tight in the last few days before the election. So far, tonight, in the early numbers, she's doing a decent job in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to bring it back to Georgia, because you were telling me in the break, these are very telling and we're beginning to see a blue mirage, which we have to bring the viewers up to speed on. But we knew we would see something like this early in the night.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, David, there's two big things that you're watching as votes comes in, where the vote comes from and what kind of what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 52.1%; WALKER: 46.2%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Starting with the what kind, about 60% of the vote in Georgia expected to be early or absentee. And we're seeing \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  winning that by about ten points. What this means is that Herschel Walker has to win the Election Day vote with about a 60-40 margin. Not impossible, but it just tells you how he has to make it up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COBB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 59.2%; WALKER: 38.8%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 56.2%, BENCHMARK, 42.1%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And when you look at where the vote is coming in, there's a couple of suburban counties that are starting to come in. We've compared to our benchmarks. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is hitting his numbers in Cobb County, just north of the city, also Gwinnett County.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GWINNETT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 65.7%; WALKER: 32.4%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 58.2%, BENCHMARK, 40.2%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Getting a little bit above where he needs to do. But, again, most of that vote so far is early and that doesn't necessarily mean it's going to hold up. So, when you see a lead for John Fetterman early on, when you see a lead for Tim Ryan, when you see a lead even for Cheri Beasley, this reflects the fact that more Democratic parts of the state are reporting early and that it's early vote.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we often see that. I want to go back to Georgia for just a second, because of this benchmark that we have, this new tool this year. I want to show viewers at home. Just show me, Rick, the metro Atlanta area tonight, just how \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is performing against the benchmark, where he would need to be in order for this to end well for him tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FULTON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 75.3%; WALKER: 23.3%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 72.2%, BENCHMARK, 26.2%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) 72%, the benchmark from FiveThirtyEight?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, that means that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , in Fulton County, huge part of Atlanta, Atlanta itself is about 50% of all the vote in the state of Georgia, Fulton County, the biggest county, and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  would need to be getting about three-fourths of the vote, about a little north of 70% to break even and he is there. Again, the question about whether this is going to tighten a little bit. We're seeing a lot of people not voting for Herschel Walker who voted the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how is \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  doing against Joe Biden's numbers there?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, the other way to look at this is, of course, Joe Biden won and you're seeing Warnock ahead of him there and in some of the other parts in and around Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very interesting. Let's swing it over to Governor Christie. You're getting some information on the ground in Georgia about Herschel Walker's performance tonight?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, earlier, David, we were saying that Walker was initially trailing three, three-and-a-half points behind Kemp's performance. That's now increased. And they're now consistently seeing across the state Walker trailing Kemp by five. Now, that becomes a huge problem for Walker. If you trail Kemp by five, that puts you probably somewhere in the 47%, 4