%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Pennsylvania tonight, the Senate race, John Fetterman up against Dr. Oz just 10% of the vote in, a sizable lead over Dr. Oz, but we're talking about the southern portion of that state, where Fetterman is from. We'll watch that closely.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHERI BEASLEY: 49.3%; TED BUDD: 48.8%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In North Carolina, Cheri Beasley had opened up an early lead. That's tightened significantly since we last checked last half hour. Republican Congressman Ted Budd tightening that race, now 49%-48%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIM RYAN: 51.6%; J.D. VANCE: 48.3%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Ohio at this hour, Congressman Tim Ryan looking to replace Rob Portman. J.D. Vance, the Republican in that state, the backing of Donald Trump. Tim Ryan appearing to hold his own with 39% of the vote in. We're going to dive into some of the counties in Ohio here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAGGIE HASSAN: 58.7%; DONALD BOLDUC\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in New Hampshire, up in New England, Senator Maggie Hassan holding on to that lead, it's tightened a bit, but only slightly, 58.7% to Don's Bolduc 40% there in New Hampshire, another race that we're watching closely.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our entire team standing by, ABC News election night in America continues right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live from New York City and across the country, "Election Night 2022." Now reporting, David Muir.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening for those just joining us on this election night in America. The night shaping up to be one of the most defining midterm elections in US history. A tug of war, a battle for control of the House. We have been saying this all night long, Republicans need a net gain of just five seats. Historically, that's a very low hurdle for Republicans, couple of flips Rachel Scott has reported on them already. And we're keeping an eye on several key races in Virginia that are turning out to be bellwether races for how Democrats might do the rest of the night as we cross the country. The future of President Biden's agenda at stake. We're going to be taking a look at these key races all this half hour.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to get over to Rick Klein at the wall because, Rick, you've been diving into two races, Pennsylvania and Ohio. And we've been talking a lot about the split tickets, whether or not people would vote for somebody for governor and then change, vote the other party for senator. And Pennsylvania was a big question with Josh Shapiro such a strong candidate for governor there.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're seeing it play out, as almost the mirror image of what we're siege tonight in Georgia. The numbers right now, don't read too much into this. This is mostly early vote. But what we do know, when you see John Fetterman racking up big numbers like in his hometown just outside Pittsburgh, look at that margin, 400,000 votes so far.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN FETTERMAN: 72.1%; OZ: 25.6%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALLEGHENY: FETTERMAN, 78.8%; OZ, 19.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We know that that same voters on the exact same night are also voting for governor. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHAPIRO: 75.9%; MASTRIANO: 22.5%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look at what they are doing for Josh Shapiro. They got about 432,000 votes. So, that is that's 25,000, 30,000-plus votes that are being cast at the top of the ticket, Josh Shapiro expected to beat Doug Mastriano. It's going to be a lot tighter in that Senate race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, no question that Shapiro is outperforming Fetterman even with the small vote in even at this hour. Ohio, you were looking at this, a close race here. Tim Ryan holding his own at least at this point in the night here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHALEY: 41.3%; DEWINE: 58.6%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, in the governor's race, we know Mike DeWine is running away with it, and it's not really a surprise.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Of course, the Republican governor. I just want to put out for folks out home.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Very popular Republican governor. We called him some time ago. He won easily, re-election. The question would be, were there split-ticket voters who would then perhaps vote for Tim Ryan?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 51.2%; VANCE: 48.7%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the answer to that is almost certainly, yes, with a large amount. So, most of the vote we have in is the early vote. So, these numbers may not hold. But so far, I'm keyed into a couple of places where Donald Trump won counties like this, just outside, just west of Cleveland on Lake Erie, Loraine County.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LORAINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 55.0%; VANCE: 45.0%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 52.78, BENCHMARK, 47.2%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our FiveThirtyEight benchmarks have Tim Ryan needing to get about 52% of the vote and he's doing just that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAHONING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 63.7%; VANCE: 36.3%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 53.1%, BENCHMARK, 46.9%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Over in his hometown of Youngstown, that's another one. Donald Trump won this county. Tim Ryan doing about ten points even above the benchmark. Again, a lot of vote to go, but it is very clear that Tim Ryan is doing what he needs to do to potentially win as a Democrat in Ohio at the same night that a very popular incumbent Republican is cruising to re-election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, this is really fascinating because Tim Ryan is an example of one of these Democrats who really kept President Biden at bay.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In fact, he'd been asked often, would he have President Biden come campaign? He said, no, it's not about who I'm campaigning with, but who I'm campaigning for.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He may not win tonight, but people are going to pay attention to a candidate that's able to win in places that Donald Trump won and still rack up big margin in and around Columbus.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRANKLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 73.6%; VANCE: 23.6%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CUYAHOGA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RYAN: 72.8%; VANCE: 27.1%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In and around Cleveland, Cuyahoga County, he's getting what he needs to get there. At the same time that, so far, tonight, he's holding his own in the rural areas.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to ask Heidi Heitkamp about this, Tim Ryan's playbook. It's an interesting to watch him keep the President at bay and try to talk about pocketbook issues this entire race.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, it hasn't just been about the President, it's been about Democrats. He's taken on the establishment. He's taken on some people would argue the progressives. He was absolutely the perfect match. He is a blue-collar union-supporting Democrat in that kind of state where we used to win. You know, he will set a path forward, hopefully for the presidential race in a couple years. Win or lose.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, this was a guy, Donna Brazile, who actually took on House Speaker Nancy Pelosi when his own advisers and friends were saying, don't do that.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, Tim has run a traditional old-style Democratic campaign. He campaigned in the rural counties as well as the suburbs. He understood that in order to make up the deficit that Democrats typically have in that bellwether state, he had to go out there and separate himself from the national Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: TIM RYAN, 51.2%; VANCE, 48.7%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: NAN WHALEY, 41.0%; MIKE DEWINE, 59.0%\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think he's run a fantastic campaign. I was in Ohio a few weeks before President Biden came and announced some key investments. And while many of the Democrats did not show up, Tim Ryan did.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) I want to ask you about this, Governor Christie, when we're seeing this split ticket potential, particularly in Pennsylvania. That's quite telling, Josh Shapiro doing much better than John Fetterman at least in these early returns.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, how about we start with the premise that candidates matter? You know, we spent so little time talking about that, we're talking about atmospherics and poll numbers. But, look, Mastriano is an awful candidate. So, Josh Shapiro is a good candidate. He's running against an awful candidate. And now, so you start with this big gap and if you're Dr. Oz now, you've got to get so many of those people to cross over. And I want folks to remember, there's a Democratic incumbent in Pennsylvania. Republicans have dominated the gubernatorial scene for the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: JOHN FETTERMAN, 69.0%; MEHMET OZ, 28.7%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: JOSH SHAPIRO, 73.6%; DOUG MASTRIANO, 24.8%\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For most of the last 20 years, they've had more Republican governors in the country than Democrats. But think about this, in the last 20 years, only five Democratic incumbent governors have been defeated, only five in 20 years of races.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In 20 years of races. So, it's still very hard to defeat a Democratic incumbent governor when they're doing what they're supposed to be doing. It hasn't happened much. And you're seeing it in Pennsylvania. If you don't have a good candidate, it's not going to happen. And Dr. Oz may really be upset at the end of this, if he comes up short, at how badly Mastriano did at the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But you saw him in his final days talking about how, I'll be a moderate in Washington and trying to encourage some of this Shapiro voters to vote for him.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) True. But the problem is, Oz has Trump's endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right? So, you know, somebody like Mike DeWine can do that, right, effectively. Somebody like Brian Kemp can do that effectively. But if you're Dr. Oz and you not only have had Trump's endorsement but, on top of it, had Trump come in for you the last weekend and you stood next to him at a rally, it's very hard to sell to some of those swing voters, David, that, well, but I'll be different if I go to Washington.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Obviously, very early in the night. But if Dr. Oz doesn't pull this off, they are going to be questioning whether or not having former President Trump there in the final day or two was the right call.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're going to be questioning that, they're going to be questioning Trump endorsing Mastriano. And by the way, all the people who were with McCormick in the primary are all going to be saying, if Trump hadn't endorsed Oz there, McCormick was the candidate, then McCormick would beat Fetterman easily. And I think there's a good argument for it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If Dr. Oz wins, Trump takes credit. That's how the night will go. Either way...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And by the way, he won't take blame if he loses. It will be Oz's fault.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll check Rick on that one. Eva Pilgrim is live in Pennsylvania. She's been covering this race, in fact, both races for us. Eva?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I mean, exactly what you're talking about there, David, the split ticket voting. We heard from so many voters in the last several days that we have been crisscrossing through Pennsylvania that they just felt like the Republican candidate Mastriano was too extreme. And when you look at his campaign, it's completely unconventional. His campaign manager is a pastor with no campaigning experience. And that's the way that his campaign has been run.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNSYLVANIA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: JOHN FETTERMAN, 69.0%, MEHMET OZ, 28.7%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: JOSH SHAPIRO, 73.6%; DOUG MASTRIANO, 24.8%\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, when people start to look at the other races on the ticket, there are questions. And particularly what Chris Christie just said, that idea of Trump coming and Dr. 