it on livestream if you are at all in question of it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But instead, stick with us. Eva, I want folks at home, just stick with us, watch the livestream off to the side on your computer. Eva Pilgrim live in Pennsylvania. Eva, we'll come back to you shortly. We've been saying all night long, 36 gubernatorial races in this country. We have news coming in, on two key governor races. We're going to have those right after the break, one includes a flip, the other include as name you'll well know. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Your Voice, Your Vote" live on ABC. You can follow ABC News Live, abcnews.com and FiveThirtyEight for the latest projections, news and analysis in these high-stakes midterm elections to determine control of Congress on Apple News.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage of this crucial midterm election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: 218 TO CONTROL; 435 IN PLAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP: 180, DEM: 120\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: REPS NEED 51 TO CONTROL; 35 IN PLAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REP: 39; DEM: 38\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You can see the tallies there. All the Republicans need is a gain of one seat in the Senate to take back control. Again, in the House, they need five seats. Democrats would be happy with a 50/50 Senate again, where the Vice President is the tiebreaking vote. We'll see at the end of the night where this math stands. We've been telling you all night long, we're tracking 36 gubernatorial races across this country. And we've got results coming in, including the key race out of Massachusetts tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MASSACHUSETTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAURA HEALEY, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The retiring moderate Republican governor Charlie Baker, very popular in Massachusetts, the Democrat has won that race, making history tonight. Maura Healy, the attorney general, she is the first lesbian governor, the first woman elected governor of Massachusetts. Martha Raddatz standing by on Maura Healey here. We'll have more on her in a moment.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ARKANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Arkansas, this is a name you will know very well. The Republican winning there, Sarah Huckabee Sanders, now the governor of Arkansas. Cecilia, Jon, you worked with Sarah Huckabee, asking her questions at the White House during the Trump years. She's now the governor. She follows in her father's footsteps there in Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARKANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN BOOZMAN, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Arkansas Senate tonight, John Boozman has been seeking a third term re-elected. He'll go back to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JERRY MORAN, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Kansas, the Senate winner there is Senator Jerry Moran, seeking his third term, headed back to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: SOUTH DAKOTA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN THUNE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) South Dakota tonight, John Thune had sought a fourth term, projected winner there. Not a surprise, though he was one of three incumbent Republicans that president, that former President Trump did not endorse in this race. He wins anyway, very popular in his state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ALABAMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATIE BRITT, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Alabama tonight, Katie Britt, the former chief of staff, by the way, to the retiring Senator Richard Shelby, she now goes from chief of staff to the senator herself. Katie Britt will serve Alabama in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OKLAHOMA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JAMES LANKFORD, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARKWAYNE MULLIN, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Oklahoma, the only state with two Senate races, James Lankford, re-elected for a second full term. And Congressman Markwayne Mullin will now serve as a senator for Oklahoma, he's been elected there as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: VERMONT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PETER WELCH, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in Vermont tonight, Peter Welch looking to trade his role in Congress to head to the Senate instead. He has won his race. The eight-term congressman now the senator from Vermont.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're here with the entire powerhouse political team. I want to bring it right over to Martha Raddatz, who's been tracking. You and I both covered Massachusetts politics for many years at WCVB. And Maura Healy is a big story in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's a big story because, as you know, David, the governor there has been Republican for most of the past 30 years. But Maura Healy, the projected winner there, she will become, as you said, the first elected female governor and also the first openly gay governor in Massachusetts. But let me just tell you a little bit about her opponent, Geoff Diehl. He continued to tie himself to Donald Trump. Donald Trump lost by 36 points in Massachusetts. As you know, David, it is a deep, deep red state. Maura Healy, an AG, sued Trump many, many, many times, about 100 times. This is my favorite quote from her, though, she loved being AG, but her second favorite job was a cocktail waitress at a beach casino.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I can relate. I can relate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We can relate. The part we can't relate to is she played I think for Harvard basketball, right? And played overseas for a short stint in Europe. Yeah. We can't relate there.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. We can't, we can't relate there. I definitely can't. I have no sports analogies this evening. Okay? Everybody else is going to have to take care of that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Okay. But it is interesting, it is a deep blue state, but they have had a history of electing Republican governors.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mitt Romney.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Mitt Romney from Massachusetts from that state as well.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Mitt Romney and Charlie Baker was a hugely, hugely popular governor there. And, you know, the opponent the Republicans put up basically didn't have a chance.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia and Jon, what do think about Sarah Huckabee Sanders? Did you picture her about being governor?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I mean, look, she kind of grew up in the governor's mansion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She did.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Her father, Mike Huckabee, was governor of Arkansas. It's astounding to see her go from a relatively junior person on the Trump campaign, junior person in the press office at first, rise up to be the press secretary and become governor. I mean, she is, you know, a star that was made by Donald Trump in terms of, you know, Republican politics. But in Arkansas, she was already obviously very well-known.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he stuck with her for a very long time. He was a fan of Sarah Huckabee Sanders.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she stuck with him.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The ads that have come out of her campaign have been fascinating to watch. There is, they have love letters to Donald Trump and highlighting her time in the White House. You see shots of her walking with Ivanka Trump and the entire first family and her in the White House with the former President Trump. So, no surprise there. And the campaign, frankly, has been, no surprise in terms of the issues that she has focused on, a lot of kitchen table issues, a lot of, you know, things like school rights particularly, and COVID rights, things like that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Cecilia, Jon, thanks. She's a name that the country will know from the Trump years, taking the questions from the reporters in that room, the two of you included. Real quickly before we go to break, Rick, you've got an update on Georgia at this hour?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, we've seen a lot more votes started to come in. Raphael Warnock has taken a lead over Herschel Walker, still only about little less than two-thirds of the votes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 51.2%; WALKER: 47.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's about 100,000 votes and counting, though, that Brian Kemp has that Herschel Walker is missing. Those split-ticket voters looming very large. We're starting to see a lot more coming in in Atlanta and the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEKALB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNOCK: 87.3%; WALKER: 11.6%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And all of these, almost all of these places, we're seeing Raphael Warnock outperform the benchmark. Stay a little bit ahead of where he needs to go. The key for him, this is where the Democratic voters are. I'm going to be keyed into some of these suburbs north of the city. Some of these places are Republican, but we're seeing we're seeing Raphael Warnock cut into the kind of margins even more Republican areas, people leaving that name blank or splitting their ticket.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's really interesting, only 64% of the vote in, but this tracks with what Governor Christie was hearing from folks on the ground there that Brian Kemp is outperforming what Herschel Walker is doing among Republicans and that this could be a state where we're talking about split ticket voters.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it has an impact at least now with the early returns. Look at that number, 51% vote for Raphael Warnock based on what we have so far. If you go over to the governor's race, we see just about the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABRAMS: 47.8%; KEMP: 51.6%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Brian Kemp north of that 50% mark. And, of course, in Georgia, 50% matters. Because that's the only way you avoid a runoff.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we possibly, possibly, could be facing a runoff if we don't get 50, plus one, right? But at least the runoff is, what, four weeks from now, instead of what it was in the last time we did this?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mercifully, it's one month instead of two months this time around. But look, if control of the Senate is on the line, it's going to be a very big deal. And I've talked to people in both parties in the last couple of days who feel like it's almost an inevitability. Right now, Herschel Walker, a little north, little south of where he needs to go to avoid it. Brian Kemp, a little north of it. We're going to see these numbers change a lot, though. Again, only about 65% of the expected vote.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you were telling us many nights now, Georgia, Georgia, Georgia. And here we are, you at the board in front of Georgia. We have news, Governor Jared Polis of Colorado has been re-elected governor there. We have news coming in just as I was talking to Rick over here. Again, Governor Polis reelected in Colorado. More coming up after the break. More quick polls closing. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News Live, "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election Night 2022." Here now, Kyra Phillips.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips in our nation's capital on this midterm election night. Thank you so much for streaming with us. Big news in a key race for governor, where Sarah Huckabee Sanders makes history twice.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ARKANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHRIS JONES: 44.9%; SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS: 53.7%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're projecting the former Trump press secretary has won Arkansas, that state's first female governor and now the first father/daughter governors. Her father, Mike Huckabee, was governor, as you know, years back.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MASSACHUSETTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAIRA HEALEY: 60.9%; GEOFF DIEHL: 37.4\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And history being made in Massachusetts as well. ABC projects Democrat Maura Healy as the state's first openly gay governor.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENTE: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN FETTERMAN: 60.7%; MEHMET OZ: 36.8%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And we're watching Pennsylvania's Senate race closely. Tight now, with 17% of the vote in, John Fetterman at almost 61%, while Dr. Mehmet Oz at close to 37%. Both candidates were out earlier today casting ballots, ending a very contentious race, one that could decide which party will control Congress.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in our Eva Pilgrim, who is there in Pennsylvania with the Fetterman campaign. Bring us up to date, Eva. How is that camp responding to the vote right now?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, at this point, they're relatively optimistic. That's what we've hear from Pennsylvania Democrats. But they know they have a long way to go, so they're being very cautious about how they're looking at things. But when they look at the exit polls, that makes them feel pretty positive about the night so far. Kyra?\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Eva, that race definitely tightened up following that debate between Fetterman and Dr. Oz. From talking to voters on the ground there, what kind of impact do you think that's had on the contest?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, the question of his health has really come up quite a bit. People seeing him during that debate. And that was something that voters asked about even on the other side of it. But a lot of voters I talked to say they weren't really that concerned about his health. Any of those that had questions, we saw a lot of them actually showing up to his events to see him in person and interact with him in person and walked away from it feeling like he was in good health and capable of doing the job. That didn't seem to be a deciding factor in whether or not they were going to vote for him. A lot of people still had questions, they weren't sure who they were going to vote for on election day. I will tell you, I saw more people going into today that were undecided than I have in many, many elections previous.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the economy and inflation major concerns for voters there, Eva. But Dr. Oz hammering on the issue of crime. What do you think, did that resonate with voters?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I will tell you, I do think that the Dr. Oz campaign going after Fetterman on crime was quite effective in Pennsylvania, it was something that a lot of voters had questions about. I had a lot of people asking me about his record on crime, asking where they could find information about his record on crime. So, that messaging, very effective here, Kyra.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Eva Pilgrim, thank you so much. We'll continue the conversation now. Let's take a look at the governor's race between Democratic candidate Josh Shapiro and Republican challenger Doug Mastriano.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOSH SHAPIRO: 65.3%; DOUG MASTRIANO: 32.9%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You can see there, with 17% of the vote in, Shapiro at 65% while Mastriano right now at 32%.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARKANSAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATALIE JAMES: 39.7%; JOHN BOOZMAN: 57.5%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in Arkansas, ABC News can project that John Boozman will win the Senate seat there. And we'll send it back to election headquarters in New York in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We are so glad you are streaming with us here tonight on ABC News live. Much more coverage straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage of this midterm election night, a critical midterm election. You see those numbers up on the board, and we repeat this all night long that the House, Republicans need just a net gain of five seats in the House. There have been a number of flips already that Rachel has reported on that will take some time as we move across the country to see what this House could potentially look like under Republican control, if they're able to get those five seats.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: DEM: 120, REP: 181\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: DEM: 38; REP: 40\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Again, historically, that's a very low bar. And in the Senate, it bears repeating tonight that they need to pick up just one seat at the end of tonight. Of course, it could be more than just tonight if we're talking about Georgia again like we were not so long ago.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do have a number of races we're tracking at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: COLORADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JARED POLIS, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VOI) As I've said just before the break, in Colorado today, we have projected the governor there, Jared Polis has been re-elected, among the first Democratic leaders to loosen COVID restrictions, ahead of the game there. Made history four years ago as the first openly gay man to be elected governor there, very popular in Colorado. He's been re-elected there tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: VERMONT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PHIL SCOTT, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Vermont, the Republican governor, Phil Scott, seeking a fourth two-year term there, he has been re-elected governor of Vermont.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: SOUTH CAROLINA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HENRY MCMASTER, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In South Carolina tonight, the Republican governor there, Henry McMaster, seeking a second full term, re-elected there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: RHODE ISLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANIEL MCKEE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Rhode Island, the incumbent Governor Daniel McKee, the Democrat there, has been re-elected as governor in that state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NORTH DAKOTA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN HOEVEN, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To the Senate we go, in North Carolina tonight, Senator John Hoeven, seeking a third term, he's been re-elected in Heitkamp's home state North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRETCHEN WHITMER: 53.0%; TUDOR DIXON: 45.4%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in Michigan tonight, we continue to track this race, a race getting national attention. The governor of Michigan, Gretchen Whitmer, with a lead over Tudor Dixon, 13% of the vote in, very early tonight, though 53% to 45%. A difference of about a little less than 50,000 votes there. We're going to track this all night long.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to our powerhouse political team right here in the studio. I want to bring in Linsey Davis, who's tracking the exits. And a very telling exit poll out of Michigan tonight about a very key issue that's also on the ballot along with the governor.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Gretchen Whitmer, who Donald Trump famously called that woman from Michigan. She may be buoyed by abortion being on the ballot because take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHIGAN: MOST IMPORTANT ISSUE WHEN VOTING\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is the top priority for Michigan residents, Michigan voters, they're saying 45%, abortion is their top priority. Now, that's trails, that's quite different from what we saw nationally. It was actually economy that was the first issue. And then, here you have inflation, 28%. And so, Michigan is one in five states that actually has abortion on the ballot, but it's the only state that actually has a key race attached to it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really interesting. I think it's the first state, actually, where we've seen abortion rights ahead of inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion first, exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring it out to Juju Chang, who is covering this race in Michigan. "Nightline" anchor Juju Chang. Juju?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, David, here I am in Detroit at the Motor City Casino. We're at Gretchen Whitmer's election night headquarters with 1,600 cautiously optimistic revelers around me. Now, polls closed here at 8:00 and you were showing those early results, giving a tip to Gretchen Whitmer. But I was speaking earlier to Senator Debbie Stabenow, the senior senator here. She said they're on track to break the midterm election turnout. She's been on the phone with local election officials. She's seeing two-hour lines at the University of Michigan, lots of young voters and energized.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRETCHEN WHITMER: 53.0%; TUDOR DIXON: 45.4%\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as you say, the "Big Gretch" may be lifted by maybe what's known as Dobb's effect because of prop 3. So many voters who are interested in voting for abortion rights are heading to the polls. Now, her opponent, Tudor Dixon, is one of those Trump-anointed former business people, former conservative pundits. And tonight, the Dobbs effect may be lifting Governor Whitmer and down ballot issues as well. You know, I talked to one woman who went to high school with Gretchen Whitmer, a life-long Republican who voted for President Trump twice, she told me, and hadn't even voted for Governor Whitmer, but felt so strongly about abortion rights that she was crossing the line.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, anecdotally, we're hearing stories like that from women I talked to, because we traveled around from Grand Rapids to Kalamazoo to here to Detroit tonight, all over Michigan, trying to get a sense of what the voters are thinking and what you might be seeing with those exit polls is that tip towards abortion rights tonight. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju Chang with us tonight. Juju, our thanks to you in Michigan. As soon as we have more on the race for governor there, we'll come back to as well. I want to kick it right over the big board to Rachel Scott, because abortion is on the ballot in a number of states, not just Michigan.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion is on the ballot this election in five states. So, California, Michigan, and Vermont are all efforts to protect access to abortion under the state's constitution. Then you have Montana and Kentucky, these are measures to further restrict access to abortion. David, I want to point out here, all the orange that we are seeing on this map. This comes just months after Roe vs. Wade was overturned. This is where there are current bans on abortion in, across the country right now. Obviously, we know that this also comes after Kansas, the conservative state, was the first state to have abortion on the ballot earlier this summer. The conservative state voted to protect access to abortion. Since then, we have seen a wave of women in key battleground states register to vote. Democrats are hoping that this becomes a driving issue for them this election day.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to be watching this all through the night because we saw there earlier, Mary Bruce, Nate Silver at FiveThirtyEight showing us when roe was overturned by the Supreme Court, how the gulf between Democrats and Republicans in the polling suddenly was quite significant, and then tightened again as we got closer to election day with the number of weeks having passed since then. But I know that you had been talking in recent days, will be interesting to see tonight if those who are driven by abortion rights will show up in a way that they haven't shown up in previous midterms, which would still make them statistically very significant tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that is certainly what Democrats, what this White House is hoping, is that even though you saw that sort of drop off in the polls that the economy was replacing it at the top, as the top issue that voters were most concerned about, that abortion was still be driving everyone to the polls. I think we have something else here. So, I'm going to pause.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We do have a race coming in right now. Michael Bennet has won in Colorado, the senator there, the Democrat in Colorado, the incumbent has been re-elected.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: COLORADO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL BENNET, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We told you that the governor was re-elected, now the senator. Michael Bennet beating Joe O'Dea, the Republican in that state, the construction company executive. Joe O'Dea taking him on, hoping to be a moderate Republican. The debates, you know, they were civil in the beginning, though the last debate, Jon Karl, there was some fireworks. Joe O'Dea did not have the support of former President Trump. In fact, he said, looking forward, he would like to have somebody new on the ticket in 2024. And Michael Bennet said almost the same thing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, look, Joe O'Dea was very critical of Donald Trump and Donald Trump gave it right back to him. In fact, Ron DeSantis endorsed Joe O'Dea just a few weeks ago and Trump criticized DeSantis, saying it was a big mistake to support him. So, this was a moderate Republican trying to win in a state that has become increasingly more Democratic (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I see Nate Silver just over your shoulder here. What does this do to the probability tonight, if we give Colorado, which we thought would go to the Democrats tonight, based on the polling in recent days, but...\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, so what happens is a little shift because this was the probable result, but still helps Democrats a little bit, now from 40% to 37%. What it does show means, we might not have a red tsunami. If they're holding up well in Colorado, New Hampshire looks good for Maggie Hassan so far, the GOP could easily win the Senate in our model right now, but you're not going to see a gigantic 55-seat GOP coalition coming in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's just say Maggie Hassan holds on in New Hampshire, she's off to a decent start tonight with early numbers coming in, what does that do for the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, now they might go to 50/50, maybe a little above, 51%-49%.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Interesting, yeah.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, if New Hampshire were called, five minutes from now, we are back to a total jump ball in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, that's a hypothetical. But let's swing it over to Rick Klein to see where we are in New Hampshire. Do you have a second, Rick, to pull that up, to show if Maggie Hassan actually holds on?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Anything for you, David, of course. Let's see what we see in New Hampshire right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You won't say that at 2:00 AM.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HASSAN: 58.7%; BOLDUC: 40.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Maggie Hassan right now with a pretty significant lead. Look, this is a state that mostly votes on election day. It's actually pretty decent with reporting. That's one of the consequences of being first in the nation for the primaries.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HILLSBOROUGH COUNTY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HASSAN: 54.2%; BOLDUC: 45.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm looking though, right now, Maggie Hassan is up everywhere. That includes some more of the Republican leaning parts of the state. Joe Biden did decently here. You see Maggie Hassan a little bit above that. Here in southeastern part of the state, again, Maggie Hassan, a little bit above. She's even leading where Don Bolduc is from, up by Lake Winnipesauke area.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 40.2%, BENCHMARK, 58.0%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the benchmarks here are important because these are parts of the state that frankly a Republican needs to do very well in. Now, these numbers could change as the nights go on. But a lot of people I've been talking to tonight, on texts, on email, they feel really good about where she stands.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's really interesting. She's not only outperforming Joe Biden, but she's significantly outperforming the benchmark of where she needs to be in multiple counties at this point of the night.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and you would expect it someplace like in and around Concord, same thing in Manchester and Nashwa. And these are where the voters are, this is the most populous part of the state and she's doing it. Look, this was a real scare for Democrats. And it could get tight, still a chance that Don Bolduc wins. But a lot of Democrats are liking what they're seeing in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. So, the reason we're spending so much time on New Hampshire is because of what Nate Silver showed us on his board because, if they're able to pull off New Hampshire tonight, and they've just won with Michael Bennet in Colorado, we're back to a 51 chance, 51% chance for Democrats, 49% for Republicans. It just shows how every single one of these Senate races are so important for really both sides in this. We have another call tonight. We can project that, in Maryland, history made in Maryland tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: MARYLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WES MOORE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The Democrat there, Wes Moore, becomes governor there. And Byron Pitts, he makes history, and this is quite the story.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Oh, David, absolutely right. He becomes the third black governor elected in the United States, following Doug Wilder in Virginia and Deval Patrick in Massachusetts. And, David, I think in many ways, he instantly becomes a star in the Democratic Party and perhaps a north star for the party.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, Martha's talked tonight about how important veterans are in this election cycle. He's a veteran. He served in Iraq. He was a paratrooper, member of the 82nd Airborne. He's a best-selling author. He's a Rhodes Scholar. And, David, I can say, as a proud son of Maryland, born and raised in east Baltimore, this race, we can say that Wes Moore didn't win because he was black and he wouldn't have lost because he was black. I think this is a case of, I have friends and family there, Democrats, Republicans, independents, in this race, we talked about the quality of candidates.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For many people, he was just seen as a better candidate. I mean, think about this. The Republican governor of Maryland, who's very popular, said of the Republican nominee, he called him, quote, "a QAnon whack job." That doesn't help the Republican Party candidate in that state.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, I think Wes Moore, again, becomes an example for the Democratic Party. And his is a wonderful American story in some ways, humble means, he's the son of Jamaican, his grandparents are Jamaican immigrants. And when he was in high school, failing, in trouble, going down the wrong road, his Jamaican grandparents mortgaged their home to send him to the military academy, because they believed so deeply in the power of education to transform someone's life, they believe in the American dream. So, I think a lot of people, certainly the folks who voted for his opponent, they will be disappointed. But I think, generally speaking, in Maryland, this is a win that I think most people feel good about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and it's a flip. Of course, Larry Hogan, a moderate Republican, a very popular Republican in that state and, Linsey Davis, I know that you went running with him, I should explain this.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's fast.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He's very fast. You're fast, too. But, Linsey, you went out across the country and ran with a number of these candidates, not as their running mate but, you know, just running alongside, and ask them a few questions. And this was kind of a very eye-opening interview for you.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) You know, one point that he wanted to make is that no party has patriotism. And he was saying that, often, Republicans try to claim that they are the patriots. And he was saying, look, I am a patriot here. I went out and actually served in Afghanistan, I actually left my family on behalf of this country.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And something I wanted to piggy back off of with Byron, because I think it cannot be understated here, when we think about the country's 246-year history, right? Of course, we did have P.B.S. Pinchback from Donna Brazile's state of Louisiana back in Reconstruction Era in 1872, but he was not elected. And so, it really is so significant, when you think about, in 246 years, that we're only now, as of tonight, getting our third black governor. Really significant. But when I did have the chance to ask him while we were running, you know, is there a weight to that, knowing you would be the third?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And he said, you know, obviously this would be history making, but I'm not running to make history. I'm running because I want a child poverty history. We're going to make racial wealth gap history. We're going to make environmental injustice history.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Making history tonight in Maryland. Wes Moore, elected governor in that state. And Lily, the director, if we can go to break on that video of Linsey running with Wes Moore, I'm going to let folks at home decide who is actually more tired. You look pretty good there, Linsey. He looked like he was breathing a little hard there.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, he was deceptively fast. He slowed down at the end for me.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You look pretty good there. All right, our live election night coverage here, this critical midterm election. There you go. All right, you let him win. You let him win. He did win tonight and he made history doing it in Maryland. We'll be right back right after the break.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is 9:45 here in the east, election night coverage here on ABC News, "Your Voice, Your Vote," the critical midterm election. And I want to head right over to the big boards with Rachel and Rick. Because, Rachel, we've been talking about these flips all night and that magic number of just five seats. You gave us the historical context of this, very low bar, and we've got a key flip right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRATS: 121; REPUBLICAN 181\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Another key flip right here in Georgia. This is the sixth district here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHRISTIAN: 38.6%; MCORMICK: 61.4%\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is projected for Dr. Rich McCormick, he's an ER doctor, conservative. He actually outperformed where former President Donald Trump did in this district. Let's take a look at where things stand right now. You mentioned it, David, Republicans only need to net five seats by the end of the night in order to take the House. Take a look at how many they have so far. They have three right here in Florida, they just added another one in Georgia. You could see on this race grid here how much defense Republicans are playing, how much defense Democrats are playing throughout the night compared to Republicans on this side. Right now, they have netted four, they need five by the end of the night, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, for folks watching at home, this grid really shows you, Democrats are on defense tonight when it comes to the House far more than the Republicans are going into the night. Rich McCormick, I know, Rick, you have the election denier graphic up here, which gives you the idea of a number of people running in this country tonight who don't believe Joe Biden won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is an astounding figure and it means people who are potentially going to be running the next election, in charge of overseeing the next election, who denied the legitimacy of the last one.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP CANDIDATES WHO QUESTIONED THE 2020 ELECTION\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many of these House members are incumbents who are returning after having voted not to certify the last election. We have a newly elected attorney general in Florida who denies the legitimacy of the last election.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The governors of Alabama, South Carolina and, yes, the former White House aide Sarah Huckabee Sanders in the state of Arkansas also raising questions about the last election. And where this really matters is in those statehouses, because these are people that could be overseeing the next election. And I'm going to keep an eye on tonight, some the battleground states like Nevada, like Arizona, like Pennsylvania, that still have people that deny the legitimacy of the election they're running.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, in Arizona, Kari Lake, who is doing very well in the polling lead up to election night, running for governor there against Katie Hobbs, of course, and she, in the last 24 hours, questioned the results of tonight before they even came.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, there's lawsuits flying there as well. She's trying to tell people that there's issues with the vote without any evidence. And she has already said to our colleague Jon Karl and others that she may not concede defeat if she loses tonight. That's a key thing to watch as the night goes on. We're looking at the vote count, we don't care what the candidates say about it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But when we talked about these election deniers, Rick, thank you. I want to bring up what Rachel just pointed out, Martha Raddatz, that this Rich McCormick has won, flipped a seat in Georgia, a Navy veteran and an election denier, still does not believe Joe Biden won the election.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely does not believe that. And you know when you look at this and you look at those numbers of election deniers, you've talked so much, we've all talked about how consequential this election is. That is one of the reasons.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP CANDIDATES WHO QUESTIONED THE 2020 ELECTION\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If you flip the House, if you flip the Senate, that's one thing, but you'll have election deniers in office. That's why Joe Biden talked about democracy so much, why he thinks it's so important.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right now, 552 Republicans for statewide or Congress, 199 fully deny the 2020 results, 61 raised doubts.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've got the Rich McCormick, you've got Don Bolduc in New Hampshire. I know he took it back, but he said in August, "I signed a letter with 120 other generals and admirals saying that Donald Trump won the election and, damn it, I stand by it." That was him in August. The letter he signed said, "Our nation is in deep peril, the conflict is between supporters of socialism and Marxism versus supporters of constitutional freedom and liberty." Those were some pretty strong words. And you've got all these veterans and we talk about these veterans, they're on either side of that, there's a quarter of them, quarter of the election deniers are veterans.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And dare I say, regionally, it depends on where you are and whether or not they're doing well. They did well in this Georgia district that Rachel just reported on. You bring up Don Bolduc. If we could show where New Hampshire stands right now, the Senate race between Maggie Hassan and Don Bolduc as Martha points out, he was an election denier. He acknowledged that that the narrative wasn't working in New Hampshire, eventually saying that Joe Biden did win.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAGGIE HASSAN: 58.1%; DON BOLDUC: 40.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But then, of course, in their final debate, he talked about busloads of voters being brought into New Hampshire illegally. The moderator pressed him and said, where's the evidence of this? He said, I believe it. And Maggie Hassan said, that right there is election denying and this is stoking the big lie. And you have to wonder, with her doing much better than perhaps what people thought going into tonight, whether or not it worked against him in this case.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It may well have because New Hampshire, as you know, is a little fussy about things like this. And he so doubled down on this in the beginning that the Democrats helped fund his race by running ads because they wanted him to win because they thought he would be easier to defeat.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They've been questioned about that heavily.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But they'll say they did the right thing if she pulls it off. I want to bring in back to Terry Moran, who's reported extensively on election deniers in the country. It's just astounding when you look at Rick Klein's board, Terry, at the success of many candidates across the country who to this day say Donald Trump actually won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. And as Martha points out, there are hundreds of candidates at every level. It's become an article of faith, a loyalty test in the Republican Party. And it's really extraordinary. And it's not just a backwards-looking issue because Republicans, majorities of them say they do not trust American elections, which is an extraordinary thing in our history. We fought about elections a lot. The civil war was basically a disagreement started with a disagreement over the presidential election when Lincoln was elected. The white southern voters decided they didn't want anything to do with that. But they didn't say it was fake.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This distrust of the very process that is seeded through Facebook, sometimes by our foreign enemies, is something new in American history. And history shows that, once countries get that kind of cynicism, that kind of dark disbelief of the very institutions, it's very hard to get it out.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I've got to ask Jon Karl about this because, Jon, you spent some time in Arizona, you interviewed Kari Lake, who's doing very well in the polling going into tonight in her race for Arizona governor. She's an election denier, solidly backed by the former President Donald Trump. But Blake Masters, who is running for Senate against Mark Kelly in that state, fairly popular incumbent, got a phone call from Donald Trump saying, you need to do more of what Kari Lake is doing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, and this happened right after they debated, after Masters and Kelly had a debate. And Masters did a little bit of what Bolduc did, where he said he acknowledge that Joe Biden was the legitimately elected President of the United States. That is a sin for Donald Trump. You do not do that. So, he called him up, he said, you can't do that. You've got to, you've got to do what Kari Lake does. And, you know, we'll see how this plays out. But there have been, tonight, Rick alluded to it, there have been issues in the voting in Arizona. They had some issues with the tabulation machines in Maricopa County. And already, Kari Lake is making loud complaints about the way the election is run.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Unfortunately, there has been the one issue. Now, they, officials there have said that they promise every vote will be counted, every vote will be counted. But she, you know, if she loses, she is not going to go quietly. And...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you pressed her, you pressed her a number of times, would she accept the results of tonight's election?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And she fell back on this phrasing that, if they are fair, transparent, and accurate, I will accept them. And I said, what if you g