ar \uc2 GRAPHICS: IS FETTERMAN IN GOOD ENOUGH HEALTH TO REPRESENT STATE?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, it's kind of squeaking it out. And when you look at, he had a stroke in May, there were polls in September that showed him 10% up and that has really gone away.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we have a popular governor who won re-election tonight standing by on the other side of this break. We're going to ask him some questions about where the country is headed next. And we'll talk to Governor Sununu of New Hampshire. He'll also weigh in on that Senate race, Maggie Hassan up against Don Bolduc. He is standing by on the other side of the break. Don't go away. Our whole team right here throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 41; REPUBLICAN: 41\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're watching live coverage here on ABC News, "Election Night 2022." We do have some projections we can make at this hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ILLINOIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAMMY DUCKWORTH, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Let's go to Illinois, the Senate race there, Senator Tammy Duckworth seeking a second term. She returns, of course, she made history, having, giving birth and bringing her newborn to the Senate floor. She returns in Illinois. A combat veteran return as senator.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ILLINOIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: J.B. PRITZKER, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The governor's race there, Governor J.B. Pritzker seeking a second term. He actually traveled the country a bit, he spent a lot of money again on his own race, re-elected there. There's been talk about presidential ambitions on his part. We're also tracking House races that are bellwethers across this country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: OHIO, DISTRICT 9\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARCY KAPTUR: 56.3%; J.R. MAJEWSKI: 43.7%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Remember that key number, Republicans need to pick up just five seats in the House. Republicans spent a lot of money trying to defeat Marcy Kaptur in Ohio, in Ohio 9. At this hour, she's got quite a lead, 56% to 43%. 65% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to get it right to Rachel Scott. Her closing argument in this race, Rachel, was let's meet in the middle, quite an effective ad. She said, we need to stand for the pledge at games, but she also said election deniers is nonsense.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE: ESTIMATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 124; REPUBLICAN 188\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Really casting herself as a moderate, because she's running in a state that former President Donald Trump won twice and her district was actually redrawn, congressional maps redrawn every ten years, running in a more conservative district. Take a look at where things stand right now. Kaptur is the longest serving woman in the House. 56% of the vote right now, she's ahead about 24,000 votes, compared to J.R. Majewski who is someone who was at there on January 6th. We talk about election deniers being on the ballot, this was someone who was at the Capitol on January 6th, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's kind of incredible when you talk about how different the district is that's she's now running in. It went from a significant Democratic lean to a Republican lean and she's still well ahead tonight.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Still well ahead tonight. And we are seeing redistricting really play in a lot of these races. Just look at Florida for example. We talked about the early flips that we saw. Democratic seats that Republicans were able to win. I'm going to draw just right here in (inaudible). I want you to keep your eye on this area, from Tallahassee to Jacksonville. Take a look at what this district looked like in 2020. You see the blue part right here? That was Florida 4. In 2022, you see that congressional map redrawn, redistricting, personally approved by the Republican Governor Ron DeSantis, what it did, is it broke up so many of the black voters across this state, and now it is divided into three different districts, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott with us tonight on the story in the House. Again, historically speaking, the president in party, the one in the White House has a tough midterm election. The question is, how many seats could the Democrats lose. On the other side of the break, we're going to check in with Mary Bruce, who has new reporting from sources up on the Hill. Republican sources, their take up the House, Democratic sources, their take on the Senate. And, of course, that interview with Governor Sununu of New Hampshire, the popular governor who won re-election, though the Senate race in that state has not been decided. And as we talk at length here tonight, this could be another one of those key states with split-ticket voters voting one way for the governor, another way for the Senate. I'm curious to hear his take right after the break. Don't go away. Our live coverage continues in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS LIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT 2022\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening, everyone. I'm Kyra Phillips in our nation's capital on this pivotal midterm election night. So glad you're streaming with us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK KELLY 56.4%; BLAKE MASTERS: 41.3%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We are paying close attention to the key state of Arizona right now as America wants to know which party will control the Senate. Democratic incumbent Senator Mark Kelly facing off with GOP challenger Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATIE HOBBS: 55.5%; KARI LAKE: 44.5%\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the governor's race between Democrat Katie Hobbs and Republican Kari Lake, who told reporters today she'll be the media's, quote, "worst freaking nightmare if elected" while the economy and inflation still a central issue in Arizona, the state seeing some of the highest gas prices in the nation.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's go out west now, where our Whit Johnson is reporting tonight from Arizona. Whit, a bit of a voting snag, shall we say, there in Arizona, fueling a lot of false information today. Explain what happened and where do we stand this hour?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Kyra, that's right. Good evening. Yes, we are getting developing news on this. Maricopa County confirmed they had a number of tabulation machines not working in about 60 different locations. Now, the tabulating machines are different than the voting machines. They say it had something to do with the printer and they were able to fix the issue and they insist that people who showed up there were still able to vote, that they were not disenfranchised, they were not turned away. They had several different options to be able to cast their ballots. They say that they fixed the issue. But Republicans, as a result, actually filed a lawsuit against the county. They quickly assembled a hearing, a judge took a look at the case and dismissed it.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, now, the voting has ended, the polls have closed and we're actually getting the first batch of results here. And it's basically what we expected. We expected a little bit of a blue wave, because the first batch of results were those early ballots, either mail-in ballots or early voting in person. And it's favoring Democrat Senator Mark Kelly and also Katie Hobbs so far. But that is what was expected because they thought that, just like we saw in 2020, you would have a little bit of a blue wave in the beginning and then the Republicans, when you get those election day ballots, would start to catch up later on. So, again, that first batch, don't want to read into it too much, we're watching more as they come in throughout the night, Kyra.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Whit Johnson now following those key races there for us in Arizona. Definitely, Whit, a critical state, too, for the battle of Senate. Let's just characterize that race for a second. What do you think, a tossup?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, absolutely. I mean, Mark Kelly had just the slightest edge in the polls coming into election day, but it was getting closer and closer. Republican Blake Masters definitely had some late momentum. And part of that was from his campaigning with Kari Lake. I mean, you see this crowd here, the Republicans in this late stretch started campaigning together. And Masters, as Lake's star started to rise with the GOP, Masters came along, as well. But still, very close and it's anybody's guess what the result will be.\par \par \uc2 KYRA PHILLIPS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, our Whit Johnson, we'll be talking, of course, throughout the night. Appreciate it so much. We'll keep a close eye on Arizona with you. We're going to send it back to election headquarters in New York in just a minute. We're so glad you have chosen ABC News Live to stream with us tonight. It's a big night in the midterm elections. Stay with us. Much more ahead on "Your Voice, Your Vote 2022."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're watching ABC News live coverage of the midterms 2022, a critical night here in America. Again, we've been reporting all night here. Republicans need a net gain of one seat in the Senate to take back control of the Senate. They need a net gain of five seats to take back control of the House. At this hour, we can project another key race that made national headlines. This is the race for Texas governor.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: TEXAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GREG ABBOTT, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We can now project that Governor Greg Abbott, the incumbent, has been re-elected to serve a third term in Texas. Of course, he was up against the Democrat Beto O'Rourke, who ran for Senate in that state, then ran for governor. Come up short tonight. Greg Abbott will continue as governor of Texas.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOM SHERMAN: 43.9%; CHRIS SUNUNU: 55.2%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As we told you earlier in the state of New Hampshire, the very popular governor there, Chris Sununu has been re-elected to seek for a fourth two-year term. He will now serve that fourth two-year term. And we are lucky to have Governor Sununu here live for our coverage.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor Sununu, welcome. First of all, congratulations. And how do you think the Republicans are doing tonight? You're watching the returns as well. Will they meet expectations tonight?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I think so. I mean, you know, nationally at least, we're looking to, again, take that net one plus seat, maybe it will be one or two. It will be interesting, because I think we all know that we're likely to get lawsuits in maybe Pennsylvania, maybe a runoff in Georgia, maybe lawsuits in Arizona. So, I'm not sure if the dust is all going to settle tonight. But here in New Hampshire, we had a great night. We're doing really, really well. So, as you can see, people are getting excited. We're having a lot of fun. But we still have results to come in. So, the expectation is, did you engage with the voter? Did you listen to them? Did you connect on their level, on their issues, which is energy cost, and fuel and inflation and those types of things? And I think the Republicans did that very well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Governor Sununu, you brought up that net gain of one that the Republicans need in the Senate. I want to ask you about Don Bolduc in the race against Maggie Hassan in your state. You had once called him a conspiracy theorist type, not a serious candidate. You eventually endorsed him in the end. How do you square that for people watching tonight?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Well, look, primaries are all about party, we know that. And we have our favorites and we have it out and we had our primary in September. But one of the things I have to do as governor is always bring that party together. And I can tell you that, while I may have differences with a candidate here and there, getting rid of the Democrat delegation out of New Hampshire is absolutely critical. They just haven't done anything. So, let's put policy aside, they don't show up, they don't debate, they don't have town halls, they don't engage with that retail voter the way they're supposed to. So, I think bringing something fresh and new to the table, you know, we might not agree on every issue, we have our differences, something fresh and new for the state of New Hampshire and for the country is really important.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But you know there's a bigger issue here, Governor Sununu. And you have a national profile. You know that there are nearly 200 election deniers on the ballot, many of them have won tonight. What does that portend for the future in this country? If you're embracing Don Bolduc, who was an election denier, saying that Joe Biden did not win the election and it was only until this fall that he changed course. Are you not at all concerned that there are 200 election deniers on the ballot in this country?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I'll tell you, I think you're wrong by saying that's a bigger issue. That is not a bigger issue.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I didn't say it was a bigger issue. I did say it was a bigger issue, I said it is an issue. And you know that's an issue, you have a national profile. What do you think for the future of this country, is it healthy to have election deniers on the ballot?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Look, obviously the election in 2020 was fair and square and Biden won and we're moving forward from that. I know the press likes to talk a lot about that. And you're right, it isn't helpful. It is because those issues aren't what's connecting with the average voter. So, the more we talk about it, the more we're not connecting with folks on their level about what's important to them. So, you have to have the hearings and figure out what happened, that's just...\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you're celebrating your victory tonight, there's going to be a lot of talk about whether or not you could potentially run for president. I want to ask you about someone else who has said he will announce next week, next Tuesday. You were asked about former President Trump announcing before Christmas and said it was a terrible idea. Why so?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. Well, because once the dust settles on this election, you know what America wants? They want to take a break. Right? They want to get back to watching football games and enjoying Thanksgiving and do some Christmas shopping. We can get to the politics in early '23. So, anyone who's thinking to drive a big political message right after the really insanity, I mean, the craziness of this election cycle, I think is missing the opportunity and missing what is really important to folks. Folks want to take the break. And whether he announces he's running for president or not, it really doesn't matter. It's not going to keep anybody out of the race. It's not going to surprise anybody.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 You know, that secret is the worst kept secret in the world if he is going to announce. So, I think he'll announce. It will be a blip on the radar, and we'll all move forward and enjoy Thanksgiving and the holidays and we'll really get back as a nation to figure out where the presidential stuff goes in, you know, early to mid-'23.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Any chance you'll run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I'm sorry, I couldn't hear you, what was that?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Any chance, yeah, exactly. Any chance you'll run?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I know. Look, my focus, I'm sorry, you're breaking up, I don't know, it's all people are having too much fun.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. Exactly. Those supporters New Hampshire are so loud. I know how it goes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I love New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know you do.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I love the state, I love being governor. And that's my focus, yeah.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. So, once you get through Thanksgiving and Christmas, as you say the former president should do as well. Come the first of the year, you haven't ruled it out? It's in consideration?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 I tell you what, in all sincerity, I've got to, I balance budgets. I know Washington doesn't do that. But I do. And I've got a budget I've got to worry about. I've got an opioid crisis we redesigned to get better results. I've got mental health. Those are the issues that, and that's where all my attention is going to go. And I don't think you're going to see anyone jumping into the presidential race. Everyone's going to have a message. And, look, I think the message needs to be, politics doesn't have to be so divisive. I think it can be a positive message. It's not rainbows and unicorns all the time, but you need a kind of leadership in this country that brings out the best in people, that inspires folks in that positive way. And hopefully, we get a little more of that the further away we get from 2020, and you'll see other candidates emerge. We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll see what happens. And just before I let you go, I just want, you're not ruling it out?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, I'm not even thinking about it. I'm not even thinking about it. So, I don't rule anything in, I don't rule anything out, I don't make promises, I don't break promises. That's just where we go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Governor Sununu, who's been re-elected in the state of New Hampshire, say hello to your supporters their behind you. We sure do appreciate you taking some time for us tonight. I know you're watching the Senate race in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (REP) (NEW HAMPSHIRE)\par \par \uc2 Did I dodge, did I dodge that well enough?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You did, you dodged. You dodged. We'll see you in 2024. Thank you, governor. Thank you, governor. Governor Chris Christie, what do you think of that? I mean, obviously, he's not saying he's not considering it, he's just not ready to go there yet.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, look, Chris is a smart guy and he's been a very good governor. And that's why he won by as much as he won by tonight as early as he did. And so, look, there's going to be this idea that somehow, you know, Donald Trump is going to drive a whole bunch of people out of the race if he announces next week is just silliness, in my view, because anybody who wants to be president, if you, if you're going to not get in because someone else announced, then you probably don't have what it takes to be president to begin with. So, I think Chris is doing the right thing. He just won, he's not going to answer your question because he probably hasn't even talked to his wife about it yet, let alone talked to the people in New Hampshire who just sent him back.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, you know, we talked earlier about Trump and what would happen tonight if some of the candidates he endorsed won and some of them lost. And I said to you, I predicted to you that he would take all the credit if they won and none of the blame if they lost. Here's his quote on TV tonight about tonight's results, "Well, I think if they win I should get all the credit. If they lose, I should not be blamed at all."\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Governor Christie.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I don't know. I think I know the guy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think you do. I think you do. We're going to come back to this conversation, I took notes, 10:37, it took us till 20 minutes to 11:00 here in the east to talk about who is running in 2024. I think we all thought we'd get there a lot sooner than that. But we have a long night ahead. And I want to, ABC News can now project in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: MISSOURI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ERIC SCHMITT, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We're going to show this in the Missouri Senate race, Eric Schmitt is the projected winner, 54% of the vote. And ABC News now projecting Eric Schmitt will win in the Senate race in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to get right over to Rick Klein at the big board on Arizona, because this is another race that the country has been watching, not only Mark Kelly against Blake Masters, but Kari Lake up against Katie Hobbs in the race for governor. Let's take a look at we know so far.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KELLY: 56.1%; MASTERS: 41.5%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARICOPA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 59.1%; MASTERS: 38.6%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, just starting in the Senate race. I mean, Arizona's geography actually isn't hard to learn, because mostly it's Maricopa County that is most of the, that is most of the state's population. And right now, you're seeing Mark Kelly racking up big numbers there, which is in line with what he needs to do in the benchmark. He's a bunch, a couple of points ahead of that right now. But we're seeing very little of the election day vote. We've got nothing at all from Tucson down to the south. That is where Mark Kelly is from. Of course, his wife Gabby Giffords represented Tucson, in the Tucson area, in Congress.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOBBS: 55.1%; LAKE: 44.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Switching over the governor's race, which, as you mentioned, Kari Lake is the Republican gubernatorial candidate, you're seeing Katie Hobbs with a lead. Now, I'll tell you, a 10- or 11-point lead for a Democrat in Arizona in early returns doesn't really tell you much because there are so much day of vote that's out there, another state where we talk about a blue mirage. We are very much keyed in to the possibility of seeing these early returns.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARICOPA COUNTY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOBBS: 58.2%; LAKE: 41.8%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But again, racking up big votes in the early vote in Maricopa County, more than hitting the benchmarks right now, but it is very early in the counting.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's take us through, because this has such interest across the country, this particular race, because you have two very different candidates being considered, really, in both races, both for the Senate and for the governor. When you look at how Joe Biden performed since we know he won the state, narrowly but won, how is Katie Hobbs in Maricopa, for example, doing up against Biden versus the benchmark that she needs to hit to win tonight?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you see her ahead of both of those things. Look, Maricopa County used to be the heart of John McCain country. He is winning Maricopa County by 15 points late in his career. So, the fact that Democrats were able to turn that around and that Joe Biden was able to win the biggest county in the state in 2020 was a very big deal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. On the flip side, Kari Lake, how did Trump do last time around? How is she doing compared to the benchmark of what she would need to do at this point tonight in Maricopa County?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, basically, as a Republican in the biggest county in Arizona, you got to run even in Maricopa County. She's not there right now. But again, what we need to be keyed into is the vote that's still outstanding. Most of the day-of vote, the people that are actually standing in line and actually voting on election day, that is still out, even though Arizona, most of the vote actually is early, we're getting just about no vote of the people that voted today.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, let's go back to the Senate race, Mark Kelly versus Blake Masters. I know it's very early but, again, I think it's helpful for people to see how they're, how Mark Kelly is doing up against, you know, Joe Biden's performance, how he's doing against his own benchmark and likewise, Blake Masters with Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, you see Maricopa County, you see he's up about 10 points.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KELLY: 56.1%; MASTERS: 41.5%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COCONINO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KELLY: 69.5%; MASTERS: 28.2%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If you want to take a more rural county up in northern Arizona, the home of the lumberjacks, out in, in Flagstaff, up about nine points over the benchmark. That's a decent margin, even though we don't have that much vote in yet. And again, nothing in Tucson, which is where Mark Kelly is from.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. So, it's very early there, but a lot of attention being paid to this race. We're going to come back to it. In the meantime, we're going to go to Arizona. Whit Johnson is covering both of these races for us. And, Whit, what are the campaigns saying tonight about how they feel going into the night?\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, David, I could tell you, here at Republican headquarters, they are very energized. And we've seen this in recent days, where most of the Republican ticket has been campaigning in one place. I mean, there are hundreds of people here behind me. They also have an overflow area. But, David, really what we've seen in the final days is a campaign of fear on both sides. Republicans saying that Democrats are destroying the country and you need to vote like your lives depend on it, like the lives of your children depend on it. Democrats saying that democracy itself is on the ballot. And what we've seen is this barrage of campaign ads. The candidates really trying to just scare the votes out of people, saying a vote for the other side, warning of dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, David, I do want to go back to something that was just noted earlier this evening. The tabulation issues in Maricopa County. It's worth talking about here, because the county officials confirmed that they did have these issues at 60 different locations. They say that it was related to problems with the printer. They were able to fix that and they insist that no voters were turned away, that they had multiple voting options to be able to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARK KELLY: 58.1%; BLAKE MASTERS: 39.6%\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But as a result of this, the Republicans actually filed a lawsuit. They quickly assembled a hearing. The Republicans were asking for an extended voting period tonight. A judge took a look at this and actually denied the suit. So, in the end, the polls closed, we saw that first batch of results coming out. But so much at stake, both for the Republicans and the Democrats in this race, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whit, excellent. We'll come back to you shortly here. And, Mark, if we could put up the slates for both the Senate race and the governor's race. First, the Senate, Mark Kelly. This is with 51% of the vote in, 51% of the vote in now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ARIZONA AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNOR: KATIE HOBBS, 57.0%; KARI LAKE, 42.9%\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mark Kelly leading 58% to 39% for Blake Masters. Again, this is Maricopa County. This is also the mail-in vote that they count first in Arizona, which, in the past, historically has leaned Democratic. And in the governor's race, Katie Hobbs, 57 %, with 50% of the vote in, to Kari Lake's 42.9%. Again, it's early. But, Jon, this is still attention grabbing.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. But very important note of caution here as we look at these results. It looks like Katie Hobbs is really building up a lead over Kari Lake. It looks like Mark Kelly's really building up a lead over Blake Masters. It also looks, by the way, like Mark Finchum, the person, another person who was at the Capitol on January 6th, also losing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Secretary of state, right?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Actually running for secretary of state is also losing from a slightly bigger margin. But, look, they count the early mail-in vote first in Arizona. And there was a big emphasis in this race by a lot of the Republicans, especially the pro-Trump Republicans to say, we are going to vote on election day, we don't trust mail-in voting. So, there's going to be a lot more Republican votes to be counted in Arizona. But even so, David, this is going to become a very interesting race to watch over the course of the night, because you are seeing a significant lead with a significant chunk of the vote in, over 50% of the vote in, for the Democrats. But don't bank it yet. There is certainly a bit of a blue mirage here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Kari Lake told you in that interview, they call it election day for a reason. She was encouraging day-of voting. Was she not?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, in 2020, 91% of Arizona voters voted early by mail. In the primaries in August, 85%. This includes the majority of Republicans. But Kari Lake's position is there shouldn't be that kind of early voting. That you should vote on election day and that you should have your results by election night.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, we also have news coming in, Jon, thank you. Also, has news coming in on two key House races tonight. Again, Republicans need a net gain of five. These are two, one is a flip, one is a Democratic victory. We're going to dive into both of them with Rachel Scott. She's standing by at the board right after the break. Our live coverage of "Election Night 2022."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE AT THIS HOUR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMOCRAT: 41; REPUBLICAN: 42\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And, again, those numbers from Arizona are going to be very interesting to watch over the course of the next hour. The race for governor, the race for Senate there and what these numbers mean. They're early. And it's great that Jon points out, it's the mail-in vote they count first, so we have to be very cautious with those numbers. We'll be right back after the break. Don't go away.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Welcome back to ABC News live coverage, now about ten minutes to 11:00 in the east. We continue to watch this crucial race in New Hampshire. The Senate race, Maggie Hassan and Don Bolduc. As soon as we have new numbers on that, we'll bring it to you. We can project at this hour, ABC News can project that, in Iowa, in the Senate race there, Chuck Grassley has been re-elected, the incumbent there.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: IOWA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHUCK GRASSLEY, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He'll serve an eighth term as senator in the state of Iowa. He's been up against retired Navy Admiral Mike Frank, the Democrat in that race. There was some hope for the Democrats in Iowa. But in the end, Chuck Grassley headed back to the Senate. First elected, first elected to Congress 60 years ago, 40 years ago when it comes to the Senate. Mark, can we go with this? Okay, we have breaking news in New Hampshire. The Senate race, ABC News can now project that Maggie Hassan, the incumbent in New Hampshire, has won re-election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAGGIE HASSAN, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Again, this is the race we've been watching tonight, didn't get a lot of attention going in. She won by 1,000 votes in 2016, tonight, that margin much greater. Maggie Hassan, the incumbent in New Hampshire, will go back to the Senate. And if you remember, Nate Silver at the board earlier, Nate, now that we have Colorado and New Hampshire, suddenly, we're in an even ball game between the Democrats and the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 NATE SILVER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this is just what we looked at earlier, it is a jump ball right now, 51% Democrat, 49% GOP.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This is really incredible. And, Rick, can we dive into New Hampshire? How did she performing up against, say, Biden and against the benchmark that she needed to perform?\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, I'm looking here at the Manchester national area, because that's an area where a Republican basically has to run even. And guess what? That is not what Don Bolduc was able to do. Maggie Hassan opening a pretty significant lead. Same thing there in the southeastern part of the state. That is, that is an area where a Republican needs to win, period, full stop. It has not happened. Donald Trump came a lot closer to Joe Biden, again, in a state he lost. And I'll tell you, this is going to be on Trump, because in a non-Trump world, it's possible that Governor Sununu, who we just had on our air, runs for this seat or someone else gets nominated who has a much better chance.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Basically, the Republican establishment cut Don Bolduc loose. MAGA tried to save him and it doesn't appear like their efforts are going to be enough. And this will be seen as a Republican possibility that was left on the table.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we were reporting this since early in the night. Remember, Don Bolduc is the candidate who said that Joe Biden didn't win over the summer. It wasn't until after Labor Day at some point that he said he did win. He also, in their final debate, the moderator had to step in, because he said they were bringing busloads of illegal voters into New Hampshire. The moderator said, do you have evidence. He said, I believe it. I believe it. And Maggie Hassan said, there you have it right there, that's election denialism. He's stoking the big lie. Martha Raddatz, you covered New England politics for many years, so did Rick Klein, by the way. And...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Fellow New Englanders here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This might be a state where they just didn't buy that flipping in such an important issue. And you heard Governor Sununu there, we didn't say it's the most important issue, but even he acknowledged that voters do care about the truth.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And once you heard how strong a statement he made, you know, let me read it again. "I signed a letter with 120 other generals and admirals saying that Donald Trump won the election. And damn it, I stand by it."\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And then shortly after that, he says, no, wait a minute, I don't. And he said, I've done more research. But I also think we have to look at what Maggie Hassan talked about abortion a lot. She really focused on abortion. And Bolduc said, on her focus on abortion, because he said he is anti-choice, your views are not consistent with the average Granite Stater, number one. Number two, get over it. This is about the economy.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As we've said, the economy is a big, big deal, but abortion clearly played a role as well.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we have exits out of New Hampshire, which actually underscore the importance of some of the issues in New Hampshire. And Maggie Hassan, any time she was interviewed in this race, she talked about the economy, pocketbook issues. And going into the winter, I didn't miss an interview where she talked about heating oil, home heating oil. These were issues that hit home, in many cases, not talking about what the rest, sort of the national discourse was.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly. She hit on everything she needed to hit on during this race.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you have, Linsey?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And if you look at the exit polls here, this is really significant. Take a look at the moderate voter. She actually outperformed herself from the election in 2016 by 12 points.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW HAMPSHIRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLITICAL LEANING, MODERATE - 42%\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She won moderates by 27 points in this election. This is somebody who obviously prides herself on being one of the most moderate senators that there is, and it looks like people are willing to take her back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mary Bruce has been talking with sources up on the Capitol Hill at the White House, Republicans and Democrats. You were telling me during the break that Democrats suddenly think they might be back in the game to try to hold on to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are feeling pretty good right now, optimistic that that massive red tsunami, that wave that they were fearful was going to knock them out, that's not what you're seeing. Now, based on what you're seeing in Colorado and New Hampshire, they are much still in this game. And sources also, on the flip side, on the Republican side, sources in the House, sources close to Kevin McCarthy and his inner circle are saying that they are also feeling good, based, especially on what they saw down in Florida, on the margins they were winning by down there. And just to return to what you were talking about in New Hampshire there for a second and the importance of abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We were getting at this earlier that, even though poling had shown that perhaps abortion had ticked down in some polls, that the economy was really top of mind for most voters, I think there's a question among Democrats that I talked to about whether that's the concern, the importance that voters put on abortion is actually underreported. That people don't respond to pollsters necessarily in the same way that they will on the economy on an issue as personal as abortion and you might be seeing that.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) With just a few moments left before the break, let's get to MaryAlice Parks who's live in New Hampshire, because MaryAlice made that same point, Mary, that abortion rights, even though perhaps it had been quiet, a bit quieter on that front as far as being a top-of-mind issue, was a significant issue for voters in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, absolutely, David. And like you guys were talking about, Senator Hassan leaned into that issue in the last few days. She kept talking about this as an issue of freedom and that played well up here.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, the other issue that you were just mentioning, the cost of energy, home heating oil being double just in the last two years. Senator Hassan would go out there on the trail and she would say, I know the cost is too high. But she stood by Democrats' investments in renewable energy. She said she still wanted to take on big oil.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She basically tried to say she was hearing voters' pains, but she didn't believe that the answer was just a green light to more drilling. But this was a really interesting point on energy. She stood by her guns. And she, like you said, likes to be seen as a bipartisan senator, not a flashy one, not a show horse, but a workhorse. And she was, you know, one block at a time, one day at a time convinced that she would pull this out in the end, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) MaryAlice Parks in New Hampshire. And as you heard Mary Bruce said from her sources on the Hill, they're relieved that they're not seeing, big picture here, a red wave at this point. And certainly if Maggie Hassan holds on, that is something the White House is going to cheer about. And you've learned that President Biden, we don't know if he's called her, but he is calling the winners tonight.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He's calling a number of winners. We know he's been watching the returns from the White House, no surprise there. But he's put out a lot of calls to supporters, Maryland, Colorado, Illinois governor, Virginia, and he's just making the calls. And look, this is a person with a long-standing friendship with a lot of these, but a little bit of confidence here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you hear that music. We hear you and we hear the music, too. J.D. Vance in Ohio, has won in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: OHIO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: J.D. VANCE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He will replace Rob Portman, the Republican. That's a hold in Ohio. J.D. Vance beats Tim Ryan in the Senate race in Ohio. Boy, we have a lot to talk about. It's now coming up on 11:00 in the east. So, a lot of breaking news happening just in the last couple of minutes. J.D. Vance beats Tim Ryan in Ohio, a state that has gone Republican, obviously, in the last couple of presidential cycles. A lot of questions about whether Ohio is ground for Democrats, just like Florida.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The conversation we had earlier tonight, how quickly they called Florida for the Republicans, the strength of the Republican Party in Florida. And, of course, the other, the opposite headline is Maggie Hassan, the Democrat in New Hampshire holding on. She goes back to the Senate. We'll be back.\par \par \uc2 Document ABSR000020221121eib800002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELECTION NIGHT 2022 FLORIDA RACES\b0\par\par\uc2 466 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, CONGRESS, FETTERMAN, OZ\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA, WARNOCK, WALKER, KEMP, ABRAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRIAN KEMP, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 ARIZONA, LAKE, HOBBS, KELLY, MASTERS\par \par \uc2 MICHIGAN, HOCHUL, WHITMER, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RON DESANTIS, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KATHY HOCHUL, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We turn now to Florida where Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is projected to handily win a second term. And in New York, Kathy Hochul took over after \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  resigned, is projected to become the first woman elected to lead that state. In Michigan, incumbent \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is projected to win reelection after a hard-fought race for governor in Michigan. Abortion was also on the ballot in Michigan. My "Nightline" co-anchor Juju Chang is there. Juju?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening to you, Byron. We've been listening to our share of Motown here at the Motor City Casino in Detroit. It's \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 's election night headquarters. And she just took to the stage to say that she's feeling damn good about tonight. There's been a blue wave throughout Michigan that's been a big night for Governor Whitmer. And Proposal 3, which was codifying abortion into the ballot, also passed.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's lifting the tide of Democratic candidates all down ballot here in Michigan. Unlike across the country where inflation, pocketbook issues like gas prices and the economy, was top of mind. Here in Michigan, it was one of the few states where abortion was the number one topic on voters' minds. And tonight here in Michigan, it seems that abortion has played a heavy role and may be perhaps providing a roadmap for Democrats moving forward. Byron?\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju, thanks so much and safe travels home back to New York. And before we go, a little bit of history made tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS, YOUR VOICE YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAXWELL FROST, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAURA HEALEY, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WES MOORE, PROJECTED WINNER\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For the first time ever, a Gen Z'er is projected to win a seat in the House. Twenty-five-year-old Florida Democrat and progressive activist Maxwell Frost, who represents Florida's 10th district. ABC News projects Massachusetts Democrat Maurey (sic), \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  will be the first openly gay woman elected governor of that state. And in Maryland, Democrat Wes Moore is projected to win the governor's race. Moore will be the first person of color to lead that state and only the third black governor in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE, YOUR VOICE YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELECTION NIGHT\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "Nightline," "Your Voice, Your Vote: Election 2022" continues. Here again, \uc2 Byron Pitts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221118eib80002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MIDTERM SHOWDOWN\b0\par\par\uc2 2,134 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, WEBER, WHITMER, DIXON, ABRAMS, DILLAHA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us on election eve. We begin with what could be the most consequential midterm election in recent history, with control of Congress up for grabs. Republicans pressing kitchen table issues like the economy and crime. Democrats saying democracy is on the line. We're on the ground in Michigan, a critical battleground state, talking to voters about what issues matter to them most. Here's my "Nightline" co-anchor Juju Chang.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah, the big hair, the '80s.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That hair is good.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Everyone had that hairstyle.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Erin Berkeley Weber does not miss her '80s hair.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So cute.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 We're all so young, but, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the commitment she's made to voting since she was 18 has stuck with her, just like her famous classmate a few yearbook pages away.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 She looks the same.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) She does look the same. She's very, very Whitmeresque.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I mean.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And while Erin served on the student council with Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , their political ideologies haven't always aligned.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I would say I'm traditionally more conservative, but not 100%.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You voted for President Trump?\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I did.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Twice?\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Twice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When Governor Whitmer ran the first time, did you vote for her?\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 No, I did not. I didn't feel, I wasn't necessarily confident in what she could do.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But this year the mom of three boys, including golden retriever Dexter, says she's skewing the other way. The Supreme Court decision on abortion changing her perspective.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I haven't known life growing up without Roe V. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Without the right?\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 Without the right to have an abortion. Seek it out if that's something that's right for me. I don't want my future potential granddaughters or my sons' friends who are women to all of a sudden have to seek procedures illegally, and potentially with very dangerous after-effects.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Erin is one of a growing number of suburban women across the country energized and galvanized this midterm election season because of abortion.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We saw an uptick in women registering to vote for the first time that seemed to outpace men. And you can tie it almost directly down to the week to the Dobbs decision overturning Roe V. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Michigan, women are out registering men by a margin of 8%. And Democrats are out registering Republicans by 18. Across the country, Democrats have centered their message on issues like reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (DEM) (GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 It is a decision that should be made between a doctor and a woman. It should only be a doctor and a woman, not a politician.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While Republicans focus on pocketbook issues, inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE ((REP)\par \par \uc2 I think the concern of women voters are obviously similar to a lot of men voters too. They're worried about the price of groceries. They're worried about the price of gas.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With election day tomorrow, pundits wonder if the post Dobbs energy from this summer is enough to push Democrats over the top. Polls show the ailing economy remains a top priority for most voters. Abortion trailing behind by 18 points.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My top three would be the economy, inflation, and probably crime and safety. Abortion doesn't even rank in my top five.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't like these high gas prices. I don't like the high food prices.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 The economy is the big, the big mover.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You don't drive past abortion clinics the same way you do gas stations. And the fact is that that matters. The one thing that hits people just about every day no matter what they do, it's price of just living your life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many political pundits say well, it's the economy, stupid. That the economy is top of mind and that this is a mistake in, you know, political strategy moving into the midterm.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 I think that's a false choice, frankly. The fact of the matter is the most important and profound economic decision a woman will make in her lifetime is whether and when to have a child. It is absolutely an economic decision. It's part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So you're saying abortion rights and the economy are not mutually exclusive issues?\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely correct.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's been front and center in Michigan's Governor's race between Whitmer and conservative firebrand Tudor Dixon.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (REP) (MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I am pro-life with exceptions for the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 I am fighting to protect our right to choose.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Here in Michigan abortion is actually on the ballot with proposal 3, which if passed would amend the state's constitution to protect abortion access.\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 Michigan is one of the states that is poised to go from being a pro-choice state to having one of the most extreme laws on the books. We have a 1931 law that makes abortion a felony, no exceptions for rape or incest, and criminalizes doctors and nurses.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four other states have abortion-related measures on the ballot this election day.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Michigan is a true battleground. You don't know where it's going to land. It has some of the closest elects in the nation. And the thought in a lot of circles is that this is going to be kind of a template.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The battle to get prop 3 passed is being waged in informal gatherings like at this barn in suburban Grand Rapids.\par \par \uc2 KELLY DILLAHA (DIRECTOR RED WINE & BLUE)\par \par \uc2 What will happen if we do not pass proposition 3, if we do not pass reproductive rights?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kelly Dillaha works for the group Red Wine & Blue. Through wine nights like this one, they're trying to reach 100,000 voters across the country by having women tap into their personal networks, what Kelly calls relational organizing.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is relational organizing different or perhaps better than canvassing or door knocking?\par \par \uc2 KELLY DILLAHA (DIRECTOR RED WINE & BLUE)\par \par \uc2 We find that if you are knocking on doors, you would only probably have about a 17%, 18% chance of getting someone to open the door. But when we call our friends and our family, we have about an 80% chance of people answering, and that's so much more effective. And that's why we encourage people to call their friends and family and neighbors.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But there are plenty of friends and neighbors opposing prop 3 as well. Millions of dollars being spent on yards signs, giant billboards and TV ads.\par \par \uc2 VOICEOVER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Proposal 3 permanently rewrites your constitution with extreme ideas.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is it dangerous?\par \par \uc2 STEVE CARRA (REP) (MICHIGAN STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 Because once you amend the constitution, it's very difficult to change what we have once we have it in the Michigan constitution.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) State Representative Steve Carra introduced Michigan House Bill 6270 just two days before the fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 STEVE CARRA (REP) (MICHIGAN STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 I believe life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If his bill passes, providers could be arrested on manslaughter charges and serve up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There are women across Michigan and across the country who are wondering why a lawmaker like you should be able to dictate what a woman does with her body?\par \par \uc2 STEVE CARRA (REP) (MICHIGAN STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 A women's right to do what?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A women's right for health care.\par \par \uc2 STEVE CARRA (REP) (MICHIGAN STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 So what is that health care doing with the pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Making a health care decision based on what she and her doctor are saying.\par \par \uc2 STEVE CARRA (REP) (MICHIGAN STATE REP)\par \par \uc2 Which terminates a life. We don't have the right to go to the bank and steal from a bank. Our rights stop when they infringe on somebody else's rights. So a woman believing that she has a right to take an innocent life, that is terminating a pregnancy, which is also killing an innocent child.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Folks that I was talking to, you know, their sincere belief is that life begins at conception. And that in their view, any kind of abortion is murder. And if you believe that, you have to do everything you can to stop abortion. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 GOV \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 I will fight for everybody's right to believe whatever they want to. But someone else, someone else's beliefs should not dictate everyone else's rights.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (REP) (MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 On November 9th, it will be a new day in the state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Whitmer's opponent, Tudor Dixon, is a former steel industry executive turned media personality, who's built her campaign on far right issues like questioning the 2020 election results and book bans.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (REP) (MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 There are a heck of a lot of parents in the state of Michigan right now that aren't happy with the pornographic material in our schools.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She did not respond to "Nightline's" request for an interview.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tudor Dixon is kind of out of the new Maga mold of conservative stardom. No political experience of any note. And if she's able to win, it's going to be because of the Trump base behind her.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The latest polling shows Dixon and Whitmer in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 The polling location just changed a couple of years ago to this church here.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Come Tuesday, Erin says she is eager to vote, no matter the outcome.\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I always feel great when I come out of voting. I feel I've done my duty as an American. I've done my duty as a citizen.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also motivating Erin, her 80-year-old mother, a retired nurse who says she's voting for a Democrat for the first time in her life.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN BERKELEY (MOTHER OF ERIN)\par \par \uc2 I've seen enough. I'm old enough. Whether it passes or not, there will always be abortion. If it passes, we will have clean, sterile, safe abortions. If it doesn't, you can still get an abortion, it will be in the back seat of a car with knitting needles and you'll die.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If prop 3 fails, how would you react? How would you respond?\par \par \uc2 ERIN BERKELEY WEBER (MICHIGAN VOTER)\par \par \uc2 I would be really disappointed and sad. What does that mean for my friends who have daughters who are living in this state right now? And what if something happened to them? I can't predict which way the vote is going to go. I can only hope that it passes.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Juju. Stay with ABC News for a live election night coverage tomorrow night. It all starts at 8:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AARON CARTER\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, Aaron Carter. Remembering the late singer after his tragic death.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221109eib700002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BALLOT INITIATIVES VOTERS DECIDING ON HOT-BUTTON ISSUES\b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, ZELDIN, HOCHUL, OBAMA, TRUMP, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, WALKER, WARNOCK\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, LAXALT, CORTEZ MASTO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLLS SUGGEST REPUBLICAN FAVORED IN SENATE RACE IN NEVADA\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, SECURITY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONCERNS ABOUT POSSIBLE POLITICAL VIOLENCE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, POLLS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS POLL: RAZOR THIN CONGRESSIONAL MARGINS\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, IRAN, WAR, WEAPONS, DRONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IRAN ADMITTING THEY SUPPLIED RUSSIA WITH LETHAL DRONES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ZELENSKYY SAYS "WE SHOOT DOWN AT LEAST TEN IRANIAN DRONES EVERYDAY"\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES, BAKER, PE\u209\'00\'D1A, HOUSTON\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, JACKPOT, LOTTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NO WINNERS FOR 1.6 BILLION POWERBALL PRIZE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JACKPOT TO RISE FOR NEXT DRAWING ON MONDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 CARTER, INVESTIGATION, LIPSHUTZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REMEMBERING AARON CARTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER TEEN IDOL FOUND DEAD IN CALIFORNIA HOME\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, COLLAZO, BRUNSON, GIRALDO\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, BALLOT INITIATIVES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's not just names you'll see on the ballot. Voters in a number of states will decide on some of the biggest issues through ballot initiatives.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BALLOT INITIATIVES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOTERS DECIDING ON HOT-BUTTON ISSUES\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our political director Rick Klein is back at the midterm monitor. All right, Rick, so we know that abortion rights are big but definitely a major voting issue in some places.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Janai, after the Roe v. Wade decision was overturned by the Supreme Court, abortion rights are essentially on the ballot everywhere. But they are literally on the ballot in five states voting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In Kentucky, voters are being asked whether they want to explicitly say there isn't a right to an abortion under the constitution. In Montana, they're looking to potentially impose penalties on anyone who doesn't try to save the life of a fetus, that even that is aborted. Meanwhile, three states, California, Vermont and Michigan, are all voting to establish potentially a right to an abortion under the state constitution. Michigan, in particular, very closely watched because they have a close governor's race as well.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we remember how big that was in Kansas when it was put on the ballot. And now, Rick, other big issues come Tuesday, marijuana and gun control. Those are also on the line.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MARIJUANA ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, these are the states, five of them, that are deciding on whether to legalize recreational marijuana. Of course, that's happening in states across the country despite the federal ban that's still in place. It will be interesting to see how that develops because most states are moving in that direction. And on gun rights, Janai, this is interesting.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GUN REGULATION ON THE BALLOT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The state of Iowa is looking to make it harder to impose any new restrictions on gun laws and Oregon might go in the opposite direction. They're looking to impose requirements around background checks, around permitting, around even firearms training for anyone who is looking to purchase a gun.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So many of those issues we've heard so much about this year. Rick, we will all be watching. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHALLENGING CONDITIONS\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And still coming up here on "Good Morning America," the challenging conditions for runners at today's New York City marathon. We like the heat, but they'll be pounding the pavement toward the finish line. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eib6000jv\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 VOTE, GEORGIA, POLL, SENATE, WARNOCK, WALKER\par \par \uc2 2022, PENNSYLVANIA, SENATE, FETTERMAN, OZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PA\par \par \uc2 NEVADA, ARIZONA, RACE, LAXALT, KELLY, LAKE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, MONITOR, POLL, BIDEN, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2 DAYS UNTIL ELECTION DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S PRE-MIDTERM APPROVAL RATING MIRRORS TRUMP'S IN 2018\par \par \uc2 SILVER, FORECAST, MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 BOOKER, MIDTERMS, SENATE, 538, BIDEN, ISSUES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS/YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 538: GOP SLIGHTLY FAVORED TO WIN CONTROL OF SENATE\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN, 2022, GOP, BIDEN, TRUMP, 2024\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VA GOV CAMPAIGNS FOR GOP CANDIDATES ACROSS COUNTRY\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, BIDEN, WALKER, TRUMP, 2024\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Three days until one of the most important elections. The outcome is gonna shape our country for decades to come, and the power to shape that outcome is in your hands.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 If you want to stop the destruction of our country and save the American dream, then this Tuesday, you must vote Republican in a giant red wave.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Biden and former President Trump both in Pennsylvania this weekend making their final arguments. The Roundtable is here to make their own final arguments, former DNC Chair Donna Brazile, former Trump Justice Department spokeswoman \uc2 Sarah Isgur\uc2 , \uc2 Democracy for America\uc2 's CEO, Yvette Simpson, and former New Jersey Governor Chris Christie. Welcome to you all. And here we are down to the wire. And Donna, I want to start with you. The economy far and away the top issue.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: 43% WORSE OFF FINANCIALLY THAN 2 YEARS AGO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC NEWS/YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our ABC News/\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll, 43 percent of Americans say they're now worse off financially in the last two years, that is pretty astounding. Why has that not been the number one issue on the campaign? You even heard Cory Booker this morning not talk about that essentially, not focus on that.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY'S FINANCIAL SITUATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BETTER 18%, SAME 39%, WORSE 43%\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you know, Martha, so you have to take a look at the entire country, because I do believe that whether you're talking about abortion, which is an economic, an economic issue, and why? Because when you are pregnant, and I haven't been, so I'll defer to those who've been pregnant and have to, that's an economic issue. You have to go to the doctor, you've got to have health care. So these issues are on the table and, by the way, the Democrats have addressed them in Congress where it matters and trying to get the supply chain back, back in line, make sure we have baby formula.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So it is an issue, Democrats understand that, but this is a very, very serious election. We're not just talking about the economy and inflation and crime and immigration, abortion, voting rights, but we're also talking about what took place on January 21st. I have not been able to put this in my recycle bin. You know why? Because the same people who gave us this are now on the ballot in all 50 states. So, while we're talking about kitchen table issues, which are very important, we also got to talk about democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is that the right strategy, Chris Christie?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, and they're gonna lose. I mean, look, when, when you don't have anything to say about the issues that the people care most about, what you try to do is set a fire someplace else and distract them. But it's not working, and, and it won't work. The fact is when people go into a supermarket...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But talk about that, people do care about democracy. They do.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: ECONOMY, INFLATION TOP ISSUES FOR VOTERS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I didn't say that. Look, they don't care as much as they care about the other things and the, all the polling shows that. And I'm not splitting the atom here, okay? All of the polling shows that what people care about the most right now is the economy and inflation. That's what they care the most about. And so in, in the end, listen, I - listen, I admire Donna. She brought props this morning. She's trying to do everything she can to distract people from what is the issue, which is they cannot afford to go to the supermarket. They can't afford to go to the gas station. They cannot afford to pay their bills, and when that happens, everything else goes to second place.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Republicans have no answer for that. The reality is...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll see.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...is if you're bad off now, if you can't buy gas, if you can't buy groceries, you're gonna be worse if Republicans win. They have no solution. And I think you're right. I think Elissa Slotkin is right. We should be messaging on the economy. The economy is better when Democrats are in leadership and Republicans only care...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you kind of agree with Chris there?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, you do. Say yes.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...I think we should be talking about it. I disagree that his party is in a better position to take care of it. And we should say that. We should say, what are Republicans gonna do? They don't have to answer the question what they're gonna do. They're talking about ending Social Security. They're gonna make it worse. So, to everybody out here who is worried about the economy, no. You might be bad off, but you'll be worse if Republicans win the House or the Senate, period.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you think of the strategy, Sarah? What Donna just talked about?\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't understand why Democrats didn't run from the beginning saying, we're talking about the economy. The Republicans are the ones passing these laws in the states on abortion. Why they ceded that whole ground to the Republican Party. And, look on the democracy issue, yes, you look, a majority of Americans think democracy is under a threat. Eighty percent think that if the opposition comes into power, that America as we know it will be under threat.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC/WAPO POLL: ABORTION TRAILS ECONOMY AS TOP ISSUE\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But when you ask them which party is the greater threat, independent voters by about seven points think it's the Democratic Party, not the Republican Party. So, while Joe Biden is talking about how democracy is on the ballot, I don't know that his message is landing the way he thinks it's landing with these voters who are going to decide the election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN WARNS VOTERS: 'DEMOCRACY IS ON THE BALLOT'\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Donna, those are incredible statistics from that poll.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That, that each side thinks the other is responsible for it. Where I think that's an issue that Democrats thought was all theirs.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, look, look, I'm not running campaigns anymore. I run my mouth on television. Which, you know, sadly I can't do a lot about what's going to take place over the next two days, but I want to ask the American people, who's on your side?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Who's out there fighting for you? Who's out there trying to lower your prescription drug prices? Who's out there trying to make sure that you get a raise in minimum wage so that you can afford the groceries and the gas when you go, when you leave your house? The Republicans refuse...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They don't think that's Joe Biden, though, because of everything he's done on energy.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no. The Republicans refuse...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's not a confusing message.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...they refuse to raise the minimum wage. It is poor people and low income people who are being squeezed.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because inflation has raised the minimum wage 'cause now you have to (inaudible) $20 to the grocery store checkout line.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, what, what drives inflation? It's not just what, who's in Washington, DC. We're being, this inflation is being driven by huge demand at a time we had two years of an economic lockdown and we need to make sure that the...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know what?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think Chris...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Listen, I'll take, I'll take Larry, I'll, I'll Larry Summers' word for it, okay? Larry Summers, Clinton's Treasury Secretary, told the Biden administration two years ago, you go ahead with the spending you're talking about and you are gonna create enormous inflation, and it's exactly what happened. And we can try to blame it on a whole bunch of other things, but when you put $5 trillion that you printed into the economy after all the money that we put in during COVID, that's why you have inflation. And you know, the fact is, though, it's got to stop at some point. And the Democrats don't wanna talk about that because their constituencies are all about, pay me more.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the end...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...in the end, Sarah's right, that they ceded this ground to Republicans because they knew that Joe Biden couldn't articulate the argument as to why he was better. That's why they have him Union Station talking about democracy. Could you go to a safer, larger Democratic place than the middle of the District of Columbia, send him over to Union Station and put him back in the White House before he causes more harm, or send him to Scranton, which is the only other place in the country he can go.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yvette, in the earlier in the year, Donna said that abortion would be a defining issue in the fall.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It has fallen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEMS HOPEFUL ROE REVERSAL WILL GALVANIZE VOTERS\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Republicans are running away from it. We didn't anticipate that, right? So they realize this was a problem. They're don't, they're not wanting ads on abortion. They're not, they're not talking about abortion on the debate stage. They're avoiding it. They realized very quickly and they retreated, and we let them, and I think we just have to continue to remind people that a 10-year-old girl having to carry her rapist's child to, to birth is inhumane, and it's wrong, and that's what's happening.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Democrats...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And women who...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Democrats misread this moment.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No...\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was never about abortion.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...no, Republicans are running from it.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Dobbs happened at the same time that gas prices went down. You can track the Democrats' poll numbers going up and down with gas prices. It's not that abortion faded. It's that there's no particular evidence it was ever going to be a meaningful issue when we had already sorted ourselves over 20 years of this...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Show me, if it's not (inaudible), then show me one Republican that's talking about it...\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, first of all, abortion's on the...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why, they're talking about the economy.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The vote, wait, wait a second. Why would Republicans, why would...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're running from it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no, Republicans made the decision to do something unique. If you actually want to win elections.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If they're proud of it, they should talk about it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, no. No, no, let me finish now. If you want to win elections, you talk about the things that the voters care about. And you look at this polling, and what they care about is economy, inflation, crime. That's what they care about, and that's why Republican candidates are resonating, not running from anything.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and, and...\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're running to the voters and what they're concerned about.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris?\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...Donna? Donna?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Chris, it's also about a vision. What's your vision of America's future? It's about your value system. And I think while it's important that we follow where the voters are leading us because, absolutely, they decide, that's why so many of them have voted early.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's democracy.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's also about vision. And this is about the vision of our future. Yes, this is a prop for some Americans, but for me and for millions of Americans, it was a reality when people stormed on the United States Capitol because of a big lie. So, yes, all of these issues are on the ballot, and we just are going to have to turn out and see what the results are.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and I'm happy that you continue to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Donna, I, I want to, I wanna turn to Yvette here.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Keep talking about it through Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I want to talk about a - specific race in Georgia. You have been down there campaigning for Raphael Warnock. What are you...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...it is so close down there, and you have the accusations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Do you feel like people will vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because they like him or because they just want to hold on to the Republican...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2ND WOMAN ACCUSES WALKER OF PAYING FOR HER ABORTION\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's all about power. Nobody's loving \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . I mean, he is not the one, and, in fact, I've heard a lot of African Americans down in Georgia say, he's an embarrassment to us. I mean, he is a, he's a, a sideshow. He's being used. He has all this scandal. He's a, he's a representative of a, of a figure that, that some Americans think Black men should be, and we don't want that representative. Raphael Warnock is an amazing human. I can't imagine why people in Georgia are making this choice.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think some of it is nostalgia. We saw what happened with Tommy Tuberville in Alabama. Football matters apparently in the South. But guess what?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's true.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He is not fit to be a senator, period. What we have seen in Georgia that I want to comment on is, record turnout in the early vote. Two and a half million people voted early in Georgia. That's more than 2018 and 2020. So, I think we're all thinking it's going to go according to the polls. I think new voters and early voters are going to win the day for...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How about African American voters, Donna? Ed Rendell, the former Pennsylvania governor told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " he's worried about African American turnout, that Black voters have not been significantly motivated to vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY 40M BALLOTS ALREADY CAST AHEAD OF TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, enthusiasm is, is, is hard to come by, especially if, when you don't talk to people that you need to get out until the last two or three weeks, So, I'm hoping that African Americans will hear the message, hear the music, get the lyrics and go out and vote, and if they do, we know what happens, especially when Black women vote. Democracy is always on the ballot for us. But I want to go back to candidates. Candidates matter.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They do matter.\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And it's not just \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , it's Mr. Oz in Pennsylvania, it's Mr. Vance, and so I do think that Democrats have a fighting chance, both in keeping, keeping the Senate and, and very might, well, you know, not lose as many seats in the United States House. And, Chris, I know I'm going to get to your soft spot. Baby, we're going to get some of those governor seats, and just, just hold on. Just hold on.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you, you'll get, you'll get Maryland and Massachusetts. After that, I'm not so sure.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: SECURING THE VOTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TERRY MORAN REPORTS ON THE INCREASED THREATS ELECTION WORKERS ARE FACING AHEAD OF THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. I'll take two.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But, but let me just say.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I was in Georgia too, on Thursday and Friday, and what's driving the politics in Georgia right now is the governor's race.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Governor Kemp is gonna be re-elected. He's gonna be re-elected by a surprisingly large margin, and that's what could really wind up helping \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , is that Kemp is going to do much better than anybody is predicting he's gonna do all the way through. And the other, and the other upset and surprise you're gonna see, I think, on Tuesday night is Joe O'Dea. The Republican candidate for the United State Senate in Colorado is gonna beat Michael Bennet and that's gonna be a huge surprise. It's gonna be...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) A candidate who does not have the support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) He does not have the support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He flat out said that he believes that Joe Biden was the legitimately elected president of the United States and, and had Trump attack him. And in Colorado, he is driving Michael Bennet to distraction. He's going to win the race.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sarah, I want to see if you think there are going to be any surprises or people have really made up their minds.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, I think it's important to think about these polls. The undecided voters are still in these polls, so when you see a 41/45 poll, there's, you know, five, seven points outstanding of folks who haven't made up their minds yet. We don't know how they're gonna break, but generally they all break in the same direction, and I think it's a fair assumption that they're going to break toward the Republicans at this point. So, yes, you could see Colorado where, by the way, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Democrats spent millions trying to have a different Republican candidate, an election denying candidate in that race...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 538: GOP FAVORED TO WIN HOUSE, SENATE CLOSER TO TOSS UP\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yep.\par \par \uc2 SARAH ISGUR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Washington state for Senate, Tiffany Smiley, the Oregon governor's race and obviously those races in New York, the Governor and the House races where you could have the head of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee lose his seat.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I campaigned for Mike Lawler against Sean Patrick Mahoney. He's gonna lose and it's probably by five or six points.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP COULD ANNOUNCE 2024 BID AS EARLY AS NEXT WEEK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Let's, let's, let's talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Last night in a rally and earlier in the week, he said, "I promise you in the very, very, very short period of time, you're going to be so happy." And, of course, he was talking about 2024 and since then it's been reported that he will likely make the announcement to run the week of November 14th. We have about 40 seconds here. Got to ask you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, you know, look, is anybody surprised?\par \par \uc2 DONNA BRAZILE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Like, why should we be surprised.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, but what does it mean? No, one's surprised.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I don't think it, look, I don't think it means anything. When something happens that you anticipated happening, it doesn't make any difference. He's gonna run. Everyone always knew he was gonna run. He can't miss the attention any more than he does, and he's going to run. Now we'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yvette, does it make a difference?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To try to avoid, to try to avoid jail.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We have, in about 20 seconds, is Joe Biden the best guy to go against him?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, anybody - over Trump, I think. You know, I think anybody over Trump...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Anybody over Trump could win?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Anybody, anybody. I think Joe Biden has to recover from this year. I mean, his, his approval rating has jumped slightly. He still looks a little tired. I think if we can get his energy back, maybe, I, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay. Thanks, all of you. We'll all be holding our breaths this week. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Up next, Terry Moran reports on growing threats against election officials and the security of our elections.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why some election officials are showing up to work now wearing body armor, and a rare look behind the scenes of the senate campaigns in Arizona and Florida. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221117eib600009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 PRELIMINARY STUDY ON BREAST CANCER VACCINE HOW IT WORKS & WHAT THE RESEARCH SHOWS\b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENSIONS ESCALATING\par \par \uc2 NORTH KOREA, MISSILES, SOUTH KOREA, JETS\par \par \uc2 ELECTION, MIDTERMS, CAMPAIGN, TRUMP, CLINTON, ADS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, BIDEN, OBAMA, SENATE, CAMPAIGNING\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, HIGH STAKES, HOUSE, SENATE\par \par \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 , MUSK, LAYOFFS, JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 RSV, PEDIATRIC, DENENBERG, SCHNEIDER, GHIAM\par \par \uc2 IRVING, NETS, ANTISEMITIC, MOVIE, SUSPENSION, DUBE\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES, ASTROS, \uc2 PHILLIES\uc2 , GAME 5\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 JOGGER, ATTACK, SUMKIN, PHANOR, FELLER\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND, FYRE FESTIVAL, FRAUD, PRISON\par \par \uc2 POWERBALL, JACKPOT, BILLIONS, WHEELER, SCHULTZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WEST STORM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SNOWFALL FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 NORTH KOREA, KIM, MISSILES, SOUTH KOREA, FIGHTER JETS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, ELECTIONS, BATTLEGROUNDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAPPENING NOW\par \par \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 , MUSK, LAYOFFS, EMAIL, JOBS REPORT\par \par \uc2 STUDY, BREAST CANCER, VACCINE, PREVENTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Alright, Michael, we're gonna turn now to this new study from researchers at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 , with early findings suggesting we may be getting closer to a vaccine for breast cancer. Our medical contributor Dr. Darien Sutton is here with all of these details. It's so good to see you in person...\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good to see you too.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...and it is good to be talking about this story. This could potentially help a lot of people and I know folks are gonna - lot of have, have a lot of questions about it. So, break down what the study tells us and what we could be talking about here in terms of this vaccine.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Absolutely. So, early research is suggesting that there's - it's possible to develop a vaccine that could target cancer cells, specifically a type of breast cancer called HER2-positive breast cancer, which encompasses 15% to 20% of total breast cancers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER VACCINE RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PREVENTS OR TREATS HER2-POSITIVIE BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFFECTS 15-20% OF INDIVIDUALS WITH BREAST TUMORS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE TRIGGERS IMMUNE SYSTEM RESPONSE TO ATTACK CANCER CELLS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Typically, this type of breast cancer is aggressive and fast growing but thanks to targeted therapy over the last 20 years, outcomes have considerably increased and improved. Now this new study suggests and supports the first ever possible breast cancer vaccine that could train the immune system to target cancer cells.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the reason why this is incredible is because this could serve as a form of treatment and also as a form of prevention for recurrence.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Treatment and prevention. That is so good to hear. Now, the next question I think everybody hearing you right now is going to ask is when can they get their hands on this? Do we have a sense yet?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHEN MIGHT SOMETHING LIKE THIS BECOME AVAILABLE TO PATIENTS?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We don't yet. Right now, the phase one study, the phase one of this study has been completed. So, they were able to secure and, and prove that it was safe and a, and even, they were able to show that there, a, patients that were able to be tested in the first part produced a strong immune response. Now, we wait for phase two and phase three. Unfortunately, that can take maybe months, even years but this is a fast-growing area of research. If we could develop a way for the immune system to target cancer cells, the way that we train it to fight infections, that could be a new frontier.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Some potentially very good news on the horizon but potentially out there.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR DARIEN SUTTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Dr. Sutton, thank you so much. George, over to you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221201eib400064\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEWS HEADLINES DANGEROUS STORM ALERT/BREAST CANCER VACCINE\b0\par\par\uc2 355 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PROBE, PELOSI, DEPAPE, ATTACK, JENKINS, IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 VOTER INTIMIDATION, ELECTION, ZYGIELBAUM, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONCERNS OVER VOTER INTIMIDATION\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH-STAKES ELECTION BATTLES\par \par \uc2 SEXUAL ASSAULT, INVESTIGATION, PHANOR, SUMKIN\par \par \uc2 SOUTH KOREA, NUCLEAR THREAT, NORTH KOREA, AUSTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NORTH KOREA FIRES MORE MISSILES\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, NUCLEAR PLANT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NUCLEAR PLANT "EXTREMELY PRECARIOUS"\par \par \uc2 DISCRIMINATION, IRVING, ANTISEMITISM\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYRIE IRVING UNDER FIRE\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, VACCINE, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DANGEROUS STORMS ALERT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tonight, we are tracking a possible night of storms ahead. Severe weather now forecast across several states, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE THREAT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Possible tornadoes, damaging winds and large hair from Texas to Oklahoma to Kansas. And then that threat spreading to more states by tomorrow afternoon, a second round tomorrow of even stronger storms from Houston to Dallas to Little Rock. They say all possible.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEAR RECORD HEAT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And near record heat expected up and down the East Coast into this weekend, temperatures in the mid- to high 70s from Washington DC to New York to Boston. Of course, the New York Marathon and those temperatures, those runners are going to face some real heat, this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER VACCINE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're also following news, tonight, about a possible breast cancer vaccine now in the works.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In an early study, researchers say a potential vaccine is showing promise with its ability to help a patient's immune system attack cancer cells. The data coming from an early phase one study showing the vaccine appears safe. Phase two and phase three will determine if it's effective. Those results could take months or years, but of course it is providing some real hope, tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here, safety officials now warning, tonight, they've ordered a do-not-drive order for vehicles at risk of exploding airbags. And then no winner. So, where is this Powerball jackpot, now? The number, in a moment.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221104eib300009\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SIX DAYS TO HIGH-STAKES MIDTERMS OBAMA & BIDEN HIT CAMPAIGN TRAIL\b0\par\par\uc2 723 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENSIONS FLARING\par \par \uc2 MISSILES, NORTH KOREA, SOUTH KOREA, ESCALATION\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, ATTACK, JENKINS, DEPAPE, LIPSON, MAYORKAS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, OBAMA, BIDEN, STAKES, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Michael. The latest now on the midterm elections. Final votes just six days away. President Biden and former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  hitting the campaign trail in the final stretch. Obama headlined a rally last night in Nevada, which could determine control of the Senate. Janai Norman on these scene in Las Vegas. Good morning, Janai.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIX DAYS TO HIGH STAKES MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OBAMA & BIDEN HIT CAMPAIGN TRAIL AS KEY RACES COME DOWN TO THE WIRE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. Former President Obama brought his hope and change optimism, encouraging Nevadans to vote early and vote blue down the ballot as vulnerable Democrats struggle with voters here. And we spoke to many voters struggling themselves who say they voted for Democrats before, but are now voting Republican hoping for change.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Overnight, Democrats in Nevada bringing in their biggest star, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , trying to shore up support in the silver state.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT BARACK OBAMA (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 This election requires every single one of us to do our part. It's that important.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Obama saying the basic foundation of our democracy is at risk, as recent polls show a dead heat between Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and challenger \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a pro-Trump Republican who sought to overturn the state's 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This race could tilt balance of power in the Senate to Republicans. President Biden himself hitting the trail, sounding the alarm in former President Trump's home state, that a Republican controlled Congress could have dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 They're coming after social security and Medicare in a big way. Folks here in Florida, you need to know this.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But across the country kitchen table issues like rising inflation and the economy appear to be helping Republicans make headway with voters. Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  on defense overnight in Ohio as those issues dominated a Senate town hall.\par \par \uc2 REP \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2  (DEM) (OHIO)\par \par \uc2 I think when it comes to inflation, we need a tax cut. We need to put money in people's pockets if we're going to weather the storm here.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republican nominee J.D. Vance trying to tie Ryan to Biden policies.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (REP) (SENATORIAL CANDIDATE FOR OHIO)\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration backed by Tim, supported a lot of borrowing and spending, throwing fuel on the fire which has caused the price of everything to go up.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA SENATE RACE NECK AND NECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HERSCHEL WALKER DENIES PRESSURING SECOND ACCUSER TO HAVE ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrats are optimistic issues like abortion can help turn the tide in razor thin races like in Georgia, where recent polls show Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  gaining ground on Senator Raphael Warnock. But Walker, an anti-abortion candidate, is facing new fallout after a second woman came forward claiming he encouraged her to have an abortion after a multiyear affair nearly three decades ago, sitting down with our Juju Chang.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (CLAIMS HERSHEL WALKER PRESSURED HER TO HAVE ABORTION)\par \par \uc2 It's very hard considering the fact that he is someone who is professing so emphatically that he is against abortion in any instance. And that is the height of hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker denies the allegation saying he's never met the woman, despite a photo of the pair from 2019, and corroboration from two women who say they were aware of the affair in the '90s.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as the races continue heating up ahead of election day, Representative \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , once a reliable Republican, endorsed two Democrats yesterday in Michigan, saying it was the first time she campaigned for a Democrat, Representative \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  there, also endorsing \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio. Goes to show, George, just how much she believes is at stake here.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, she is on a mission. Okay, Janai, thanks very much. Amy.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221201eib200004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SECOND \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  ACCUSER SPEAKS OUT SAYS CANDIDATE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE AN ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,337 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PELOSI, DEPAPE, ATTACK, JENKINS\par \par \uc2 RSV, VACCINE, GRUBER, MIKELS, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, FETTERMAN, OZ\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, CORTEZ-MASTO, LAXALT\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, WARNOCK, WALKER\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, SENATE\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, GAS PRICES, OIL, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN TAKES ON BIG OIL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACCUSES COMPANIES OF "WAR PROFITEERING", THREATENS WINDFALL TAX\par \par \uc2 STAMPEDE, SOUTH KOREA, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 RACE, AFFIRMATIVE ACTION, COLLEGE ADMISSIONS\par \par \uc2 EMPLOYMENT, SALARY TRANSPARENCY, LAW\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LEVEL THE "PAYING" FIELD?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SALARY TRANSPARENCY LAW TAKES EFFECT IN NYC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION, ALLRED, JANE DOE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to get the latest now on Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECOND \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  ACCUSER SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SAYS GOP SENATE CANDIDATE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE AN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju Chang interviewed a second woman accused him of pressuring her to have an abortion. The accuser is now choosing to show her face and respond to Walker's denial of her claims. Good morning, Juju.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, George. Well, as you know, two women have now come forward to say that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the staunchly anti-abortion Senate candidate, pressured them into having abortions. When he called the allegations lies, the second accuser says that's when she decided to sit down with us and share her story.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I've kept this to myself for 30 years. I protected him. And I wanted this to remain private for obvious reasons.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last week, a second woman, her face and name withheld, accused Republican Senate candidate and former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  of pressuring her to get an abortion. Walker denouncing her claims as foolishness and lies.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Saying that's a lie and I've moved on and they want me to play these guessing games and all this, but I'm not. I'm not into that. I'm into winning this great seat for the great people of Georgia because that's what this is about.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, Jane Doe responds. At her request, we agreed not to used her name, calling her Jane Doe instead. But she said she now wants to show her face.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, what makes you want to speak about it now?\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 When I saw the first woman coming forward a few weeks ago, he immediately called her a liar and said, I never signed anything with the letter H. And I knew I had many cards from him where he signed the letter H. And so, I believed then that she was telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe showed us cards and letters she says she received from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , some signed with a flourish, that "H." She says she took this photo of Walker asleep. They had met, she says, in the late '80s when he was married to his first wife and she says had an affair that lasted six years.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 We saw each other several times a week, usually in the mornings. And we saw each other before games, home games, away games, that kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe shared hotel receipts from that era and a voicemail she says is from Walker after he went to the Olympic Games in 1992.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He often told me in writing and every time we had a conversation how much he loved me and how much I meant to him and that I was who he wanted to be with.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says, despite being on birth control, she got pregnant in 1993. Walker, she says, pressured her to get the abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He was very clear that he did not want me to have the child and he said that, he said that because of his wife's family and powerful people around him that I would not be safe and that the child would not be safe.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's very menacing.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It is very menacing. It is very menacing. And I felt threatened and I thought I had no choice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says, at first, she went to an abortion clinic but couldn't go through with it. But Walker, she says, talked her into going back with him.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He came to my house and picked me up and drove me to the clinic. And I went in alone and he waited in the car while I went in and had the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says Walker gave her cash to pay for the abortion and has no records or receipts of the payment or the procedure. She says, after the abortion, everything changed.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 Everything shifted. He distanced himself from me almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says she left her life in Dallas within days and never moved back. Over the years, she says Walker reached out occasionally.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I told my parents that I had a miscarriage because I couldn't tell them the truth. Yeah, and I told a few friends the same thing because I couldn't tell them the truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why in your mind did you feel like you couldn't tell them the truth?\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It just was very shameful, and I felt like I had been manipulated.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We spoke to two women identified as friends of the accuser by her lawyer Gloria Allred, they each told us Jane Doe confided to them in the '90s she got pregnant during a years' long affair with Walker. One woman said she took this photo of Jane Doe and Walker meeting at a conference in 2019, years after their alleged affair. Her friend describing how she says they greeted each other.\par \par \uc2 WITNESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 He embraced her. And the embrace, it was very emotional. It had lasted longer than a normal embrace. It was like they had known each other for years and years and years, which I had known they had.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Jane Doe says her feelings changed after she saw Walker denied that he'd ever paid for an abortion. That, says her lawyer, is when she decided to come forward.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (ATTORNEY)\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , look the American people in the eye after she spoke out last week and said it was a lie, both women were liars. And she's now looking the American people in the eye and she's telling her truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what makes you think he is not fit to be a US Senator?\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I think, I think honesty matters.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, it's worth noting that Jane Doe is a registered independent and she told us that she voted for Donald Trump twice. She says she's not motivated by politics, but by what she says matters more, the truth. Now, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's campaign told ABC News to refer to that previous statement in which he said "It's a lie, I've moved on. They want me to play these guessing games, but I'm not into that. I'm into winning." Guys?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Juju.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was something. Thank you, Juju.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 7:41, SUSPECT CHARGED IN DELPHI MURDERS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up next, we have the latest on the Delphi murder case. The suspect charged and why officials say the investigation is far from over. Stay there. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221121eib10000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ANOTHER JANE DOE COMES FORWARD HERSCHEL WALKER ACCUSATIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,030 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 WALKER, DOE, ABORTION, LOVE, LIAR, ALLRED\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thanks for joining us. Tonight, you're about to meet a woman who says it took a lot of courage to show her face to our cameras. But she says she's coming forward in the name of the truth, as Herschel Walker, the anti-abortion Senate candidate from Georgia, calls her a liar. Tonight, why she says she's ignoring the potential backlash to tell us her story.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I've kept this to myself for 30 years. I protected him. And I wanted this to remain private, for obvious reasons.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This woman says she never intended to go public. She remained silent about a six-year love affair she said she had with then-football star, Herschel Walker.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He often told me in writing and every time we had a conversation how much he loved me and how much I meant to him, and that I was who he wanted to be with.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's the second woman to accuse Walker, a staunch anti-abortion candidate for Senate, of paying for a secret abortion.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It's very hard, considering the fact that he is someone who is professing so emphatically that he is against abortion, in any instance. And that is the height of hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The first allegations initially reported by "The Daily Beast" were made by an anonymous woman last month, claiming to be Walker's ex-girlfriend. That woman provided what she says was a get well card from Walker, signed with the letter "H." ABC News has not spoken with the first accuser or independently confirmed her story. Walker responded on Fox News to the first woman's allegations.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF HERSCHEL WALKER INTERVIEW ON FOX NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HERSCHEL WALKER RESPONDS TO ABORTION ALLEGATIONS: THIS IS A FLAT-OUT LIE\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER (REP) (GEORGE SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 It's a lie, and I'm gonna continue to fight. You know, I tell you, that's what they want. They want this seat.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what makes you want to speak about it now?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 When I saw the first woman come forward a few weeks ago, he immediately called her a liar and said, "I never signed anything with the letter 'H.'" And I knew I had many cards from him where he signed the letter "H." And so I believed, then, that she was telling the truth. I think "H's" stand for hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The second accuser says that's when she decided to come forward...\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF PRESS CONFERENCE\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I am here today as a woman, and most importantly, a mother.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...giving a press conference by phone to protect her identity. Now, she wants to show her face for the first time, with her attorney Gloria Allred by her side. At her request, we agreed to call her Jane Doe.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 And the final decision was ultimately one that I, I shared with my daughter, I'm sorry. And my decision to show my face at this point is because of what she said to me. Which was, "Mom, you were never a woman to sit on the sidelines, and you didn't raise me to be one." And so that is why I'm sitting here today.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe showed us cards and letters she said she received from Herschel Walker. She says she took this photo of Walker asleep.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And that's you?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They met, she says in the late '80s, during the height of his football career.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 We saw each other several times a week, usually in the mornings. And we saw each other before games, home games, away games. I was invited. And, and I always insisted on paying half of my expenses when I went.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I didn't want to feel like I was being kept.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe showed us hotel receipts from the years she says they were together and a voicemail she says from Walker after he went to the Olympic Games in 1992.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: I WANT TO SAY I LOVE YOU. OKAY, BYE.\par \par \uc2 VOICE MAIL (MALE VOICE)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to say that you were in love with him?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 We were, yes, we were in love. He not only expressed it to me often, in writing, and, and on the phone and in person, obviously. He met with my parents. He met with them on several occasions.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How did that go about?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He wanted to meet with them and explain to them that he loved their daughter. And he looked my parents in the eye and told them how much he loved me and that he was not doing me "wrong," quote, unquote, and that he intended on being with me and that he would protect me.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says despite being on birth control, she got pregnant in 1993.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I had a great life. But when I found out I was pregnant, I wanted to have the child.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 You know, it, it's, I felt it was the right decision for me, at the time.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker, she says, pressured her to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He was very clear that he did not want me to have the child. And he said that, he said that because of his wife's family and powerful people around him, that I would not be safe, and that the child would not be safe.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's very menacing.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It is very menacing. It is very menacing. And I felt threatened, and I, I thought I had no choice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says she went to an abortion clinic, but couldn't go through with it.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I guess it's part of the procedure, then they did an ultrasound. And I saw the ultrasound, and I couldn't go through with it. So, I left the clinic and went back home.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Walker, she says, talked her into going back with him.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He came to my house and picked me up and drove me to the clinic. And I went in alone. And he waited in the car while I went in and had the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says Walker gave her cash to pay for the abortion. She has no records or receipts of the payment or the procedure. After the abortion, she says she and Walker's relationship changed.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 Everything shifted. He distanced himself from me, almost immediately.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did he ever break up with you or explicitly say anything?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 No. I left Dallas, immediately.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why did you leave Dallas?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I didn't feel like I could stay there anymore, after this.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says she never lived in Dallas again.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I told my parents that I had a miscarriage, because I couldn't tell them the truth. Yeah. And I told a few friends the same thing, because I couldn't tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why, in your mind, did you feel like you couldn't tell them the truth?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It just was very shameful. And I felt like I had been manipulated. And I just, I just couldn't. I couldn't, at the time. I did later.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last week, after Jane Doe's press conference, the Republican Senate candidate denounced her claims.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN STOP\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER (REP) (GEORGE SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, I'm done with this foolishness. I've already told people this is a lie, and I'm not gonna entertain to continue to carry a lie along.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He alluded to the allegations at a campaign event, today.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN SPEECH\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER (REP) (GEORGE SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are going crazy right now, aren't they? They are going crazy. They're coming after me with everything, right now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TEXT OF STATEMENT\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Also, today, Walker releasing a statement saying, "This was a lie a week ago, and it's a lie today. Seven days before an election, the Democrats trot out Gloria Allred and some woman I do not know." But we spoke to two women who refute that claim, identified to us as friends of Jane Doe by her lawyer, Gloria Allred. They each told us Jane Doe confided in them during the '90s that she got pregnant during a years-long affair with Walker. One woman saying she took this photo of Jane Doe and Walker meeting at a mental health conference in 2019, decades after their alleged affair. This is how she described their greeting.\par \par \uc2 FRIEND (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 He embraced her. And that embrace, it was very emotional. It, it lasted longer than a normal embrace. It was like they'd known each other for years and years and years, which I, I had known they had.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says she and Walker stayed in occasional contact, but her feelings changed after she saw Walker deny the first accuser's claims.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (PARTNER AT ALLRED\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker looked the American people in the eye after she spoke out last week and said it was a lie, both women were liars. And she's now looking the American people in the eye, and she's telling her truth.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is a sense that maybe you're some sort of puppet master, that this is a political hit job, that you're a political operative.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (PARTNER AT ALLRED\par \par \uc2 I have attacked many Democratic candidates for office, and elected officials, not just Republicans, over the years.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) People are immediately gonna assume that you're politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I voted for Donald Trump twice. I vote based on the person, not the party. And I have no, this is not about politics, for me.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so what do you hope happens, moving forward?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I hope he doesn't become a US Senator. And I hope that I can go back, I want to go back to my life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What makes you think he's not fit to be a US Senator?\par \par \uc2 "JANE DOE" (HERSCHEL WALKER'S SECOND ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I think honesty matters. I think it is not appropriate for a United States senator to say one thing and do another.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHADWICK BOSEMAN\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And up next, ahead of the release of the "Black Panther" sequel, "Wakanda Forever," Chadwick Boseman's widow reflects on his life and legacy.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221102eib100002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SECOND WALKER ACCUSER SPEAKING OUT CLAIMS GOP SENATE CANDIDATE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE AN ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 816 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 PELOSI, DEPAPE, ATTACK, LAKE, HOBBS, JENKINS\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT MANHUNT AFTER OFFICERS SHOT\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION, JANE DOE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECOND WALKER ACCUSER SPEAKING OUT\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to turn now to the ABC News exclusive, tonight, the second woman who accused Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  of pressuring her to have an abortion speaking on camera tonight for the first time, with our Juju Chang. Walker, who has campaigned against abortion rights, his response, tonight, and what she brought to the interview. Here's Juju Chang.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAIMS GOP SENATE CANDIDATE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE AN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the second woman to accuse Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  of paying for an abortion appearing on camera for the first time in an interview with ABC News.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 I've kept this to myself for 30 years. I protected him.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At her request, we agreed to call her Jane Doe. She claims she had a six-year affair with Walker that began in the late '80s, when he was married to his first wife. In 1993, she says, she got pregnant.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He was very clear that he did not want me to have the child. And he said that, he said that because of his wife's family and powerful people around him, that I would not be safe and that the child would not be safe.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's very menacing.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 It is very menacing. It is very menacing. And I felt threatened, and I, I thought I had no choice.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She tells us she went to the abortion clinic, but couldn't go through with it, claiming that when she later told Walker, he became upset.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He came to my house and picked me up and drove me to the clinic. And I went in alone, and he waited in the car while I went in and had the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe says Walker gave her cash to pay for the abortion. She has no records or receipts of the payment or the procedure. Walker today denounced her claims as "a lie" from "some woman I do not know."\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 They're going crazy, right now. Are they not? I'm like, wow, man. I'm doing things I never knew I even done before. But it's okay, because we only got a couple days left.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Though Walker says he does not know her, Jane Doe showed us cards and letters she said she received from him. She showed us hotel receipts from that era, and this photo she took of Walker asleep. She shared a voicemail she says he left her when he went to the Olympic Games in 1992.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I want to say I love you, and I was thinking about you.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe's lawyer, Gloria Allred, put us in touch with two women who told us that, in the 1990s, Jane Doe confided them about her relationship with Walker and her pregnancy. One of those women told us she took this picture of Jane Doe and walker at a conference in 2019.\par \par \uc2 WITNESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 He embraced her, and the embrace, it was very emotional. It lasted longer than a normal embrace.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jane Doe is a registered independent who told us she voted for Donald Trump in 2016 and 2020. But she says she decided to come forward now after watching Walker deny another woman's claim that he paid for her abortion, too. As a candidate, Walker is a staunch opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 That is the height of hypocrisy, the height of hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is it hypocritical?\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He's saying one thing and doing another.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, let's bring in "Nightline" anchor Juju Chang, tonight. And, Juju, of course, as you know, this race is so close in Georgia, it could determine the control of the Senate. What are we hearing from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's opponent, tonight, Senator Raphael Warnock?\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, as you know, David, it is essentially a tossup. And today, Walker's opponent, the Reverend Raphael Warnock, said that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not only not ready for the job, he's not fit, because he repeatedly lies about the basic facts of his life. He says character matters. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Juju Chang with us, tonight. Juju, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221102eib100004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 EIGHT DAYS TO HIGH STAKES MIDTERMS WHAT ISSUES ARE IMPORTANT TO VOTERS?\b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HORRIFYING CROWD SURGE\par \par \uc2 HALLOWEEN, CROWD, CRUSH, STORY, MAMOJONOV, SEOUL\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, ATTACK CHARGES, DEPAPE, JENKINS\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, CONGRESS, THREATS, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , DHS, BULLETIN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, DEBATE, KEMP, ABRAMS, ATTACKS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, STAKES, ISSUES, POLL, VOTERS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Steve Osunsami, thanks. Now to our new poll, which pinpoints the issues likely to determine who controls Congress. Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott has the details. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EIGHT DAYS TO HIGH STAKES MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT ISSUES ARE IMPORTANT TO VOTERS?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. Well our latest ABC New/IPSOS poll asked voters what is the single most important issue to them this election day. Take a look, the economy and inflation topping the list. When you add these two numbers together you get 50% of registered voters who say the economy and inflation is the most important.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And this exactly why we are seeing Republicans really zero in on this issue in the final stretch, especially in critical states like Nevada and in Arizona, where the state's inflation rate there is higher than the national average. Republicans are making a play out west, Democrats are on defense, and the Senate races in those two states are neck and neck, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Rachel, what about abortion?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, well, George, let's go back to our latest poll here, you can see that abortion does rank third. 16% of registered voters say that this is the most important issue to them. But even more than that, you have the majority of Americans, a little over 60% of registered voters say that abortion should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That serves as a sharp contrast to the restrictive abortion measures that we are seeing sweeping the state. Now abortion will be on the ballot in five states this November, California, Michigan and Vermont will be measures to protect access to abortion. Montana and Kentucky will be measures to restrict access, making this midterm election even more consequential. George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No question about that. Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Eva.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eiav000ba\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MIDTERM MONITOR TOP ISSUES AMONG REGISTERED VOTERS\b0\par\par\uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, HUSBAND, ATTACK, DEPAPE, SCOTT\par \par \uc2 SENATE, MASTO, VOTERS, POLL, CWU, O'DEA, BENNET\par \par \uc2 2022, MONITOR, SENATE, VOTERS, ISSUES, POLL, 538\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, let's take a closer look, now, at just how much the economy has become the driving issue in the midterms with our political director, Rick Klein, back in DC, at the Midterm Monitor. Rick, it's been a long and winding campaign, but our brand-new ABC poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  sheds some light on what voters truly care about.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP ISSUES AMONG REGISTERED VOTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMY 28%, INFLATION 22%, ABORTION 16%, GUN VIOLENCE 7%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, we asked voters, what's the number-one issue driving your decision this fall, and you can see, overwhelmingly, pocketbook issues, the economy, inflation. You add those two numbers together, that's half the country saying that those are the issues that are primarily driving their votes. You see other issues ranking as well, abortion, gun violence. Those are issues that Democrats are somewhat stronger on. But, on those pocketbook issues, on the economy, it's Republicans who are pushing that narrative, pushing that in the way that people really feel it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VIEWS ON CANDIDATES & ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEEPING ABORTION LEGAL AND AVAILABLE 48%, LIMITING ABORTION EXCEPT TO PROTECT THE MOTHER'S LIFE 33%, ISSUE WOULD NOT MATTER 18%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And interestingly, on abortion, our poll, like most polls, show that most Americans think that abortion should be legal in most cases. And almost half the country says that makes a difference in the kind of candidate that they're likely to support, but there's another third of the country that actually feels the opposite. They're more likely to support a candidate who wants restrictions on abortion rights, and another one in five Americans telling us, with this poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2 , it doesn't make any difference at all. So, that gives you a sense of the limits of the messaging that Democrats are hoping pushes them over the top.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It, it sure does. And Rick, you know, we were in the state of Nevada. The Senate race there hasn't gotten as much attention as Georgia or Pennsylvania, but this could really be a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SENATE: WHAT IF?: REPS NEED 51 TO CONTROL\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Martha, it's a 50/50 Senate. Coincidentally, right now our partners at FiveThirtyEight think there's 50/50 chance for either party to take over control. These are the 12 states that are almost certainly going to determine control of the Senate in, in November. And, and take a look at Nevada, because the Republican has been leading, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , over \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . If the Republicans flip Nevada, FiveThirtyEight sees it as now a more than seven in 10 chance that they take the entire Senate.\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) On the other hand, if Nevada stays blue, if Senator \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 's able to withstand those headwinds, then, suddenly, the Democrats are a three in four chance to hold the Senate. So, there are going to be a lot of eyes, Martha, on, on, out West, very late into the night on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and here in Texas, Democrats have long had visions of turning this state blue. That doesn't really look realistic, this year.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2018 TEXAS SENATE RESULTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRUZ 51%, O'ROURKE 48%\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. Yeah, you're right, Martha. Look, this is the last time that \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  ran statewide, it was in 2018 against Senator Cruz. He came close in a much better environment for Democrats. He's running again, this time for governor, against Greg Abbott. There aren't many people in either party, frankly, who feel like that \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 's gonna do better in a worst environment against a stronger incumbent candidate, but how he does and how Democrats do still matters.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 TEXAS CONGRESSIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 24 GOP-LEANING SEATS, 13 DEM-LEANING SEATS, 1 COMPETITIVE SEAT\par \par \uc2 RICK KLEIN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Take a look at the Texas congressional map. There are a couple of seats that I've got my eye on here in south Texas, in the suburbs of Austin, in San Antonio, down into the Rio Grande Valley. You're talking about suburban voters, who inflation is a big deal for, Latino voters, these are the kind of voters, Martha, that Democrats need to keep in the fold, this year and beyond, if they're going to be competitive. There's an interesting microcosm for the trends we're seeing around the country, right there in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And thanks so much, Rick.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221107eiau00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RESTRICTIONS RISING SINCE ROE REVERSAL PROVIDERS STRUGGLING AMID ABORTION RESTRICTIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, HUSBAND, ATTACK, DEPAPE, SCOTT\par \par \uc2 SENATE, MASTO, VOTERS, POLL, CWU, O'DEA, BENNET\par \par \uc2 2022, MONITOR, SENATE, VOTERS, ISSUES, POLL, 538\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, MIDTERMS, POLL, VOTERS, ECONOMY, CRIME, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 SCOTT, PELOSI, MIDTERMS, SENATE, 2020, DENIERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE FOR THE SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH NRSC CHAIR SEN RICK SCOTT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, MIDTERMS, CONNOLLY, PROVIDERS, KUMAR\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LISA MURKOWSKI (REP) (ALASKA)\par \par \uc2 I do support codification of Roe versus Wade. I also recognize that abortion should not be without limitation.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM WEDNESDAY\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GRETCHEN WHITMER (DEM) (MICHIGAN)\par \par \uc2 People expect their government to protect their rights to their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I want women, doctors, local political leaders, leading the democracy that's always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The government should not be making this decision for her.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP ISSUES AMONG REGISTERED VOTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE ECONOMY 28%, INFLATION 22%, ABORTION 16%, GUN VIOLENCE 7%\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Abortion is on the ballot this November, as both parties count on this divisive issue motivating voters to get out to the polls next week, and, according to our new ABC/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll this morning, ranks third among the issues voters care about most behind the economy and inflation. Chief medical correspondent Dr. Jen Ashton joins us with a report on how the Supreme Court's Dobbs ruling overturning Roe has changed the lives of providers and patients. And good morning to you, Jen.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION AND THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESTRICTIONS RISING SINCE ROE REVERSAL\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Martha. And it certainly has changed that. The day Roe v. Wade was overturned by the Supreme Court, many pregnant women in this country woke up to far fewer choices, and their doctors are now having to help them navigate the care they get across the country.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 We knew from the second it happened that our lives, the way that they provided medical care were going to change, and that our patients were going to be so much more desperate.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTIONS HAVE CEASED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION ALLOWED UP TO 22+ WEEKS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Since the Supreme Court's Dobbs abortion decision, at least 14 states have ceased nearly all abortion services, leaving patients to travel to one of 24 states which allow abortion up to 22 weeks or beyond. While the US has been dealing with a maternal mortality crisis for years, the impact of abortion becoming illegal in some states was felt instantaneously by doctors who take care of women at places like Planned Parenthood in California.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) How quickly after that decision was announced did you start to see things change?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 When Roe versus Wade was overturned last summer, we saw an additional 200 percent increase in patients coming from out of state.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROVIDERS STRUGGLING AMID ABORTION RESTRICTIONS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Texas, where nearly all abortions are illegal, this Planned Parenthood clinic is still trying to help patients as best they can.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR BHAVIK KUMAR (TX PLANNED PARENTHOOD MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 We're providing all the other care that we've always provided, access to contraception, STI testing and treatment, breast and cervical cancer screenings. All of those things are still available, and there's actually more of a demand for those services given the landscape that people are navigating.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And timing can make the difference between life and death for a woman with an undesired pregnancy who is seeking termination.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 A lot of people have irregular menstrual cycles, and so they just don't know when they got pregnant. And, of course, every week that goes by, it becomes more expensive, more challenging, and across the country we're seeing that abortion providers in states where abortion remains legal are being overwhelmed by the influx of patients from out of state.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the emotional or psychological toll on these women and their doctors is significant.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What is it like for you, in your experience? And, and are there times where, you know, you run through a gamut of emotions yourself?\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CRIMINAL CHARGES POSSIBLE AGAINST SOME PROVIDERS\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 Abortion is just one type of health care, and I provide a lot of types of health care. And so, what I would say is that it's not - medical care or providing medical care that takes a toll on me. It's working within a system that provides for neither health care, nor is it actually a system. It's the, the structural challenges that weigh on me the most.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible) abolish abortion!\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now, something that was previously inconceivable, a legal or criminal threat to doctors who treat women in states where abortion is illegal.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Can you fathom what it would be like to risk criminal and legal consequences for doing your job as a physician?\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 No. It's so challenging. I mean, imagine being a doctor and having before you a patient with an ectopic pregnancy, and you're not even sure if you can provide a life-saving medical treatment. You're literally choosing between saving the life of your patient and going to jail.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Martha, it is not just doctors taking these risks. It's patients, too, traveling out of state much more of an inconvenient, and much more than an inconvenience for these women. It is putting them at higher risk medically, not to mention the mental and financial burden this additional transportation is taking on them. It is significant.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is, it is, indeed, significant. And, and Jen, as you know, we've got this new data this morning from FiveThirtyEight showing there have been 10,000 fewer abortions in two months since the Supreme Court decision in June, and if that trend continues, there could be 60,000 fewer abortions. What does that mean for women facing this?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You know, I think it's important when people hear those numbers to understand that when this decision came out, all it did was put a stop in many states in the country to safe and legal abortion. It's not putting a stop to all abortion. So, those numbers are legal, reported numbers. Abortions are still going on. They, this is not a procedure that women should DIY it. There are significant medical risks at stake. So, it's a significant concern, literally, for life-and-death issues.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and in our poll, 62 percent think that abortion should be legal, compared to 38 percent who don't. But, beyond the numbers, you're an Ob/Gyn, you deal with this. What's been your experience dealing with women who want to terminate a pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think the angle that has not been discussed in this national debate and very emotionally dialogue is that this is not an easy decision for these women. I have never seen a woman smiling ear to ear as she's rolled into the operating room. There are significant emotional and psychological and social issues at play, both for the woman and the partner oftentimes, and it's, it's not insignificant. So, I think we have to be able to look at it medically, but also emotionally and socially, as well. And what is happening now is just putting more obstacles in from front of these women who are already facing not just emotional and psychological obstacles, but logistical, financial and now medical.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, we know you always take great care, and it's so great to have you here with us this morning. Thanks so much, Jen.\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR JENNIFER ASHTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The Roundtable returns to weigh in on all of this, plus what Elon Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover means for free speech in America. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221107eiau00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 2,130 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, HUSBAND, ATTACK, DEPAPE, SCOTT\par \par \uc2 SENATE, MASTO, VOTERS, POLL, CWU, O'DEA, BENNET\par \par \uc2 2022, MONITOR, SENATE, VOTERS, ISSUES, POLL, 538\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 VOTE: MIDTERM MONITOR\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, MIDTERMS, POLL, VOTERS, ECONOMY, CRIME, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 SCOTT, PELOSI, MIDTERMS, SENATE, 2020, DENIERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BATTLE FOR THE SENATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH NRSC CHAIR SEN RICK SCOTT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, MIDTERMS, CONNOLLY, PROVIDERS, KUMAR\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM THURSDAY\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, MIDTERMS, BORDER, IMMIGRATION, \uc2 TWITTER\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM FRIDAY\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (FORMER UNITED STATES PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 There may be a lot of issues at stake in this election, but the basic question, the fundamental question that you should be asking yourself right now, is who will fight for you? Who cares about you? Who sees you? Who believes in you? That's the choice in this election.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) President Obama Friday, in Georgia. State Democrats are increasingly nervous they might lose, which could cost them the Senate majority. The Roundtable is now back. Welcome back, everyone, and I do want to get back to the issues, first. Let's go back to the issue of abortion. The blue wave that was initially predicted after Roe was overturned has really seemed to subside, Heidi, but with more states limiting rights, can Democrats get people to rally around that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL ROE REVERSAL DRIVE VOTERS TO THE POLLS?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, you forget that, in Kansas, the measure that would've outlawed abortion was winning by over four points, and then eventually lost by almost that many, if not more. And so, there is an undertone of human rights and what does this mean for us. Obviously, the Democratic base heavily gender-biased towards women, and I think women have not forgotten that reproductive rights are on the agenda. And I think it will matter in some legislative races, House races, but I think it's also gonna matter in some of our Senate races. And so, we'll wait and see, but don't, don't assume just because you saw those numbers about it's not as important as the economy, that it's not an important voting issue. It is.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, Chris, it is. It's number three in the polls, and, and you've also got 62 percent saying it should be legal in all or most cases. How do Republicans square that?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IPSOS POLL: ABORTION IS THIRD TOP ISSUE IN THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I, I don't think they square that, and I don't think that it matters. I mean, look, yeah, it's, it's the third issue, but it's three-and-half times less important than the economy in this race. And I think the Democrats have made a serious strategic error. Remember, too, the intensity of voters matters in a midterm election, and I've seen a number of polls where the intensity of the pro-life side of this is higher than the intensity of the, then pro-choice side of it. In the end, look, I think people who are voting that as their number-one issue had their jerseys on a long time ago, Martha.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They were voting Democrat if they're pro-choice. They're voting Republican if they're pro-life. And the folks at, the vast number of folks in the middle where that issue is not their number one issue, they're making this decision based on the economy and crime, education, drug abuse and drug overdoses. Those are the things that are affecting them much more in the neighborhood every day. And so, I think that the Democrats who have made this a centerpiece, and as I've been traveling around, I've seen a lot of ads about this from Democratic candidates, I think are gonna look back on it and say they should've come up with a different strategy.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Maria Elena, let's, let's look at immigration. It, it, it is not just an issue in a border state like here in Texas. This has really spread across the country. How big of an issue do you think this is?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABC: OVER 2 MILLION BORDER APPREHENSIONS UNDER BIDEN\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is. It is an issue in Texas and also in Arizona, but to a different view, with different views. There are some people who are concerned about the border, and they want immigration to stop. But, to a majority of Latino voters, for example, one of the most important things is immigration reform, and it's very, very difficult, very complicated to have immigration reform. Both administrations and both parties have attempted to do so in the past, and they have not been able to do that. So, what do voters, what would voters like to see?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BORDER HIGHLIGHTS DIVIDE BETWEEN BIDEN, BORDER-STATE DEMS\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, there's a sector that would like to see controlling the border and that is very, very complicated. As long as you have poor countries with very complicated political issues at home, they're always gonna try to come for a better life. I think that the issue has been taken out of proportion. All these busing of immigrants into these cities and then treating them as if they're invaders, and we forget that those people who are here, who have crossed the border are asylum seekers and that they have the legal right to request asylum, that they have the, that we as a country have the legal - authority and the, and the legal obligation to at least listen to their case.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But what's really needed is immigration reform, and that's what they want, that leads to some kind of legalization for the Dreamers and for the 11 million undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And Chris, what will they get, if Republicans take charge? What about that wall?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT DO REPUBLICANS PLAN FOR THE BORDER, IMMIGRATION?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, well, look, I, you know, the, the problem here is, and, and, and, and this is where I disagree with Maria a little bit, is that, you know, Democratic politicians in Northeastern states and in the far West have been able to declare themselves as sanctuary cities, sanctuary counties, sanctuary states with no ramifications for that. They, they grandstand it for political purposes, and then there are no ramifications for it. Now that you have folks who are saying, okay, if you're a sanctuary state in New York, you're a sanctuary city in New York City? We're gonna send folks up there.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and you said that's what you wanted, you want to be a sanctuary. You know, I think that this is problem for politicians in both parties. When you can say things without ramifications, you know, they do it. When the ramifications come home to roost, and you've seen this on disaster aid. Governor Desantis voted against disaster aid for Sandy, which happened 10 years ago yesterday in my state, but when the, the hurricane comes to the gulf coast of Florida, he wants that aid from the federal government, and he wants it right away.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When you're a congressman, you can grandstand, and it doesn't matter. When you're a governor and you have to look into the eyes of misery and loss, you want the help right away. And I think it's the same thing on this. You say a sanctuary state, but don't send anybody here.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think immigration is really complicated, but you can't separate it from the economy. You grow the economy when you have more people being productive. Right now, one of the reasons why inflation is up is because we don't have a workforce that can fill the number of jobs that we have, and that reduces productivity. It increases wages, which is a good thing, but we've got to have more workers. And so, you can't, this is an issue that is conflated with the economic issues. And ironically, the Koch Brothers have been some of the, the biggest proponents of immigration reform. You know, this is left/right issue that merges, in some ways, as people look at greater opportunity for a bigger and more productive workforce in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Heidi, I want to stay with you for a minute. I, I, I want to ask you, this, we've said this could be one of the most consequential elections. If Republicans take over, that is a huge change. And I want to ask you the same question that I asked Rick Scott about the election deniers, nearly 200, with 67 percent of those will probably get in. How does the country change?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. I, I think there's election deniers who are true believers. They believe whatever is told them about mules or, or ballots. And then there's election deniers who are opportunists, right? They're saying it because they want the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsement. I think the consequences...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And yet that validates the people who believe it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 MIDTERM ELECTIONS JUST 9 DAYS AWAY\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, right. No, I, I agree. I agree. And, and the problem you have with this election, and I've been preaching this every place that I've gone, this is just the second chapter of 2020 on whether we take back the country and have some semblance of normalcy in terms of addressing our policy issues. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  elects his slate of candidates, the Republican Party will be cemented for a decade as a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  party, and that is bad for this country, because he is the most divisive figure that has ever sat in the, the President's chair.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Look, I, I, I don't buy of much of what Heidi just said in terms of what it does to the party, going forward. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's not on the ballot this time. That changes the dynamic of a lot of these races. And secondly, '24 will come when '24 comes, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's either going to be a part of it or he's not. And, and that's when people will, will start to make those fundamental decisions about what kind of leadership they want in the Oval Office and what kind they'll reject. And I don't think anywhere near making that call, yet.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Maria Elena, I, I, we're, we're running out of time, here, and I want to quickly, speaking of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  now owns \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Do you expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be back on there, and how much difference that will make?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MUSK TAKES OVER TWITTER, PROMPTING STANDARDS CHANGES\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, let's see how long it lasts. He says he'd rather be on Truth Social, but he has much less followers on Truth Social than he does on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . But, you know, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has been talking a lot about, yeah, let's free the bird. But he paused almost immediately saying, no, we're gonna have a content moderation panel before we make any changes, and he's already lost the \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , and I'm sure he's doesn't want to lose more.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're dying to say something. You've got 10 seconds.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Trump, Trump, Trump won't be back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , because he can't make money there. He's trying to make money on Truth Social. He'll stay where he can try to make money.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI HEITKAMP (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Every outrageous thing that Trump says is already on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Let's admit it. Right? So, it's like, he's off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . No he's not.\par \par \uc2 MARIA ELENA SALINAS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They are. He's got an army going for him, anyway.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Thanks to all of you. Great to have you here. More from Dallas, in a moment. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221107eiau00008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THIS WEEK WITH GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS\b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Just nine days to the midterm elections, as races tighten in the final stretch. We're out on the campaign trail. A special edition of "This Week" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The choice couldn't be clearer. The stakes couldn't be higher.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 America is in crisis. Revival is coming.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 When we vote, we win.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Over 20 million votes already cast, midterm turnout on track to break records. We're on the road, crisscrossing states that could flip control of Congress. Las Vegas is all about hospitality, restaurant jobs, hotel jobs, and not all of those jobs have come back.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 What's driving voters to the polls. So, are you hoping for a red wave?\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Kind of. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Any other issues important to you?\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Well, the divisiveness.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Political rhetoric reaching a breaking point. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband attacked in their San Francisco home, the intruder looking for the Speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's political violence. There's no place in our country, you know, for this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PIERRE THOMAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEN. RICK SCOTT\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 This morning, Pierre Thomas on increasing threats against elected officials. Reaction from the Republican tasked with flipping the Senate, Senator Rick Scott. And...\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP FROM INTERVIEW FOOTAGE\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR SHANNON CONNOLLY (CA PLANNED PARENTHOOD ASSOCIATE MEDICAL DIRECTOR)\par \par \uc2 Abortion providers in states where abortion remains legal are being overwhelmed by the influx of patients from out of state.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 A look at one of the key issues motivating voters. Dr. Jennifer Ashton reports on the nationwide impact of abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RICK KLEIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Plus, political director Rick Klein with our new ABC poll, and the Powerhouse Roundtable joins us live here in Texas to break down all the week's politics.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 From ABC News, this is a special edition of "This Week," live from Dallas, Texas. Here, now, Martha Raddatz.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning and welcome to "This Week."\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NINE DAYS UNTIL MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Nine days until Election Day, all indications this midterm will draw record turnout and be among the most consequential in a generation. And this morning, our brand-new ABC News poll with \uc2 Ipsos\uc2  finds a clear issue driving voters, the economy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP ISSUES AMONG REGISTERED VOTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ECONOMY 28%, INFLATION 22%\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Half the country says economic concerns and inflation are driving their votes this fall. I spent the last week traveling out West, exploring the races that could define the control of Congress and, by extension, the future of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's presidency.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) America remains bitterly divided, and this week we've seen that horrors officials have warned political polarization could fuel.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The 82-year-old husband of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  viciously attacked in the middle of the night at the couples' home in San Francisco by a suspect police say was in search of the Speaker herself, drawing haunting comparisons to those who hunted for her on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221107eiau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TWO FRIENDS JOURNEYS DOCTOR & FRIEND FOR COLLEAGUE WITH BREAST CANCER\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PAUL PELOSI UNDERGOES SURGERY: SPEAKER'S HUSBAND ATTACKED\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, SPEAKER, ATTACK, HAMMER, SCOTT, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 PELOSI, ATTACK, SUSPECT, SOCIAL MEDIA\par \par \uc2 SECURITY, ELECTION, MIDTERMS, VOTING, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 SENATE, CONGRESS, RACE, ELECTION\par \par \uc2 FED, INTEREST RATES, INFLATION, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 FLU, RSV, COVID, BROWNSTEIN, KITAGAWA, JARVIS, COMBS\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, CONTROL, PLATFORM\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, DRONES, ATTACK\par \par \uc2 CRUMBLEY, MICHIGAN, SCHOOL, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, HOUSTON, WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 BRADY, BUNDCHEN, DIVORCE, TRIGGS, HIGGINS\par \par \uc2 DELPHI, ARREST, ROWE, GARRETT, WILLIAMS\par \par \uc2 AIRLINE SEATS, FAA, LOGAN, HUDSON\par \par \uc2 FAMU, ALUMNA, NIKE, SHOE, DAVIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, PELOSI, ATTACK, DEPAPE\par \par \uc2 FLU, RSV\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRIPLE VIRUS THREAT PUSHING HOSPITALS TO LIMITS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: THIS IS WORST FLU SEASON SINCE 2009\par \par \uc2 BRADY, DIVORCE, BUNDCHEN, HIGGINS\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, REASOR\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, turning now to "Thriving in Pink" and a story we first reported here on "GMA" Saturday, two friends now speaking to us about their journey together.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOGETHER IN PINK\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) One an oncologist who balanced being doctor and emotional support while her med school classmate was treated for breast cancer. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWO FRIENDS JOURNEYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONCOLOGIST BECAME DOCTOR & FRIEND FOR COLLEAGUE DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When Dr. Cheryl Law was diagnosed with breast cancer six weeks postpartum, the first call she made was to Dr. Erica Stringer-Reasor, an old friend from medical school.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 My husband was with me. We were both in shock as well as fearful. And one of the first thoughts that came to both of our minds was to call my friend Erica, because she is an oncologist.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Erica dropped everything to be by her friend's side.\par \par \uc2 DR ERICA STRINGER-REASOR (ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I know you're nervous. I'm heading over there.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Erica examining Cheryl herself, bracing her for the battle ahead.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 She flat out said it, you're going to have chemo, you're going to have radiation, you're going to lose your hair and it's going to be tough. I now know that she probably was fighting back tears when she did her exam and told me these things.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With Erica's support, Cheryl sought treatment at the O'Neal Comprehensive Cancer Treatment Center.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I really wanted to lean on Erica, not only as a friend, but as a black woman.\par \par \uc2 DR ERICA STRINGER-REASOR (ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I knew that this was going to be more of a marathon.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The friends now speaking out about early detection and urging black women to advocate for their own health.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm learning through this process of having breast cancer that advocacy is so important and needed especially for our black community.\par \par \uc2 DR ERICA STRINGER-REASOR (ONCOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 Speak up. Ask questions. Don't delay. You're never bothering your health care provider by coming in and getting an evaluation.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Cheryl forever grateful for the support of her former classmate.\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't know how she was that strong, but I'm so grateful that I have someone that's able to help me throughout this journey because many people didn't expect me to be here this long and I am.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And as they said, speak up, ask questions and don't delay. Our thanks to both doctors for sitting down with us.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And really an important story.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eiat0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALL EYES ON GEORGIA PRESIDENT OBAMA HITS CAMPAIGN TRAIL\b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELON MUSK'S TWITTER TAKEOVER\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, CEO, CFO, CONGER, SHERR\par \par \uc2 MORTGAGES, RATES, ECONOMY, GDP, FED\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, OBAMA, GEORGIA, CAMPAIGN, VOTERS, SCHUMER\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Those are some head spinning numbers there, Rebecca, okay, thank you so much. We're gonna turn now to those midterms just 11 days away, and one of the key states in these races, Georgia is now front and center this morning. The stakes are so high, Democrats are calling on former President Obama to rally voters there, as Republicans are coming to the defense of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  facing these new allegations. Our senior White House correspondent Mary Bruce is there in Atlanta with more on this story. Good morning, Mary.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT OBAMA HITS CAMPAIGN TRAIL AS SENATE RACE TIGHTENS\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Cecilia. Yeah, we are getting into the home stretch here, the get out the vote effort now well under way. And in a must-win state like this, they're now calling in reinforcements.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, both sides calling in their party heavyweights in the final stretch here in Georgia. Democrats turning not to President Biden but his old boss, former President Obama, today with his first rally of the campaign season, throwing his support behind \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  for Governor and Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) He's locked in a tight race with Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Top GOP leaders like Senators \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Ted Cruz now rallying around Walker, coming to his defense after a second woman claimed Walker paid for her to have an abortion. The former football star, who is publicly opposed to abortion, has said it's a lie.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (SENATOR CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I can tell you right now they can't take me down because I'm a bad man, and I can tell you that right now.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But he's not answering further questions.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you know the woman behind the latest allegation? Were you in a relationship with her, Mr. Walker? Have you ever pressured a woman to have an abortion, Mr. Walker?\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With the race tightening, Walker supporters tell us the allegations, even if true, don't matter.\par \par \uc2 AVERY HOWELL (SUPPORTER OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 There's no proof, and it's 13 days before the election, so it has no bearing with me at all.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If Walker helped women, pressured a woman to get abortion in the past, does that matter?\par \par \uc2 KATHY TOTTEN (SUPPORTER OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 What you do when you're in your sinning phase of life, which, you know, I would not hold it against him.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democratic leaders showing some concern. The top Democrat in the Senate, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , caught on an open mic.\par \par \uc2 SEN CHUCK SCHUMER (DEM) (SENATE MAJORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 The state where we're (inaudible) going downhill is Georgia. And it's hard to believe that they will go for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . But our vote, our early turnout in Georgia is huge, huge.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, Schumer later doing a bit of cleanup on that, telling a reporter he thinks Democrats are doing well across the board, that he thinks they will keep the Senate. And as you guys know, as always, so much of this is going to come down to turnout. And here in Georgia they're breaking records, nearly 1.3 million ballots have already been cast, that's nearly on par with the early vote in the 2020 presidential race. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eias0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  FACING NEW ABORTION ALLEGATION WOMAN CLAIMS GEORGIA CANDIDATE PAID FOR HER PROCEDURE\b0\par\par\uc2 771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON THE ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT WILL NEW GDP REPORT SAY ABOUT ECONOMY & RECESSION CHANCES?\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION, FETTERMAN, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  FACING NEW ABORTION ALLEGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN CLAIMS GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE PAID FOR HER PROCEDURE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're going to go to politics now. And with the final midterm votes just 12 days away, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is facing a new abortion allegation in the key Senate battleground of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is tracking the race. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, George, good morning. And I can tell you, this is hardly what \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wants to be talking about so close to election day. His campaign rocked by controversy yet again, this time from a second woman. She says, even though Walker claims to oppose abortion, that he paid for her to have one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is a staunch opponent of abortion. But a second woman has now come forward claiming he also paid for her to have an abortion. The woman is remaining anonymous because, she says, she fears retaliation.\par \par \uc2 ACCUSER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he is against women having abortions. But he pressured me to have one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) During a news conference held by her attorney, Gloria Allred, she said on speaker phone that she and Walker were in a relationship when she became pregnant in 1993. The woman, whose claims have not been verified by ABC News, says the former football star gave her the money to have an abortion. She says she traveled to a clinic in Dallas, but couldn't go through with it. She says Walker was upset and drove her back to the clinic the next day, allegedly waiting outside for hours while she got the procedure. The woman and her lawyer released a voicemail, photo receipt and card, saying it's evidence of her relationship with Walker, but did not provide evidence of the abortion itself. Walker strongly denying it all.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I'm done with this foolishness.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Earlier this month, another woman, the mother of one of Walker's children, said that Walker paid for her to have an abortion. Walker denying that allegation in an interview with ABC's Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever, to your knowledge, give money to pay for the cost of an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Yes, she's lying.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The first woman provided multiple news outlet receipts from the abortion clinic and a copy of a $700-check she says Walker gave her for the abortion. Walker has acknowledged giving her the check, but said it was not for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Walker is locked in a tight race with Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock. In a statement, his campaign says Walker has a problem with taking responsibility for his actions, but even with all this controversy, this race remains a dead heat, George.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PENNYSLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATES BACK ON THE TRAIL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN: "DOING THAT DEBATE"\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Rachel in Pennsylvania, both \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Oz return to the campaign trail yesterday after the debate on Tuesday night. And Fetterman admitted that the debate was not easy for him.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEMOCRATIC SENATORIAL CANDIDATE PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 To be honest, doing that debate wasn't exactly easy. I may not get every, every word the right way, but I will always do the right thing in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, Democrats increasingly worried about this race.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, they are. His campaign even tried to lower expectations before the debate, but both campaigns felt like their candidates did what they needed to do on that debate stage. But look, this is still a very close race.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FETTERMAN & OZ HIT THE TRAIL WITH 12 DAYS TO GO\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We're told plans are coming together for President Biden to travel back to the state and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be there too right before election day, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, thanks very much. Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, George, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eiar00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOWDOWN IN PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, DEBATE, FETTERMAN, OZ, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 PEDIATRICS, RSV, FLU, COVID, MULLET, SHANE, PHILIPS, JHA\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, CHILDREN, TRIPLEDEMIC, HOSPITALS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARSENAL, SACK, BELL, KUCZKA, HARRIS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, NUCLEAR, DRILLS\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , 5G, INTERFERENCE, AIRPLANES, DELAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CANTU, LIFE SUPPORT, CASAREZ, SHOT, BRENNAND, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 CHECK WASHING, MAIL, CRIMINALS, MAIMON, PATTON\par \par \uc2 BAKER, EL CAPITAN, HARRINGTON, BALLINGER\par \par \uc2 RIHANNA, "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOWDOWN IN PENNSYLVANIA: HIGH-STAKES SENATE DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's 8:00 AM. Overnight, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Doctor Mehmet Oz face off in the high-stakes election's only debate, fighting for the state's open Senate seat. Fetterman's recent stroke front and center as the candidates clash over abortion and crime.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNING SIGNS FOR PARENTS: "TRIPLEDEMIC"\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Triple threat. As cases of respiratory viruses spike, children's hospitals across the country filling up, some running out of space. And at one Virginia high school, hundreds of students calling out sick with flu-like symptoms. The growing concern this morning over the flu, covid and rsv.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DIGITAL SIMULATIONS OF CELEBRITIES: RISE OF "DEEPFAKES"\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The rise of "deepfakes" in advertising. How some companies are using digital simulations of celebrities to sell products. From Tom Cruise to Leonardo DiCaprio, sometimes without their permission. This morning, the possible impact on consumers about knowing what's real and what isn't.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LAST MINUTE COSTUMES: HALLOWEEN CRUNCH\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Halloween costume crunch time. With just five days to go, the one costume you probably won't be able to find this year. And if you're still shopping, tips to save, the unexpected place to shop, and where to look for a costume in your own home.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK: MEN AND BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Raising awareness of breast cancer in men. The sibling survivors on a mission.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 You look up male breast cancer and we found that there was just no information out there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 How they're offering insight, education, and support to male patients.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE IN TIMES SQUARE: TONY HALE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And all hale "Veep" star Tony Hale, from "Arrested Development" to "Hocus Pocus."\par \par \uc2 TONY HALE (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 And then she got so busy because she went on something called "Gma," which is, like, some acronym for fancy people. I don't really (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America?\par \par \uc2 TONY HALE (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 But, I got her to come back.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 We are happy to have Tony back in Times Square and he's saying...\par \par \uc2 TONY HALE (ACTOR)\par \par \uc2 Oh, "Gma," good morning, America. Got it. Got it. Took some time, but I got it.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Oh, that was good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And that is why he is one of our faves.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tony Hale, always so much fun, has so much going on, and we're looking forward to catching up with him soon.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Yes were are. And our Maggie Rulli, she is live in Scotland this morning. The country is on Nat Geo's new The Best of the World 2023 list. Scotland making the list not only for its rugged beauty, but for its innovative approach to conservation. That is coming up.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also ahead, more on the social media star fighting the rise of antisemitism by sharing her family history.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221117eiaq0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INCREASING MALE BREAST CANCER AWARENESS SIBLING SURVIVORS SHARE THEIR STORY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,163 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOWDOWN IN PENNSYLVANIA\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, DEBATE, FETTERMAN, OZ, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 PEDIATRICS, RSV, FLU, COVID, MULLET, SHANE, PHILIPS, JHA\par \par \uc2 BROWNSTEIN, CHILDREN, TRIPLEDEMIC, HOSPITALS\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, ARSENAL, SACK, BELL, KUCZKA, HARRIS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, NUCLEAR, DRILLS\par \par \uc2 FAA\uc2 , 5G, INTERFERENCE, AIRPLANES, DELAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CANTU, LIFE SUPPORT, CASAREZ, SHOT, BRENNAND, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 CHECK WASHING, MAIL, CRIMINALS, MAIMON, PATTON\par \par \uc2 BAKER, EL CAPITAN, HARRINGTON, BALLINGER\par \par \uc2 RIHANNA, "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOWDOWN IN PENNSYLVANIA: HIGH-STAKES SENATE DEBATE\par \par \uc2 PENNSYLVANIA, SENATE, FETTERMAN, OZ, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 FLU, COVID, RSV, CHILDREN, SPIKE\par \par \uc2 AIRLINE, GIVEAWAY, MIDDLE SEAT, VIRGIN\par \par \uc2 HALLOWEEN, COSTUMES, \uc2 EBAY\uc2 , DAHMER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 TUCKER, HOLOCAUST, STORY, TIKTOK, GREENBLATT\par \par \uc2 NATIONAL GEOGRAPHIC, BEAUTY, SCOTLAND, GIBBENS, ROWSE\par \par \uc2 "REBOOT", COSTELLO, PUMPKIN, HACKS\par \par \uc2 HALE, "THE MYSTERIOUS BENEDICT SOCIETY"\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, MEN, SIBLINGS, SINGER, WOLF\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now on "Gma" with "Thriving in Pink." But this morning, we're adding some blue, as well, increasing awareness for breast cancer in men. While men account for about one percent of all breast cancer cases in the US, they are more likely to be diagnosed in more advanced stages. One pair of sibling survivors hopes to change that.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Siblings Harvey Singer and Vicki Singer Wolf have always had a special bond.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 Harvey and I were a little over three years apart. It's great having a big brother.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I was very protective of my sister.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But, something would happen that he couldn't protect her from.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I had breast cancer the first time when I was 37 years of age. It was shortly after our mother had been diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I didn't understand the ramifications of it all. I just thought it was, oh, my mom had it, she had a lumpectomy. She's fine, she'll be fine.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As Vicki fought breast cancer, her concern would quickly turn to her brother.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I had my genetic testing completed and I found out that I carried the BRCA-2 genetic mutation.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 She called me and goes well, you could get breast cancer. And I said, well, I'm a guy, I'm not getting breast cancer. It's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) What he didn't know was that having inherited mutations of the brca gene can increase the risk of developing breast cancer. Soon, he began noticing some changes.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 I had noticed a change in my left breast area, especially my nipple was folding in a little bit and I didn't think that much about it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Four months later, a hug would bring on another startling symptom.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 This friend of mine, big guy, hugged me. I got this shooting pain that went right from my left nipple into my left ear.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in October of 2008, Harvey was diagnosed with stage 1 breast cancer. Now his sister Vicki was thrust into the protector role, searching for resources.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 You look up male breast cancer and we found that there was just no information out there.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They said this was the moment they noticed the breast cancer field was geared more towards women.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 You get shuffled into a room for a mammogram and there's nothing but pink gowns and they don't want you to be there because you might see another woman's breasts. They shuffle you around like you don't belong. It was very unnerving.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Knowing something had to change, Vicki and Harvey created His Breast Cancer Awareness.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 It's a small foundation that we put together that we do with our love and our hearts.\par \par \uc2 VICKI SINGER WOLF (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 Because we were not able to find information out there and the harshness of the stigma, the experiences that he had being a man diagnosed with breast cancer, we just knew we had to make a change.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their goal is to offer insight, education, and support to male breast cancer patients.\par \par \uc2 HARVEY SINGER (CO-FOUNDER HIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS FOUNDATION)\par \par \uc2 This diagnosis is going to change your life. It's only one percent of all the breast cancers in the United States, but the mortality rate is higher because we're guys. We don't go for mammograms, we don't go for screenings, we ignore symptoms. Our hope is that we'll keep doing this, we'll make guys more aware, they'll get checked sooner. You're helping save men's lives.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I am so happy we're shining a light on this. I know my brother, and I know your, your brother...\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) My brother, too.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...as well have gone through this and they say men ignore signs, don't get tested, feel like it's just something that doesn't affect them, but I know I've lived through it, as well as you, Robin, and our brothers, in which I'm happy they both are fine.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible) well.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank goodness. And some possible signs of male breast cancer to watch out for are a lump or swelling, redness or flaky skin and irritation or dimpling in the breast area. Some men also experience a discharge or pain and, you know what, make sure you heed all those signs.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Didn't Matthew Knowles, doesn't he do...\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Matthew Knowles. I did an interview with Matthew Knowles a few years ago where he went through it and he wanted to bring more awareness to it, as well. And I think that there are so many guys, I love that His Breast Cancer Awareness because they say you go and you feel like you don't belong.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So I think it's very important (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you for bringing that to us.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221117eiaq0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ACTRESS TALKS NEW MOVIE & WOMEN'S RIGHTS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,275 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GERMAN, TELLES, HENRY, REID, FERRARO, MURDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, WALKER, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 BANKS, "CALL JANE", ABORTION, WEAVER\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Actress Elizabeth Banks is passionate about her new film "Call Jane," and she's hopeful that the story of a 1960s suburban woman seeking an abortion will resonate and inspire men and women today. She stopped by and spoke with my "Nightline" co-anchor, \uc2 Byron Pitts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 My children have fewer rights than I did, growing up. And I am now fighting for something that my mother and my grandmother already won.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This particular film, is it a space in which your activism and your acting are aligned?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 I think when you're an artist and you can make something that aligns with your values, there's a real special significance to it.\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "CALL JANE"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Take a deep breath.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In her latest film, "Call Jane," Elizabeth Banks is stepping into a role that couldn't be more timely.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 (VO) The film coming out right now does feel like there's a real urgency in it. And hopefully, this film presents people with the real circumstances pre-Roe America, in which women were in back alleys and dying.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) "Call Jane" is set in the late 1960s, when abortion was widely illegal. Banks plays Joy, a suburban Chicago housewife forced to make a difficult decision about a life-threatening pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Griffin, your pregnancy is endangering your life.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 The character I play, you know, she's a 40-year-old woman who finds herself pregnant, who never in a million years thought that she would have to seek abortion health care.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Inspired by a real group known as The Jane Collective...\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Joy?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Get in.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Joy turns to an underground network of women to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 So, which one of you is Jane?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 We're all Jane.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their leader, played by Sigourney Weaver.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 This woman's pretty judgmental of people who seek abortion health care and has a real political awakening by realizing that, like for everybody, it's life and death.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The writer, director, producer and actress has made a name for herself playing dynamic characters on screen...\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "THE HUNGER GAMES"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Happy Hunger Games.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...in "The Hunger Games"...\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 May the odds be ever in your favor.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ...and in "Pitch Perfect."\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM MOVIE, "PITCH PERFECT"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 John, you're so right, everything else seems wrong.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Banks, "Call Jane" is both personal and political. It's the continuation of years of activism and advocacy. She chairs the Creative Council at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM PODCAST, "MY BODY, MY PODCAST"\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Hi. Elizabeth Banks.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And last year, Banks released the six-part podcast called "My Body, My Podcast."\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And in it, you advocate for men to get involved in the conversation. What's the conversation, and why is this engagement important to you?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 I'm raising two sons. So, my goal is to involve them, particularly, in these conversations, and to educate them about women's rights.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What do you say to those who say Hollywood should not be engaged in taking sides in these very sensitive, personal issues?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. I'll just say this. I'm here today as a storyteller who made a fictionalized version of a historical event. That's what I did, as an artist. I talk to you about my stance on abortion as a citizen of the United States. I have every right to talk to anybody I want to about how I vote.\par \par \uc2 ACTRESS (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 You don't have to tell my mom, right?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 That's between you and your mom.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) "Call Jane." It comes out at a particular time in our nation's history. Accident, coincidental?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 We knew that we were making a film that would hopefully honor the story of The Jane Collective of Chicago, this group of women who provided safe abortions at a time when abortion was illegal and put too many women into dangerous life-threatening situations.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some members of the real Jane Collective were even arrested. Charges were later dropped after the Supreme Court's historic 1973 decision that gave women the constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Even though this is an issue that directly impacts women, men somehow have this unbalanced participation in the process.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Is there a chance that she can survive the pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Maybe 50%.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Resulting in the birth of a healthy child.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 I'm here. I'm right here.\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I don't know, gentlemen.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 You know, that was the reality, back then. Eleven thousand patients had a free abortion health care from The Jane Collective in the late '60s, in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 It reminds me that abortion is normal. I think the whole point of the film is choice.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The movie also addresses the lingering inequities experienced by women of color.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Well, these women need our help, just as much as the ones with money.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 We can't change what it costs, Joy. It's economics.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 It's interesting how economics always seems to mean that black women get screwed.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) There is the series of moments in the movie where it seems that you deal with the issue of abortion and race and equity.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm. There's no doubt that abortion bans disproportionately affect women of color in this country, and that the maternal mortality rates for black women, especially, are abysmal.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Banks is hoping "Call Jane" makes an impact, especially in the lead-up to the midterms. She and her co-stars are teaming up with abortion providers to screen the film across the country.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 We must protect independent abortion clinics and ensure that everyone has access to the reproductive health care they deserve.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Are there particular audiences that you want to see this film?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("CALL JANE")\par \par \uc2 If the movie can do anything, it can hopefully inspire people's empathy for a path that they may never walk, but other women will feel that they need to.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Byron. "Call Jane" is in theaters Friday.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIHANNA RETURNS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, the wait is almost over. Rihanna fans are all fired up about her new single, "Lift Me Up."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221028eiaq00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 EXPLOSIVE NEW ALLEGATION A SECOND WOMAN COMES FORWARD WITH NEW ABORTION ACCUSATION\b0\par\par\uc2 498 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 GERMAN, TELLES, HENRY, REID, FERRARO, MURDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, WALKER, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we turn now to the explosive new allegation against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . An unnamed new woman has come forward, accusing the Republican Senate hopeful of pressuring her into having an abortion. Here's ABC's Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A second woman is now coming forward, claiming Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for her to have an abortion. The woman, who is remaining anonymous, spoke via speakerphone held in the office of her attorney, Gloria Allred. The woman claims she and Walker were in a relationship, and that when she became pregnant in 1993, he pressured her to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ALLEGED ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He encouraged me to have an abortion and gave me the money to do so.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The woman, whose claims have not been verified by ABC News, said she went to a Dallas clinic, but couldn't go through with it. She said she left and told Walker, who allegedly became upset. The next morning, she claims, he drove her back to the clinic himself and waited outside until she had had the procedure. The woman provided what she calls evidence of the relationship, but not of the actual abortion itself. And today, Walker denied it all.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm done with this foolishness. I've already told people this is a lie, and I'm not going to entertain and continue to carry the lie along. And I also want to let you know, I didn't kill JFK either.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker has also previously denied paying for the abortion of another woman, the mother of one his children. She's provided multiple news outlets receipts from the abortion clinic and a copy of a $700-check, which she says Walker gave her to pay for the abortion.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever have a conversation with this woman, at any time, about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, she's lying. Yeah, she's lying. Yes, she's lying.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker later acknowledged he did give her the check, but claimed it was not for an abortion. As a candidate, Walker is a staunch opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm pro-life. I believe, I believe in, I believe in life. I'm a Christian. I believe in life.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Linsey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ELIZABETH BANKS\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When we come back, "Hunger Games" actress Elizabeth Banks on her new film, "Call Jane," and why it couldn't have been more timely.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221028eiaq00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 NEW ACCUSATIONS AGAINST WALKER SECOND WOMAN CLAIMS HE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 573 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 VERDICT, BROOKS, SORENSON, PARADE\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW ACCUSATIONS AGAINST WALKER\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the explosive new allegation against Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia, just 13 days, now, until Election Day, a second woman now alleging that he pressured her to have an abortion. This issue is taking centerstage in this race, because, as a candidate, walker has been a staunch opponent of abortion rights. Again, tonight, Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECOND WOMAN CLAIMS HE PRESSURED HER TO HAVE ABORTION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A second woman is now coming forward, claiming Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for her to have an abortion. The woman, who is remaining anonymous, spoke via speakerphone in a news conference held by her attorney, Gloria Allred. The woman claims she and Walker were in a relationship, and that when she became pregnant in 1993, he pressured her to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (ALLEGED ACCUSER)\par \par \uc2 He encouraged me to have an abortion and gave me the money to do so.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The woman, whose claims have not been verified by ABC News, says she went to a Dallas clinic, but couldn't go through with it. She says she left and told Walker, who allegedly became upset. The next morning, she claims he drove her back to the clinic himself and waited outside until she had had the procedure. The woman provided what she calls evidence of the relationship, but not of the actual abortion itself. And today, Walker denied it all.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I'm done with this foolishness. I've already told people this is a lie, and I'm going to entertain, continue to carry a lie along. And I also want to let you know, I didn't kill JFK, either.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker has also previously denied paying for the abortion of another woman, the mother of one of his children. She has provided multiple news outlets receipts from the abortion clinic and a copy of a $700-check that she says Walker gave her to pay for the abortion.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever have a conversation with this woman, at any time, about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Yes, she's lying. Yeah, she's lying. Yes, she's lying.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker later acknowledged he did give her the check, but claimed it was not for an abortion. As a candidate, Walker is a staunch opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE IN GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I'm pro-life. I believe, I believe in, I believe in life. I'm a Christian. I believe in life.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) David, the woman who came forward today insists that this is not about politics. She claims that she's an independent and voted for Donald Trump in 2016 and in 2020. As for Walker's opponent, Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock, his campaign released a statement saying that Walker, quote, "has a problem with the truth and a problem taking responsibility for his actions." David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Linsey Davis, on this, again, tonight. Linsey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221028eiaq00003\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CONTENTIOUS SENATE DEBATE PIVOTAL SHOWDOWN IN BATTLE FOR PA SEAT\b0\par\par\uc2 829 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 VERDICT, BROOKS, SORENSON, PARADE\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW ACCUSATIONS AGAINST WALKER\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN, OZ, MIDTERM ELECTIONS, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CONTENTIOUS SENATE DEBATE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And now to the other key Senate race making news, tonight, the Pennsylvania Senate race, which also could determine who controls the Senate, front and center, tonight, after the debate last night. The Democrat, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , recovering from a stroke. On that stage, what he said about the stroke at the beginning of the debate, and then how he performed while answering the questions.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) What his campaign is now saying, tonight. What voters in Pennsylvania are saying. And Dr. Oz and his answer on abortion rights, saying that women, their doctors, and local political leaders should decide this issue.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, in Pennsylvania, again, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PIVOTAL SHOWDOWN IN BATTLE FOR PA SEAT\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's campaign saying their candidate did what he needed to do in his only debate with Dr. Mehmet Oz, knowing it was going to be difficult after having a stroke. At the beginning, Fetterman addressing what he called the elephant in the room.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I had a stroke. He's never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together. But it knocked me down, but I'm going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The questions and answers were closed captioned on large screens, so Fetterman could read them to help with lingering auditory processing issues. At times, he did stumble over his words. When asked why he changed his position on the issue of fracking...\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I do support fracking, and I don't, I don't, I support fracking, and I stand, and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fetterman was asked why he has not released his detailed medical records.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GENERAL \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I believe, if my doctor believes that I'm fit to serve, and that's what I believe is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Dr. Oz facing pointed questions, too. Pressed on his position on abortion rights and whether he would vote for a federal ban on abortion at 15 weeks. It was the answer from Oz that made immediate headlines.\par \par \uc2 DR MEHMET OZ (REP) (PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I want women, doctors, local political leaders letting the democracy that's always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward, so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, Oz's team standing by that answer, to include local political leaders in that decision. Meanwhile, Fetterman's campaign today held a press conference of doctors speaking out against Dr. Oz. We asked, where's Fetterman's doctor?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why are we hearing from you, instead of Fetterman's doctors? And why hasn't he released any of his medical records in public?\par \par \uc2 DOCTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 So, first, I have to push back against your answer, because he has released a detailed report from his doctor saying that he's fit to serve.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, voters we spoke with were split on the debate. Tom Lawlor (PH), an attorney and Democrat, still undecided.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure if Mr. Fetterman's people should have him in this race. I feel bad for him. If my decision had to be made today, I think I would lean for Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 But Richard Ferro (PH), a retiree from Pittsburgh, says he's sticking with Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Okay, he has a little bit of trouble talking. His mind is okay. I mean, if he, if it affected his mind in some way, I could say, okay, but he's still a smart guy. It has nothing to do with his speech.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You still have full confidence in Fetterman?\par \par \uc2 VOTER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Right, I do. In fact, I already sent my vote in.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, with us from Pennsylvania, tonight. In fact, in Pittsburgh. And, Rachel, really both campaigns here saying they accomplished what they needed to do last night, which we know is the one and only debate between Fetterman and Oz?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, David. Fetterman's campaign says they are thrilled by his performance, that they raised $1 million since the debate. President Biden and former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  reportedly expected to visit this state and campaign for Fetterman in the coming days. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  expected to visit Pennsylvania, as well. He's backing Dr. Mehmet Oz. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel Scott, in a key state, again, tonight. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221028eiaq00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HAIR CARE LAWSUIT CLAIMS UTERINE CANCER CAUSED BY CHEMICALS\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, HARRIS, JONES, FAULSTICH, KUCZKA, SACK\par \par \uc2 KITAGAWA, BRAND, FLU, RSV, CHILDREN, COVID-19, SIGNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PEDIATRIC HOSPITALIZATIONS ON THE RISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 75% OF BEDS FOR KIDS FILLED NATIONWIDE AS RESPIRATORY ILLNESSES SURGE\par \par \uc2 CRIST, DESANTIS, MIDTERM ELECTIONS, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTIONS, CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWO WEEKS TO HIGH-STAKES MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF PLAY IN THE BATTLE FOR CONGRESS\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOAN FORGIVENESS, BIDEN, JEAN-PIERRE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUDENT LOAN FORGIVENESS SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ADMINISTRATION RESPONDS TO GOP CHALLENGE TO PROGRAM\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, BIDEN, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW PUSH FOR PEACE TALKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROGRESSIVE LAWMAKERS URGE BIDEN TO PURSUE "PROACTIVE DIPLOMATIC PUSH"\par \par \uc2 GRINER, APPEAL, HEARING, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: APPEAL HEARING FOR BRITTNEY GRINER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 WNBA\uc2  STAR SERVING NINE-YEAR DRUG SENTENCE IN RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WEST, ADIDAS, ANTISEMITISM, GREENBLATT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADIDAS TERMINATES PARTNERSHIP WITH KANYE WEST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GROWING OUTRAGE OVER RAPPER'S ANTISEMITIC REMARKS\par \par \uc2 UNILEVER, RECALL, DRY SHAMPOO, CANCER\par \par \uc2 JORDAN, TRIBUTE, CAR, CRASH, MULLALLY, HAYES, BIALIK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LESLIE JORDAN PASSES AWAY AT 67\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRIBUTES POUR IN FOR BELOVED "WILL & GRACE" ACTOR\par \par \uc2 CLIMATE CHANGE, GLACIER MELT, SWITZERLAND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, HARRIS, SACK, FAULSICH\par \par \uc2 AGING, DEMENTIA, WARNING SIGNS, TIPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AGING & DEMENTIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY: ONE-THIRD OF OLDER ADULTS ALREADY SHOWING SIGNS\par \par \uc2 LAWSUIT, UTERINE CANCER, GAINERS, CRUMP, MITCHELL\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Back now with our "GMA Cover Story." A woman who developed uterine cancer after using chemical hair relaxers or straighteners for almost two decades is suing major hair care companies.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAIR CARE LAWSUIT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN CLAIMS HER UTERINE CANCER WAS CAUSED BY CHEMICALS STRAIGHTENING PRODUCTS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And this comes after a National Institute of Health study suggested a link between chemical hair straightening products and increased risk of the disease. Mona Kosar Abdi is here with the story. Good morning, Mona.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) George, good morning. Hair relaxer products promise to turn curly, frizzy hair into smooth, straight locks. But now, a Missouri woman says, for her, trying to fit that societal mold came at a dangerous cost, her health.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, major beauty brand \uc2 L'Oreal\uc2  and other makers of chemical hair straighteners under fire.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MITCHELL (FILED LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 I am the first voice of many voices to come. We'll stand up to these companies and say, no more.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Army veteran Jenny Mitchell and her attorneys, including Ben Crump, filing a lawsuit against five companies claiming Mitchell's uterine cancer was directly caused by chemicals found in hair relaxers sold by those brands, which Mitchell said she started using as a child.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MITCHELL (FILED LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 I underwent a full hysterectomy. Yeah, that was harder than the cancer diagnosis because I will never be able to carry my own kids.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP (ATTORNEY)\par \par \uc2 The science was always there for everybody to see, and so we don't accept willful ignorance.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAIR PRODUCTS & UTERINE CANCER STUDY\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mitchell was diagnosed with uterine cancer at 28. This lawsuit comes as the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  released a study that suggested a link with hair straightening products and an increased risk of uterine cancer in women who used them. Though the cancer is rare, black women develop it at a higher rate than women of other races.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MITCHELL (FILED LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 As an African American woman, it is the societal norm to have your hair look a certain way. Don't wear your natural hair because it looks unprofessional.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Researchers didn't identify a particular ingredient in the trial. They also state more research needs to be done to link hair relaxers to cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR MADELIENE GAINERS (DERMATOLOGIST)\par \par \uc2 I tell my patients to avoid these products and transition to natural. I think that it's going to make a huge difference in our health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mitchell wants to share her experience with other women.\par \par \uc2 JENNY MITCHELL (FILED LAWSUIT)\par \par \uc2 I'm hoping to be a voice for millions other African American women out there. I don't want another me at 28 not being able to have a family of your own.\par \par \uc2 MONA KOSAR ABDI (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Two other individual lawsuits have been filed in California and here in New York. We reached out to \uc2 L'Oreal\uc2  for comment regarding the lawsuit, but we have not heard back yet. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, important to share. Okay, Mona, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eiap000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CELEBRATING CAREGIVERS HONORING THE PEOPLE AT OUR SIDE DURING ILLNESS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,147 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PUSHING BACK ON "DIRTY BOMB" CLAIMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH UKRAINIAN COMMANDER\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, DIRTY BOMB, ALLIES, SYRSKYI, TROOPS, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 UVALDE, MARIN, DPS, SHOOTING, VIDEO, DOOR\par \par \uc2 STORM, SNOW, WINDS, RAIN, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, ELECTION, SENATE, MASTO, LAXALT, POLL\par \par \uc2 UK, TURMOIL, JOHNSON, PM, TRUSS, SUNAK\par \par \uc2 MLB, ASTROS, \uc2 PHILLIES\uc2 , WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER THREAT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "BLACK PANTHER: WAKANDA FOREVER"\par \par \uc2 KIDNAP, ADAMSON, VIDEO, BURGUAN\par \par \uc2 REPORT CARD, NATION, MATH, TESTING, SCORES\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOAN, COURT, APPEAL, GOP\par \par \uc2 HALLOWEEN, CANDY, PRICES, INFLATION, CID, COUPONS\par \par \uc2 QUAD AXLE, MALININ, SOCCER, DUNN\par \par \uc2 SNOW, STORM, WINTER\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM, SNOW\par \par \uc2 MATH, TEST SCORES, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FALLING BEHIND?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "NATION'S REPORT CARD" DRAMATIC DROP IN TEST SCORES\par \par \uc2 WORLD SERIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORLD SERIES SHOWDOWN SET\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ASTROS & \uc2 PHILLIES\uc2  ADVANCE TO FACE OFF IN THE FALL CLASSIC\par \par \uc2 TRIPLEDEMIC, RSV, CHILDREN, FLU, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 ANXIETY, LIONS, "THE ANXIETY AUDIT", SIMMONS, TIPS\par \par \uc2 HEUGHAN, "WAYPOINTS", "OUTLANDER"\par \par \uc2 "POWER TRIP", LAKE, YOUNGKIN, MIDTERM ELECTIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "POWER TRIP" HITS THE CAMPAIGN TRAIL IN ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRIBUTE, CAREGIVERS, HOLTZ, KRUSE, THOMAS, LAIGN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING CAREGIVERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HONORING THE PEOPLE AT OUR SIDE DURING ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Couldn't say it better. We love, love hearing all those thank yous for the wonderful caregivers out there. That is what this morning's "Thriving in Pink" is all about, how caregivers are a thriver's secret weapon no matter what condition they're facing. And my sweet Amber and I have been there, we know firsthand what the physical and emotional toll can be on both. But too often, the caregivers are forgotten. We have three thrivers and their caregivers with us this morning, Serena and her husband Maurice. I love saying that name. Beverly and her wife Heidi. Ebony and her mother Kimberly. These caregivers think that we're honoring their thrivers. No, it's about you.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 Ooh.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's about, yes, yes. We surprise, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE HOLTZ (CAREGIVER)\par \par \uc2 What?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, okay. Okay. We want you to take a look at what they want you to know about your love and your care. Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thrivers around the world know it takes a village.\par \par \uc2 SERENA HOLTZ (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 When someone is diagnosed with breast cancer, everybody joins in.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) When Serena Holtz learned in May her breast cancer metastasized, spreading into liver, spine and lungs, her husband Maurice there by her side.\par \par \uc2 SERENA HOLTZ (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 He drove me to the appointments, he sat with me, and he was my second set of eyes and ears. He cooks and cleans and does the grocery shopping. He'd wash my hair.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A devoted husband and cancer thriver himself. Maurice has the constant can-do spirit that keeps Serena smiling.\par \par \uc2 SERENA HOLTZ (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 That's what we do, in sickness and in health.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY KRUSE (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 When I told her it's cancer, she just was, all right, we're going to figure it out, you know, we'll do this. We can do this.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For mom of two Beverly Kruse, after her diagnosis in December, her wife Heidi going above and beyond.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY KRUSE (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Heidi really stepped up. She has a full-time job of her own and she did every pickup, every drop off.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Through it all, Heidi even encouraging Beverly to pursue her master's.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY KRUSE (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 At the time I was diagnosed, I thought, oh, I can't do everything and I'm going to have to stop something and it's just not possible. And she told me, no, it's been a life goal and she said we'll do it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Ebony Thomas helping her mom through breast cancer in 2010. But in July, Ebony receiving her own diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 EBONY THOMAS (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 If my mom can do it, I know I can do it because she definitely raised me to be really strong. After (inaudible) and for good.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kimberly now her daughter's primary caregiver and biggest cheerleader.\par \par \uc2 EBONY THOMAS (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 She's my Uber driver, she's my therapist. She makes sure I eat, because sometimes I don't even have the energy and I'm truly grateful.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, they're sharing a salute to their caregivers for all they do.\par \par \uc2 EBONY THOMAS (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 I love you and I appreciate you. Everything you do does not go unnoticed. You are the best.\par \par \uc2 SERENA HOLTZ (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Maurice, I love you very much, thank you for loving this hard-headed woman.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY KRUSE (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Heidi, getting through this year would not have been possible without you and your support of the family. You're a blessing to everybody you touch.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow. See there? Now, Amber was in on the surprise. But you all didn't know this was happening. Did you?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 I did not know.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, because Amber taught me what's like to be a caregiver. Let me get through this segment, okay?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 Okay.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how, you know, we're not supposed to take the attention away from those that we love.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But at times, we do need some help. How does, for your daughter to say to you that you are her rock, that you are, yes, you are her mother, you're her best friend, what do those words mean to you?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 It means everything because sometimes as a mom you do the things that you need to know, to do for your child, you just do them, because you're always there. And I'm just so surprised really right now because I thought I was here for her. And I was like, I'm not going to say much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Aw.\par \par \uc2 MOTHER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 But I'm just grateful and I just always want to be there for her. That's it.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh. You all are so beautiful together. Heidi, I'm going to used, now these are Beverly's words here, you managed the whole circus. That's how she says it because between the kids and the pets and your elderly mother lives with you all as well, but you've also been able to operate from a place of joy, a place of joy. Has that ever wavered or how have you been able to keep that up?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI (WIFE OF BEVERLY KRUSE)\par \par \uc2 I don't, Beverly is so strong that I think that, like she said, when you're in it, you're in it. And I say choose joy with whatever you do. And sometimes things go right and sometimes things don't, and it's a challenge. We were blessed to have two wonderful kids that stepped up and took on 17 chickens, one rabbit.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That is a circus.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI (WIFE OF BEVERLY KRUSE)\par \par \uc2 A cat and two dogs. It's a lot and there's a lot of feeding and a lot of things that go into that. And, you know, we had a great support team with team Beverly and she is one tough chick.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We see that.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI (WIFE OF BEVERLY KRUSE)\par \par \uc2 So, it was really easy honestly and she is a strong woman that I'm proud to be next to.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's beautiful. And we do need to give a nod because we know there's a lot of support teams. That is not just the one, that you are the primary caregivers but there are so many that are part of our team.\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Your friends, my family. All right, Maurice, all right. In sickness and health, Maurice, you heard, Serena say that. So, what are your feelings right now, Maurice?\par \par \uc2 MAURICE HOLTZ (CAREGIVER)\par \par \uc2 I just love her so much that I would do anything for her anyway. And when she was diagnosed with cancer, I'm a natural-born caregiver.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Oh.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE HOLTZ (CAREGIVER)\par \par \uc2 My grandmother was deaf and got hit by a bus when I was a kid, and so, I, we didn't put people in nursing homes, we kept them in the house.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Amen.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE HOLTZ (CAREGIVER)\par \par \uc2 And so, being a caregiver for her was easy. Most of my jobs have been working with people in crisis. And with her diagnosis the second time, I just stepped right up and did things she thought I wasn't able to do and I just I would do anything for this woman. And I made a promise to her when we've got married that I would keep her laughing.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you have done that.\par \par \uc2 MAURICE HOLTZ (CAREGIVER)\par \par \uc2 I do that.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I can tell. I can just tell by that face. Can you tell like that? But the role reversal that you all have gone through, and Ebony and Kimberly as well. And we have gone through that, you know, Amber was there for me twice, you know, just really there in every way kind of way. And now, I'm doing my best to be there for you. So, how has the role reversal, I feel so weird talking, how has the role reversal been for you, honey?\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Well, I'll tell you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay.\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 For me, it was really difficult being the patient. I found it much easier to be the caregiver. I had, you know, I knew what I had to do. I had to keep comfortable, keep family and friends in tune to what was going on, listen to the doctors, that was the plan. That was easy for me. Flip it around being the patient, not so much. We're so yin and yang.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 You're very coachable.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I love being the patient. I mean, I love it. But I'm a better patient than I think that I am as a caregiver, because all I do is cry, it seems like I'm so emotional all the time because I know, I know what she's going through.\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because I walked that path and I think that's what we figure out that part of the...\par \par \uc2 AMBER LAIGN (THRIVER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah. But you also told me and you reminded me, you know, every journey is everyone's own journey. So, do not compare. But also, it's as much mental as it is physical. And not knowing with my treatment had so many hiccups and not having like a clear path, that was difficult for me.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. She had a procedure this weekend and it's just something ongoing. I know Serena you said the same thing as well. But, you know what, thank you, honey. And thank you all. And I love the fact that you thought it was about the thrivers and we do think of you and we're so proud of each and every one of you. But today is about your caregivers, and we have some good friends at Elements and also Dry Bar. They're going to give you a spa day, they're going to give you a big caregiver's or need support and you'll get a day of spa. And you know what, we'll let the thrivers go along with you, how about that? We'll let them go as well. But just a little something to give you a little TLC that you all need.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay? And caregivers need support to give their best to their loved ones. You can find links to information and support groups on our website. To Kimberly, Heidi and Maurice, and to you Amber, thank you, thank you, thank you, thank you, thank you for all that you do. All right?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY (MOTHER OF EBONY THOMAS)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You got it. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221109eiao0000r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 TWINS SHARE BREAST CANCER JOURNEYS AFTER ONE'S DIAGNOSIS, SISTER GOT RISK-REDUCING MASTECTOMY\b0\par\par\uc2 1,213 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE INVESTIGATION: DONALD TRUMP SUBPOENAED\par \par \uc2 SUBPOENA, JANUARY 6TH, TRUMP, TEXTS, RECORDS\par \par \uc2 JANUARY 6TH, SUBPOENA, TRUMP, OPTIONS\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOAN\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, PUTIN, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, WAR, INVASION\par \par \uc2 DEMONSTRATIONS, PROTESTS, IRAN, AMINI\par \par \uc2 VOTING, NEVADA, MIDTERMS, SENATE\par \par \uc2 ELECTIONS, BALLOTS, POLL WORKERS, HOBBS, \uc2 FBI\par \par \uc2 BURGLARIES, CALIFORNIA, HUNTINGTON BEACH\par \par \uc2 COLLEGE FOOTBALL, GEE, WIDOW, HEAD TRAUMA\par \par \uc2 PHILADELPHIA, \uc2 PHILLIES\uc2 , NLCS, SERIES, \uc2 PADRES\par \par \uc2 RSV, RESPIRATORY, CHILDREN, HOSPITALS, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 HOUSING, DEALS, SLUMP\par \par \uc2 TURKEY, SHORTAGE, PRICES, THANKSGIVING, COSGROVE\par \par \uc2 SWIFT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, SUBPOENA, BANNON, CAPITOL, RIOT\par \par \uc2 RSV, BELMONT, PEACOE, FAUCI, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALARMING SURGE IN RSV PATIENTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHILDREN'S HOSPITALS OVERWHELMED BY RESPIRATORY ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, "MIDNIGHTS"\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TAYLOR SWIFT'S MUSIC VIDEO, "ANTI-HERO"\par \par \uc2 SHAKIRA\par \par \uc2 SHORTAGE, CHILD CARE, CANDIA, SPINA, BALLIVAN\par \par \uc2 CARMEN, LOPEZ, BREAST CANCER, KESMODEL, SCREENING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And turning now to "Thriving in Pink," all October long telling stories of courage and resilience.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWINS SHARE BREAST CANCER JOURNEYS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AFTER ONE'S DIAGNOSIS, HER SISTER GOT RISK-REDUCING MASTECTOMY\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This morning, the journey of identical twins, one diagnosed with breast cancer and the other getting a risk-reducing mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Identical twins Mary Carmen and Lucy Carmen Lopez are as close as sisters can be, growing even closer when Lucy Carmen was diagnosed with breast cancer at the age of 41.\par \par \uc2 LUCY CARMEN LOPEZ (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I was terrified. When I heard it, I heard the word cancer, I'm like, oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMEN (SISTER)\par \par \uc2 She calls me. Yeah, and all I hear is I have cancer. I'm going in for a lumpectomy.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lucy Carmen seeking treatment at the Sylvester Comprehensive Cancer Center at the \uc2 University of Miami\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LUCY CARMEN LOPEZ (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 So, that's where I met Dr. Kesmodel. Immediately, she said, you guys are twins. I'm going to adopt your sister as well. She also needs genetic testing.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her genetic test revealing the BRCA 2 gene mutation, which is linked to a higher case of breast and ovarian cancers.\par \par \uc2 LUCY CARMEN LOPEZ (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 My doctor called me, she said, Lucy, your course of action is going to change. You're no longer getting a lumpectomy. I highly recommend a mastectomy because you're BRCA 2 positive. I'm concerned about your sister, she needs to get testing, as well.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Together, the sisters deciding to undergo a double mastectomy. Lucy Carmen first to remove the cancer and Mary Carmen next as a risk-reducing measure.\par \par \uc2 LUCY CARMEN LOPEZ (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 I said, I'm going to do this. My sister is going to do this. And I have to be strong, not only for me, but for her that's going to undergo this.\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMEN (SISTER)\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's scary, but you're going to go ahead and get your double mastectomy and I'm with you right after.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The sisters finding strength in their bond, taking turns being patient and caregiver.\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMEN (SISTER)\par \par \uc2 She went through it initially in the flesh. And I went through it emotionally, taking care of her.\par \par \uc2 LUCY CARMEN LOPEZ (DIAGNOSED WITH BREAST CANCER)\par \par \uc2 That's what literally saved my life and my sister's.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And joining us now is the twin's surgeon, Dr. Susan Kesmodel. Thank you so much for being with us this morning. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAMILY HISTORY & RISK-REDUCING CANCER SURGERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO ASK YOUR DOCTOR ABOUT THE TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Thanks for having me. It's really emotional listening to their story.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah. And Mary Carmen, speaking about her story, had such a difficult decision to make about whether to have that risk-reducing mastectomy. What are some of the primary factors she and others should consider about risk?\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Well, so, with some of these gene mutations, as you know, she and her sister have a BRCA 2 mutation. The lifetime risk of developing breast cancer can be as high as 80%. So, in those women, when we see them, we do discuss what treatment options we have. And risk-reducing mastectomy is one of the things we consider. Other options that we discuss include enhanced screening protocols, including breast MRI or sometimes chemo prevention, where we use medications to reduce breast cancer risk. So, family history is very important that women know their family history on both sides of the family for multiple generations.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LOOK AT CANCER HISTORY IN FIRST, SECOND & THIRD-DEGREE RELATIVES\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 And then, also understand what the modifiable and nonmodifiable risk factors are for breast cancer, so that they can discuss their personal risk factors with their primary care physician and discuss an appropriate screening protocol for them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CHECK MATERNAL & PATERNAL RELATIVES\par \par \uc2 WILL REEVE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what type of testing should people get to determine whether or not a risk-reducing mastectomy is right for them?\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, sometimes when we look at a person's family history, we suspect that there might be a hereditary cancer syndrome. This is typically where we see multiple generations of cancers. So, in cases of high-risk breast mutations, we see multiple generations where women have developed breast cancer, frequently at an early age of onset or male breast cancer or other kinds of cancer like ovarian, pancreas and prostate. So, if we see that kind of cancer history in the family, we might suspect that they have a hereditary cancer syndrome and they could be sent to a genetics, for genetics consultation to determine if genetic testing is indicated.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, we saw there Lucy Carmen's cancer was found because of early detection. What are the current guidelines for who should be getting mammograms and just how often?\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 So, we currently follow the guidelines from the \uc2 American College of Radiology\uc2 , which recommend that average risk women start screening at the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAST CANCER SCREENING GUIDELINES\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 We know that screening mammograms do reduce breast cancer mortality and the greatest reduction in deaths is seen when women start at the age of 40. When we suspect that a woman is at higher risk or know that they're at higher risk because of family history, or because they have a gene mutation or a high-risk breast lesion, which has been identified, we frequently start screening earlier and we may add supplemental screening such as breast MRI.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That screening so incredibly important. Dr. Kesmodel, thank you so much for being with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 DR SUSAN B KESMODEL (SURGICAL ONCOLOGIST \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MIAMI\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221107eiam0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIFE AFTER BREAST CANCER THE WAYS SOME SURVIVORS STRUGGLE FOR SUPPORT\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT NEW WARNING\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, DAM, EVACUATIONS, POWER, TROOPS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, CAMPAIGN, FETTERMAN, SENATE, HIGH-STAKES\par \par \uc2 CHILDREN, RESPIRATORY, SCHNEIDER, MIENTKIEWICZ\par \par \uc2 TRUSS, RESIGNATION, BRITAIN, TURMOIL, JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 TWINS, ABUSE, GOOD SAMARITAN, MCSHAN, DUNCAN, ARREST\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, SURVEILLANCE, SECURITY, CHINA, \uc2 FCC\par \par \uc2 MLB, PLAYOFFS, ASTROS, \uc2 YANKEES\uc2 , \uc2 PADRES\uc2 , \uc2 PHILLIES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WINTER OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CHHEDA, MURDER, DEDHIY, PURDUE, WEITE, ROOMMATE\par \par \uc2 MUSK, TWITTER, EMPLOYEES, JOBS\par \par \uc2 BISON, CLARK, GORED, HIKING, CORWIN\par \par \uc2 SWIFT, "MIDNIGHTS", DROP, FANS, ANIFTOS\par \par \uc2 JSU, HOMECOMING, HARRIS, EDWARDS, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 PREDIATRIC, RESPIRATORY ILLNESS, SURGE, HEALTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALARMING SURGE IN CHILDREN'S RESPIRATORY ILLNESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 71% OF PEDIATRIC HOSPITAL BEDS NOW FULL NATIONWIDE\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, SURVEILLANCE, SECURITY, CONCERN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIKTOK SURVEILLANCE?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT RAISES NATIONAL SECURITY CONCERNS\par \par \uc2 WEIGHT LOSS, GUIDANCE, MEDICATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WEIGHT LOSS GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MEDICATION RECOMMENDED AS FIRST-LINE TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 SANDERS, JACKSON STATE, FOOTBALL, HOMECOMING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CATCHING UP WITH "COACH PRIME"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEION SANDERS ON THE WATER CRISIS, JACKSON STATE\par \par \uc2 BREAST CANCER, SUPPORT, HIBBERT, PATTERSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL MAP\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, it is time for our Thriving in Pink series. Thankfully, there are more and more breast cancer survivors and thrivers, like the folks you see in these photos all around me. Over the past three decades, the death rates from the disease, they have thankfully decreased by about 40 percent. But for so many people, the transition to life after treatment can be really tough.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mother of four Aisha Patterson was diagnosed with stage 2 breast cancer in 2020.\par \par \uc2 AISHA PATTERSON (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 My husband was able to run his business from home, so he was my main caretaker.\par \par \uc2 DJ PATTERSON (HUSBAND OF AISHA PATTERSON)\par \par \uc2 I just went into get-stuff-taken-care-of mode, taking care of her, taking care of the kids, taking care of the business.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Her husband, friends and family all rallied around her during treatment, including surgery, chemotherapy and 33 rounds of radiation.\par \par \uc2 AISHA PATTERSON (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Two days after I had my double mastectomy, they called and said that I was cancer-free. I was thinking, "I'm going to be done. I'm going to be so happy that I can go on and live my life."\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She struggled to return to the life she led before her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 AISHA PATTERSON (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I didn't realize how hard survivorship was going to be. I didn't realize that I would be so afraid of the cancer coming back. It literally felt like a dragon just breathing down my neck all the time. And I was left to rebuild and figure it out on my own, which was an extremely trying process when you're already so broken.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As survival rates increase, more women are navigating life after treatment, where they say the support they had often fades.\par \par \uc2 AISHA PATTERSON (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 The support just kind of fizzles out as you go through treatment. Like, by the time I got through radiation, the casseroles weren't coming.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAKING THE TRANSITION FROM TREATMENT TO RECOVERY\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Doctors suggest creating a survivorship plan.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANNE PARTRIDGE\uc2  (DANA FARBER CANCER INSTITUTE)\par \par \uc2 It's a process. Just like you had to adjust to the diagnosis of breast cancer, you're going to have to adjust to the survivorship phase.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But often, building that new normal can be difficult.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA HIBBERT (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Your clothes fit differently. Your body looks different. You know, every time you see yourself in the mirror, you're reminded every time you have a symptom. It brings it up.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Clinical psychologist and breast cancer survivor Christina Hibbert is hoping to use her experience to support others.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA HIBBERT (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 I'm at the hospital where I had a bunch of my surgeries, and it is very nerve-wracking.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says patients need more resources as they transition from treatment to recovery.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NAVIGATING LIFE AFTER TREATMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THE SUPPORT FADES AWAY\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA HIBBERT (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 When you're told you're done, all of a sudden it's like crickets. Like, nobody is there.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANNE PARTRIDGE\uc2  (DANA FARBER CANCER INSTITUTE)\par \par \uc2 We as the medical community need to build programs and systems and guidelines in order to support them well, to optimize their care and their ultimate long-term outcomes.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CREATING A SURVIVORSHIP PLAN & A COMMUNITY\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now survivors like Aisha and Christina are using social media to create that community.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA HIBBERT (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 We don't have to pretend. We don't have to have that mask on or be, feel like we have to be tough or strong or the warrior. We can just say how hard it is.\par \par \uc2 AISHA PATTERSON (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Keep living life on your terms. Try to add in intentional time to just do life and not just go through the motions anymore.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. For survivors looking for support after treatment, for looking for that community, there are several groups online who can help you connect with all of these resources out there like, of course, cancercare.org. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  or the Susan G Komen, who provided us with all of these beautiful pictures, Juju. Love seeing all these smiling faces up herewith us.\par \par \uc2 JUJU CHANG (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What a great story, Cecilia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221118eial000ha\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FLORIDA SENATE SHOWDOWN CANDIDATES FACE OFF OVER GUN CONTROL & ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TACKLING GAS PRICES\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, GAS PRICES, RESERVE, BARRELS, OIL\par \par \uc2 IRS\uc2 , ADJUSTMENTS, LOWER, INFLATION, TAXES\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, DEMMINGS, RUBIO, GUNS, ABORTION, MIDTERM\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rebecca, thanks for that. To the midterm elections now, less than three weeks away and the key races are heating up. Democrats pressing the issue of abortion rights, including at a debate showdown last night between the Senate candidates in Florida. Our congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is in Orlando with the latest on all of that. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLORIDA SENATE SHOWDOWN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATE FACE OFF OVER GUN CONTROL & ABORTION IN HEATED DEBATE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Amy, good morning. Democrats are hoping that abortion rights becomes the defining issue this midterm election. And it was certainly a flash point at a Florida Senate debate, which was heated from the very start.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It was a contentious face-off in the sunshine state.\par \par \uc2 SEN MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the lying and the cheating...\par \par \uc2 REP VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 And that's not true, how gullible do you think Florida voters are?\par \par \uc2 SEN MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 The lying, the lying...\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  sparring with his Democratic challenger, Congresswoman Val Demings, in their first and only debate that aired on ABC affiliate WPBF.\par \par \uc2 SEN MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 She's been in Congress for over half a decade, she's never passed a bill.\par \par \uc2 REP VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 I'm really disappointed in you, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , because I don't, I think there was a time when you did not lie in order to win.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The two at odds over gun control. Asked about raising the legal age limit to purchase assault-style weapons, the type of firearms used in the Parkland and Uvalde mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 SEN MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Denying the right to buy is not going to keep them from, here's the fundamental issue. The fundamental issue is why are these kids, why are these people going out and massacring people? 'Cause a lot own AR-15s and they don't kill everyone.\par \par \uc2 REP VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 People who are families of victims of gun violence just heard that and they're asking themselves, what in the hell did he just say? You've done nothing, nothing to help address gun violence and get dangerous weapons out of the hands of dangerous people.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But the night's tensest exchange came over abortion. Rubio has backed a 15-week nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 SEN MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 I believe that innocent human life is worthy of the protection of our laws. That said, every bill I've ever sponsored on abortion, every bill I've voted for, has exceptions. The extremist on abortion in this campaign is Congresswoman Demings.\par \par \uc2 REP VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Senator, how gullible do you really think Florida voters are? I think those decisions are made between the woman, her family, her doctor and her faith.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRES BIDEN: CONGRESS WILL ACT ON ABORTION RIGHTS IF DEMOCRATS WIN\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Across the country at least 14 states have stopped nearly all abortion services. And with just three weeks to go until election day the President putting the issue front and center. Promising Democrats will protect access to abortion under federal law if they win enough seats this November.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Together we'll restore the right to choose for every woman in every state in America. So vote. You've got to get out the vote. We can do this if we vote.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, of course, that will be an uphill challenge. Democrats have control of Congress right now and they still don't have enough votes to protect access to abortion under federal law. And they are facing political head winds, we've been traveling across the country, inflation, the economy, the top two issues that we hear from voters. And polls show that more voters trust Republicans on those issues, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221104eiaj00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HEATED SENATE DEBATE CANDIDATES DEBATE GUN CONTROL & ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, HEAT, SNOW, CAR, CRASH\par \par \uc2 TAX, GAS, PRICES, BIDEN, MCCARTHY\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, MARTIAL LAW, BLINKEN, WAR\par \par \uc2 RESPIRATORY ILLNESS, BRANCATO, SEREVADO, SHANE\par \par \uc2 RECOVERY, FLORIDA, HURRICANE IAN, TOMASKO, PATRONIS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM ELECTION, RUBIO, DEMINGS, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the meantime, we turn now to "Your Voice, Your Vote," 20 days now to the midterms. And tonight, the fireworks in the debate stage in the race for Senate here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Democrat Val Demings, the former police chief right here in Orlando, aiming to use her background in law enforcement against the incumbent, Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) From abortion rights to young people buying guns, what was said. And was a promise broken? Rachel Scott, right here in Orlando, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEATED SENATE DEBATE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES DEBATE GUN CONTROL & ABORTION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, it's the race that pits the former Orlando police chief against the incumbent Republican senator, Congresswoman Val Demings hoping her background in law enforcement will help defeat \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . In their only debate, Rubio was pressed on an issue close to home, the pledge he made three days after the Parkland School shooting to the families of the victims.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 You said, quote, "If you're 18 years of age, you should not be able to buy a rifle. I will support a law that takes that right away." Would you still support that federal law?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you why that law doesn't work, and why that proposal doesn't work. We had a shooter last Thursday, tragic, in North Carolina. He was 15. Where did he get the gun? He didn't get it from a gun show. He certainly didn't buy it. He's 15 years of age. So, I think the solution of this problem is to identify these people that are acting this way, and stop them before they act.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I want to stop you here senator, and I want to make sure that I understand that what you said in 2018 is not what you believe is the solution today. There should be not be a ban on 18 or 19 or 20-year-olds...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Well, a 15-year-old just got a gun, got a shotgun and killed a bunch of people.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 But that's one case. I want to make sure...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 No, it's more than one case. Denying the right to buy it is not going to keep them from doing that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Demings pouncing on a promise unkept.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 The people who are the families of victims of gun violence just heard that, and they're asking themselves, "What in the hell did he just say?" Our primary responsibility is the safety of Floridians. And, senator, 24 years in elected office, and you have not yet risen to that occasion. And then when asked about it, you say something that makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The candidates then sparring on a key issue this year. Rubio asked if he would vote for a federal abortion ban with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Number one, I'm 100% pro-life, because I, but not because I want to deny anyone the rights, but because I believe that innocent human life is worthy of the protection of our laws. That said, every bill I've ever sponsored on abortion, every bill I've ever voted for, has exceptions. Every one of them does, because that's what can pass and that's what the majority of people support.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE VAL DEMINGS (DEM) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 Senator, how gullible do you really think Florida voters are? Number one, you have been clear that you support no exceptions, even including rape and incest. Now, as a police detective who investigated cases of rape and incest, no, senator, I don't think it's okay for a 10-year-old girl to be raped and have to carry the seed of her rapist. No, I don't think it's okay for you to make decisions for women and girls.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Many believe that Congresswoman Val Demings was once on the short list for potential vice presidential candidates, along with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . In the meantime, though, now she's trying to take on an incumbent, here, Senator Rubio, in a tough race. He's been ahead.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It is a tough race, David. And Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is leading by roughly 5 points in the polls. The economy a top issue for voters, here, in Florida. And in this state, inflation is higher than the national average, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right. Twenty days to go. Rachel, thanks so much, again, tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) To the other news, here, this evening, and there is news tonight on that Iranian athlete who competed in South Korea without that required traditional head covering.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Elnaz Rekabi arrived back in Tehran, greeted by cheering crowds. Late today, seen with Iran's minister of Youth Affairs and Sports, and, in an interview, saying it was an accident that she competed without a head covering. But Iranian human rights activists tonight suspect she will be punished.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When we come back here from Florida, the Customs and Border Protection officer shot and killed during a training accident in Miami, and what we've now learned, tonight.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221020eiaj00007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BLACK WOMEN AND BREAST CANCER DISPARITIES IN CARE BETWEEN BLACK AND WHITE WOMEN\b0\par\par\uc2 1,122 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WALKER, MIDTERM, KEMP, ABRAMS, VANCE, RYAN\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, RECESSION\par \par \uc2 SNOW, WIND CHILL, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BITTER COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FREEZING TEMPERATURES & HEAVY SNOW SLAM MIDWEST\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, SNOW\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, DRONE STRIKES, KLITSCHKO\par \par \uc2 TRUSS, TAX CUTS, ENGLAND, ECONOMY\par \par \uc2 MANHUNT, PRENTICE, MURDER, CHASTAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUADRUPLE MURDER MANHUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES SEARCH FOR PERSON OF INTEREST AFTER BODIES FOUND IN RIVER\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, INVESTIGATION, SECRET SERVICE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SECRET SERVICE OVERCHARGED?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOUSE PANEL FINDS TRUMP HOTELS CHARGED AGENCY "EXORBITANT" RATES\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , CURRY, JAMES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 NBA\uc2  SEASON TIPS OFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 GOLDEN STATE WARRIORS\uc2  BEGIN THEIR TITLE DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM TV SHOW, "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 YU, CHEN, POISONING, BINGHAM, HITTELMAN, DERMATOLOGIST\par \par \uc2 BLAIR, "DANCING WITH THE STARS"\par \par \uc2 "THE WATCHER", WIEDEMAN\par \par \uc2 DOG, TREADMILL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIDTERM ELECTIONS: HIGH STAKES\par \par \uc2 GEORGIA, CANDIDATES, DEBATE\par \par \uc2 STUDENT LOAN\par \par \uc2 BIEBER, GOMEZ, POSE, PHOTO, FEUD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 HOUSING, MARKET, BUYING, NOW, COOL, OFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA CONSUMER ALERT\par \par \uc2 BLACK, BREAST CANCER, OSSON, NEWMAN, MITCHELL\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, George. Now to "Thriving in Pink." Breast cancer accounts for about 30 percent of all new cancers for women. For black women, that diagnosis often comes at a later stage than for others. Deborah Roberts has more on the disparity, a very, very personal story for her. Good morning, Deb.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good morning, Robin. Yeah, I'm here for my overdue breast cancer screening. Like a lot of women, I've put off my exams. But my doctors, my team, Doctors Cassell, Doctor Mittnick, and Doctor Cashman don't want to hear that. They want me triple screened for breast cancer, which includes a mammogram, a sonogram, and today an mri. I am at higher risk because I have a family history and I'm a woman of color, and that's a combination I now personally know can be deadly.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Breast cancer awareness is paramount for my sisters and me. In 2009, we lost Benny to the disease at age 56. Then in 2019, Janet was diagnosed too. After chemo and radiation, thankfully she's now cancer-free. But our worries haven't gone away. Bernadette Osson knows that feeling.\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE OSSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 My mortality was right there in my face.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 2021, like Janet, she was diagnosed with invasive ductal carcinoma and she soon learned that age and lifestyle are not predictors for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE OSSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I don't eat red meat. I was walking four miles a day.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Then another sobering discovery. Black women are 41 percent more likely to die from breast cancer than white women, despite having similar incidence rates.\par \par \uc2 DR LISA NEWMAN (BREAST SURGERY WEILL CORNELL MED CTR/NEW YORK PRESBY HOSP)\par \par \uc2 Incidence rates of breast cancer among black women coupled with the fact that black women are more likely to get biologically aggressive patterns of the disease has resulted in a widening of the mortality gap.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For 20 years, Doctor Lisa Newman has been researching key socioeconomic, genetic, and systemic race disparities that compounded, stack high against black women.\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE OSSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 They have done leaps and bounds with breast cancer research. It is not a death sentence. It is actually a life sentence.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And as Bernadette, a single mom of two knows, early detection is key.\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE OSSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 My treatment is done, this course has run, and I am on my way.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After chemo this summer, she's turning the page. But, that's not the case for so many other black women, Newman says.\par \par \uc2 DR LISA NEWMAN (BREAST SURGERY WEILL CORNELL MED CTR/NEW YORK PRESBY HOSP)\par \par \uc2 Poverty rates, likelihood of being underinsured. These all result in delays in making a breast cancer diagnosis, inadequate treatment, incomplete treatment.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So survivors, like Donna Robinson in remission 11 years, have found a calling.\par \par \uc2 DONNA ROBINSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 The story that captured me was a young black girl that was going through chemotherapy and she didn't have transportation and she would go for her chemo treatment and catch the bus and ride home.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In Saint Louis, Missouri, where the black/white disparity in breast cancer death is among the fifth highest in the country, Donna is giving new meaning to purpose driven.\par \par \uc2 DONNA ROBINSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Treatment is hard. It's hard emotionally, it's hard physically.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She's a volunteer with the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 's Road to Recovery, a program pairing cancer patients in need of transportation with drivers like herself.\par \par \uc2 DONNA ROBINSON (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 If one person's heart is a little lighter, I've done something that makes volunteering worthwhile.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But not all disparities are financial.\par \par \uc2 DR LISA NEWMAN (BREAST SURGERY WEILL CORNELL MED CTR/NEW YORK PRESBY HOSP)\par \par \uc2 Systemic racism affects all aspects of society, including public health and it unfortunately remains true that there are some pretty appalling disparities in delivery of breast cancer care.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA MITCHELL (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 My first experience was not enjoyable.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's why the Mitchell sisters out of Arkansas are embracing the Grabbing Their Girls campaign and taking on health together.\par \par \uc2 TONYA MITCHELL (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It is a scary thing to go to the doctor for anything, definitely to go for screening for cancer and no matter what the outcome, no matter what the news, having great support there is key to everything.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These survivors teach us that self-advocacy is so urgent in taking care of ourselves and finding, you know, early detection. So, it's up to us to push our doctors, our insurance companies, anything we can do to stay vigilant and, of course, to stay up on our screenings. And I'm going in for mine right after this, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And how are you feeling about that, Deb?\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I'm a little nervous. I'm always a little nervous when I do these things but, you know, I'm also very hopeful, too.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know. Well, we'll check in with you later. You know that. All right, Deb, take care.\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's check in now with Ginger. Ging.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221103eiai0003f\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BIDEN PLEDGES ABORTION RIGHTS LAW VOWS LEGISLATION WILL PASS IF DEMOCRATS WIN\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, SNOW\par \par \uc2 PLANE CRASH, INVESTIGATION, PRATER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY PLANE CRASH INFERNO\par \par \uc2 GUILTY, VERDICT, SMART, FLORES\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, STEELE DOSSIER, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STEELE "DOSSIER" CASE ACQUITTAL\par \par \uc2 ELECTIONS, BIDEN, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN PLEDGES ABORTION RIGHTS LAW\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, to Your Voice, Your Vote, 21 days until the midterms. And tonight, the new promise from President Biden that if Democrats hold the House and the Senate, the President says the first bill he would sign would codify Roe vs. Wade, to make it federal law. But what are the chances tonight that Democrats can hold the House and the Senate? And do Democrats or Republicans have the momentum? Here's our senior national correspondent, Terry Moran, now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VOWS LEGISLATION WILL PASS IF DEMOCRATS WIN\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, President Biden with a last-ditch effort before the midterms to rally voters who support abortion rights, promising that Democrats will pass a federal law guaranteeing the right to choose abortion if they win at the polls.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're short of a handful of votes. If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ABORTION BANNED OR SEVERELY RESTRICTED\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just three weeks from Election Day, the President is trying to galvanize voters by focusing on abortion, pointing to the restrictions already put in place in at least 14 states, where nearly all abortion services have ceased since Roe vs. Wade was overturned by the Supreme Court in June.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The only sure way to stop these extremist laws that are putting jeopardy women's health and rights is for Congress to pass a law.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The President warned that if Republicans take power, they will try to ban abortion at 15 weeks nationwide, noting that Senate Republicans like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  have already introduced such a bill and arguing that only more Democrats in the House and Senate can stop them.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade. And when Congress passes it, I'll sign it in January, 50 years after Roe was first decided as the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISSUES IMPORTANT TO VOTERS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But Biden and the Democrats are facing political headwinds on the issue. An ABC News/"Washington Post" poll recently found that voters rank the economy, education and inflation well above abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Twenty-one days, as we said. Terry, you'll be with us right here on election night. Right now, you're live at the White House, as we can see. And I know you checked in with our partners at FiveThirtyEight. Just for the viewers at home, what are the numbers, tonight, the chances tonight, given all the polls, what does it look like for Democrats holding the House? What does it look like for them holding the Senate? And, really, who has the momentum, here? The Dems or the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, right now, David, polls, our poll and other polls suggest that there's a slight but definite shift in momentum towards the Republicans. That's one of the reasons you heard President Biden say today to voters, "Remember how you felt when the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade." He wants that momentum, at that time, Democrats were in the lead. Right now, our colleagues at FiveThirtyEight, they're saying that Democrats are slightly favored to win the Senate. They put it in a two in three chance. And Republicans more favored to win the House. But there's some time left, and it will all depend on who turns out to vote. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, better chances for the Republicans with the House, right now, and the Democrats appear to have an edge when it comes to the Senate. But again, 21 days, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That's right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be talking with you many more times before election night. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221019eiai0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ARREST, SERIAL KILLER, MCFADDEN, BROWNLEE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, MOTIVE, VICTIMS, HOWARD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEARCH FOR A MOTIVE\par \par \uc2 STORM, FREEZE, SNOW, TEMPERATURES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COLDEST AIR OF THE SEASON\par \par \uc2 SEC, VIOLATIONS, TRUMP, INVESTIGATION, KLAUSNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CO-FOUNDER: TRUMP MEDIA VIOLATED LAWS\par \par \uc2 MIDTERMS, OBAMA, GEORGIA, PENNSYLVANIA, ARIZONA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OBAMA TO CAMPAIGN IN KEY RACES\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, SOLDIERS, ATTACK, KLITSCHKO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RUSSIAN TRAINING FACILITY ATTACK\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PRISON, FIRE, DEAD, INJURED, PROTESTS, AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY IRANIAN PRISON FIRE\par \par \uc2 AMBUSH, BRISTOL, CDC, COVID\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY OFFICER AMBUSH\par \par \uc2 HEARING AIDS, STUDENT DEBT, VOLUNTEERS, FANS\par \par \uc2 ALVAREZ, MAMMOGRAM, CANCER, RETAIL, NURSE\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, tonight, "America Strong." October is breast cancer awareness month, and we want to introduce you to one woman whose diagnosis led to a dramatic change, not just her outlook, but her occupation.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For 23 years, Darleen Alvarez worked in retail management. Then, at 43, after finding a lump, she had her first mammogram.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 I had not gotten a mammogram in my whole life.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Later that day, she underwent more tests and found out she had breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 Within a month and a half, I had my port inserted, and I had my first round of chemotherapy. After that was done, I had a mastectomy to have my breast removed, and then after that, I had 36 rounds of radiation.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That time as a cancer patient wasn't just life-changing, it caused her to change her career. Hanging up retail for good, believing there was value in her empathy and experience. At 45, she started training to become a nurse.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 Knock, knock. Here I am.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Going on 10 years of being cancer-free, Darleen now works as a nurse on the oncology floor at Morton Plant Hospital in Clearwater, Florida.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling, today?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) On her work badge, a photo of herself from when she was undergoing treatment.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 I carry this with me so that patients going through cancer can see that I understand where they're coming from.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Similar stories were on parade today across the country, as thousands took part in Making Strides Against Breast Cancer walks to raise awareness and money for breast cancer research. Back in Florida, Darleen credits that mammogram with saving her life, and she hopes this message for women just might save theirs.\par \par \uc2 DARLEEN ALVAREZ (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR/NURSE)\par \par \uc2 Please go get your mammogram. It is not painful. It's quick, and one screening can save your life.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Great advice, there. Thanks so much for watching. David Muir, back here tomorrow night. I'm Linsey Davis, in New York. Have a great evening. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020221018eiag0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:00 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS SHOOTING: SEARCH FOR ANSWERS\par \par \uc2 NORTH CAROLINA, SHOOTING, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, JANUARY 6TH, TESTIFY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, RIOT, INVESTIGATIONS, LIABILITY\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, CANDIDATES, SENATE, MIDTERMS, GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 UK, PRIME MINISTER, TREASURY CHIEF\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, MILITARY, HIMARS, AMMUNITION\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT, RECALL, BABY FORMULA, COLUMBUS\par \par \uc2 KROGER, ALBERTSONS, MERGER, PRICES\par \par \uc2 BIRD STRIKE, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 , FRANCZAK, DOSHEN\par \par \uc2 HIKER, RESCUE, BOULDER, REINFIELD, WEBLEY\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP "HARRY POTTER AND THE CHAMBER OF SECRETS"\par \par \uc2 NIKOLA MOTORS, MILTON, TECH, STRAUSS\par \par \uc2 SOROKIN, DELVEY, HOUSE ARREST, DEPORTATION\par \par \uc2 COLTRANE, "HARRY POTTER", HAGRID, ACTOR\par \par \uc2 SAVINGS, INTEREST RATES, BANKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's our second hour.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLICE IDENTIFY 15-YEAR-OLD SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The search for answers in a mass shooting after a 15-year-old suspect allegedly opened fire, killing five people.\par \par \uc2 CALLER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Active shooter. Active shooter.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The 911 calls from the crime scene as families grieve losing their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: INTEREST RATE HIKES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MONEY SMART\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Money smart. The Fed's hikes make borrowing more expensive. How to make interest rates work for you. Plus, what to know about shopping around for a savings account.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSING AWAY AT 72\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING ROBBIE COLTRANE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Celebrating Robbie Coltrane, passing away at 72 known by generations as Hagrid in "Harry Potter."\par \par \uc2 DANIEL RADCLIFFE ("HARRY POTTER AND THE CHAMBER OF SECRETS")\par \par \uc2 There's no Hogwarts without you, Hagrid.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 The tributes overnight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW THIS MORNING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ISSA RAE CALLING OUT HOLLYWOOD\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Issa Rae calling out Hollywood, speaking out about Ezra Miller. What the Emmy nominee is saying about an effort to protect the actor who she calls a repeat offender of bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: METASTATIC BREAST CANCER AWARENESS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Also, this morning, thriving in pink. The top tips for eating healthy this Breast Cancer Awareness Month and beyond. Plus, going global for Metastatic Breast Cancer Awareness Day, how nearly 300 landmarks around the globe showed support for the fight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JODI PICOULT & JENNIFER FINNEY BOYLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MAD HONEY"\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 It's the instant best-seller that has readers buzzing. Jodi Picoult and Jennifer Finney Boylan, the authors behind the "GMA" October pick "Mad Honey" opening up. Why they say the book could be banned.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER FINNEY BOYLAN (CO-AUTHOR "MAD HONEY")\par \par \uc2 The most common books could be banned, but always books about LGBTQ experience.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "BLACK ADAM" STAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MOHAMMED AMER LIVE\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And he's the scene stealer sharing the big screen with The Rock.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED AMER ("BLACK ADAM")\par \par \uc2 Did he just get a rocket?\par \par \uc2 SARAH SHAHI ("BLACK ADAM")\par \par \uc2 He got a rocket.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Mohammed Amer is live spilling superhero secrets as we say, good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 It's Saturday morning, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221102eiaf0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CANCER-FIGHTING FOODS? WHAT TO ADD TO YOUR DIET\b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MASS SHOOTING: SEARCH FOR ANSWERS\par \par \uc2 NORTH CAROLINA, SHOOTING, FREEMAN\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, JANUARY 6TH, TESTIFY\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, RIOT, INVESTIGATIONS, LIABILITY\par \par \uc2 DEBATE, CANDIDATES, SENATE, MIDTERMS, GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 UK, PRIME MINISTER, TREASURY CHIEF\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, MILITARY, HIMARS, AMMUNITION\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT, RECALL, BABY FORMULA, COLUMBUS\par \par \uc2 KROGER, ALBERTSONS, MERGER, PRICES\par \par \uc2 BIRD STRIKE, \uc2 UNITED AIRLINES\uc2 , FRANCZAK, DOSHEN\par \par \uc2 HIKER, RESCUE, BOULDER, REINFIELD, WEBLEY\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP "HARRY POTTER AND THE CHAMBER OF SECRETS"\par \par \uc2 NIKOLA MOTORS, MILTON, TECH, STRAUSS\par \par \uc2 SOROKIN, DELVEY, HOUSE ARREST, DEPORTATION\par \par \uc2 COLTRANE, "HARRY POTTER", HAGRID, ACTOR\par \par \uc2 SAVINGS, INTEREST RATES, BANKS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 INTEREST RATE, HIKE, BANKS, SAVINGS ACCOUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GETTING THE MOST FROM YOUR SAVINGS ACCOUNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BANKS OFFERING WIDE RANGE OF INTEREST RATES\par \par \uc2 FORMULA, RECALL, ABBOTT, SIMILAC\par \par \uc2 RAE, MILLER, VALENTINO\par \par \uc2 FELLER, METASTATIC BREAST CANCER, FOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we are now thriving in pink this morning. We are focusing on the foods that could possibly make an impact on breast cancer and beyond. Anti-cancer foods may lower the risk of developing cancer, and we are breaking them down with nutritionist Maya Feller. Good morning to you. Last time I saw you was at the farmer's market.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, literally buying all these anti-cancer foods.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes. So, what makes a food anti-cancer?\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 So, really, what we're talking about is the antioxidant capability of that food. And why do we care about antioxidants, is because they help to protect the cells from free radical damage.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANCER-FIGHTING FOODS?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO ADD TO YOUR DIET THIS BREAST CANCER AWARENESS MONTH AND BEYOND\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 And so, when we're talking about cancer, we're not saying cure, I want to be really clear, what we're talking about is reducing the risk of developing some cancers by engaging in eating these high antioxidant capacity foods.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH IN ANTIOXIDANTS HELP FIGHT FREE RADICALS\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Usually with diet, we're talking about taking things out of your diet.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Right.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This you actually want us to add things in.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes, we want to you add things in. So, this is what I have to say to everyone. I want you to think about the plants that you're currently eating. Do you like those plants? And how many more of them can you add?\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Vegetables.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Right. Exactly, add to your plate. We want more on your plate. And if you're not eating plants, then I want you to think about the ones that you're willing to give a try to.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ADD COLORFUL PLANTS, HERBS & SPICES\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And different ways that you can get a vegetable into your diet.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, 100%.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, you brought us some examples. What do we have here?\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Okay, so what we have here is just a plethora of gorgeousness. I want to highlight all of the different colors that we're seeing here. So, when I'm talking about antioxidants, I'm talking about polyphenols, I'm talking about flavonoids. And what we usually say is eat the rainbow. You're going to notice here that there's grapes and there's blueberries.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GRAPES, GUAVAS & BLUEBERRIES HAVE A GREAT ANTIOXIDANT CAPACITY\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 By the way, did you know that grapes are also a berry? I was floored when I learned that, right? And we're talking about things that has this deep colored skin. So, you see the kiwi, you see the pineapple. You also see the broccoli, these are the colors that are really rich in antioxidants. So, we want more of those to show up in a person's pattern of eating.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, there are a lot of fresh ones here, but there's also a couple of frozen ones as well.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Okay. So, this is going to be massively disruptive. Frozen and fresh are the same. Things that are frozen are picked at the peak of the growing season, have just as many nutrients as the fresh. It cuts down on time. It's a huge time saver so fresh or frozen, just read that nutrition facts label. Be an informed consumer. But you can have either on your plate.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Also, it keeps them from spoiling in your refrigerator faster.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 100%.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And you also say grains, beans, those kinds of things are important.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Onions.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Yes. So, one of the things I love about this plate here, you're going to notice the colors are different than that. When we say eat the rainbow we really mean incorporate everything from the browns, the whites, all the way to the purples. These grains here, so we have fonio, millet, and teff. They're ancient grains, full amino acid profile. What does that mean? All of the proteins that you need coming from plants, plus incredibly nutrient rich. I like to actually put them together and make a porridge or sometimes I toss them into my baked goods and it's really satiating.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANCIENT GRAINS NUTRIENT-DENSE & PLANT-BASED PROTEIN\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Sometimes I add them to rice because I like rice, but I really don't like these, but it hides them.\par \par \uc2 MAYA FELLER (NUTRITIONIST)\par \par \uc2 Ooh, yes. I'm with you on that 100%.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Maya, thank you so much for being with us. We appreciate you. And now, let's send it over to Janai who is sitting down with two very inspirational women this morning.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, thank you, Eva.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221102eiaf0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 LIVING WITH METASTATIC BREAST CANCER WOMEN SHARE THEIR INSPIRING STORIES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,138 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DOCUMENTS, CAPITOL, RIOT, SUBPOENA\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, GAS, PRICES, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON NEW INFLATION NUMBERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAVE RISING PRICES FINALLY PEAKED?\par \par \uc2 JONES, VERDICT, PARKER, SANDY HOOK, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILLION DOLLAR VERDICT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALEX JONES SLAPPED WITH MASSIVE JUDGMENT IN SANDY HOOK CASE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, PARTRIDGE, WAR, Y, MILLEY\par \par \uc2 MULLEN, NAVY SEAL, INVESTIGATION, UNIGLICHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON NAVY SEAL CANDIDATE'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYLE MULLEN'S MOTHER SPEAKS OUT ABOUT "HELL WEEK" TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BOOSTER, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 DRUEKE, HUYNH, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, CAPTIVE\par \par \uc2 BRADY, CLIJSTERS, PICKLEBALL, GILBERT\par \par \uc2 GOOSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: MAR-A-LAGO DOCUMENTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 MAR-A-LAGO, DOCUMENTS, TRUMP, SURVEILLANCE\par \par \uc2 CDC, COVID, BOOSTERS, KIDS\par \par \uc2 ADDERALL, SHORTAGE, FDA, DELAYS, MANUFACTURING\par \par \uc2 BUY NOW, PAY LATER\par \par \uc2 METASTATIC BREAST CANCER, HOPE, QUICK, KEANE, COMEN\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, George, now to "Thriving in Pink." It is Metastatic Breast Cancer Awareness Day. According to the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 , around 164,000 women in the United States are living with metastatic breast cancer right now and the prognosis can be stark. The relative survival rate for five years is less than a third and now some women are sharing their story in hopes of helping others.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Kansas City native, Amanda Quick was 29 and living her dream life in New York City when in the spring of 2021 she found something.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I felt, under my left armpit, just a swollen lymph node, lump.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says she dismissed it until a few months later when it had grown larger and harder.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 They had me get a sonogram, a mammogram, a biopsy.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The diagnosis, metastatic breast cancer, or stage 4, meaning the cancer had already spread to other parts of her body.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You get a stage 4 metastatic breast cancer diagnosis. I cannot even imagine how you process that.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 For some time, I definitely didn't. I was definitely in denial. Get screened, head down to the bottom floor.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She started on hormone therapy, including a targeted drug and monthly injections.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 The days that I go into treatment, those are the hardest. I think everything just always comes back.\par \par \uc2 JENNA LYNN LYONS (BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I have stage 4 breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  estimates that of the women diagnosed with breast cancer each year, six percent are at stage 4. In 2016, while 36-year-old new mom Anne Keane was working out.\par \par \uc2 ANNE KEANE (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I had noticed some pain along my sternum.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says she thought she was just out of shape, but the pain persisted. A week later she noticed some dimpling in the breast.\par \par \uc2 ANNE KEANE (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It was metastatic breast cancer. It had already spread to my bones, that was the pain that I was feeling in my sternum. It was in my lymph nodes and also in my liver.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She started chemotherapy and the medication sent her into early menopause.\par \par \uc2 ANNE KEANE (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I had no inclination that this was even possible to have stage 4 breast cancer at such a young age.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Stage 4 cancer is usually considered incurable. Treatment is typically focused on preventing the spread of cancer cells, treating symptoms, and improving quality of life.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CTR)\par \par \uc2 It's not just about survival and numbers and months and years which are, of course, incredibly important, but also thinking about how we are living our days.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: METASTATIC BREAST CANCER AWARENESS DAY: INSIDE THE TREATMENT OPTIONS FOR THOSE LIVING WITH THE DISEASE\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) New research is vital to moving the needle on outcomes for patients.\par \par \uc2 DR ELIZABETH COMEN (MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING CANCER CTR)\par \par \uc2 About 55 percent of patients with metastatic breast cancer have a relatively new type of classification of that breast cancer called her2-low metastatic breast cancer and there's a new treatment called T-DXd or Enhertu that is improving the survival of these women.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Results from the clinical trial show the targeted treatment slowed the spread of the cancer and improved survival rates by 35 percent with those with her2-low metastatic breast cancer. And new technology is looking to use blood tests to change the way cancer cells are diagnosed and tracked. Six years after her diagnosis, Anne, now 42, is hoping to inspire others.\par \par \uc2 ANNE KEANE (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I really thought I wouldn't see my kid go to kindergarten, and now I wake up in the morning, I work out, I'm working full-time. I can go pick up my daughter from school. It's really beautiful.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now, she's training to run the Boston Marathon again in 2023 to raise money for research. Amanda is also using her diagnosis to reach others.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What have you learned most through your journey so far?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA QUICK (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 Every day is a gift. If there's one person that can say, hey, I saw your experience, I saw your story and you've helped me get through today, then that's amazing.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And the financial realities for some patients with metastatic breast cancer can be enormous and time short. A bipartisan bill aimed at relieving some of that burden by removing the waiting periods for access to benefits and health care from Social Security Disability and Medicare expires at the end of the year if it's not passed. But, you know, I have two dear friends who are metastatic and I do have to say, these, these new drugs provide so much hope. We need more research, we need more awareness because this has been a disease that people avoid because it's, it's a sad one, but we need people to get together to raise awareness and to help these women...\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...but you can live beautifully and amazingly with metastatic breast cancer, and women are proving it every day.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And they showed it right there.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They really did. All right, Lara, you got some "Pop News" for us.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221101eiad0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 8:00 HALF HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 515 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TRUMP, DOCUMENTS, CAPITOL, RIOT, SUBPOENA\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, GAS, PRICES, INFLATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON NEW INFLATION NUMBERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAVE RISING PRICES FINALLY PEAKED?\par \par \uc2 JONES, VERDICT, PARKER, SANDY HOOK, SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BILLION DOLLAR VERDICT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALEX JONES SLAPPED WITH MASSIVE JUDGMENT IN SANDY HOOK CASE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, PARTRIDGE, WAR, Y, MILLEY\par \par \uc2 MULLEN, NAVY SEAL, INVESTIGATION, UNIGLICHT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW REPORT ON NAVY SEAL CANDIDATE'S DEATH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KYLE MULLEN'S MOTHER SPEAKS OUT ABOUT "HELL WEEK" TRAGEDY\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, STORM\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BOOSTER, CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 DRUEKE, HUYNH, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, WAR, CAPTIVE\par \par \uc2 BRADY, CLIJSTERS, PICKLEBALL, GILBERT\par \par \uc2 GOOSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING OVERNIGHT: MAR-A-LAGO DOCUMENTS BATTLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. It's 8:00 AM. Breaking overnight, a dramatic new development in the Mar-A-Lago documents investigation. Reports that an aide to Former President Trump told federal investigators that Trump requested the documents be moved after he received the subpoena. The transfer caught on surveillance tape as the January 6th Committee prepares to deliver what is essentially its closing argument, focusing on Trump's state of mind around the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID BOOSTERS FOR KIDS: SHOT OF HOPE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 New shot of hope. The CDC signing off on new covid boosters for kids. Who is now eligible to get it?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THRIVING IN PINK: METASTATIC BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Thriving in pink. Women with metastatic breast cancer. What some are saying about living with the terrifying diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 ANNE KEANE (METASTATIC BREAST CANCER PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I really thought I wouldn't see my kid go to kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Their inspiring message for others, and the breakthrough treatment options bringing new hope.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BUY NOW, PAY LATER: HOLIDAY SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Buy now, pay later rising in popularity with prices on the rise. This morning, the pros and cons you need to know ahead of the holidays, and can it hurt your credit score?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIVE ON GMA: "BLACK ADAM" STARS\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 More superheroes in Times Square. Dwayne Johnson's "Black Adam" co-stars, Quintessa Swindell and rom-com Romeo Noah Centineo join us live to talk about suiting up for their new movie.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPER SAVINGS FOR YOUR HOME: DEALS AND STEALS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 And Tory is in the house bringing great "Deals & Steals" for your home and she's saying...\par \par \uc2 TORY JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 Live in Times Square, this is "GMA."\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 Good morning, America. You know, we love "Deals & Steals," and this morning, Tory has terrific bargains starting at just six bucks.\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) $6. All right, looking forward to that. And then we have Charlie and Kate Gibson here. They are sharing their love of mystery books. They're cracking the case on what makes a great one. That is all coming up.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we have a little quiz for them, as well, coming up.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221101eiad00037\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ALL EYES ON GEORGIA REPUBLICANS TO RALLY FOR \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY, RUSSIA, MISSILE ATTACK, BIDEN, G7, WAR\par \par \uc2 CYBERATTACK, RUSSIA, HACKING, AIRPORTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CYBERATTACKS TARGET U.S. AIRPORTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HACKERS DISRUPT AT LEAST 12 MAJOR AIRPORT WEBSITES\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, RUSSIA, UKRAINE, CYBERATTACK\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, IRAN\par \par \uc2 WALKER, ELECTION, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALL EYES ON GEORGIA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TO REPUBLICANS TO RALLY FOR \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  AMID ABORTION SCANDAL\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to the midterm elections just 28 days away with control of Congress up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE, YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 28 DAYS TO ELECTION DAY\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And many eyes on Georgia as those abortion allegations are rocking \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's campaign. Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is here tracking that race for us. Good morning, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Robin, good morning, this race could decide which party controls the Senate. And that's exactly why Republicans are going all in for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, Florida Senator Rick Scott who is the head of the Republican Senate campaign arm and Senator Tom Cotton will travel to Georgia to show their support for Walker. Scott saying, Republicans are proud to stand with him. The former college football star has been battered by a series of scandals in recent weeks, including allegations that he paid for a woman's abortion despite opposing abortion rights without any exceptions. Democratic incumbent Raphael Warnock making it clear that he supports abortion rights in this final stretch, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, Rachel, as you know, that's not the only key Senate race. There was a tense debate last night for the open Senate seat in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 28 DAYS TO HIGH-STAKES MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OHIO SENATE CANDIDATES CLASH IN DEBATE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, this was the first debate between Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and venture capitalist and Republican J.D. Vance and it was contentious.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The two candidates clashing over abortion rights and crime. At one point, Vance insisted Ryan supported policies that led to a 10-year-old girl in Ohio being sexually assaulted. Ryan also keeping his distance from Democratic leadership, saying, he does not believe President Biden should run for re-election, calling for a new generation of leadership, Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel, thanks so much. George?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Okay, Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221031eiab00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  EXCLUSIVE INTERVIEW\b0\par\par\uc2 2,614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WALKER, SENATE, ABORTION, SON, \uc2 TRUMP\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. Thank you for joining us. Tonight, there are four weeks to go before the critical midterm elections, and big-name Republicans are standing by embattled Georgia Senate hopeful, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And now the former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star is trying to set the record straight on recent allegations against him. Here's ABC's Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 While training, I'm like\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who's long been known for his strength, speed, and agility on the football field, is now training for perhaps the most grueling competition of his life.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) What's more difficult, when you're training for a debate prep, or for a championship game?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Debate is hard. Only reason it's hard, because you never know what direction they're gonna go in.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, that's true. Wait, but on a football field, you don't, either.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, a football field, you got another person. Doesn't matter where you go, I got to get him out of the way.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Friday Walker will face off against incumbent Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock in the first and only scheduled debate between the two.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN SPEECH\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 There's gonna be a debate. And I told him to quit running from me, quit running, 'cause I will catch you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NATIONAL US MAP\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The stakes in Georgia are incredibly high. This Senate race could potentially tip the balance of the Senate, and an early October surprise has been keeping Walker in the headlines.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF NEWS REPORTS\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRINCIPLES OR POWER? KEY REPUBLICANS RALLY AROUND \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , LIVE CNN\par \par \uc2 REPORTER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Walker allegedly paid for his ex-girlfriend to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) ...as a candidate, opposes abortions with no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "\u8230\'20\'26\uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  HAD URGED HER TO HAVE AN ABORTION\u8230\'20\'26" THE DAILY BEAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "\u8230\'20\'26HE URGED HER TO TERMINATE A SECOND PREGNANCY\u8230\'20\'26" THE NEW YORK TIMES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "MR. WALKER HAD BARELY BEEN INVOLVED IN THEIR NOW 10-YEAR-OLD SON'S LIFE\u8230\'20\'26" THE NEW YORK TIMES\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) An explosive report by "The Daily Beast" and later "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " alleges Walker paid for the cost of an abortion for an ex-girlfriend back in 2009. According to those accounts, she says Walker encouraged her to get a second abortion two years later, but she refused, and now has a 10-year-old son with Walker. ABC News has not independently confirmed the reports.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know initially last week, you were saying you weren't even sure who the woman was. But this week\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Which is true.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But at this point, you now know who she is?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever have a conversation with this woman, at any time, about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever, to your knowledge, give money to pay for the cost of an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, she's lying. Yeah, she's lying. Yes, she's lying.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And just to be absolutely clear, I know in the past we have said sometimes that there are things you don't remember. In this situation\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, that was, that was that was before, that was like, what, 20 years ago? Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, in this current situation\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \u8230\'20\'26are you saying a flat-out denial to any knowledge of an abortion? Or is possible it happened\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Flat-out denial. Nope.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) \u8230\'20\'26and you don't remember?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Flat-out denial. Lie, lie, lie, lie. And, you know, what's sad about it, they had, what was it, a receipt and had a check and had all that. They hadn't shown anything. Hadn't shown me, having a, saying something about an abortion. That's, that's what's terrible.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) This same woman says that you have a 10-year-old son who you've only seen three times. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Uh, well, that's not true. I've seen him a little bit more than that. But that's one of the things I don't get into because they make it difficult for me to see him. And that's one thing I say I won't do. When he's, when he's young, I'm not gonna sit there and make his life miserable.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Like this.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We met up with the father of four at an Atlanta gym.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 All I know is what I'm supposed to do. And what I'm supposed to do is go out and represent the Georgia people.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Many Republicans in Washington are standing firmly behind the 60-year-old in the wake of the controversy, applauding his outspoken stance against abortions.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Tell me about your, your position on abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 My position, I'm, I'm, I'm pro-life. I believe, I believe in, I believe in life. I'm a Christian. I believe in life.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the case of incest or rape or the mother's life is at stake, if she brings the pregnancy to full term?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, the Georgia Heartbeat Bill accounts for all that. And that's what I said. It's, it's a, it's in the democratic process of the people. And that's what I've, I've said. I say, "Guys, I believe in life. I'm a Christian."\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) His oldest son, whose name is Christian, says his dad is a liar.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF SOCIAL MEDIA POST\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER (SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 I've been silent, lie after lie after lie.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A conservative influencer with a large presence on social media says every member of Walker's family had begged him not to run in the first place.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Christian says, though, that you're an absent father. Do you disagree?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, well, I love Christian. And that's what's so funny. I love Christian. And, and, and I always will love Christian.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But I don't think anybody's questioning your love for your son.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, they know I've been with my son through everything. And that's what I'm saying. You know, Christian was someone that was in this same area where I was at. So, I was with him every minute of the day that I could be.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Another potential obstacle on the road to victory is Walker's turbulent past, as allegations of violence or threats by three women, including ex-wife Cindy, resurfaced.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) With regard to Christian, he talked about the atrocities against his mother, your ex-wife, Cindy. But you have no violent tendencies, at this point?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Never have with Christian or anyone else, and that's what's so sad. Never have.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Our Bob Woodruff spoke to both Cindy and Herschel back in 2008.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF 2008 INTERVIEW\par \par \uc2 CINDY WALKER (\uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 'S EX-WIFE)\par \par \uc2 He got a gun, and he put it to my temple.\par \par \uc2 BOD WOODWARD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Put the gun right to your temple?\par \par \uc2 CINDY WALKER (\uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 'S EX-WIFE)\par \par \uc2 Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BOD WOODWARD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And what did he say?\par \par \uc2 CINDY WALKER (\uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 'S EX-WIFE)\par \par \uc2 I'm gonna blow your effing brains out.\par \par \uc2 BOD WOODWARD (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you not remember something like that? Because you think that was another alter? Or do you wanna get out of having to talk about it?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No, no, no, no. I, I, if I can tell you all, I'm talking about everything else. If I can remember, I'll talk about it.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In 2008, Walker attributed his behavior to his diagnosis with Dissociative Identity Disorder. He now says he's symptom-free.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) For the people who are concerned about your mental health, obviously, you've been very public and talked about your Dissociative Identity Disorder. Do you still have that?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No. Well, you're, you're always gonna have it. You're always gonna be, everyone has a mental challenge. Right now, you know, do people know about redemption? Do people know about overcoming? You know, I've overcame so much in life. That's the reason I'm the best senator out there, because I know how to get up, and I don't sit there wallowing, and I take responsibility for who I am.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Do you have any symptoms of it?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No, no.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 None of that. You don't have to have symptoms. And I think that's the problem people have. You know, they don't understand it. And I have helped so many people. Because people have, have demonized mental health, and I will always be a champion for it. I think that's the problem. People have demonized mental health. People have challenges that they don't think they can get help from, so they hide it. They don't need to hide it. I let them know that you don't need to hide it. You can go get help.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, guys. Thank you, guys, so much.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Early on in Walker's campaign, he received thwacking from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a man he calls a family friend. The two have a relationship that spans several decades.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN SPEECH\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has got everything, and we love Herschel. Come up, Herschel.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You've said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a mentor for you. In what way?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Right. I've known Donald 40-something years, not just as no politician. I didn't know him as a politician. I knew him as a man. I knew him as a father, as a businessman. So, you know, I looked at what he was doing, and I said, oh, I, I can do that.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN SPEECH\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Let me hear, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Come on, get it out.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why did you decide to run?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I've been blessed. And I think anyone that has been blessed in America and sees what's going on, and don't try to stand up and speak truth, don't need to be in this country. Right now, this country is hurting. And when God has blessed you, you've got to give back and bless others.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming from humble beginnings, Walker grew up in the small town of Wrightsville, Georgia, one of seven in a Christian household. He describes his childhood as tough, a chubby kid with a stutter and no friends. But he says he took setbacks and turned them into success.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 And I've come from some tough, tough times. I grew up with nothing. You know, I, I know how it is to be poor. I grew up this stuttering, little fat kid, but God picked me out of this little town in middle Georgia, I've been all over the world. I've met people with more money than I've ever seen in my whole entire life. But one thing I don't like, when I see my family, and I call all of America my family, when I see them hurting, see them fighting, divided, and it's coming from Washington. It coming from all the leaders.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Just four weeks away from the election, the latest polls indicate Warnock has a slight lead in this razor-thin race. This afternoon, Senators Rick Scott and Tom Cotton attended an event to show their support for the Senate hopeful.\par \par \uc2 CLIP OF CAMPAIGN SPEECH\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Hey, thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But some of Walker's credentials and qualifications have been called into question, including claims that he graduated at the top of his class, that he trained with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the overall success of his business.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You don't have the experience in politics. What would you point to for people to say, you can trust me, you can elect me as your next senator, because?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, you can trust me, because I've shown it time and time again. I have proven myself time and time again. Through my business - business experience. They lie. I have proven myself through the athletic world. You know what? I've competed on the highest level in everything I've ever done, on the highest level on everything I ever done. You can't lie your way through that. You have to go out and you have to prove it through hard work, and that's what I've done.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) You're really passionate about this.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I am. I, you know, I told everyone I'm not running because I wanna be a senator. I'm running because I'm sick and tired. People are dying. People can't eat. Because of the people in Washington, what they're doing to people. And then they lie. This is not a new normal. Let's get it straight. This is not a new normal.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The emotion that you're feeling, where, what is that? Where is that coming from?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, that comes from me being a competitor. I work. And they ask me, am I'm ready for this. I'm ready for this.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our thanks to Linsey.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEENA DAVIS\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Up next, my interview with Geena Davis, her love for acting and how her "Thelma and Louise" co-star changed her life.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020221012eiab00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  ONE-ON-ONE WALKER KNOWS WOMAN ACCUSING HIM OF FUNDING AN ABORTION\b0\par\par\uc2 1,263 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WALKER, ABORTION\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Good evening. And it's great to have you with us here on a Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING NEWS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we begin tonight with the ABC News interview, one-on-one with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican Senate candidate in Georgia. As a candidate, Walker opposes abortion with no exceptions, but now finds himself battling claims from a woman who says he paid for her abortion, then encouraged her to get a second abortion, as well. She says she refused to get that second abortion. Tonight, as you'll see right here, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says she is lying.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker, in his first network interview since these allegations broke, one-on-one with our Linsey Davis. Linsey asks, does he know the woman? Has he talked with the woman? And just today in Georgia, Republican leaders embracing Walker, despite the controversy.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They know how tight this race is. Walker is running against Democratic Senator Rafael Warnock, one of just a handful of Senate races that will determine which party controls the Senate after the midterms. Tonight, Linsey Davis, with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , on the allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WALKER KNOWS WOMAN ACCUSING HIM OF FUNDING AN ABORTION\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Inside this Georgia gym, Republican Senate candidate and former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  sitting down for his first network interview, answering the claims against him.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) As you well know, there is a woman who reportedly says that she was in a long relationship with you, has a 10-year-old son with you, that you paid for the cost of an abortion. Were you ever aware of her having an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I know nothing about an abortion, and I know it's a lie. And I said it was a lie, and I just move on. So, October surprise is not going to faze me.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I know, initially, last week, you were saying you weren't even sure who the woman was.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Which is true.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But at this point, you now know who she is.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And have you had a conversation with her?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Not at all. So, I didn't know who it was until last week. And I went, "Oh." And I said, "That's not true." And, but they still ran with it. And so, I said, you know, that's not true. I would say that's a lie. I called it a lie. And right now, I said, I'm going to go back to campaigning, and people can continue to do whatever they want with the story.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) If I can just get you to say a yes or a no, did you ever have a conversation with this woman, at any time, about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Did you ever, to your knowledge, give money to pay for the cost of an abortion?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 No.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes, she's lying. Yes, she's lying. Yes, she's lying.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WALKER DENIES CLAIMS: "I KNEW IT WAS A LIE AND I SAID IT WAS A LIE?"\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News has not independently confirmed the woman's account, but "The Daily Beast" and "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report she showed them a receipt from the abortion clinic, a $700-check she says is from Walker and a get-well card she says he sent her after the abortion. She told "The Times" that Walker later urged her to have a second abortion when she became pregnant again, but she says she refused.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) The same woman says that you have a 10-year-old son who you've only seen three times. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, that's not true. I've seen him a little bit more than that, but that's one of the things I don't get into, because they make it difficult for me to see him. And that's one thing I said I won't do, when he's young. So, I'm not going to sit there and make his life miserable.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We also asked him about this video that's made headlines. His own son, Christian, from his first marriage, deeply critical of his father.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER (SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Family values, people? He has four kids, four different women, wasn't in the house raising one of them. You don't get to pretend you're some moral family guy. You don't get to pretend all these things.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Why is he coming out saying, "Don't try to pretend that you're a moral family man?"\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, like I said, I love Christian. And everybody that knows us know how much I love Christian, how much he's been with me, how much I've supported him, how much I've been with him.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) But in fairness, let me just interrupt one second.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Because loving somebody, you can love him from afar, but it's different to be present and involved.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 But that's what I'm saying. People know I've been present with Christian.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, when he says that you were an absent father, that's not a fair critique.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Oh, everyone knows that it's not. People that know me know that it's not.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We reached out to the woman to get her response to Walker's claims against her. So far, David, we have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) In the meantime, Linsey, where does this race stand, tonight? We know \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is challenging Raphael Warnock, a very close race. We know Republican leaders were actually in Georgia, today, embracing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) That's exactly right, David. Today, Walker was joined by Senator Rick Scott, who heads the Republican Senatorial Campaign Committee and decides which candidates should get crucial funding. David, this is a sign of just how important this race is. Republicans are counting on Walker to defeat Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock. Warnock has steered clear of Walker's personal life, but he has said, "We've seen a disturbing pattern, and it raises questions about who is actually ready to represent the people of Georgia." But make no mistake about it, David, the polls have them virtually in a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) We'll be watching it straight through election night. Linsey, thank you. And Linsey will have much more of her full interview with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  on ABC News Prime. That streams on ABC News Live, later tonight.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221012eiab0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 COUNTDOWN TO THE MIDTERMS CANDIDATES HIT THE TRAIL IN KEY RACES\b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNDER ATTACK\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, ATTACK, RUSSIA, STRIKES, BRIDGE\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, STRIKES, UKRAINE, BRIDGE, CIVILIANS, PUTIN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM, CANDIDATES, DEMS, GOP, FIVETHIRTYEIGHT\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, T.J., we turn now to "Your voice, Your Vote." We are just 29 days away from the crucial midterm elections with the House and Senate at stake. Candidates in key races are dashing across their states in a final push for votes. Congressional correspondent Rachel Scott is right here in studio with us and the very latest. Rachel, good morning,\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CANDIDATES HIT THE TRAIL IN KEY RACES WITH LESS THAN A MONTH TO GO\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Hey, Whit, good morning. Well believe it or not we are just one month out from election day for what is shaping up to be one of the most consequential midterm elections in decades.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With just one month to go, candidates are crisscrossing their states making their final pitches to voters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 Send me to DC, and I'll be that 51st vote.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Republicans are fighting to win back the House and the Senate, and they see the path to victory through several key battleground states. In Georgia, GOP leaders rallying behind Senate Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  even as his campaign is rocked by controversies, facing allegations he paid a woman to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WALKER (REP) (SENATE CANDIDATE GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 This here abortion thing is false, it's a lie.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker opposes abortion, no exceptions. His democratic rival, Senator Raphael Warnock, making it clear he supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 SEN RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 I support reproductive choice. My opponent on the other hand is talking about a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Pennsylvania talk show host and Republican candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz is facing off against Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , back on the campaign trail after suffering a stroke just months ago.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (DEM) (LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 Oz doesn't care about Pennsylvania, never has.\par \par \uc2 DR MEHMET OZ (REP) (SENATE CANDIDATE PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 I'm the guy for change and Pennsylvanians want change.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And in Nevada, vulnerable Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , is hoping to fend off GOP candidate \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a former state attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT (REP) (SENATE CANDIDATE NEVADA)\par \par \uc2 Folks, we only one shot to save this state and save America. Let's go out and get it done. Get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Both parties framing this election as a referendum. For Republicans it's all about the economy and inflation, while Democrats hope abortion rights will become a defining issue. playing out across the gubernatorial races too.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO THE MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF PLAY IN CONGRESS AHEAD OF HIGH STAKES ELECTION\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, and election denials have also been taken center stage this election. FiveThirtyEight found that 60% of Americans will have a candidate who denies the election on the ballot. We know this is especially critical in those gubernatorial and secretary of state races, which will have a major influence on how elections are carried out in the future, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Rachel, let's take a closer look here and break down the state of play as we head into these midterms, starting with the Senate.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, Whit, you got it. It doesn't get more high stakes than this, the Senate is split 50/50, as you see here, but the Vice President casts the tie-breaking vote, giving Democrats the edge. Now there are 35 seats on the ballot this November for the Senate races. Republicans only need a net gain of one seat to get the majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Our friends at FiveThirtyEight believe that Democrats are favored to keep the Senate. But I want you just to take a look at how dramatically this changes if they lose just one key race. For example, if Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wins in Georgia the chances of Republicans taking the Senate go up to 61%. And this is exactly why we are seeing Republican leaders rally behind Walker in the way that they are, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, a single race can make a big difference. And in the House, Rachel, Republicans feeling a bit more confident there.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yeah, they definitely are. And this is all going to come down to roughly a dozen toss-up seats. Republicans need to net five seats in order to take the House. And FiveThirtyEight shows that they have a 70% chance of doing just that. But their odds have declined following the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe Versus Wade, Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) All right, Rachel, thanks so much. Robin.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221028eiaa0005o\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMERICA STRONG THE PILOT, THE PASSENGER, AND THE SURPRISE\b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, PUTIN, WAR, ZELENSKYY, AIRSTRIKES, UKRAINE\par \par \uc2 UKRAINE, RUSSIA, BIDEN, WAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN CONDEMNS RUSSIAN ATTACK\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, RAMOS, GONZALES, CANTU, BRENNAND, PROBE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICER FIRED AFTER SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 RACISM, RESIGNATION, MARTINEZ\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIAL RESIGNS AFTER RACIST REMARKS\par \par \uc2 SEARCH, CAMPBELL, KNAPE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PARENTS PLEA FOR HELP\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, RUSSIA, CYBERATTACK, HACKING, KILLNET\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CYBERATTACKS ON U.S. AIRPORTS\par \par \uc2 IRAN, PROTESTS, AMINI, ESMAILZADEH, SHAKARAMI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY PROTESTS IN IRAN\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, WEINSTEIN, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOATERS RESCUED FROM SHARKS\par \par \uc2 FLU, VACCINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE INDEX\par \par \uc2 OLDHAM, BREAST CANCER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: #AMERICASTRONG\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Finally, the pilot, the passenger, and the surprise.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jyrl Oldham, a mother of three from Vineyard, Utah, has been battling stage 2 breast cancer. Through hair loss and chemo, her family with her every step of the way. Through treatment, they would come up with ways to encourage her. And they told her they would send her to Hawaii when she beats this. Well, she did, ringing the cancer bell, boarding the flight, the Southwest crew hearing about Jyrl, and just listen to the pilot.\par \par \uc2 PILOT (MALE)\par \par \uc2 I would like to add a special welcome to a special guest on today's flight. We have a passenger who's headed to Hawaii with us today to celebrate her victory over late-stage breast cancer. Please join me in giving our Jyrl Oldham a round of applause.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And, right here, tonight...\par \par \uc2 JYRL OLDHAM (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Jyrl on that moment.\par \par \uc2 JYRL OLDHAM (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 They didn't know me, but they were all just, I could just feel the love. I could feel the support.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, Jyrl, and her message.\par \par \uc2 JYRL OLDHAM (BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR)\par \par \uc2 You're not alone. There are so many people out there who are rooting for you. And that helps you to be so much stronger than you ever thought you could be.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we're rooting for you, Jyrl. Love the smile. Good night.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020221012eiaa0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 REPUBLICANS STANDING BY WALKER OFFICIALS SHOWING FULL SUPPORT DESPITE ALLEGATIONS\b0\par\par\uc2 663 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 EXPLOSION, BRIDGE, UKRAINE, WAR, RUSSIA\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, WAR, NORTH KOREA, MISSILE LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 GERMANY, TRAIN, SABOTAGE, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GERMAN OFFICIALS SAY TRAINS WERE SABOTAGED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CUT CABLES CAUSED MASSIVE 3 HOUR HALT IN SERVICE\par \par \uc2 WALKER, WARNOCK, ABORTION, BAN, CRUZ, SCOTT, DUNCAN\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now to politics. And former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  fighting those reports that he paid for a girlfriend's abortion and later urged her to have another.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: REPUBLICANS STANDING BY WALKER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OFFICIALS SHOWING FULL SUPPORT DESPITE ALLEGATIONS\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The allegations casting a shadow on his Republican campaign for Senate. ABC's MaryAlice Parks is on Capitol Hill with the very latest. Good morning, MaryAlice.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Erielle, good morning. Yeah, we're almost exactly a month from the midterm elections and this race in Georgia has just been racked by these headlines. We've seen some Republican leaders, especially those here in Washington rushing to their candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's defense while others in the state of Georgia not so much.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, Republican leaders not only defending GOP Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but gearing up to stand by his side. The National Republican Senatorial Committee chair Senator Rick Scott heads to Georgia to campaign with Walker, who finds himself tangled in allegations he paid for a woman to have an abortion despite his staunch anti-abortion rights stance on the trail. Scott saying in a statement, "I'm on Herschel's team. They picked the wrong Georgian to mess with."\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 Let me hear \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , come on.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Other Republicans too still pushing to elect Walker.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 I believe \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to be the next senator. I'm proud to support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Though some Republicans in the state of Georgia distancing themselves from the Senate candidate.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR GEOFF DUNCAN (REP) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  won the primary because he scored a bunch of touchdowns back in the '80s and he was Donald Trump's friend. And now, we've moved forward several months on the calendar and that's no longer a recipe to win.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Walker has so far denied the allegations published in "The Daily Beast" that he paid for an abortion or pressured a woman into one.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  PUSHING BACK ALLEGATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIGHTING REPORTS HE PAID FOR A WOMAN'S ABORTION\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 This here, this abortion thing, is false, it's a lie.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " now reporting Walker urged the same woman to have a second abortion two years later, but she refused, giving birth to his child instead.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEORGIA'S TIGHT RACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: POLLING SHOWS WARNOCK & WALKER CLOSE IN NUMBERS\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The firestorm roiling the pivotal race just a month before election day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D) : 47.7%; \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) : 42.7%\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The latest polling showing the race neck and neck as Walker and his Democratic opponent, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , make their final case to Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR REVEREND RAPHAEL WARNOCK (DEM) (GEORGIA)\par \par \uc2 What the state needs right now is some public servants who are committed to the people.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (REP) (GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE)\par \par \uc2 I'm here to win the seat for the Georgia people because the Georgia people need a winner.\par \par \uc2 MARYALICE PARKS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Now, Senator Warnock and Walker are set to debate this Friday. It's expected to be their only debate. And you can imagine the issue of abortion rights at the very least will be front and center. Erielle?\par \par \uc2 ERIELLE RESHEF (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It certainly will be. MaryAlice Parks on Capitol Hill for us, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020221026eia90002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 3,255 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 WAR, UKRAINE, RUSSIA, PUTIN, BRIDGE, CRIMEA\par \par \uc2 NUCLEAR, WAR, PUTIN, BIDEN, MISSILES, N. KOREA\par \par \uc2 KIRBY, BIDEN, NUCLEAR, ARMAGEDDON, PUTIN, JONG-\uc2 UN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN WARNS OF NUCLEAR 'ARMAGEDDON' AMID PUTIN'S THREATS\par \par \uc2 MULLEN, JONG-\uc2 UN\uc2 , MISSILES, BIDEN, PUTIN, NUKES, UK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HIGH ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ONE-ON-ONE WITH ADM MIKE MULLEN\par \par \uc2 MIDTERM MONITOR, WALKER, SENATE, GOP, DEMS, 538\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: TUESDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 30 DAYS UNTIL MIDTERMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: YOUR VOICE: YOUR VOTE 2022\par \par \uc2 2022, WALKER, \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , BIDEN, SAUDI, PARDON, BORDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Let's bring in our Roundtable, now, "Washington Post" White House reporter Yasmeen Abutaleb, ABC News senior White House correspondent Mary Bruce, Jane Coaston, host of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " 'The Argument' podcast, and ABC News senior national correspondent Terry Moran. Good morning to all of you. And, Terry, I'm gonna go straight to you and let's start with "The Daily Beast" reporting and there's been reporting since then, definitely an October surprise for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's campaign. What kind of impact do you think this will have?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HERSCHEL WALKER DENIES REIMBURSING WOMAN FOR ABORTION\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not as great an impact, obviously, as it would've had 10 years ago, and that there is a sense now that as long as the guy is on your team, that the most important issue in American politics isn't what you believe in, it's who do you hate? And while Walker is not a hater, parties are now that antagonistic that it's clear millions of Republican voters will overlook something that would have destroyed a candidacy just a few years ago. The problem for Walker is, what else is he running on, except that he was a football player and a Christian? And while his football record can't be questioned, at all, I think this does damage him, and he doesn't really have a lot of other things to say.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jane Coaston, do you agree with that? And, and look specifically, if you will, at independent voters. This is a man who says he is staunchly anti-abortion. He's got a conservative son who's also speaking out against him.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL 'OCTOBER SURPRISE' IN GEORGIA SENATE RACE HURT GOP?\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Well, I think that the, the choice will not be that independents and Republican leaning voters will then vote for Raphael Warnock. I think that, that some of them might just stay home. Look back at the special election in Alabama involving \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2 , where you saw a host of Republicans saying, like, I just can't do this. They leave that name off their ticket, or they just stay home, and so I think that this could depress some votes. I don't know if it's going to activate a ton of Democratic voters or get a bunch of people to switch to Raphael Warnock.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I do think it's going to cause a bunch of people to say, hey, this is a person who said that they opposed abortion in almost every circumstance, but when it came to it, he demanded allegedly a woman have an abortion twice, a woman who already had a child with him who he has allegedly basically ignored his entire life. And, you know, I, I keep saying that this, if this is a conversation about abortion on demand, well, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  demanded the woman have an abortion twice. And I think that for a lot of voters, particularly pro-life voters, they're stuck between a rock and a hard place, and I think they might decide, okay, I'm gonna vote for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , and then I'm going to not vote in this particular race.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Mary, you have seen, as, as Terry alluded to, national Republicans quickly come to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's defense. You've got Rick Scott going down there from Florida. You've got Tom Cotton from Arkansas going down there, as well. Do they believe him, or is it just those midterms creeping up?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOP RALLIES BEHIND WALKER AMID ABORTION SCANDAL\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think this has a lot less to do with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and everything to do with Georgia. I think if this were a different state, this would probably very likely be playing out differently. But because you do have the midterms hanging in the balance, control of the Senate very much could come down to this state, and that is why you are seeing, once again, Republicans at all levels, you know, coalescing around him. You mentioned you have Rick scott, the head of the Republican Senatorial Campaign Committee going down on Tuesday to, to lend his support, and we have seen this time and time again, where Republicans are willing to look past allegations because the prize is just so big.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) They're willing to say that their national policy priorities, their ambitions outweigh any concerns about personal behavior, but it is a risky gamble. It always is. And I think it's one of those reasons, you heard \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the top Republican in the Senate, say just a few weeks ago that he has questions about and concerns about the quality of their candidates.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Exactly what I was going to come to you on, Yasmeen, so pick it up from there. There are issues with other senatorial candidates, in particular, but people are just coalescing around them.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2022 MIDTERM ELECTIONS LESS THAN A MONTH AWAY\par \par \uc2 YASMEEN ABUTALEB (WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 I think that's right. It's also too late to replace \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  on the ballot with someone else, so they really, I think Republicans feel like they don't have a choice but to stick with him. The Senate is 50/50 right now and so if they want any hope of controlling the Senate, they can't just cut loose on this race. But you, like Mary said, you, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said he is concerned about the quality of candidates particularly in the Senate. I think because these races are statewide, and he even said the quality just matters more than in the House and, of course, it's a tighter margin.\par \par \uc2 YASMEEN ABUTALEB (WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 But you've seen, you know, Oz in, in Pennsylvania, Blake Masters in Arizona, you've seen a number of these candidates who just, you know, Republicans feel like they could run away with these states, and instead these, these states are really going to come down to the wire.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Terry, 30 days to the midterms, less than a month to go. The Democrats were feeling pretty hopeful about their chances. Do you still get that sense?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Nope. I think the air went out of that balloon, in part because, look, the economy is so tough for so many people, food prices, rent spiking, if they've got retirement funds, those are evaporating. And even the issue of abortion, which did drive several special elections in that remarkable referendum result in Kansas, while there are millions of people for whom that will be the number-one issue, I just think the economic headwinds are so tough.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Biden is, he just doesn't have the oomph as a candidate anymore. People don't really want him around, and he can't really make his case that, that I don't think the Democrats are in any better place. And I would also say, in this country and in other countries polls are broken, right? It is clear that lots of people on the right just don't answer anymore. They were worse in 2020 than they were in 2016. And so you look at those polls, it's close. If it's close, it's a Republican win.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Jane, pick up on that too and, and the economy, and we've got the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cutting oil, we've got \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cutting oil production, which could lead to higher gas prices.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GAS PRICES LIKELY TO RISE AFTER OPEC+ OIL PRODUCTION CUT\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Right, and I think that for a lot of people, that is going to, you know, have a big impact on how they decide to vote. But, what I keep thinking is, what are they voting for, then? I know that it's a midterm election and in general midterm elections are we don't like this guy elections. But, with the Republican Party, what, what are they offering in comparison? What's their fix on inflation? Their fix on inflation is, let's scream some more about books in schools?\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, I think that for a lot of American voters, you can see why there's increased interest in Libertarian Party candidates and then third party candidates, because you see these two sides who offer very few actual solutions, and you have Republicans seemingly rooting for a recession, and then you see a lot of...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMING UP: EXCLUSIVE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PIERRE THOMAS REPORTS ON THE CHICAGO POLICE DEPARTMENT'S EFFORTS TO CURB THE CITY'S GUN VIOLENCE EPIDEMIC\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) So, what does it boil down to?\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think it boils down to this is a tough time for a lot of people, and politics isn't offering a lot of answers. And I think that, for a lot of people, they're seeing...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Not exactly, not exactly getting them to the polls with that.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) No, it's not getting to the polls, and I think that that's something you're going to see in the midterms, is that if people, if we see in December and after these midterms that, you know, voting was not as high as people expected it to be, I think it would be, because people look at a lot of the - people running and say, what are you, how, what can you do for me? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Mary, you, you've been out in the country, but you were also, to talk to voters, but you were also on that trip with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to Saudi Arabia. So, let's go back to \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , for a minute. That was a huge disappointment for the White House. I know they're saying it wasn't about oil. If it wasn't about oil at all, I, I, they, they missed out on something.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S SAUDI TRIP FACES SCRUTINY AFTER OPEC+ OIL CUT\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It was about oil, and, and you know, the President took a huge risk in going on that trip, but they thought it was worth it to try and bring down oil prices, and now the Saudis are really, you know, thumbing their nose at the President on this. Biden says he has options, here. He's deciding what to pursue, but the options aren't great, right? I mean, he can try and take some kind of action to limit \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's control over the energy market. There's legislative options. We've seen him not rule out the possibility of rolling back some sanctions on Venezuela, but none of that is immediate. You have the midterms 30 days away. This is a huge risk for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARY BRUCE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) I think the big question now is just how do they play this? How do they message this? You know, are you gonna to hear them getting out there and touting more steps that the, the administration has taken to try and lessen dependency on oil, tout the other areas of the economy that are growing, but it's a real challenge for them. Will they, to Jane's point, note, you know, that Republicans have a lack of alternatives. I think you'll hear them do that, but how much will they lean into that, because this is, as you noted, the October surprise they were not hoping to have.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and, and Yasmeen, you've seen some influential Democrats. You've seen Chris Murphy, Dick Durbin call for the US to end its alliance with the Saudis. Is that going to happen?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DURBIN: TIME TO 'IMAGINE A WORLD' WITHOUT US-SAUDI ALLIANCE\par \par \uc2 YASMEEN ABUTALEB (WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 I don't think so because - these were the same Democrats who when President Biden was getting ready to go to Saudi Arabia put out statements saying the US needed to overhaul or, or, or just end its relationship with Saudi Arabia. But it was mostly Democrats who were very critical of Biden's meeting with MBS. But, you know, this has, this has always been a sticking point for the US that, especially for President Biden who says he wants to put human rights at the center of his foreign policy agenda, I think there are just certain political realities that every US president has contended with, with Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 YASMEEN ABUTALEB (WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 I think most, if given the choice, would not want to have the relationship, because it is so problematic. But no one has actually been able to cut off that relationship because of the oil relationship, because of the security relationship and the need for a partner like that in the Middle East, even with all of the problems and headaches that that relationship comes with.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Terry, there was some, some little side, good news, I suppose, or a distraction for the Biden administration with the marijuana laws or the marijuana imprisonment. Those guys can...\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN PARDONS THOUSANDS FOR MARIJUANA POSSESSION\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) It's, it's a gesture, but one that would be popular with, essentially, the Democratic base. It doesn't affect a lot of people, but it crystallizes, in our oldest president, the desires of the youngest Americans, which is that it doesn't make sense anymore to, to young Americans and lots of Americans. I mean, let's face it, weed is now kind of a, a national pastime in lots and lots of places. And this is, as I say, only a few thousand people will be affected, but it also gets at a deeper problem, that the drug laws, you know, were vicious, in some ways.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) These are people who had no violence, no, they weren't dealing. They weren't doing anything. They were, they were holding a drug that is widely popular, has been for 50 years, and the President sending a message, "I'm on your side," hopes that'll resonate a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Jane, I, I wanna go back to what Republicans, in particular, are campaigning on. They're, they're kind of going dark, here, talking about crime, but there was quite the sound bite last night from Tommy Tuberville. Let's listen to this.\par \par \uc2 VIDEO CLIP: SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOMMY TUBERVILLE (REP) (ALABAMA)\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Party, they have a majority. They could stop this crime, today. They're not soft on crime. They're pro-crime. They want crime. They want reparation, because they think the people that do the crime are owed that. Bull (censored by network). They are not owed that.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Whoa.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Wow, he really, he has not improved since he was...\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) That was at a Trump rally.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Yes, that was very much at a Trump rally. He really hasn't improved since he was coach at Cincinnati, has he? But I, I think that what you're seeing from this, one, is, it's a Trump rally. He's talking to a very specific audience. But I think also it's this idea, specifically with regard to marijuana, that the people who use marijuana are this, you know, are Black men, when, actually, marijuana use is incredibly popular across a wild specter, including older people. If you go down to the Villages, in Florida, you see a lot of Trump voters who are eating a lot of edibles.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And so, I think that changing the scheduling of, I mean, I think that's the more important part of Biden's effort, is to look at changing the scheduling of marijuana, which is currently listed as being the same as LSD or heroin, which, you know, I think we're all pretty clear it isn't. But I, I think that this is an effort to go back to kind of that 1980s tough-on-crime attitude, particularly coming from a, you know, a political party that isn't offering much of an alternative.\par \par \uc2 JANE COASTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talks about how we need to fund the police, Republicans are responding with, no, no, no, no, no - yes? And so, I think that it just, it's, it's a don't know. I was going to say that it was kind of like a retro take on a tough-on-crime attitude. But if you're just screaming about how Black people want reparations because they're the people who commit crimes, that's a, that's a, that's a throwback we don't need.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And, and Yasmeen, I just, I also want to talk about immigration. You had Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly criticizing the President's handling of immigration. We have about 40 seconds, here. Where does this go? Is there really a strategy?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN ADMIN GRAPPLES WITH MIGRANT SURGE AT SOUTHERN BORDER\par \par \uc2 YASMEEN ABUTALEB (WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE REPORTER)\par \par \uc2 Well, I think Mark Kelly has actually been critical of Biden's immigration strategy for some time, now. It's not actually new to the election. He was critical last year, sort of preparing for these attacks. I don't know that it goes anywhere, because Democrats, this has always been an issue for them, and they know if the race is about immigration, they're losing. So, I think it's a challenge. I think Mark Kelly is, is trying to distance himself from Biden, here, but immigration is just generally not a winning issue for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) And we got a long way to go on that. Thanks to all of you for joining us, this morning.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Coming up, after a two-year surge in gun violence nationwide, new data shows it may be trending downward from the pandemic peak. And Pierre Thomas has rare access inside the \uc2 Chicago Police Department\uc2 's latest efforts to combat the spike. That's next.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 MARTHA RADDATZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (OC) Pierre Thomas' report on Chicago gun violence is next. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020221013eia900007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 INTRODUCTION TO THE 09:30 HALF-HOUR\b0\par\par\uc2 494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEATHER ALERT: COLD BLAST\par \par \uc2 COLD 